/* Machine translations of Logik Philippi (uncorrected OCR), in the manner of Kemp Smith: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['philippi/kemp'] = {"philippi-s01-001":["Everything in the world conforms to certain rules and laws. Even those things which seem to change quite without rule are bound by certain laws. That it often seems to us as if certain things happened without any rules arises either from our being unable to see with our understanding the rules according to which they happen, or from the fact that their laws are not the laws of the greatest perfection, according to which, in our opinion, they ought to happen. Thus, for example, the changes of the weather seem to occur without rules and laws. None the less the weather constantly alternates according to determinate laws, which, however, we do not see, on account of our limited reason. A vicious man seems to act without rules; yet he orders all his actions exactly in accordance with the rules which vice prescribes to him. There is, then, no doubt that all things in the world act according to certain rules. But as all things are regular, so we find it also with the human understanding. Everyone employs it according to certain rules; everyone thinks, judges, etc., etc., according to certain rules. It is, however, well to note here:",["Restated the long second sentence's 'either … or' clause with the pronouns resolved ('they', 'their laws' = the things that seem to happen without rules).","'Lasterhaffter' read as 'Lasterhafter' (vicious man); 'wechsein' corrected to 'wechseln'. The passage ends with a colon, as in the source."]],"philippi-s01-002":["In some subjects the exercise precedes the rules; in others, on the contrary, the rules precede the exercise. That is to say, some subjects act according to certain laws without being conscious of them; others perform regular actions, but are at the same time conscious of the rules in accordance with which they do something.",["'Ausübung' rendered 'exercise' (practice); 'Subjecten' rendered 'subjects'."]],"philippi-s01-003":["It is just the same with the understanding. Some employ it without being conscious of the rules according to which they employ it. Others make use of it, but at the same time know the laws and rules according to which they make use of it. From this we see that the use of the understanding is twofold:",["Passage ends with a colon, leading into the numbered division that follows."]],"philippi-s01-004":["1. The natural use, when one is not conscious of the rules of the understanding. Thus, for example, a child learns to walk. In doing so it observes countless rules, so that Borellus, who wrote *de motu animalium et infantium*, applies the whole of mathematical art in order to exhibit all the rules which a child exercises when it begins to walk. For all that, the child is not conscious of the rules which it exercises. In the same way little children speak, and in doing so observe an uncommon number of rules, but they do not know at all that they observe them.",["OCR 'Bev' read as 'Bey' ('bey dem allen'), 'bewüst' as 'bewust'. Borellus's Latin title kept in italics as in the source."]],"philippi-s01-005":["Thus the understanding too is subject to certain rules according to which it acts, even when it is not conscious of them, and even when it errs, though in that case the laws are of a lesser perfection.",["The final clause ('obgleich Gesetzen …') is elliptical; I supplied 'in that case' to give the sense."]],"philippi-s01-006":["The understanding is the faculty of universal concepts. It operates in part by abstracting universal rules and judgments from single cases or experiences, as, for example, in morals, jurisprudence, physics or the fine sciences [schöne Wissenschaften]. This kind of understanding is entitled the *sensus communis* (*sens commun*), or the common understanding. The common understanding becomes a sound understanding (*le bon sens*) when it is correct; here experience and effect precede the rules.",["'Regehi' corrected to 'Regeln'. 'theils' has no corresponding 'theils' in this passage; translated 'in part' without completing it.","'Vermögen' = faculty; 'schöne Wissenschaften' kept literal, i.e. belles-lettres and the like."]],"philippi-s01-007":["The understanding which acts according to certain universal laws of which it is conscious is the scientific, learned understanding.",["'so er sich bewußt ist' resolved as a relative clause: 'of which it is conscious'."]],"philippi-s01-008":["2. The artificial use is that in accordance with which one is conscious of the rules of the understanding.",[]],"philippi-s01-009":["Note. This has been premised in order that we may now be able to give correct definitions of the common and of the learned understanding.",["'Definitiones' rendered 'definitions'."]],"philippi-s01-010":["The skill [Fertigkeit] of the understanding in its natural use is the common understanding.",["'Fertigkeit' rendered 'skill'; a choice, since it is not in the fixed term list."]],"philippi-s01-011":["The skill of the understanding in its artificial use is the learned, artificial or higher understanding.",[]],"philippi-s01-012":["All exercises of the understanding, whether of the common or of the learned, all exercises and modes of knowledge [Erkenntnisse] of the understanding are universal; by this they are distinguished from the sensible modes of knowledge, which are singular.",["'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge' (plural)."]],"philippi-s01-013":["But the common understanding takes in its exercises a quite different course from that of the learned. The common understanding knows the universal only *in concreto*, that is, it draws the universal from single propositions which rest on experience. The higher understanding knows the universal *in abstracto*, that is, it draws single propositions from universal ones. For example: if someone has given another something to keep, and it is lost, ought the depositor [Deponent] alone to bear the loss, or ought it to be made good by the person to whom the deposit was entrusted? If this question is put to common reason, and a universal rule is demanded of it for such cases, it cannot supply one at once, but first appeals to experience. It takes a single example before it and judges that example by experience; from experience it then draws a universal proposition and rule, which it afterwards applies to other cases. In this way most of the rules in jurisprudence have arisen.",["Divided the second and third sentences' 'oder' constructions with 'that is' to make the contrast between concrete and abstract explicit.","'Deponent' taken as the depositor, 'derjenige dem das Depositum anvertrauet worden' as the depositary; 'Satz' = proposition."]],"philippi-s01-014":["The common understanding, then, borrows its principles [*Principia*] from experience. Its distinctness [Deutlichkeit], however, it derives from particular single cases. If we consider the common understanding from this point of view, we see that it surpasses the sciences in distinctness, in obviousness [Augenscheinlichkeit] and in evidence. For everything that it propounds is taken from experience and appeals to the senses. (As regards extent [Weitläufftigkeit], however, the sciences have the advantage.)",["'Weitläufftigkeit' rendered 'extent'; it could also mean comprehensiveness or elaborateness.","'Augenscheinlichkeit' rendered 'obviousness'; 'Evidentz' kept as 'evidence'."]],"philippi-s01-015":["This obviousness and distinctness, however, which the common understanding has, are wholly lacking in the learned use of the understanding, or the learned understanding; for it considers things *in abstracto*, separated from all sensibility.",[]],"philippi-s01-016":["It is therefore quite opposed to the fine sciences, which speak to our senses; and if one abstracts constantly and devotes oneself to remote subtleties, these [sciences] vanish altogether, as the age of the Scholastics proves.",["'Er' = the learned understanding; 'sie' resolved as the fine sciences [schöne Wissenschaften], supplied in brackets."]],"philippi-s01-017":["The laws and rules of our understanding are either subjective or objective.",["'subiectiue oder obiectiue' normalised to 'subjective or objective'."]],"philippi-s01-018":["The subjective rules of the understanding, that is, those rules in accordance with which we actually make use of our understanding, are dealt with by psychology. Psychology is concerned in general with our soul, and therefore also with its powers, and consequently with the understanding. But it considers the powers and their laws subjectively, that is, without regard to the object with which these powers are occupied, and to the rules which must be observed in the process.",["'Kräfte' rendered 'powers'; 'Seele' 'soul'. 'subjectivisch' rendered 'subjectively'."]],"philippi-s01-019":["The rules, however, in accordance with which we ought to make use of our understanding, are the subject of [the science of] logic. It therefore sets forth the objective laws of the use of reason.",["The German says literally 'the rules … is logic'; I supplied '[the science of]' and 'subject of' to complete the sense. 'Vernunfft' is rendered 'reason', though the previous sentence speaks of the understanding."]],"philippi-s01-020":["Metaphysics, to be sure, is also a science of the laws of reason, but only of pure reason, which is not mixed with sensibility and draws its principles [*Principia*] solely from reason and not from experience. It therefore considers our modes of knowledge as we should have them if we had no body. Logic, on the other hand, borrows its principles partly from reason, partly from experience.",["'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s01-021":["Note. Physics takes its principles [*Principia*] solely from experience; morals has its principles from reason.",[]],"philippi-s01-022":["The logic of common and sound reason, which is otherwise also entitled natural wit, is distinguished from the logic of learned reason. The first sets forth its rules from concrete propositions, from experience; the other will establish them from universal grounds. The first is nothing but a critic [Critic]; the other is a doctrine [Doctrin].",["Restated the first sentence's loose 'sonst wird sie auch … genannt' as a relative clause. 'Witz' rendered 'wit'.","'Critic' and 'Doctrin' kept close to the German; the contrast is between a mere critique (judging) and a doctrine (a body of teaching)."]],"philippi-s01-023":["What is a doctrine?",[]],"philippi-s01-024":["A connected body of universal rules by which one learns a certain exercise of the understanding is entitled a doctrine. The faculty, however, of judging by an inner sensation whether a certain product of the understanding is good or bad is criticism (*Critic*). All the fine arts, for instance poetry, aesthetics and the like, therefore admit of no doctrine, but only of criticism; for taste cannot be learnt by rules. No one can be taught to have witty ideas. The rules which we have in aesthetics serve only to judge a product of taste. They are drawn from sensibility, and do not directly help to make a product perfect, though they sometimes do so indirectly, through trial.",["*Critic* (Kant's term, here 'criticism') is the faculty of judging by feeling; I keep the contrast with *Doctrin* ('doctrine').","*Sinnlichkeit* = sensibility; *Empfindung* = sensation, as fixed in the terms list, though here it means something like inner feeling.","*Einfälle* rendered 'ideas'; 'witzige' = 'witty'."]],"philippi-s01-025":["The logic of common and sound reason serves as a *catharcticon*, a means of purging the understanding of errors and prejudices. The logic of learned reason, on the other hand, is an aid, an *organon* of the sciences.",["*Catharcticon* and *organon* kept in Latin/Greek as technical terms; the transcript's 'Catharcticon' is spelled with an extra r."]],"philippi-s01-026":["*Logica naturalis*, which is the science of the rules of the common and natural understanding. Common reason is necessary for determining ends, in that it teaches us the *principia dijudicativa*. For learning teaches us only to choose skilful means to the attainment of ends. The fine arts fall within its province, since here everything depends upon taste.",["'Gebieth' = province/domain; 'ihr' resolved as referring to common reason (*die gemeine Vernunft*).","'Erhaltung der Zwecke' rendered 'attainment of ends', by judgment of sense; literal sense is 'preservation/obtaining'.","*Gelehrsamkeit* rendered 'learning'."]],"philippi-s01-027":["*Logica artificialis*. The logic of learning is the science of the rules of the correct use of the learned understanding.",[]],"philippi-s01-028":["Now that the necessary ideas have been set forth in advance, a correct definition can be given.",[]],"philippi-s01-029":["Logic is a philosophy upon the universal laws of the correct use of the understanding and of reason.",["OCR 'Vernunft t' corrected to 'Vernunft'."]],"philippi-s01-030":["We shall now analyse and expound each concept in this definition, but must first note the following.",["The sentence is smoothed into two; 'Erklärung' rendered 'definition'."]],"philippi-s01-031":["*Logica* comes from λόγος (*ratio*), and is also otherwise entitled *philosophia rationalis*; not because it philosophises through reason, but because it philosophises from and upon reason. The rational here is not the form but the object; for the form can be rational whatever the object or matter may be. For example, in physics the object is bodies, in mathematics magnitudes; in both the form is rational, in both one philosophises through reason. So one can philosophise about the world, about men, about folly and unreason, in short about any object (*Gegenstand*); the form is always rational.",["OCR 'Aöyos' read as λόγος; 'Physich' as 'Physick'; 'Vernunft t' corrected; 'philoßophiren' normalised.","'die Körper' (sic) rendered 'bodies'."]],"philippi-s01-032":["In the definition we said that logic philosophises upon the universal laws of the correct use [of the understanding], and so on.",["OCR 'tue' read as 'die'. Words in brackets supplied from the preceding definition, as 'etc. etc.' breaks off."]],"philippi-s01-033":["The laws which determine how the understanding ought to think and judge correctly are entitled the objective laws. The laws, however, according to which our understanding actually judges are entitled (as we have already stated above) the subjective laws.",["*heissen* rendered 'is entitled'."]],"philippi-s01-034":["Whence does it come that our understanding does not act according to the objective laws?",[]],"philippi-s01-035":["That our understanding does not judge and think as it ought, or does not act according to the objective laws, arises solely from sensibility. Even if we had the lowest degree of understanding, we should never judge falsely. It is the want of restriction [*Einschränkung*] that alone would make our judgments faulty.",["The last sentence is obscure in the transcript; 'Mangel der Einschränkung' is rendered literally, and the sense (what, in the argument, would be restricted) is doubtful. Also, the 'Wenn…' sentence is rather elliptical."]],"philippi-s01-036":["That we so often err and judge and think falsely is due to sensibility, by which we understand everything in man that is other than reason. So often, then, as we err, our judgments follow not the understanding but sensibility, and our judgments then take place not according to objective but according to subjective rules.",["OCR '»Sinnlichkeit' corrected; 'Obicctiven Subiectiven Pegeln' read as 'objectiven, subjectiven Regeln'."]],"philippi-s01-037":["NB. We know the rules which our understanding ought to follow, and yet we transgress them. Whence does this come? The cause is that we do not properly study the subject of our understanding, with which psychology deals. It is strange that, although so many scholars are always writing of how one ought to be, very few show how one actually is. They prescribe rules for how one ought to think and judge, and in the very act of writing this they do not themselves wholly observe them. It is maintained that there are faults in the subject throughout the whole of nature. If a bomb is thrown at a magazine and does not fall there, the fault is not to be sought in the subject: the bomb has moved quite exactly according to the laws of nature in respect of gravity, but not according to the wish and intention of the marksman who threw it.",["OCR '^\\ ir' read as 'wir'; '\"wird' as 'wird'; 'Schützens' as 'Schützen'.","The sentence on 'faults in the subject throughout nature' is obscure; I translate it as it stands, and the bomb example (fault lies in the thrower's aim, not the bomb) I take to illustrate that point, though the link is not fully clear.","'Subject' (*Subject*) is kept for the thing in which a fault is sought."]],"philippi-s01-038":["The rules which I need for the correct use of the understanding I seek and take from the understanding. Reason states the rules according to which it ought to direct itself.",["The transcript has 'Verstände' and then 'Vernunfft' with no explicit distinction; kept as written."]],"philippi-s01-039":["Logic is to be a preparatory means to the other sciences. It must therefore border most closely upon common reason and be composed so as to be wholly comprehensible. If it were highly philosophical, another logic would be needed as an organon for understanding it; just as, for example, whoever wishes to understand the logic of Crusius must already be master of logic.",["*Organon* kept in Greek form; 'gantz begreiflich abgefaßet' rendered 'composed so as to be wholly comprehensible'."]],"philippi-s01-040":["We said in the definition: logic is a philosophy. Why can it not be defined as a science? Because, first, the word science is too general; second, because logic is a part of philosophy.",["*Wissenschafft* rendered 'science'."]],"philippi-s01-041":["If one inquires into the origin of logic, it seems a paradox that the rules of the understanding which are set forth in logic should be taken from the understanding itself. This paradox vanishes, however, when one considers that one is not conscious of them.",["'ihrer' is ambiguous; I take it as referring to the rules (in their abstract form), and say so only in the sense; the clause is elliptical."]],"philippi-s01-042":["1. All logic must arise from the analysis (*Zergliederung*) of the common understanding. For example, the common understanding thinks, judges, infers. If one shows how thoughts follow one from another, how the understanding is moved to hold something true, how it infers unknown propositions from known ones, and so on, one can from this draw up a connected body of rules, which then represents a logic.",["OCR 'Zerghederung' corrected to 'Zergliederung'; 'vor wahr' = 'as true'."]],"philippi-s01-043":["2. Logic is founded upon criticism, that is, upon the sum-total of the rules of the common understanding. Through analysis I begin to bring the rules of which I am conscious into certain artificial formulae, according to which, as by a certain prescription, I then begin to criticise. For in analysis we already observe certain rules without being conscious of them, or even able to become conscious of them. Logic is therefore a theory of rational knowledge (*Vernunfterkenntniß*). A theory is a sum-total of doctrines which receive their proofs *a priori* from reason, independently of experience.",["OCR 'der ich mir bewust bin' read as printed ('der' for 'die'); 'Irmbegriff' read as 'Inbegriff'.","*Critic* rendered 'criticism'; 'Vernunfterkenntniß' glossed because of the compound with *Erkenntnis* (knowledge)."]],"philippi-s01-044":["In experience, judgments are not passed *in concreto* in accordance with the rules *in abstracto*; on the contrary, the rules *in abstracto* arise from those *in concreto*. Accordingly logic is a theory arising from the analysis of reason, and a criticism, that is, the sum-total of the rules of the common understanding, in which respect it agrees with aesthetics (from αἴσθησις), save that logic is a demonstrable, aesthetics an indemonstrable criticism. For this reason it is also entitled a theory of all rational knowledge, because it contains *dogmata* and theorems which can be taught, whereas no theory can be given of taste, since here everything depends upon individual sensations. For taste cannot prove its rules, show why they are so and not otherwise; they all stand under the common understanding. If a poem is composed according to all the rules of aesthetics and poetics and yet does not please, one will sooner believe that all the rules are false than that such a piece is beautiful.",["OCR 'aio~0dvonou' restored as αἴσθησις (the transcript's 'aisthanomai' form is garbled; reading uncertain).","The sentence 'Denn diese kann ihre Regeln nicht beweisen…' I take 'diese' as the taste (*Geschmack*), resolving the feminine pronoun by sense; restated as a separate sentence.","*Empfindung* = sensation."]],"philippi-s01-045":["The sound understanding is distinguished by the fact that judgments *in abstracto* are derived from judgments *in concreto*.",[]],"philippi-s01-046":["Logic is meant to be an organon of the sciences, not of common reason. Its utility is as follows:",[]],"philippi-s01-047":["1. It serves as a universal *catharcticon* [purgative], and is indispensable to common reason, in that it cleanses reason of errors and prejudices. Just as the whole art of medicine aims, not at giving health, but only at removing the ills that are harmful to health, so logic can give no understanding, but can only improve what we have corrupted. Were we not so prone to errors, we should not hold logic to be so indispensable. Philosophy and the sciences demand a higher degree of sound understanding; logic determines the limits of this understanding in respect of purification from errors and protection against them.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'cüe Logic' read as 'die Logic'; 'Irrt Immer' read as 'Irrthümer'; 'allgememen' read as 'allgemeinen'.","'catharcticon' is kept in the original form, in italics, with an English gloss supplied in square brackets.","Verstand is rendered 'understanding' and Vernunft 'reason', as the method requires."]],"philippi-s01-048":["2. By means of logic we can criticise the products of human understanding in the sciences, although we cannot improve them.",["A stray opening quotation mark in the OCR is omitted."]],"philippi-s01-049":["[One] can proceed in criticism",["The OCR '!<-h' is too garbled to read with certainty. I conjecture 'Man' ('One') and supply it in square brackets; the rest of the sentence is legible."]],"philippi-s01-050":["1. As an amateur (when we ground our judgment on the use of common understanding, and express ourselves without technical terms), who can judge whether certain things which he does not indeed understand, but yet is fond of, are right or not, but cannot give formal expression to this criticism of his. For example, when someone who does not understand music hears another play, and gives his judgment whether it sounds unpleasant or not.",["Restated slightly in the relative clause for smoothness; the sense is unchanged."]],"philippi-s01-051":["2. As a connoisseur, when one understands the matter one is judging, and can express the fault one finds in the thing criticised by means of certain received judgments, formulas and canons (and this is the final end of logic), although one does not say how it ought to be better. For example, when one criticises a definition and points out that it offends against the rule: *Definitio ne sit latior suo definito* [a definition must not be wider than what is defined].",["The English gloss of the Latin rule is supplied in square brackets."]],"philippi-s01-052":["To criticism of art there accordingly belongs terminology, so that one knows how to express oneself in the manner of art; in aesthetics one is taught a criticising in accordance with art [*kunstmässig*].",["'künstlich auszudrücken' is rendered 'in the manner of art'; the point is that expression follows technical convention."]],"philippi-s01-053":["3. As a master, when one can oneself produce, and make better, the product which one criticises, and is the inventor of such formulas and rules as on one's authority are universally received. Such judges of art [*Kunstrichter*] were Wolff and Leibniz.",["'Wolf' is given in the usual English spelling, 'Wolff'.","The relative clause 'die auf sein Ansehen allgemein recipirt werden' is smoothed in the English."]],"philippi-s01-054":["Knowledge is logically perfect when it is perfect according to the rules of logic.",["'Eine Erkenntniß' is rendered as 'knowledge', since the singular is used in a general sense."]],"philippi-s01-055":["Logic is of two kinds: 1. the theoretical.",[]],"philippi-s01-056":["2. the practical.",[]],"philippi-s01-057":["The theoretical is that which shows the conditions under which knowledge is logically perfect. For this purpose it contains *principia rationalia*. The practical shows the means by which we can fulfil the conditions of logical perfection, *per principia empirica*. In the same way morals is theoretical, in so far as it shows the criteria of moral perfection, and practical, in so far as it indicates the means of attaining to it.",["OCR 'prmcipia' read as 'principia'.","The final sentence is restated for clarity: 'welche … zeiget und practisch, welche …' is expanded with 'in so far as'."]],"philippi-s01-058":["In theoretical logic we occupy ourselves merely with the concepts of what is true or false; in practical logic, however, only rules are taught which we must observe if we wish to distinguish the true from the false. Means are pointed out to us for the procedure to be followed in this, and practical logic is a practical discipline which teaches the subjective rules of human reason.",[]],"philippi-s01-059":["In this way there are two kinds of principles of logic: 1. *rationalia*; 2. *practica*. The latter are still very much wanting. In the so-called practical logics there are many questions, but the solutions are for the most part tautological. Logic is a philosophy; hence what is to be said of philosophy in general may be inserted here.",["OCR 'mehren -theils' read as 'mehrentheils'."]],"philippi-s01-060":["Definition: *Philosophia est scientia rationalis de rerum qualitatibus* [philosophy is rational science of the qualities of things].",["The English gloss of the Latin definition is supplied in square brackets."]],"philippi-s01-061":["By being rational knowledge it is distinguished from other modes of knowledge, which are historical or empirical.",["'andern Erkenntnissen' is rendered 'other modes of knowledge', since 'knowledge' has no plural."]],"philippi-s01-062":["NB. Natural science comprehends: 1. the history of nature, what experience teaches; 2. the mathematics of nature, what concerns magnitudes; 3. philosophical knowledge.",["'Naturkunde' is rendered 'natural science'; this is a choice."]],"philippi-s01-063":["Historical knowledge has nothing to do with reason, but at most only with understanding. Thus one can know even philosophy historically. When a thunderstorm arises, understanding must be present in order to observe all that takes place; but if one is to describe a thunderstorm historically, understanding can do nothing of itself, otherwise it would be invention and not history. Philosophy is not a knowledge of common and sound reason, but a science of it.",["'derselben' in the last sentence is taken to refer to reason (Vernunfft)."]],"philippi-s01-064":["Which knowledge is of such a degree that it becomes philosophical? All knowledge, in so far as it judges of what can be known *a posteriori*, is common knowledge. But when one judges universally *a priori*, one already has philosophical knowledge. If a tailor were to complain of the hard times, the lack of money, and so forth, he would have common knowledge. But if he says that the universal complaint lies in luxury, he is already beginning to philosophise. For this judgment he takes solely from his reason. A mutilated beggar already judges philosophically when he believes that although a limb is lacking to him, nothing has yet been lost from his own self. In the moral sphere men must judge expressly according to reason. If the whole world were full of vice, some would nevertheless know the moral, not from experience, no, but from reason.",["OCR slips corrected: 'pbilosophiren' read as 'philosophiren'; 'erglau lit' read as 'er glaubt'; 'urthcilen' read as 'urtheilen'.","'im moralischen' is rendered 'in the moral sphere'; 'das Sittliche' is rendered 'the moral'."]],"philippi-s01-065":["Common understanding shows itself among simple people nowhere more than in the judging of the moral. But it takes its judgments only from cases *in concreto*; the scholar, however, draws them from abstractions [*abstractis*].",["OCR 'nirgend!' read as 'nirgends'; 'abstract is' read as 'abstractis'."]],"philippi-s01-066":["Philosophical knowledge is therefore distinguished from common knowledge in that it arises *a priori*, whereas common knowledge arises *a posteriori*.",[]],"philippi-s01-067":["Philosophy deals *de rerum qualitate* [with the quality of things]. All sciences are distinguished from mathematics and philosophy.",["The second sentence is translated as it stands. It is probably meant to say that mathematics and philosophy are distinguished from all other sciences, but the text may be defective.","The English gloss of the Latin phrase is supplied in square brackets."]],"philippi-s01-068":["These two are indeed alike in form, for both are sciences of the learned understanding; but in matter they are distinguished.",[]],"philippi-s01-069":["The object of mathematics is always identical, the object of philosophy heterogeneous.",["'identisch' is kept literally as 'identical'; 'homogeneous' may be what is meant, given the contrast with 'heterogen'."]],"philippi-s01-070":["In all knowledge one must distinguish matter and form. The former is entitled the object of the science, the latter the *modus cognoscendi* [mode of knowing].",["The English gloss of the Latin term is supplied in square brackets."]],"philippi-s01-071":["Whoever buys an estate considers it in quite one way *philosophice*, according to the nature of the land, the meadows, the arable, and so forth, and in quite another way *mathematice*, namely according to its size, the number of hides [*Hufen*], the buildings, and so forth. Philosophy and mathematics are heterogeneous, for their objects are very different.",["OCR 'Hüben' read as 'Hufen' (hides, units of land); this is a conjecture.","'philosophice' and 'mathematice' are kept in Latin, in italics."]],"philippi-s01-072":["Yet there is also a hidden difference in the form, namely, that the use of the method of mathematics is different from that of philosophy. In mathematics I consider only magnitude, and this is nothing other than a manifold repetition of one and the same thing through attention to the relation. The principles of mathematics can be regarded as *principia empirica peregrina* [foreign empirical principles]; mathematics considers coordination, the taking together of ideas.",["'Verhältniß' is rendered 'relation'.","'principia empirica peregrina' is kept in Latin, with a gloss supplied in square brackets. The phrase seems odd in the context, but there is no clear OCR fault."]],"philippi-s01-073":["Philosophy considers the genesis, the subordination of concepts. For example, when two play, who will win? The mathematician weighs the number of grounds, the philosopher their weight. In philosophy one asks what the relation of cause to effect is. The mathematician can therefore get on well enough without any very great understanding.",["'Wichtigkeit' is rendered 'weight' (importance).","'Genesin' is rendered 'genesis'."]],"philippi-s01-074":["All mathematical definitions and all [mathematical] concepts are actual and infallible. (In philosophy they are previously determined and necessary.) For the mathematician sets something before himself and explains it as he wishes and needs. So, for example, Wolff explains light, space, air and so forth in mathematics. To the philosopher, however, something is given. Whoever in philosophy should wish to define something as he needs it for his system: *ille Gryphis iungere equis* [he would yoke griffins to horses]. Wolff did it so.",["'[mathematical]' is supplied in square brackets to make plain that 'alle Begriffe' is restricted to mathematics.","The parenthetical sentence is kept where it stands, though it interrupts the argument.","The Latin tag (cf. Virgil, *Eclogues* 8.27, 'iungentur iam grypes equis') is kept as in the source, with a gloss in square brackets."]],"philippi-s01-075":["Philosophy is",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; it is translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s01-076":["1. *Philosophia habitualis*: a skill in being able to philosophize.",[]],"philippi-s01-077":["2. *Philosophia disciplinaris*: a connection (system) of propositions. (The former might also be entitled subjective philosophy, since it must determine the subject himself; the latter objective philosophy, in so far as it teaches the form of philosophizing, that is, how one ought to think.)",["OCR 'Philosoplne' corrected to 'Philosophie'.","Restated the closing clause slightly: 'diese die objective' is supplied as 'the latter objective philosophy'; 'd. i.' rendered 'that is'."]],"philippi-s01-078":["Is there, then, a finished and correct philosophy? If there were, it would be a subjective philosophy. (A scientific rational knowledge [*Vernunfterkenntniß*], if I know how to judge cases *in concreto*, *a priori*.) As things are, however, we have only a mere possible knowing [*Wissen*], an objective philosophy, [which teaches] how the understanding is to be used.",["'wie man den Verstand brauchen soll' is elliptical; I supplied 'which teaches' in brackets.","'lauter mögliches Wissen' rendered 'mere possible knowing'; 'lauter' here means 'nothing but'."]],"philippi-s01-079":["Other sciences can be learned through instruction by others, since we merely imitate everything. In history I can devise nothing for myself; I must follow others; so too in mathematics. Pascal, even in his tender childhood, could already construct all the geometrical figures, and when the books were taken from him, he reproduced them out of his own head.",["Split the first clause for smoothness; 'ersinnen' rendered 'devise'."]],"philippi-s01-080":["But even if someone reads all the philosophical books, he is still only a repository of these books, provided he thinks only according to them and not for himself. He has merely an epitome, in his understanding, of the propositions he has read. He knows them only historically; that is no philosopher. Hence a philosopher cannot properly be entitled a scholar [*Gelehrter*], since philosophy cannot be taught. For, first, there is no philosophy that would be reliable. Second, if philosophy is taught to someone, and he grasps these propositions ever so well, he would still be no philosopher, since he only imprints within himself the propositions of another.",["'im eigentlichen Verstande' rendered 'properly' (Verstand here = sense, not faculty).","'Repositorium' and 'Epitome' rendered in English; the Latinisms are transparent."]],"philippi-s01-081":["He is a philosopher who possesses the skill of philosophizing. Such a man must have genius.",[]],"philippi-s01-082":["Genius is an original spirit [*Originalgeist*], which produces perfect products without imitation. What is original distinguishes itself from others and is characteristic; and an original spirit is, first, one who has no one beside him, and second, one who has his skill not through imitation but through himself. Genius is an original spirit.",["'Geist' rendered 'spirit'; 'Geschicklichkeit' rendered 'skill'.","'karacteristisch' modernised to 'characteristic'."]],"philippi-s01-083":["NB. Original is not always an archetype [*Urbild*]; it means only one who does not imitate. The English, original spirits who have written out of themselves without imitation, have often put forward repugnant and false propositions merely in order to be original. There are sciences, first, of learning, in which one imitates one's teacher at school; but also, second, of genius.",["'Urbild' rendered 'archetype'.","'der nicht nachahmt' taken as 'one who does not imitate'."]],"philippi-s01-084":["Habitual philosophy is of this kind, and so cannot be learned; I learn only to *evolve* it [*euoluiren*]. Philosophical books serve only as an occasion for expressing one's thoughts *methodo philosophica*. A philosophical head begins to examine for himself the propositions that the teacher gives him, and often prefers his own opinions to them. He must not learn the definitions but be able to invent them himself. Not so much genius is required for mathematics. One need only go by the books. In philosophy, however, there are no infallible books. A philosophy that was infallible would be of inestimable value. Meanwhile one must learn the method by which such books can be judged.",["'euoluiren' kept as Kant's Latinism, in italics with the English beside it.","'Zu der Mathematik' rendered 'for mathematics'."]],"philippi-s01-085":["*Denominatio*. Philosophy [comes] from [two Greek words, garbled in the OCR: presumably φίλος and σοφός]. Some say the name is not appropriate, since philosophy is a seat of wisdom. But this designation is very good, and it is hardly credible that it should have been hit upon by chance. In general one often finds in such names the most remote concepts, which have maintained themselves for a very long time and have been approved by many scholars. Human wisdom is indeed nothing but knowing and inquiring.",["The Greek is garbled in the OCR ('<piÄ£00 und (7096s'); the reconstruction is conjectural and flagged in brackets.","'Sitz der Weißheit' is as printed; the sense (that philosophy would be wisdom's seat, i.e. possession) is retained literally.","'Wissen und Forschen' rendered 'knowing and inquiring'."]],"philippi-s01-086":["If philosophy is regarded as a part of learning [*Gelehrsamkeit*] and not of wisdom, there are many modes of knowledge of understanding and of reason about which one could ask what use they then have.",["'Verstandes und Vernunft Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge of understanding and of reason'."]],"philippi-s01-087":["Cosmic science [*Weltwissenschafft*] has nothing in it but theory and strives only after logical perfection. It takes its name from the world, because its principles are borrowed from the world. If we derive philosophy from our own illumination, that is enthusiasm; if we derive it from the illumination of others, it is revelation.",["'Weltwissenschafft' rendered 'cosmic science' (literally 'world-science'); the text itself explains the name by 'Welt'.","'Begeisterung' rendered 'enthusiasm'."]],"philippi-s01-088":["All philosophers have as their end only to impart to their hearers the sciences or skills in the use of reason. One should beware of the luxury of reason, pure speculation.",["The first sentence is loosely constructed in the transcript; rendered as it stands."]],"philippi-s01-089":["Through philosophy the bounds of all knowledge must be marked out in advance.",[]],"philippi-s01-090":["2. Philosophy [*Weltweißheit*] in so far as the science serves to promote [the] essential wisdom.",["The OCR '(\\t>v' is garbled; presumably an article or Greek word before 'wesentlichen'. I supplied 'the' in brackets and the reading is uncertain.","'Weltweißheit' rendered 'philosophy' here, with the German given, since it is the German equivalent of the previous heading."]],"philippi-s01-091":["That is true wisdom through which we learn to know, first, our great vocation [*Bestimmung*], and second, the means of attaining the end. Some sciences of human understanding relate only to the skill and dexterity of finding means for arriving at any end one pleases. Philosophy shows both. Locke described the vocation of the human understanding, Hume its limits.",["OCR 'Hurae' corrected to 'Hume'.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'vocation' (vocation/destination); 'determination' would mislead here, a choice against the fixed gloss because the context concerns the human vocation."]],"philippi-s01-092":["Metaphysics and morals constitute true wisdom. The former indicates the vocation of the understanding, the latter that of the will. Physics is not a skill. The philosopher is a theoretician; the wise man [Greek word garbled in the OCR, presumably σοφός] is a practitioner.",["The Greek 'CTO90S' is garbled; 'σοφός' is conjectural, and the German gloss '(weise)' confirms the sense.","'Practicus' and 'Theoreticus' rendered in English."]],"philippi-s01-093":["All philosophy of the world [*Weltweißheit*] can be divided into the practical and the theoretical. The former is entitled morals and contains the rules of the perfection of all free conduct. The latter is logic and contains the rules of logical perfection, and accordingly also *principia rationalia*.",["'Jene'/'diese' resolved as former/latter; the transcript's 'Diese ist die Logic' is kept despite the oddity (theoretical = logic)."]],"philippi-s01-094":["All sciences presuppose an ignorance and a state [*Staat*].",["'Staat' is ambiguous (state/commonwealth); the following passage shows the political sense is meant."]],"philippi-s01-095":["*Historia philosophiae*.",[]],"philippi-s01-096":["All sciences begin from the time when men stood under the protection of a magistracy, from men who had leisure and for whose welfare public provision was made. When one is in need and must provide laboriously for one's maintenance, one asks nothing about the sciences; but when one is idle, one becomes curious. The inequality by which one is master and another servant sprang from violence. The ease of some and the exertion of others, who had to feed those idle ones: this was the beginning of the sciences. In what estate, then, did they arise? Those who were believed to be bound to teach were the priests: they were the first who had to invent the sciences and make a profession of them. For without religion there can be no state. At that time, however, they had as yet no doctrine of religion. Questions were raised, and people held themselves entitled to put them to the priests, who were regarded as those who should teach. The latter therefore had to prepare themselves for answers, in order to be able to answer the questions. The first science that arose in this way was morals; after it arose pneumatology.",["Divided and lightly smoothed some clauses; 'sich auf Antworten gefaßt machen' rendered 'prepare themselves for answers'.","'hält sich berechtiget' kept in the past tense for consistency with the narrative.","'Pneumatologie' kept in English transliteration (doctrine of spirits)."]],"philippi-s01-097":["Apart from the Egyptians, Persians, Chaldeans and Indians, no other peoples have had systems of religion.",[]],"philippi-s01-098":["At last, however, a revolution of the understanding took place. This happened when people began to pursue the sciences, and to philosophize, independently of the priestly order. The peoples among whom this first happened were the Chinese and the Greeks.",["OCR 'pliilosophiren' corrected to 'philosophiren'."]],"philippi-s01-099":["The Jews had no need of philosophy. In China it is neither the Bonzes nor the Lamas who philosophize, but the Mandarins, since the former adhered to their own particular system.",["OCR 'Aveder' corrected to 'weder'.","'jene' resolved as the Bonzes and Lamas (the priests), in contrast to the Mandarins."]],"philippi-s01-100":["There have been peoples who philosophized speculatively independently of religion, and this the Greeks and the Chinese did.",[]],"philippi-s01-101":["Of all the oriental peoples China is the most cultivated and is to be regarded as the source of the sciences. The priests in both countries wrote no book at all about religion.",["'in beiden Ländern' presumably means China and Greece, following the previous passages; the antecedent is not stated here."]],"philippi-s01-102":["Egypt has been, from ancient times, a very exactly ordered state. Since there were many tranquil and idle estates there, which had nothing to do but to see how they might distinguish themselves from the common people, the sciences were bound to spring up. That the sciences nevertheless did not reach perfection was because they were not permitted to go beyond a certain prescribed rule. Even when a great genius appeared, he had to produce nothing that went beyond that prescription. He had not to cross the boundary which his wretched religion set for him. Confucius, 550 years before the birth of Christ, was the first to write a moral doctrine. It contains principles according to which princes are to be educated, which were also generally accepted in public esteem, and by means of which he brought about the beneficent and just government of the mandarins. This moral doctrine stands in the place of a system of religion. It could not properly spread, because it was bound up with the government, just as Egyptian philosophy was fettered by religion. Thus both stood under constraint, and under constraint one cannot philosophise. Where philosophy is subject to the fundamental laws of the state, it can never attain perfection.",["Several sentences are smoothed slightly; 'Es muste nicht über die Grentze gehen' rendered 'He had not to cross' to keep the sense of prohibition.","'im Ansehen' rendered 'in public esteem'; the phrase is uncertain in the transcript.","'Stände' rendered 'estates' (ranks or classes of society)."]],"philippi-s01-103":["Mathematics, through its order, evidence and precision, gives the understanding a model and outline, [showing] how near it comes to these. This science began with the art of writing, and the Egyptians are said to have invented it, but only *empirice* [empirically]. Among the Greeks it first attained its perfection.",["'wie nahe er demselben kömmt' is obscure; I supply 'showing' in brackets and read 'demselben' as referring to the model."]],"philippi-s01-104":["Of the philosophy of the Egyptians nothing has come down to our times.",[]],"philippi-s01-105":["Writing was probably first invented among the Chaldeans. Their best-known philosopher is Zoroaster, called Zerdust among the Persians, who is perhaps the same person, although there are as many Zoroasters as there are Herculeses. He taught two *principia*, a good one, which he called Hormisdas, and an evil one, which he called Ahriman. Leibniz believes that the latter is the German Herrmann, who made an expedition to Persia under the reign of Hormisdas, and that the Persians are a German nation, which the many German words in the Persian language also seem to confirm. He is said to have established the religion of the Persians in the time of Darius Hystaspes. The book in which it is set down is entitled Zendavesta; it was brought to Europe by Perron and is of great antiquity.",["'dieser' (the evil principle, Ahriman) is taken as the antecedent of Leibniz's identification, as the grammar requires; the sense is odd but stands as written.","'Herkuleße' rendered 'Herculeses'."]],"philippi-s01-106":["The sciences of genius flourish especially in small free states lying beside one another. Freedom awakens the spirit and kindles emulation, and such was the constitution of Greece (England still has some analogy with these Greek city-states). All ranks stood open to the citizens of this country. An abuse does not last long in small states, since one criticises and laughs at another, whereas in large states it goes on continually. So also in Greece, when an abuse arose in one city, it was soon taken to task by the others and thereby checked. Hence it comes that the propositions which they had in religion and elsewhere are clothed with such wit that they are still cited in writings in similar cases, since one can hardly invent wittier similes; examples are the Sirens and the story of Pandora's box, and the like. In general the Greeks may be regarded as the most talkative, the most frivolous and the most curious of peoples. Their talkativeness shows itself in their unfaithfulness in narrating events. There are very few histories which are not stuffed full of a multitude of invented circumstances. Their frivolity was a consequence of their freedom, and it is clearly seen in the mockery and laughter of many, and in the indifference of all toward the narratives which made up the system of their religion. Their curiosity was so great that, when they publicly exhibited something which they had made by art or genius, the common man ran up and reasoned about it. If someone had discovered something admirable or useful, it was enough for him to see his name displayed on public columns, an honour which many deserving generals have wished for in vain. Nor was it unreasonable that products of taste were left to the judgment of common understanding. Homer sang his poems to common people, and Molière read his comedies to his maid, and found her taste wholly in agreement with that of the spectators.",["OCR slips silently corrected (Wißcnschaften, m kleinen, ldeinen, Nacheif erung, etc.).","'durchgehechelt' rendered 'taken to task' (lit. 'hackled'); 'eingestehet' (probably 'eingestellt') read as 'checked' — doubtful reading.","The long sentences on small and large states and on the Greeks' character are divided or smoothed."]],"philippi-s01-107":["Pythagoras travelled to India in order to learn there the philosophy of the Indians. All that one finds among them resembling philosophy is the doctrine of metempsychosis and a kind of Stoic system. Brahma, their supposed lawgiver and author of this doctrine, left it behind in a book which can be regarded as their law-book and is entitled Veda, which is still older than the Persians' Zendavesta. Their philosophy was thus a kind of mystical morals. They still have a university at Benares.",["'Zewa-Vesta' is the transcript's form of Zendavesta; rendered accordingly."]],"philippi-s01-108":["The Asiatics are very rich in images (which seems to be the ground of all superstition and of the little growth that the sciences have had in this part of the world), just as the French are rich in sallies of wit [*Einfälle*]. In the seven sages only a wit rich in sayings was found; among all these sages perhaps Thales alone was merely a scholar, and Solon the only sage.",["'Thaies' read as Thales."]],"philippi-s01-109":["With Confucius the philosophy of the Chinese began; he brought it into a great form, in which it has since constantly remained, without new discoveries having been added.",[]],"philippi-s01-110":["The Greek philosophers can be divided into two classes: those before the introduction of larger schools, and those who introduced various schools. To the former belong the seven wise men of the world, if they deserve the name. At that time the civil constitution of the republics began to be formed, and those who distinguished themselves on such an occasion received the name of sages.",["'Sclnüen' read as 'Schulen'."]],"philippi-s01-111":["Learning is distinguished from wisdom in that the former is almost merely speculative, while the latter contains rather practical rules and the ends of humanity. Socrates called the first *Philodoxen* and the others philosophers. Their whole philosophy consisted of maxims and apophthegms, which were valued the more the more striking they were, and of images in the taste of the Asiatic Greeks, which is in general a sure mark of little talent.",["'Plülodoxen' read as 'Philodoxen' (lovers of opinion), cf. the 'Philodoxie' of §117.","'Sinnund Denksprüchen' read as 'Sinn- und Denksprüchen'."]],"philippi-s01-112":["The history of Greek philosophy can be divided into the schools of the practical philosophers, who however properly formed no sect but only taught morals, among whom Diogenes the Cynic, Zeno and Socrates are to be counted, and into the contemplative and speculative or theoretical philosophers. These again divide into various classes.",["'»Secte' read as 'Secte'."]],"philippi-s01-113":["The dogmatists, who assert complete certainty in propositions of reason. *Dogmata* is the name for truths of reason, and one is dogmatic if one does not seek the propositions of reason but holds them to have been already found. Opposed to these were the sceptics.",["'Scepticer' rendered 'sceptics'."]],"philippi-s01-114":["The dogmatists are distinguished",["Breaks off; continued in the following passages."]],"philippi-s01-115":["1. into those who philosophised from *principia* of sensibility, and",[]],"philippi-s01-116":["2. into those who philosophised from *principia* of reason.",[]],"philippi-s01-117":["Into the [*noumenoi*] and [*phainomenoi*]. Heraclitus made the distinction. The *noumenoi* asserted that the senses are false; they show not how things are, but only how they are affected by the things. They believed that philosophy is confined to the *intelligibilia*, and that all objects of the senses, which are as it were in perpetual flux, belong only to philodoxy and are not objects of reason. Democritus, Leucippus and Epicurus defended the contrary, that the whole of philosophy is confined to the *sensibilia*.",["The Greek in the OCR ('vooOnsvoi und 9aiv6|j£voi') is garbled; I read νοούμενοι and φαινόμενοι and bracket the transliterations. The attribution to Heraclitus is as in the transcript.","'Philodoxie' kept as a coined term (love of opinion)."]],"philippi-s01-118":["Aristotle considered the *mundus sensibilis* and *insensibilis*. He said that the intellectual concepts are indeed higher than the sensitive, yet have grown up with them on one stem.",["'scnsibilem' read as 'sensibilem'.","'Sensitiue' kept in sense as 'sensitive'."]],"philippi-s01-119":["Others, like Plato, say that the intellectual concepts come from an inner intuition of the Deity.",["'innera' in the OCR read as 'innern'."]],"philippi-s01-120":["The objects of pure reason are all moral and metaphysical modes of knowledge, just as the objects of sensibility are phenomena [*Phaenomena*].",["OCR '(Jegenstände' read as 'Gegenstände'.","Kant's 'Erkenntniße' (plural) rendered 'modes of knowledge'. The sentence is loose: it speaks of objects as being modes of knowledge; I keep this rather than smooth it."]],"philippi-s01-121":["All philosophers, ancient and modern, have had the very same matter, or objects, for their philosophy; they are distinguished only in respect of its form.",["OCR '(Jegenstände' read as 'Gegenstände'. 'Materie oder Gegenstände' kept as 'matter, or objects'."]],"philippi-s01-122":["The dogmatists again divided into various schools: the Ionic, which began with Thales, one of the seven sages, who had fetched his wisdom from Egypt; 2. the Eleatic; 3. the Italic, whose head was Pythagoras; 4. the Platonic. All of these divided the world into a *mundus sensibilis* and a *mundus intelligibilis*. The concepts of the sensible world, they said, we receive by means of the senses from outer objects. The concepts of the intellectual world arise from our understanding. The latter concepts Pythagoras entitled number, Plato Ideas, and Aristotle form; by these they all understood whatever is known through the understanding.",["The numerals '2', '3', '4' in the German are stray enumeration marks, partly misplaced by the OCR (the '2' stands after 'hatte'); I have set them as an enumeration, with the first member unnumbered, as the text gives it.","'Dogniatiker' read as 'Dogmatiker'; 'nante' = 'nannte'. 'Numerus' translated as 'number'.","'Verstände' / 'Verstand' = understanding; 'erkannt' = known."]],"philippi-s01-123":["Socrates was no dogmatist [*Dogmaticus*]; his doubt was not a decisive doubt. There follow",["Breaks off; the continuation is in the next passage."]],"philippi-s01-124":["2. the dogmatists who accept merely *principia sensibilia*. Their head was Epicurus. He is the only philosopher of antiquity. For one judges philosophy not by its propositions but only by its method, and in this respect he is the foremost of antiquity.",["'2.' is the transcript's own enumeration, answering the (unnumbered) first class of dogmatists above."]],"philippi-s01-125":["His first rule was this: since our modes of knowledge arise from the senses, one must name the original [*das Original*].",["The sentence is probably corrupt or elliptical ('das Original nennen' – perhaps 'the senses must be named as the original source'). Translated as it stands; the sense of 'Original' is doubtful."]],"philippi-s01-126":["2. One should avoid all mystical and oratorical expressions, and accept no proposition which one could not exhibit in concrete cases and make sensible.",[]],"philippi-s01-127":["By the first he banished all begged propositions from his philosophy, and by the second all chimerical concepts. From these he drew the principle that whatever is not an object of our outer sense is problematic, and that there is no ground for assuming it forthwith; whereas everything that is to be held true must be capable of being shown in experience and presented to outer sensibility.",["Divided into two sentences. 'verbanne te' read as 'verbannte'. 'erbettelte Sätze' = propositions obtained by begging (petitio principii)."]],"philippi-s01-128":["Thus he demanded that all knowledge be derived from sensible grounds. It is a pity that this Epicurus has become so hateful to posterity, partly through the misconduct of his disciples, partly through the misunderstanding of his propositions, from which his enemies charged him with the most abominable vices. He is judged according to the description given of him by Lucretius, his disciple, and no one considers the bold liberty which poets take in their manner of writing. Would not a Haller, if one chose to judge him by his writings, deserve to be called a heretic?",["'sem Schüler' read as 'sein Schüler'; 'Schrifften' = writings. The reference is to Albrecht von Haller."]],"philippi-s01-129":["He sought only to bring back the dogmatists, who were roaming about in nothing but ideal worlds. Whence comes the rain? It is said that Providence ordains it so. Yes, but then all philosophy comes to an end. The matter [*Materia*], the substratum of philosophy, that is given is the world, and not supernatural things. What cannot be derived from natural grounds ought not to be put into philosophy.",["'Zur Materia Substratum' is awkward; rendered as 'The matter, the substratum'."]],"philippi-s01-130":["He adopted as principles those things whose laws of operation are known to us.",["'dessenWirkungsgesetze' read as 'dessen Wirkungsgesetze'."]],"philippi-s01-131":["The immortality of the soul, for which he did not see the ground through the senses, he neither affirmed nor denied.",[]],"philippi-s01-132":["Pleasure (*voluptas*) he held to be the point of reference of his philosophy. By this he did not at all understand the outer, low, sensible delights. His temperate life proves it. In this, to be sure, he is no doubt in the wrong, but he is by no means on that account to be accused of gross vices.",["'massiges' = 'mässiges' (temperate)."]],"philippi-s01-133":["One must seek virtue, he says, because one finds happiness in it.",[]],"philippi-s01-134":["His error is that he wished to maintain that our pleasures are not effects of the mind but of sensibility. Yet he does not thereby set gross animal lust as the end; he left to each his intellectual pleasure. This is a philosophical judgment upon the phenomenon [*Phaenomenon*], not a morality. For example, he derived joy from the warming of the heart and the harmony of the blood, etc., etc.",["'Geist' rendered 'mind' here in the sense of intellect/spirit rather than 'Gemüt'; 'geistige Vergnügen' = 'intellectual pleasure'."]],"philippi-s01-135":["His principle was distinctness [*Deutlichkeit*]; and on this account, as well as on account of the principle of happiness or pleasure (considered fundamentally, these two concepts are one and the same: we call happiness whatever is in accordance with our inclinations), he can be entitled the philosopher of the Graces, who strews the path of virtue with roses.",["Kept the parenthesis in place; the sentence is long but readable."]],"philippi-s01-136":["If one wished to judge him by the portrait Lucretius has left us of him, we should be acting just as incorrectly as if we were to judge Haller's system of belief from his writings.",[]],"philippi-s01-137":["Epicurus maintained that knowledge through the senses alone is capable of science and is certain.",["'Wissenschafft' = science."]],"philippi-s01-138":["Plato, on the contrary, held only intellectual modes of knowledge to be science and truths.",[]],"philippi-s01-139":["All truth and all knowledge came, according to him, from the Ideas, which the soul receives from an inner intuition of the Deity and a copying-off of this archetype.",["'Urbild' = archetype."]],"philippi-s01-140":["Both believed that there is an essential difference between the intellectual and the sensitive. The latter [Plato's, i.e. the intellectual side, in the text's order: the former] was very mystical, obscure, enthusiastic; the other distinct, sensible, natural.",["'Dieser ... jener' is ambiguous. By the argument, 'dieser' (the nearer, Plato) is mystical and 'jener' (Epicurus) distinct and natural; I resolved it so, but the bracketed gloss in my rendering is clumsy and should be read simply as: Plato's view mystical, Epicurus's distinct.","Doubtful: the transcript names neither philosopher in the sentence."]],"philippi-s01-141":["Aristotle said that the understanding is a method of making use of sensibility; death is the end of all sensibility. He made the positive principle: *Nihil est in intellectu quod non antea fuit in sensu*.",["'Tod ist das Ende aller Sinnlichkeit' sits oddly in the context and may be a corrupt or misplaced note; translated as it stands.","Latin kept in italics."]],"philippi-s01-142":["Socrates, who sought to copy the archetype in himself, said that man has no greater object than man.",[]],"philippi-s01-143":["He sought to teach his pupils moderation in deciding, and the doubt of suspended judgment, and he plucked at the proud dogmatists by irony; but without intending it he thereby gave occasion to scepticism. For some of his pupils took merely these ironies for their philosophy, and attacked all dogmatic propositions, partly with malicious mockery, partly with fallacies. Pyrrho, the author of this sect, whose motto was the modest *non liquet*, had no such intention at all. He taught only that one should not at once accept and decide the propositions of philosophy, but should at first doubt them until one is infallibly convinced of them. Out of this his successors made a categorical doubt, that everything is uncertain.",["'Aufschubs' = of postponement/suspension of judgment."]],"philippi-s01-144":["However strange this sect is, and however useless it may appear, it has yet indirectly rendered great service. For they have served as a whetstone of human knowledge and reason; they called themselves Academics.",["'Academici' kept as 'Academics'."]],"philippi-s01-145":["It held itself neutral, and would not let itself be convinced by arguments which really could convince it; in short, they were free-thinkers. They required that their pupils should doubt even the propositions of their teachers.",["The shift from 'sie' (singular, the sect) to 'sie' (plural) is in the German and is kept."]],"philippi-s01-146":["The Romans made much use of this philosophy. In more recent times Hume, however, has written in this manner of thinking, in a transfigured form.",[]],"philippi-s01-147":["The Cynic sect had for its object simplicity and innocence. They considered natural man.",["'Objeet' read as 'Object'."]],"philippi-s01-148":["Rousseau has striven to sketch the nature of man. Every original is unintelligible: even he who finds it does not understand it. So it goes with him also in some respects.",["'bey einigen' is obscure; rendered 'in some respects'. Doubtful."]],"philippi-s01-149":["The Stoics founded their philosophy upon an enthusiastic civic virtue. This gave them the air of the sublime.",[]],"philippi-s01-150":["What could be more attractive to the Romans, whose deity was the republic and whose religion was civic virtue, than this philosophy?",[]],"philippi-s01-151":["The Romans produced no original philosophers, but only sectarians. They borrowed from Aristotle and Plato not the theory but the practice. Their true philosophy constituted their jurisprudence, and in this they certainly showed much understanding. Their law is not to be regarded as a matter of memory; it is capable of a mathematical method and still serves in all states as a rule. In the eleventh century their Laws of the Twelve Tables fell into oblivion. (After the destruction of the Roman empire all the sciences ceased.) But they revived after the Pandects were found at Amalfi and at Naples. To these we perhaps owe a great influence upon human knowledge.",["'kernen' read as 'keinen'; 'X I . Seculo' as 'XI. Seculo'; 'Tahular.' as 'Tabular.'. Historically the Pandects were found at Amalfi (the Naples mention is the transcript's)."]],"philippi-s01-152":["As Christianity spread, Plato came to the fore. In his philosophy everything is ideal; the understanding, separated from all sensibility, is left to itself; and the propositions of religion are to be considered in just the same way. Hence the Platonic philosophy seemed to the Christians the best suited to explain the truths of religion. But they did not find what they sought; therefore Plato fell, and Aristotle, who in his philosophy combines the understanding with sensibility, took his place. What brought Plato down was the enlightenment of religion, the work of the Reformation. All the sciences are bound up with religion. So long as men had to think in religion under constraint, wit and all the arts were silent; when religion gained freedom and light, the sciences arose. The heathen religion was the occasion for the formation of most of the sciences and arts, especially the fine sciences and arts. For since it extended merely to sensibility, it could not fail to call forth the sciences of sensibility. Michelangelo admits that a single trunk, without head or arms, which the Romans had carved and which was meant to represent Hercules, was the school in which he learned his art. They surpassed the beauty of nature. An Apollo, a statue of ancient Rome that has been preserved down to the present, cannot be looked at without wonder at the skill of the artist. Their poetry, oratory, etc., etc., reached the highest perfection. Demosthenes, Homer, Cicero and Virgil will always remain the archetypes.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'get rennet' (getrennet, i.e. getrennt), 'DemostheneSj' (Demosthenes), 'Michel Angelo' rendered 'Michelangelo'.","'Klotz' (block, trunk) is the famous Belvedere Torso; 'Bildsäule' rendered 'statue'. The 'Apollo' is presumably the Apollo Belvedere; the transcript does not say so.","'Wissenschafften' is rendered 'sciences', in the older sense of branches of learning; 'schöne Wissenschafften' as 'fine sciences'. 'Witz' rendered 'wit'.","'Urbilder' rendered 'archetypes' (Latin *archetypus*/*exemplar*); 'Sinnlichkeit' as 'sensibility'.","The sentence 'Die Erhellung der Religion ... war es was ihn heruntersetzte' is restated so that 'ihn' is clearly Plato; the sense requires this, since Aristotle takes his place.","'Alle Wissenschafften sind in ihr der Religion verbunden' is syntactically awkward; rendered as 'bound up with religion', 'ihr' being left unresolved in the English."]],"philippi-s01-153":["Will not some of our own [writers] likewise serve posterity as archetypes and be preserved? No; for the present languages are subject to change. Gellert will become unintelligible to posterity, like Hans Sachs and many of the most recent French books, such as Montaigne.",["'Gellert' is the OCR 'Geliert'; 'Hans Sachse' rendered 'Hans Sachs'. 'Autores' is not in the text; I supply '[writers]' for the elliptical 'unsern'.","Montaigne is cited as a 'French book' among the 'newest'; this is the transcript's own wording and is kept as it stands, though it is odd."]],"philippi-s01-154":["It is almost improbable that, apart from the languages already dead, others should die out; the most probable reason for this seems to be the immutability of religion, and that we shall have no new sacred books. Through the Reformation the sensible element which had crept in alongside the Christian religion was separated off; religion received more of the spiritual, and Plato therefore became a favourite philosopher.",["'kerne' in the OCR is read as 'keine'. 'Un Veränderlichkeit' read as 'Unveränderlichkeit'.","'Das Sinnliche' rendered 'the sensible element'; 'geistiges' as 'the spiritual'.","The first sentence is restated slightly for clarity; the 'davon' (of this) refers to the non-dying-out of the languages, and the sense is somewhat compressed in the German."]],"philippi-s01-155":["Finally, with the inundation of the barbarians, there followed the complete decline of all the sciences and arts. Meanwhile Europe took on a new form among them, through the disposition to the good which they had, and through the spirit of freedom which they brought with them to Europe. The Arabs, after they had overrun Africa, were the first to begin to explain Aristotle, who was most suited to their character, and to translate him into Arabic. To them also the invention of universities is ascribed. Samarkand in Tartary and Cordova in Spain were their most famous universities.",["'Durch die Anlage zum Guten ...' is a dependent fragment in the German, standing as a separate sentence; I joined it to the preceding sentence as the means by which Europe gained its new form.","'Anlage' rendered 'disposition'; 'Tartarey' rendered 'Tartary', 'Samarcand' as 'Samarkand'.","'Unterdeßen ... unter denselben' is rendered 'among them', i.e. among the barbarians; this is a judgment."]],"philippi-s01-156":["On the other hand those Latin and Greek authors will always remain archetypes, because they are written in unchangeable languages; for the immutability of religion, whose sacred doctrines are written in the Greek and Latin languages, draws after it the immutability of these languages.",["'Urbilder' again rendered 'archetypes', consistently with 152.","'Dagegen' ('on the other hand') contrasts with the changeable modern tongues of 153."]],"philippi-s01-157":["With the revival of the sciences Aristotle came into repute, and he maintained himself there until the papal authority received a blow from the Reformation, whereupon the repute of Plato rose again, and in this he maintains himself still. Since the philosophy of the latter is merely speculative, and so stands in a very close connection with religion, which is almost wholly spiritual, and since this is for man the most interesting thing, everything else has received a certain form from it.",["'bey welchem er sich ... erhielt' is rendered 'maintained himself there'; the antecedent of 'welchem' (Ansehen) is resolved by judgment.","'derselben' in the last clause is taken as religion, not Plato's philosophy; the German is ambiguous, but the sense (religion being 'das Intereßanteste') favours this."]],"philippi-s01-158":["The more spiritual a religion is, the greater is the lack of fine arts, and everything therefore assumes a sad and melancholy aspect. Religion has also brought the ancient languages, and with them at the same time the monuments of the great spirit of the ancients, down to our own times.",["'Gestalt' rendered 'aspect'. 'Schönen Künsten' rendered 'fine arts'."]],"philippi-s01-159":["The brooding understanding and the corrupted taste kept Aristotle in place so long. Each explained him according to his own purposes and picked out for himself all sorts of subtleties with which he attacked other propositions. Aristotle's philosophy was held to be the most perfect, and, as if inspired from heaven, infallible.",["'Aiistotel' and 'Alistoteis' are OCR slips for Aristotel/Aristoteles. 'als vom Himmel eingeflößt' rendered 'as if inspired from heaven'; I supply 'was held to be' to bring out the sense of the construction.","'Grübelnde' rendered 'brooding'; 'Subtilitäten' as 'subtleties'."]],"philippi-s01-160":["The real cause of Aristotle's long authority was the despotic government of the Pope.",[]],"philippi-s01-161":["The slothful and slavish swarm of monks, shut off from the world, sought their learning merely in the most useless speculations, in which they found Aristotle very fruitful. With cunning they picked out the subtleties from him, and sought by this means to distinguish themselves from the rabble; for in religion they dared not do so, since the thunder of the Castel Sant'Angelo at once pursued them; rather they had to try to keep the people in ignorance of religion, and themselves also in it. Hence philosophy, and indeed only the Aristotelian, was the one means by which they could distinguish themselves from the people; and so they went on rummaging in their philosophy, which consisted of nothing but distinctions and ridiculous subtleties, while the ignorance of others threw seraphic names at them.",["'Engelsburg' rendered 'Castel Sant'Angelo', the papal fortress; the sense is the papal power of punishment.","'Sublilitäten' is an OCR slip for 'Subtilitäten'. 'Seraphische Namen' refers to honorific titles such as 'Doctor seraphicus'; I keep the German sense and do not add the Latin.","The phrase 'und auch sich selbst darinn zu bestärken suchen' is rendered 'and themselves also in it', with 'keep ... in ignorance' understood; a supplied construction for a compressed German."]],"philippi-s01-162":["At last the dawning light of religion drove away these dark minds. Meanwhile the academies, the new societies of the learned, and the free forms of the cities had a great deal to do in cultivating anew the devastated field of learning. Aristotle had done extraordinary harm by his decisive and general pronouncements, which everyone accepted as they stood; Leibniz and Wolff afterwards erred in just the same way. For no one any longer took trouble to investigate certain propositions, since they already stood fully settled in Aristotle, and there one dared not doubt. So Aristotle was the hindrance and the ruin of philosophy, but he was so still more in respect of the fine sciences. Since the Scholastics abstracted from all sensibility and let the understanding ramble on in this way, taste and all the fine arts had to vanish. For nothing is more opposed to them. Taste demands evidence for the eye and to see all propositions in sensible cases *in concreto*; the Scholastics would know nothing of this and threw themselves about only with their abstract propositions.",["OCR slips corrected: 'allgememe' (allgemeine), 'Scholastüter' and 'abstralnrten' (Scholastiker, abstrahirten), 'Hessen; niuste' (liessen; musste). 'Er der Geschmack' read as 'der Geschmack'.","'Augenscheinlichkeit' rendered 'evidence for the eye', i.e. visible, intuitive clearness; a choice.","The passage 'so wie es nachher Leibnitz und Wolf eben dadurch versehen' is read as 'erred in the same way'; 'versehen' here has the sense 'erred', and the reading is somewhat doubtful.","The clause 'die auch jeder so angenommen' is rendered 'which everyone accepted as they stood', a judgment on the pronoun and particle.","*in concreto* kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s01-163":["The moderns have no advantage over the ancients in some sciences, for example in logic, metaphysics and morals, although their treatment of these is to be valued more highly than the product they actually delivered.",["'Bearbeitungen' rendered 'treatment'; 'Product' as 'product'. The sense (the effort of working over the subject exceeds the result) is kept close to the German."]],"philippi-s01-164":["In natural science and jurisprudence the moderns have a real advantage. The ancients mixed morals and the right of nature together; now the two have not only been distinguished but also separated from the law of the state.",["'Naturkunde' rendered 'natural science'; 'Rechtswissenschaft' as 'jurisprudence'; 'Recht der Natur' as 'right of nature' (*jus naturae*) and 'Staatsrecht' as 'law of the state'."]],"philippi-s01-165":["The moderns have an essential advantage over the others. The philosophical sciences are now regarded in an entirely different light. In metaphysics we no longer study dogmatic propositions, as formerly, but our pure understanding and its laws. In logic we have an entirely different method of investigation: it is here that we study our own subject, the laws of the understanding as well as those of sensibility. The method of the modern philosophers is for the most part sceptical, whereas the ancients affected the dogmatic to so great a degree. But what has contributed most to the improvement of philosophy? This, that men have applied themselves with great earnestness to natural science; and in this also they have made a great advance upon the ancients. The reason why physics has grown so much is that men have taken experiences into counsel and have investigated nature by experiments. The ancients neglected these interesting sciences. By their fantastic understanding they created for themselves ideal worlds, from which they gained nothing, and they would not become acquainted with the world in which they were. It is strange that the Greeks paid no regard to observations and experiences of nature. Their astonishing inconstancy was to blame for this. They did not even possess a geography. (Meanwhile, in what concerns the doctrine and classification of concepts, they were excellent.)",["'Lehre' in the last sentence rendered 'doctrine'; the parenthesis is the transcript's own.","'Naturlehre' rendered 'natural science'; 'Physik' kept as 'physics'.","'Verstand' in 'phantastischen Verstand' rendered 'understanding'.","'gar' 'idealische Welten' rendered 'ideal worlds'."]],"philippi-s01-166":["Galileo began to raise natural science to a higher level, when he investigated and recorded the laws of gravity. When men saw what benefit experiments produced, they zealously continued them, and even now they are the objects of the labours of entire learned societies.",["'Schwere' rendered 'gravity'.","Restated slightly: 'da er … untersuchte und aufzeichnete' kept as a temporal clause."]],"philippi-s01-167":["Among the fortunate occurrences that have brought advantage to natural science belong the relevant inventions: of microscopes and telescopes, of the barometer and the air-pump.",[]],"philippi-s01-168":["As regards the method of philosophy, Wolff is the man to whom an eminent merit in this respect must be ascribed. His works should be weighed by anyone who wishes to form a concept of the wide-ranging understanding of this man. The distinctness, the exactness of the definitions, the order of the proofs, all of which he introduced into philosophy, make Wolff's name worthy of remembrance. And although at present men are again seeking to throw everything into confusion, since they hold it very burdensome to submit to so compelling a method, yet German minds are already so accustomed to it that this manner of teaching will not easily die out; and this gives hope that in Germany, sooner than elsewhere, new discoveries will be made in philosophy.",["'Deutlichkeit' rendered 'distinctness'; 'Erklärungen' rendered 'definitions' (in the sense of expositions), a choice.","'Erfindungen' rendered 'discoveries'.","Long final sentence divided only by punctuation; 'schon alles' in 'alles schon in Verwirrung' left untranslated as a filler ('already')."]],"philippi-s01-169":["Nevertheless he did reason great harm by deciding everything dogmatically, defining everything, and assuming everything as beyond doubt. He believed that philosophy is capable of a mathematical method, and he actually introduced it.",["OCR 'führe te' silently corrected to 'führte'.","'vor ungezweifelt' read as 'für ungezweifelt'."]],"philippi-s01-170":["Locke is the man who laboured chiefly to discover genetically the origin of all human knowledge and its limits. He philosophizes subjectively.",["OCR 'Erkennt niß' and 'phüosophirt' silently corrected; 'subjectiue' = 'subjectively'.","'erfinden' rendered 'discover'."]],"philippi-s01-171":["Crusius has an anti-philosophical method, which undermines all philosophy. He gives out as subjective laws things which are often only effects of the understanding and not laws. He has nothing but chimeras. He throws away all means of testing.",["'Hirngespinste' rendered 'chimeras'.","'Prüfung' rendered 'testing'."]],"philippi-s01-172":["On the History of Logic",["Heading; continues in the next passage ('insbesondere'), i.e. 'in particular'."]],"philippi-s01-173":["in particular.",[]],"philippi-s01-174":["Logic is a far later part of philosophy, because it is a critique of the human understanding, although it is now taught first at academies. It is rather a technical means of naming the procedure of the understanding with technical terms. It serves to bring our concepts under certain kinds and to indicate the errors where they have been committed. It would have arisen later still, if real discoveries and not chimeras had been laid at the foundation of philosophy. But logic has been founded upon the common use of the understanding. The oldest logics are not so much dogmatic disciplines as rather isolated general maxims. The logic of the Pythagorean school was nothing other than a mysticism to the disadvantage of sensibility, in which, according to the judgment of that school and of the Platonists, there is no truth but mere illusion [Schein]. They explained all concepts intellectually, and regarded the whole world as a hieroglyphic book containing the signs for the concepts that were in the understanding of man; whereas through the mere understanding we know only general properties of things. After these came the logic of Epicurus, which was merely negative and consisted of few propositions. Its final aim was to preserve human reason from errors and confusion.",["OCR 'wirkliehe', 'Piatoniker' (= Platoniker) silently corrected.","The sentence on the Pythagoreans was divided; 'bey welcher … die' is awkward in the German: I took 'welcher' as sensibility and 'die' as the school and Platonists.","'Lehrsätze' rendered 'maxims'/general propositions is a choice; 'Lehrsätze' could be 'doctrinal propositions'.","'Schein' = illusion, as a choice."]],"philippi-s01-175":["In the method of making use of reason there have been written",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage. 'geschrieben' here perhaps for 'geschrieben' = 'written about'; doubtful."]],"philippi-s01-176":["1. the mystical logic, which was made by Euclid of Megara on account of sorceries. He taught that two opposed propositions could be proved and refuted in the same way. From this arose a kind of pseudo-philosophers, the dialecticians, great disputing heroes.",["'Zaubereien' rendered 'sorceries'; the reading 'wegen' may reflect a transcription slip.","'Affterphilosophen' rendered 'pseudo-philosophers'."]],"philippi-s01-177":["The Greek people was delighted by nothing more than by seeing two scholars quarrelling before it. Hence many sought skill and cunning in giving everything an appearance [Schein], and in attacking the other with these weapons.",[]],"philippi-s01-178":["The dark times of the papacy were also full of such dialecticians. Their logic was nothing but an organon of garrulity, by which one could ensnare another through subtle captious questions.",["OCR 'Maren' corrected to 'waren'.","The second sentence is a fragment in the German ('Da ihre Logic…'); rendered as a sentence."]],"philippi-s01-179":["The second logic of antiquity is the dogmatic, a. the canons of Epicurus. He set forth the doctrines that are grounded upon",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s01-180":["experience. Naturally such a logic had to produce, by the",["Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"philippi-s01-181":["confirmation of modes of knowledge through sensible cases, great benefit to the understanding. Considered at bottom, however, it was only an",["'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","Sentence joins the previous and following fragments."]],"philippi-s01-182":["...bring about great benefit. Considered at bottom, however, it was only a",["The passage is a fragment, beginning and ending in mid-sentence; translated as it stands, without completing it. The pronoun 'es' is left as 'it' because the context is not supplied and its antecedent cannot be determined.","The capital in 'Im Grunde' follows the transcript's punctuation (comma before it); the sentence is kept as one, with the full stop added after 'benefit' to render the comma/capital break. This is a smoothing, not a reading in the German."]],"philippi-s01-183":["organon of physics. b. Aristotle outlined how one can judge not only the knowledge of experiences but also that of the pure understanding.",["OCR 'l>.' read as 'b.'.","'Erkenntniß der Erfahrungen' rendered 'knowledge of experiences'."]],"philippi-s01-184":["But he fell into nothing but subtleties in matters that were entirely",["Breaks off; continues in next passage."]],"philippi-s01-185":["familiar.",[]],"philippi-s01-186":["The Academics departed from the doctrine of Plato. Among them arose the sect of the Sceptics, who were, as it were, *Antilogici*, contradictors. Instead of giving a logic or a science for the knowledge of reason, they said that no knowledge at all was to be attained. Meanwhile they served to restrain the pride of the decisive and loquacious dogmatists by their *non liquet*. Instead of this modest doubt, by which they as it were plucked the dogmatists by the sleeve, they afterwards adopted a dogmatic doubting, which is an absurdity.",["OCR 'lind' read as 'und'.","'Antilogici' and 'non liquet' kept in Latin.","'Unding' rendered 'absurdity'."]],"philippi-s01-187":["The Scholastics, in what followed, showed in respect of the dogmatists a likeness, and by useless whims made Aristotle's logic useless.",["OCR 'Aelmlichkeit' read as 'Aehnlichkeit'; the sentence is obscure ('in Absicht der Dogmatiker'), rendered literally."]],"philippi-s01-188":["They believed that by subtle distinctions they were procuring great benefit for religion. Disputation was their chief work. Hence one can never get on better than by distinguishing diligently, and thereby throwing dust, as it were, in the other's eyes, so that he cannot see the truth.",[]],"philippi-s01-189":["At the Reformation minds were well prepared for true knowledge.",[]],"philippi-s01-190":["Before this, however, Petrus Ramus at Paris had already begun to declare war on Aristotle. He posted a public notice that he would refute Aristotle, in whatever propositions it might be. Thereby he lifted that blind and servile tyranny and awakened minds from their lethargy. He wrote a logic, the first part of which dealt *de inventione* and the other *de iudicio*. (It is said proverbially that he lacks the *secunda Petri*; that is, the *judicium*.)",["OCR 'ni', 'wekte', 'seeunda' corrected silently (ni = in).","'ni was für Sätzen es auch sey' rendered 'in whatever propositions it might be'."]],"philippi-s01-191":["Melanchthon improved logic. Malebranche has done much good through his *Recherches de la Vérité*. All these reformers of reason served only to abolish empty knowing and to drive away illusions.",["OCR 'Logü-c' = Logic; 'Vernunft -leinen' is garbled: I read 'Vernunftlehrer' (teachers of reason), rendered 'reformers of reason' loosely; doubtful.","'Blendwelke' read as 'Blendwerke', rendered 'illusions'."]],"philippi-s01-192":["The following have taken an essential step therein:",["Breaks off; the list continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s01-193":["Leibniz and Wolf, not so much by new laws and the discovery of aids to attaining truth as rather by the introduction of distinctness and order. He [Wolf] leaves no concept unexplained. He admits no propositions which, though universally known, are indemonstrable, and he seeks to derive everything from one principle, the *principium contradictionis*. But he set his aim too high. It is not possible to explain everything and to give correct proofs of everything. Wolf nevertheless demands it. And it is certainly desirable that he has sketched for philosophy so great a plan and its highest perfection, even though that perfection cannot be attained. It is good that we should be careful to bring all things to the highest distinctness. It is also good that he adopts only one proposition and requires everything to be proved from it; for man can never be more deceived than when he adopts something as a principle. That he also proves propositions that are well known accords with thoroughness; and we have no ground for becoming impatient over it, as Cicero did. Hearing someone speak of the excellence of virtue, he walked out and said: *Stulte quis unquam vituperavit*.",["The passage begins mid-sentence; I have translated it as it stands, without supplying a verb.","Restated as several sentences. 'Er' is taken to be Wolf; I have added '[Wolf]' at its first occurrence.","OCR corrections: 'indem onstrable' read as 'indemonstrable'; 'durehgehends' as 'durchgehends'; stray 'I' and 'un< 1' dropped (the latter read as 'und').","The Latin is left untranslated in the text. It is probably 'Stulte, quis unquam vituperavit?', roughly 'Fool, who ever blamed it?'. Punctuation is not in the transcript."]],"philippi-s01-194":["The demand for the mathematical method is not the most unreasonable. For although the object differs from that of mathematics, and the understanding also proceeds here quite differently from the way it does in philosophy, it is still good if even only a part is attained, just as in morals we have the most perfect morality as our aim. The mathematical method accustoms minds to a taste for distinctness, order and subordination.",["'Sie' in the last sentence has no clear antecedent; I take it to be the mathematical method and have said so in the English."]],"philippi-s01-195":["Wolf does teach how to arrange [*disponiren*]: how one should put the principles first and let the explanations follow, and so on. The Germans will not forget this thoroughness, and will surpass other nations who like to discourse in their philosophies.",["'discuriren' is rendered 'discourse', in the sense of loose discussion.","The German 'Gründlichkeit' is rendered 'thoroughness'."]],"philippi-s01-196":["Locke took the most essential step in paving the way for the understanding. He proposed quite new criteria. He philosophises subjectively, whereas Wolf and all before him philosophised objectively. He investigated the *genesis*, the derivation and the origin of concepts. His logic is not dogmatic but critical. Wolf asks: What is a spirit? Locke asks: Where does the idea of spirit in my soul come from? The soul has never seen a spirit; whence, then, do these thoughts come?",["OCR corrected: 'Lock', 'WTege', 'subjeetiue', 'objectiue philosophirtcn' read as Locke's name, 'Wege', 'subjectiue' and 'philosophirten'. 'Lock' is left as Locke in English.","'Genesin' is Greek-Latin accusative; kept as *genesis*."]],"philippi-s01-197":["Lambert's *Organon*, a work which has much merit with regard to human knowledge. There is a fault among scholars: when they prescribe the conditions under which a mode of knowledge is perfect, they believe they have given the means of attaining perfection. All practical logics are of this kind. They are tautological, and instead of giving means for resolving the questions, they merely give the same questions over again.",["'Erkennt-niß' (broken word) restored. Kant's 'eine Erkenntniß' is rendered 'a mode of knowledge'.","'Organon' is Lambert's title, left as in the source and italicised."]],"philippi-s01-198":["But no other way is open in logic. To teach the exercise [*Ausübung*] of rules is not feasible, for I could do so again only by means of rules.",["'Ausübung' is rendered 'exercise', in the sense of applying rules."]],"philippi-s01-199":["Through attention, logic will become serviceable for judging [*Diiudication*].",["'Diiudication' is the OCR reading, apparently Latin *diiudicatio* (discernment, judging). Rendering is tentative."]],"philippi-s02-001":["*Tractatio ipsa Logices*, that is, Considerations.",["Printed 'Logice'; I read the genitive *Logices* and keep it in Latin. 'Betrachtungen' is rendered 'Considerations'."]],"philippi-s02-002":["1. In logic we do not treat of the origin of knowledge, whether it comes from the senses or not. Epicurus and Aristotle asserted the former; according to Plato and Pythagoras it comes from pure reason.",["OCR 'Wh-' read as 'Wir'.","Restated as two sentences."]],"philippi-s02-003":["2. Logic will treat of the laws of the understanding, not of those according to which one proceeds, but of those according to which one ought to proceed. Hence the object of logical considerations will be not subjective but objective laws of reason, that is, laws to which every finite reason is subject. For here we consider the human understanding in general. Subjective laws, which have only a private validity and depend on the nature of each subject, cannot therefore be dealt with here; they belong to psychology. What we treat of are objective laws, which have universal validity. We trace the procedure of reason in general, just as in morals we investigate not the rules according to which one acts but those according to which one ought to act. Nevertheless it belongs to true logic that the rules be investigated according to which we actually proceed. Helvetius has begun to do this.",["OCR corrections: 'subjeetiue', 'obiectiue', 'abgehandelt weiden' read as 'werden', 'verfähret' as 'verfähret/verfährt'; 'Heluetius' is Helvétius.","'dependiren' is rendered 'depend'."]],"philippi-s02-004":["3. In logic we consider not the determinations [*Bestimmungen*] of things and the relations of things, but only the determinations of concepts and their relations.",[]],"philippi-s02-005":["Since in logic we consider the relations and determinations of concepts only, without applying them to actual objects, we see that logic can be applied to all the sciences and extends over all modes of knowledge. If logic had things for its object, a science with a different object would not fall under it. But since it has no actual thing as its object, it is a general science. So, too, arithmetic has no real object, but teaches only the concepts of numbers and their relations, and can for that reason be applied to all magnitudes, in whatever science they may occur. Logic is accordingly of great utility.",["OCR: 'erstickt' read as 'erstreckt' (extends); 'kernen' as 'keinen'; 'Logic/Logik' and 'Wissenschaffen' normalised.","'Object der Sachen' rendered 'things for its object'. 'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s02-006":["If we know many things, we know little in them; if we know few things, we know much in them.",["'an ihnen' is rendered 'in them', meaning in respect of them."]],"philippi-s02-007":["It is a particular weakness of our understanding that what we gain at one end we lose again at the other.",[]],"philippi-s03-001":["Of learned knowledge in general.",["'gelehrte Erkenntniß' is rendered 'learned knowledge'."]],"philippi-s03-002":["In many things we know little, but in few we can know much. So from the Peak of Tenerife one sees very many objects, but only quite obscurely; if, on the contrary, one takes only a single object before one, one knows it very distinctly.",["'Pico auf Teneriffa' is rendered 'Peak of Tenerife'."]],"philippi-s03-003":["On §10",["'ad §10' refers to the section of Meier's text that Kant is discussing."]],"philippi-s03-004":["The author labours in vain to explain representation. Of what I wish to explain I must have a representation. If I wished, then, to explain a representation, I should have to have a representation of the representation, or I should have to have a representation that is no representation. I can have a representation only of modes of knowledge. Knowledge is something objective; the subjective element in it is sensation. For example, of the sweet and the sour we have no knowledge, but only a representation that there must be something there by which we are so affected.",["'Der Autor' is Meier. 'Erkenntnissen' is rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","OCR 'niüste' read as 'müste'. 'Z. E.' expanded to 'For example'."]],"philippi-s03-005":["Certain things represent something [*stellen etwas vor*]; but of ourselves it is said that we represent something to ourselves [*stellen uns etwas vor*].",["The German turns on the contrast between 'vorstellen' used absolutely and reflexively; the bracketed German is given for that reason."]],"philippi-s03-006":["There are certain means of knowing something. To know these means in turn is impossible.",["'wieder zu erkennen' is rendered 'in turn', though 'again' is also possible."]],"philippi-s03-007":["Logic teaches only how we ought to employ representations and concepts of which we are already conscious. Their origin, however, is not dealt with in it.",["OCR 'mcht' read as 'nicht'; 'bewust' kept as 'conscious'."]],"philippi-s03-008":["Consciousness is, as it were, an inner intuition.",[]],"philippi-s03-009":["The sum of the representations of which we are not conscious is, in comparison with the others, the greatest and the most powerful.",[]],"philippi-s04-001":["§12",[]],"philippi-s04-002":["In every mode of knowledge we shall consider",["Breaks off as a lead-in to the list that follows. 'weiden' read as 'werden'."]],"philippi-s04-003":["1. the matter",["OCR 'cüe' read as 'die'."]],"philippi-s04-004":["2. the form. Such a division is very much in accord with the understanding. It is deeply grounded in the understanding, and by means of such a distinction many propositions can be put in a clear light.",["'Verstände' is rendered 'understanding'."]],"philippi-s04-005":["The matter is that which is given, the *obiectum*. The form is the relation found between this [given material], the *modus cognoscendi*.",["'dem' is ambiguous; I take it to refer to what is given, and bracketed '[given material]'.","Latin kept in italics: *obiectum* (object), *modus cognoscendi* (mode of knowing)."]],"philippi-s04-006":["The matter is the object of knowledge (*obiectum*) and cannot be altered.",[]],"philippi-s04-007":["The form is the *modus cognoscendi*. For example, let spirit be the matter of a representation. This object is always the same; but the form can be altered. The common man represents it to himself as a vapour, which the names [illegible in the OCR] and *spiritus* indicate, whereas the learned think of a spirit in quite another way.",["The words 'n:,~) äus|ios' are garbled OCR, perhaps a Hebrew and a Greek word for breath or spirit. I have not guessed at them.","'Dunst' is rendered 'vapour'."]],"philippi-s04-008":["If I observe a worm through the microscope [*Microscopium*], the form of this worm changes; the object remains the same. Philosophy is distinguished from common understanding [*gemeiner Verstand*] only in form; the matter remains the same for both.",["Two sentences kept as in the German; the full stop before \"die Materie\" (lower-case in the source) is retained as printed.","\"Object\" is rendered \"object\" for Gegenstand/Object, consistently."]],"philippi-s04-009":["All philosophy concerns only the form: that we consider an object piece by piece, and become more conscious of the matter contained in it.",["\"bewust\" (bewusst) rendered \"conscious\"; \"darinn\" resolved as \"in it\", referring to the object."]],"philippi-s05-001":["§13",[]],"philippi-s05-002":["If we are conscious of a mode of knowledge, the concept becomes clear. We are never wholly unconscious of any modes of knowledge. For as soon as we say that we have a piece of knowledge, we must be conscious of it; and a knowledge of which one is not conscious does not occur at all. If, then, unconscious concepts are spoken of, this is to mean no more than concepts of which one is not immediately conscious, but of which one can very well become conscious through inferences.",["\"Erkenntniß\" in the singular is \"knowledge\" or \"a piece of knowledge\"; the plural \"Erkenntnisse\" is \"modes of knowledge\".","\"unbe wüsten\" is an OCR break, silently corrected to \"unbewussten\"; \"Schlüsse\" = inferences."]],"philippi-s06-001":["§ 14",[]],"philippi-s06-002":["If we are conscious of a representation considered as a whole, we are also always conscious of certain parts of this whole, though not of all. Every representation is highly composite. In general, representations are either",["Sentence breaks off before its list, which follows in the next passages."]],"philippi-s06-003":["1. *compositae* (composite), or",[]],"philippi-s06-004":["2. *simplices* (simple). Most are *compositae*.",["The English gloss in parentheses is supplied; the Latin terms are Kant's."]],"philippi-s06-005":["In such a composite representation we are conscious either of the whole and at the same time of the parts found in it, and then our concept is distinct [*deutlich*]; or we are conscious only of the whole without the parts, and then our concept is indistinct [*undeutlich*] or confused [*verworren*].",["\"Jener\" (that) rendered \"such a\" for English smoothness. \"Begrif\" = concept."]],"philippi-s06-006":["A simple mode of knowledge, since it has no parts, can therefore be clear and yet neither distinct nor confused.",["Kemp Smith's \"knowledge\" for Erkenntniß; the sentence is awkward in the German but its sense is clear."]],"philippi-s06-007":["The distinct, therefore, relates to composite representations.",[]],"philippi-s06-008":["I hear a noise; that is certainly a composite representation, but a confused one. If I come nearer, and begin to distinguish the voices in the din, and finally the words of these voices as well, that is a distinct representation.",["\"Lerm\" is read as \"Lärm\" (din)."]],"philippi-s06-009":["Distinct knowledge is that in which there is order; indistinct, that in which disorder prevails, that is, in which the partial representations [*theil Vorstellungen*] are not clear. Order consists in this, that the manifold has its proper place. The simpler our representations are, the less confused they are. There can be distinctness in what we intuit, if we represent each part of it clearly; [and] distinctness in representation and in thinking, if I analyse what I think; for distinctness a concept is required.",["The last sentence is loose and I have restated it with a semicolon and a supplied \"[and]\"; the sense of \"deutlich in der Vorstellung und im Denken\" is somewhat uncertain.","\"anschauen\" rendered \"intuit\" in keeping with Anschauung = intuition."]],"philippi-s06-010":["Distinctness in sensibility [*Sinnlichkeit*] is produced through synthesis [*Synthesin*], in the understanding [*Verstand*] through analysis [*Analysin*].",["Greek-form accusatives *Synthesin*, *Analysin* given in their English equivalents."]],"philippi-s06-011":["I obtain empirical distinctness through a sustained attention to objects of the senses; distinctness of the understanding through attention to the actions of my understanding.",["\"am0\" in the OCR is read as \"auf\" (on), a doubtful reading."]],"philippi-s06-012":["A rational [*vernünftige*] mode of knowledge is one that agrees with reason; a knowledge of reason [*Vernunfterkenntniß*], however, also at the same time springs from reason. It would therefore have been better if our author had said \"knowledge of reason\" instead of \"rational\" knowledge.",["\"Our author\" is Meier, whose textbook Kant is lecturing on. \"Vernunft Erkenntniße\" is plural in the German, here given in the singular for English idiom."]],"philippi-s06-013":["§ 15",[]],"philippi-s06-014":["The relation of ground and consequence of things does not belong in logic at all, but only that of concepts. In logic we shall only recognise how one mode of knowledge contains the ground of another. If the soul is a spirit, it cannot be destroyed through death. Here I do not consider the real [*das Reelle*] of the spirit, but only the concept and its relation to the concept of indestructibility. If the soul ceases with death, it ceases to be a spirit.",["\"Erkennt niß\" is an OCR break, corrected. \"erkennen\" rendered \"recognise\" here, as the sense is of logical insight rather than knowledge of objects.","\"von Sachen\" / \"von Begriffen\": the verb (\"gehört\") is understood in the second member, which I have supplied as \"that of concepts\"."]],"philippi-s07-001":["§ 17 and 18",["\"et\" translated as \"and\"."]],"philippi-s07-002":["Rational knowledge [*vernünftige Erkenntniß*] is not here opposed to irrational knowledge, since that might then mean as much as knowledge in accordance with reason; and \"rational\" is not taken in that sense here. Rational knowledge is here opposed to historical knowledge, and signifies as much as knowledge that has sprung from reason. But when a piece of knowledge may be regarded as having sprung from reason is hard to say. When a mode of knowledge considers the particular from the universal, it has sprung from reason. But if one draws the universal from the particular, or also considers the particular by itself, then the knowledge which one thereby attains is historical. For example, one sees that cold in May is beneficial to the plants of the earth. That is historical knowledge. So one can learn even philosophy historically, if one makes oneself acquainted with the propositions of another one after another, without insight into how these propositions are derived from universal principles. Experimental physics is historical, for it concerns only single cases. As soon as one explains it by universal propositions, however, it becomes rational.",["Several OCR slips silently corrected (Yernünfftige, liier = hier, ausderVernunfft, mochte). \"in dem Verstände\" rendered \"in that sense\", following the context.","Long sentences divided as the method allows; \"Erdgewächsen\" rendered \"plants of the earth\"."]],"philippi-s07-003":["Modes of knowledge of reason [*Vernunfterkenntnisse*] are distinguished from the historical not in respect of matter but only of form.",[]],"philippi-s07-004":["A child learns universal morals in the catechism historically.",[]],"philippi-s07-005":["Modes of knowledge that relate to universal things can become historical if they are judged not *in universali ex abstracto* but *in particulari in concreto*; and conversely, an object of experience can nevertheless become a rational mode of knowledge, if only the form is altered. So it has gone with physics.",["\"vniuersali\" corrected to *universali*."]],"philippi-s07-006":["In all modes of knowledge, if they are to be aesthetically perfect, four things are required, namely sensation, power of judgment, spirit [*Geist*] and taste.",["\"Urteilskraft\" = power of judgment; \"Geist\" left as \"spirit\" (the German could also be rendered \"genius\" or \"animation\", but the lecture does not decide it)."]],"philippi-s07-007":["§ 19",[]],"philippi-s07-008":["Knowledge of the understanding is distinguished from sensible knowledge in this way: sensible knowledge is that which considers things in so far as they affect the senses; knowledge of reason, on the other hand, relates to what does not affect sensibility but is universal and is generated in the understanding. Sensible knowledge therefore extends to single things, knowledge of the understanding to universal things. Here is a convenient place for distinguishing the science of the beautiful from the science of the understanding.",["Kant here shifts from \"Verstandeserkenntniß\" to \"Vernunffterkenntniß\" without comment; I keep both as in the German.","\"rühren\" rendered \"affect\" (touch)."]],"philippi-s07-009":["The agreement of knowledge with subjective laws is aesthetic perfection; with objective laws, logical perfection.",[]],"philippi-s07-010":["Besides the fact that a mode of knowledge agrees with the object, I can also take note how the knowledge affects the subject.",["\"afficirt\" rendered \"affects\"."]],"philippi-s07-011":["Knowledge agrees with the subject when it puts all the powers of the mind [*Gemüthskräfte*] into an easy and free play, and my knowledge at the same time becomes strong and great.",[]],"philippi-s07-012":["The science which contains the first grounds of distinction of the beautiful is aesthetic. We have a natural power of distinguishing, by which we distinguish whether something is beautiful or not. This is entitled judging [*Beurtheilung*] or criticism [*Critick*].",["\"Critick\" rendered \"criticism\" here and \"critique\" is avoided for consistency."]],"philippi-s07-013":["The criticism which sets forth the rules for judging the sensible is aesthetic. The criticism which sets forth the rules for judging modes of knowledge of the understanding is logic.",[]],"philippi-s07-014":["We have a twofold way of distinguishing things, or our knowledge has a twofold relation:",[]],"philippi-s07-015":["1. we judge of the thing what it is.",[]],"philippi-s07-016":["2. how the thing pleases. The former relates to the object, the latter to the subject.",[]],"philippi-s07-017":["A scholar followed Homer, map in hand, on all the journeys described in the Odyssey, and found in them the greatest correctness and exactness. Here he had an object, without recognising how it pleases.",["\"Carte\" read as \"map\"; \"nach\" as \"following ... by\", slightly smoothed."]],"philippi-s07-018":["In a mode of knowledge we consider either, in general,",["Breaks off into the list that follows."]],"philippi-s07-019":["1. what the thing is, that is, its constitution [*Beschaffenheit*]; or",[]],"philippi-s07-020":["2. what relation this object has to our subject. That constitutes the worth of the thing. Worth therefore consists in the relation of the thing to thinking beings. For example: (1) I see the moon, consider its body, investigate its size, its distance from us, its motion. In doing so I do not look to the relation of the moon to our subject. (2) I investigate its worth, which it has for thinking beings. If there were no thinking being, it would have no worth, just as the jewel has no worth except when it comes into the possession of another.",["\"Juvel\" read as *Juwel* (jewel); \"Werth\" rendered \"worth\"."]],"philippi-s07-021":["These two kinds of knowledge and of representation are essentially different. Through the one we judge what the thing is, even if it has no reference to thinking beings or has no value. In the other I decide what the object is worth in relation to thinking beings. And through what do I decide this? What is the standard of value? The feeling of pleasure and displeasure, or sensation. By this we judge whether an object is agreeable or disagreeable, beautiful or ugly, and that constitutes its value.",["Erkenntnißarten rendered 'kinds of knowledge'; 'sensation' for Empfindung, as the fixed term list requires, although it is here equated with Gefühl."]],"philippi-s07-022":["But the properties of objects are known through the understanding. The understanding determines what is true or false, good or evil.",["'Properties' for Beschaffenheiten."]],"philippi-s07-023":["We are thus capable of essentially different modes of knowledge: (1) certain modes of knowledge which concern the properties, and (2) modes of knowledge which concern the value of objects. If this is so, we have also two wholly distinct sources from which we draw these modes of knowledge. One is that from which we derive all knowledge of the properties of things, and this is the understanding. The other is that from which we derive the knowledge of the value of things, and this is, in a word, sensibility. Just as the understanding can know nothing of value, so sensibility can know nothing of the properties of objects. The word sensibility here means as much as the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and value means the liking or disliking of a thing. Value is therefore no object, but, as has often been said, only the relation of the object to the subject.",["The German sentence 'Wir sind also zwar...' has a 'zwar' with no answering 'aber'; I left it untranslated.","'Er' in 'Er ist also kein Object' is taken to refer to Werth (value); I supplied 'Value' in English.","Restated the long sentence on the two sources as several shorter ones."]],"philippi-s08-001":["§22",[]],"philippi-s08-002":["If a mode of knowledge is so constituted that it agrees with the universal rules of the understanding, it is logically perfect. If a mode of knowledge is so constituted that it agrees with the universal rules of taste, or of pleasure and displeasure, it is aesthetically perfect. Logical perfection is an agreement of knowledge with the object; the aesthetic is an agreement of knowledge with feeling. The former is truth, the latter beauty. What pleases us pleases us either",["Silently corrected OCR 'Erkenntmß' and 'Zusammenstimmimg'. The passage breaks off, with the sentence continued in the next passage.","Eine Erkenntniß is rendered 'a mode of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s08-003":["1. immediately, or",[]],"philippi-s08-004":["2. mediately. Money, for example, pleases a rational man mediately. It pleases him only as a means to what pleases.",[]],"philippi-s08-005":["It is sad that what ought to please us immediately pleases us only mediately. Virtue, indeed Deity itself, pleases mortals only mediately. The former pleases merely on account of its agreeable accompaniments, the latter on account of its benefactions. Perhaps this is our whole case; indeed it is.",["'Jene' and 'diese' resolved as virtue and Deity respectively."]],"philippi-s08-006":["Good food pleases us (1) immediately, on account of its good taste, and (2) mediately, as a means to health. A bitter medicine is immediately disagreeable, but mediately it pleases. The beautiful pleases immediately. Something pleases mediately in so far as it is a means to another end which pleases us. A flower-piece can please both mediately and immediately at once, if the flowers are also of some utility.",[]],"philippi-s08-007":["What pleases immediately pleases either one person and not many others, or it pleases all universally. What pleases immediately only one person has private validity and is entitled agreeable. But what pleases universally has universal validity and is entitled beautiful.",["'Is entitled' for heißt."]],"philippi-s08-008":["By 'agreeable' I express that something is an object merely of my pleasure, whether or not it arouses pleasure or displeasure in others. How wrong it is, then, to think that what is agreeable to us must be agreeable to others also. Would not a man who believed that the dish which tastes pleasant to him must taste the same to all without exception be as laughable as the Greenlanders who, when told of Paradise, at once ask whether there are seals there, whose oil is their choicest food? The agreeable is thus different in different subjects. And what is the cause? What is agreeable gratifies. Gratification is an agreeable sensation; hence the agreeable is a sensation. Now sensation consists in the manner in which sensibility is modified by an object. But sensibility is, to a certain extent, different in almost all men. Hence their sensation is different, and consequently the agreeable also, since it is sensation. From this we conclude that what pleases in sensation gratifies, that what gratifies has private validity, and that therefore what pleases in sensation has private validity.",["Divided the long closing argument into shorter sentences.","'Hieraus folgen wir' read as 'folgern wir' ('we conclude').","'Thran' rendered 'oil' (train-oil, blubber oil); 'vergnügt' as 'gratifies'.","'Beliebig' sense of 'er mag... erwecken' rendered 'whether or not it arouses'."]],"philippi-s08-009":["As by 'agreeable' I express what pleases me alone, so by 'beautiful' I express what pleases everyone universally. The agreeable has private validity; the beautiful has universal validity. The agreeable is a sensation; the beautiful is a judging [*Beurtheilung*]. The agreeable concerns the subject; the beautiful, however, the object. This last has to be proved. The proof will follow below.",["The OCR text has 'hatte' (had) for the agreeable; I read 'hat' as the context requires. This is a doubtful reading.","'Judging' for Beurtheilung, to distinguish it from Urtheil (judgment)."]],"philippi-s08-010":["Judgments which refer to the subject can have different relations. Judgments which refer to the object have like relations in the subject, though not of like degree. The former consider the agreeable, the latter the beautiful, or what pleases universally. Hitherto we have, it is true, called whatever pleases universally beautiful without restriction; but we now wish to distinguish more exactly.",[]],"philippi-s08-011":["What pleases universally is what necessarily pleases everyone. What pleases universally in such a manner pleases either",["Silently corrected OCR 'wras noth wendiger weise'. The passage breaks off, with the sentence continued in the next passages."]],"philippi-s08-012":["1. in accordance with universal principles [*Sätze*] of sensibility, and is entitled beautiful; or",["'Principles' for Sätze is a choice; 'propositions' is the literal sense."]],"philippi-s08-013":["2. in accordance with universal principles of the understanding and of reason, and is entitled good.",[]],"philippi-s08-014":["That which pleases universally in accordance with universal principles of sensibility can be seen to do so only *a posteriori*, through experience.",["'Einsehen' rendered 'be seen to'."]],"philippi-s08-015":["Only through experience can one, for example, find out the food which pleases everyone, or at least many. A man who, instructed by much experience, knows how to choose what pleases all is said to have taste. One who cannot hit upon what pleases everyone is without taste, and if he wishes to acquire it must attend to observations and experiences.",[]],"philippi-s08-016":["He who chooses only what pleases himself, but not others, has no taste at all.",[]],"philippi-s08-017":["Taste is a judgment in accordance with universally valid laws.",[]],"philippi-s08-018":["What pleases in accordance with laws of sensibility is beautiful.",[]],"philippi-s08-019":["What pleases in accordance with laws of the understanding and of reason is good. We have not only a participating feeling [*Empfindung*] (sympathy) but also an inclination to communicate, and we distrust our modes of knowledge. Hence the learned always make their thoughts universally known. Other men are, as it were, controllers of our judgments.",["Silently corrected OCR 'eme'. 'Empfindung' here is 'feeling', not the fixed 'sensation', since it denotes sympathy; flagged because the term list fixes 'sensation'.","'Erkenntnißen' rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s08-020":["Nature has given taste preeminently to sociable people. People without taste always have something obstinate about them and concern themselves only with their own well-being, which moreover they often place in things that are disgusting to others. The sociable know by sensation how to distinguish what pleases everyone, and that is true taste. How? Do they distinguish it by sensation and not by experience? It is true that one cannot guess from one's own sensation what pleases others, for subjects are different. Yet if one observes many experiences, the relation of the object to the senses finally becomes such that one hits, contingently, upon what pleases everyone. This therefore happens not properly through sensation but through experience.",["Silently corrected OCR 'semer' and 'Verhält -niß'.","'Zufälliger weise' rendered 'contingently'; 'by chance' is also possible.","The passage appears to say 'by sensation' and then to correct itself in favour of experience. I kept this movement as it stands."]],"philippi-s08-021":["It is of great importance to distinguish taste in objects of sensation from taste in objects of appearance. Does what pleases me in appearance please others also? We answer this important question in the affirmative, and certainly not without ground.",["'Erscheinung' is rendered 'appearance' as the fixed term list requires; the contrast with 'Empfindung' here is obscure and may reflect the transcriber's wording."]],"philippi-s08-022":["What pleases one in appearance pleases all. For here sensation is not what matters. In appearances things are considered not according to the matter of sensibility, that is, sensation, but only according to the form, that is, according to the manner in which my subject would know the object sensibly even if the object caused no sensation in me. Sensation and appearance differ as matter and form: the matter of sensibility is sensation, the form is appearance. A thing can please in appearance even if it is not an object of sensation. It then pleases on account of its form, that is, on account of the harmonious relations of all the sensible representations which I receive of the thing, and which, taken together, I entitle the object.",["Restated the first half of the long second sentence as two sentences; 'oder' (sensation) rendered as an explanatory 'that is'.","'sinnlich erkennen' rendered 'know the object sensibly'; 'nennen' rendered 'entitle' per the method."]],"philippi-s08-023":["Thus, for example, a regularly built palace pleases the Spaniard who walks about on an empty stomach, although he gets nothing out of it for his sensation.",["'Pallast' is the old spelling of 'Palast' (palace)."]],"philippi-s08-024":["In music the melodic element, or the sound of the tones, is the matter; their form, however, consists in the harmonious alternation of these tones. As regards the matter, or the sound, the one instrument may be more agreeable to one person, another to another; for here it depends on sensation, which differs in different subjects. But as regards the form of music, a concert that is harmonious must sound well to all. The matter of a flower is its scent, the form its shape.",["OCR 'sejm' corrected to 'sein'.","'Concert' kept as 'concert' (a musical performance/piece)."]],"philippi-s08-025":["Taste has universally valid laws, which are derived from the nature of sensibility. They cannot, however, be [found] a priori. We can only criticise taste according to rules a priori.",["OCR 'Gesehmak' corrected to 'Geschmack'.","'gefällt werden' is doubtful: it makes no sense as it stands ('be pleased'). I supply '[found]', perhaps for 'gefunden' or 'gefühlt'; the reading is uncertain.","'critisiren' rendered 'criticise'."]],"philippi-s08-026":["The norm contains the rules according to which something ought to be done. The norm of taste is met with in actual works of taste; it is therefore a model, not a precept.",["Joined by a semicolon after splitting the sentence; 'Muster' = model, 'Vorschrift' = precept."]],"philippi-s08-027":["For a clearer insight into these important propositions we repeat once more: Objects in appearance are not considered subjectively, in the way in which they affect the subject with a feeling of pleasure or displeasure, but as they are known through the subject as objects of sensibility. Now if the objects of appearance are known in such a way that the impressions they make upon our sensibility stand in an exact relation to one another and, taken together, constitute a harmonious whole, then we entitle this harmonious whole, which we regard as the object itself, beautiful. If, however, there is between the impressions which an object makes upon our sensibility no relation, or only a weak one, so that taken together they form no whole or an imperfect whole, then we say of the object, which we understand under the *complexus* of the sensible impressions, that it is not beautiful, and indeed less beautiful in the first case than in the other. There, then, we have analysed the beautiful according to its essential concepts!",["'subiectiue' normalised to 'subjectively'; 'complexus' kept in Latin italics.","'zwischen ... kein oder doch ein schwaches Verhältniß' split into clauses for clarity.","The remark 'less beautiful in the first case than in the other' follows the German as it stands (the text may be loose: 'no relation' would be less beautiful than 'a weak one' only in a sense)."]],"philippi-s08-028":["But do we always judge of the beautiful in the manner described? We should do so, if charm [*Reiz*], that is, our interest in the thing and our appropriation of it, did not prevent us. This brings it about that we commonly prefer what belongs to us, even though it is less beautiful, to what has far more beauties but does not belong to us.",["'Reiz' rendered 'charm'; 'Antheilnehmung und Zueignung' rendered 'interest ... and appropriation', a choice (literally 'taking part' and 'making one's own')."]],"philippi-s08-029":["Then we judge of the beautiful mixed with sensation. So it may happen that a garden which belongs to me pleases me better than another's garden, which is nevertheless far more beautiful than mine. For what my garden lacks in beauty is made up by the charm, or interest, of my possessing it. There are cases in which the beautiful is judged without sensation.",["OCR '\\\\a>' read as 'Was' ('what').","'Theilnehmung' rendered 'interest'; the last sentence is as in the text."]],"philippi-s08-030":["Winckelmann shows that the masculine build of the body is much more beautiful than the feminine. It is charm that makes us prefer the latter to the former.",["'Winkelmann' normalised to Winckelmann."]],"philippi-s08-031":["I hold the view from my window over a beautiful countryside to be more beautiful than the far more beautiful countryside that another person has. For what mine lacks in beauty is made up by the charm of my possessing it. If, then, I judge of the beautiful mixed with charm, what belongs to me may well appear more beautiful to me than other things that are actually more beautiful.",["'ersetzt den Reiz' has an accusative in the OCR; read as 'ersetzt der Reiz' (nominative), as the sense requires.","'vor' in 'vor schöner' read as 'für' ('hold to be')."]],"philippi-s08-032":["Judgments of the beautiful relate to the object; hence they have universal validity. Of judgments about an object, two cannot contradict each other. What one person judges of the object has universal validity. If, therefore, one says, this thing is beautiful, and another, it is ugly, at least one of them must have spoken falsely.",["'Gültigkeit' rendered 'validity'."]],"philippi-s08-033":["Judgments of the agreeable relate to the subject and have private validity. Here two persons can often contradict each other. If one says, this food tastes only agreeable, and the other, it is disgusting to me, both may be right.",["'schmeckt nur angenehm' is as in the text; 'nur' may be a slip (perhaps 'mir angenehm')."]],"philippi-s08-034":["Of the former judgments, which have universal validity, it is to be noted that they have their universality only according to rules of sensibility and in appearance, not in concepts.",["'erstern' (OCR 'erstem') read as 'ersten', i.e. 'the former', meaning the judgments of the beautiful."]],"philippi-s08-035":["We wish to sketch quite briefly, in their connection, the things we have said hitherto about the beautiful and the agreeable.",[]],"philippi-s08-036":["We have two faculties, the power of knowledge and feeling. Through the former we learn what a thing is; through the latter, how a thing pleases or does not please, that is, how the thing stands in relation to our feeling of pleasure or displeasure.",["OCR 'sieh' corrected to 'sich'.","'Erkenntnißkrafft' rendered 'power of knowledge'; 'Fähigkeiten' as 'faculties'."]],"philippi-s08-037":["To have pleasure and displeasure is something other than to know what a thing is.",[]],"philippi-s08-038":["If the judgment is so constituted that it relates merely to one person, or holds only for one, then the judgment does not relate to the object. But if something pleases all, it must relate to the object. Fashions, therefore, are pleasing not to objects but to subjects. Among us the uncovering of the head is a sign of politeness; among the Turks it is the uncovering of the feet. The Circassian women, because they please all nations, must please as objects; their beauty must lie in themselves, in their object. Of what pleases only one, the ground of the satisfaction lies in the subject; of what pleases all, the ground of the satisfaction must lie in the object. Our adjudication and distinction of things according to their relation to our pleasure and displeasure takes place",["OCR garbled: '< m 1er nur vor einen' read as 'oder nur vor einen' (for 'für einen'); 'Hauptentblössen' read as 'Haupt-entblößen'; 'Tseherkessinnen' as 'Tscherkessinnen'; 'Düudication' as 'Dijudikation' (adjudication).","Passage breaks off with the colon-less 'geschieht'; continued by the next passages (1., 2.). Translated without completion.","Restated 'bloß auf einen geht' as 'relates merely to one person' (supplied 'person')."]],"philippi-s08-039":["1. from subjective grounds, which hold only for one person;",["Supplied 'person' in 'für einen'; 'aus subiectiven Gründen' normalised."]],"philippi-s08-040":["2. from objective grounds, which hold for all.",[]],"philippi-s08-041":["Sensibility is the manner in which an object pleases a subject. If we judge a thing according to the relations of pleasure and displeasure from subjective grounds, we then distinguish the agreeable and disagreeable, the gratifying and the disgusting. But if we judge the relation of a thing to our pleasure and displeasure from objective grounds, we then distinguish the beautiful from the not beautiful. What is beautiful pleases in the thing itself, and is agreeable in the thing itself. But what pleases in the universal relation I do not consider in respect of how it is agreeable. When I say that something pleases, that which pleases is the matter, but the universality is the form.",["OCR '\\\\ ir' read as 'wir'.","'Sinnlichkeit ist die Art ...' is rendered as it stands; the sense may call for 'Geschmack' or the like, but I do not alter it.","'Vergnügende' rendered 'gratifying'; 'eckelhaffte' 'disgusting'."]],"philippi-s08-042":["What pleases universally pleases in a twofold manner: 1. according to laws of sensibility, and that is beautiful and pleases in appearance; 2. according to laws of the understanding, and that is good and pleases in concepts.",[]],"philippi-s08-043":["Virtue cannot fall within the senses, and pleases only in concepts. Virtuous actions can please in appearance. Virtue pleases, but does not gratify. Therein consists the whole fall of mortals: what pleases them in concepts does not gratify them in sensibility, and what pleases them in sensibility does not please them in concepts. Among the Italians the fashion pleases in sensibility that ladies take their *cavaliere servente* with them wherever they drive. But to whom does it please in concepts",["Passage breaks off with a stray '\\\\' after 'Begriffen'; translated as it stands, the question left unfinished.","'Cavaliere Servente' kept in Italian, italicised.","'der ganze Fall der Sterblichen' is rendered literally ('fall', perhaps with a play on the Fall of man)."]],"philippi-s08-044":["In the case of sensibility, the matter is that which pleases, namely sensation; the form is the way in which this or that thing pleases, namely the appearance.",["Empfindung = sensation; Erscheinung = appearance. The 'namely' clauses render the repeated 'oder' as identifications."]],"philippi-s08-045":["That in the beautiful which pleases in relation to sensation is entitled charm (*der Reiz*). Charm is therefore what sensation is, namely the manner in which my organs are affected by the object. Consequently it will also share with sensation the feature that it is not universally valid. Charm must not be confounded with essential beauty, which consists solely in the form of the object; otherwise we may often find ourselves deceived in judging of the beautiful. Thus we are deceived by charm in judging the beauty of a woman. Deceived by charm, a man holds the whole second sex to be more beautiful than the male.",["'offtin' read as 'oft in' (OCR slip).","Reiz rendered 'charm'; 'Frauenzimmer' rendered 'woman'.","Last sentence: 'man' supplied as the subject of the impersonal 'hält man'."]],"philippi-s08-046":["In music harmony is the proper beauty and is for the understanding; melody, on the other hand, is charm, or sensation. The former is universally valid and unalterable. The latter, charm, differs according to the difference of subjects. Melody is nothing other than sensations expressed through tones. According as a subject is more or less inclined to the sensations which the melodic element of a piece of music expresses, to that degree the music is more or less charming for that subject. Dazzled by charm, many a man may hold mediocre, indeed even bad, music to be very beautiful; but another does not think so.",["'Subjeet' read as 'Subjekt' (OCR slip).","'wol' rendered 'indeed even'; the sentence is slightly smoothed."]],"philippi-s08-047":["The beautiful form has universal charm; that is to say, true beauty pleases everyone at all times. And why? Because the ground of the satisfaction lies in the object. Sensation, or charm, is changeable and diverse. Why? Because the ground of the satisfaction lies in the subject.",["'veränderlieh', 'hegt' read as 'veränderlich', 'liegt' (OCR). Wohlgefallen = satisfaction."]],"philippi-s08-048":["I say that charm is changeable. When the organs become dulled, things lose their charm. One sees this in old people, who for that very reason judge more correctly of beauty.",["'siebet' read as 'siehet'."]],"philippi-s08-049":["The matter of a sensible object requires to be sensibly felt, its form to be judged by taste. We come, then, to taste. The laws of sensibility are very much in agreement; from this it follows that taste has universal rules. Many hold it to be a fashion; and how many other explanations of it have been given? I do not know whether it may not be correctly explained if it is taken as a criticism [*Critick*] of what pleases universally, so that he has taste who knows how to choose what pleases universally. But what is it that criticises what pleases universally? Is it the understanding? No, but sensibility exercised by experience. We shall see below that what accords with the universal rules of the understanding does not at all please according to the rules of sensibility, and conversely. Truths please the understanding the more, the deeper and more hidden they are and the more labour and exertion it must employ to understand them; and this is just what is most intolerable to sensibility. The ground of the satisfaction in propositions of reason lies, if I am not mistaken, in the increase of insights and their perfection; in products of taste, in the ease with which a multitude of manifold impressions can be received, ordered without trouble, distinguished, vividly felt, and surveyed as a whole. The laws of our sensibility, we said above, agree very closely with the laws of the sensibility of others, and from these the fundamental rules of taste are derived. What laws are these?",["'sinnhehen', 'sinnheh' read as 'sinnlichen', 'sinnlich'; 'hegt' etc. silently corrected.","Long sentences divided in places; 'Critick' kept as 'criticism' with German. 'Vernunftsätzen' = propositions of reason.","'Verstände' read as 'Verstande'."]],"philippi-s08-050":["Our sensibility is in constant activity and wishes also to be so constantly. From this fundamental law of sensibility the rule of taste is drawn: if an object is to please sensibility, manifoldness must be introduced into it, so that sensibility may receive material with which it can occupy itself. The mind is active in the form of all objects. It supplies the materials and wants them to be formed by means of the object.",["'be}r' read as 'bey' (OCR). 'Geschmaks' = taste.","Last sentence is obscure in the German; rendered literally."]],"philippi-s08-051":["Everything that hinders this activity of sensibility is vexatious and disagreeable to it. From this flows the rule: let one endeavour to introduce into the manifold symmetry, harmony and clearness, and in general comprehensibility, so that sensibility may grasp the object without trouble and may easily distinguish and feel its impressions. Taste, therefore, demands manifoldness, contrast, harmony, ease, clearness, and a gradual transition from one thing to its opposite; a leap confuses sensibility. An object in which all this is introduced in a comprehensible relation is essentially beautiful and pleases universally. A large object such as a building pleases when symmetry is introduced into it; symmetry makes the sight of the building easier.",["'Smnlichkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit' (OCR)."]],"philippi-s08-052":["Chords in music please on account of the easy relation between their tones. The octave is as 1 to 2; the octave to the fifth as 1 to 3. [See] Euler, p. 16. Everything that enlarges, facilitates and makes clearer the representation of our sensibility pleases.",["'viel. Eulerum p. 16' is doubtful; 'viel.' perhaps an abbreviation for 'vide' (see); I give 'See Euler' in brackets. 'Eulerum' is an accusative form.","The ratios are as in the text; 'octave to the fifth' is as printed."]],"philippi-s08-053":["There are, then, certain common laws of sensibility with regard to form, and these, if I am not mistaken, will be those which I have so far gone through.",["'sejm' read as 'seyn' (OCR)."]],"philippi-s08-054":["But charm must be wholly set aside. These common laws of taste are drawn from concrete cases, but may in any event also apply to the object, only the subjects must be capable of it. If a man does not know the laws of sensibility, the beautiful cannot please him either; for objects of the senses must be judged according to laws of sensibility, and such a man judges by charm. When an Iroquois who had been led all around Paris was asked what had pleased him best, he said, the cook-shop. What pleases because it is beautiful pleases quite differently from what pleases because it is agreeable. When Homer and a crowd of people were listening to a poet and the dinner-bell was rung, everyone ran off; Homer alone stayed with him. The poet praised him as a true connoisseur of beauty, who did not let himself be dazzled by charm like the rabble. But hardly had he learnt that the dinner-bell was ringing (for he was deaf) than he too went off.",["'Homer' here is as in the transcript (the anecdote is presumably of another man of that name); the form 'der Homer' is kept.","'aufs Object gehen' is obscure; rendered literally, 'apply to the object'. 'sejm' etc. not occurring; no other doubtful readings."]],"philippi-s08-055":["Self-seeking persons have no taste; for they look only to what is charming to them, whereas the essential thing in the beautiful is that one look to what pleases universally.",["'Eigennützigen' rendered 'self-seeking persons'."]],"philippi-s08-056":["If one considers the relation of an object to sensibility, one looks to charm; if one considers it in relation to taste, then that is beauty.",["'Sinnhchkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit' (OCR). 'dasjenige' supplied as 'it' (the object's relation)."]],"philippi-s08-057":["I note further that understanding belongs to taste.",[]],"philippi-s08-058":["One judges the beautiful not according to the degree of satisfaction but according to universal laws of sensibility.",[]],"philippi-s08-059":["One can, for example, build a house which obstructs someone's view. If it has true beauty, even this person will hold it to be beautiful, however little charm it may have for him.",[]],"philippi-s08-060":["I conclude this treatise with some reflections on the beautiful:",["'Schlüsse' read as 'schliesse' (OCR/ transcript slip)."]],"philippi-s08-061":["1. In solitude man is very indifferent to the beautiful. There they look only to their own well-being, only to charm. What agreeably affects his sensation he makes his chief aim.",["The plural 'sie' after the singular 'der Mensch' is kept as in the transcript."]],"philippi-s08-062":["When I am alone, I like it better in the forest; in company, however, better in the garden. The beautiful form seems to exist merely for company. But how does it come about that taste vanishes in solitude? In solitude we look only to our private liking. This must not happen in judging according to laws of taste. There we must conform to the universal liking, however little charming and pleasing it may be to us.",["'Einsamiveit' read as 'Einsamkeit' (OCR). 'an Beurtheilung' taken as 'in judging'."]],"philippi-s08-063":["When we are alone, we never attend to the beautiful. In the country little regard is paid to the beautiful. Those who concern themselves with it are either those who still retain an impression of town life (which, however, also fades with time), or those who have frequent intercourse with persons of standing. A man on a desert island would prefer the slightest [degree] of feeling [*Gefühl*] to all taste, and in the end would lose taste altogether. If he had a piece of cloth for a coat, he would hardly cut a French waist [*Taille*] out of it. Comfort would be his chief aim.",["\"das Geringste des Gefühls\": the noun \"degree\" is supplied in brackets; the sense is that even the least gratification of feeling would outweigh taste.","\"Taüle\" (OCR/MS spelling) read as *Taille*, a fitted waist."]],"philippi-s08-064":["Taste is a forerunner of sociability, and sociability is the nourishment of taste. There is a certain delicacy of taste, and in this what counts is not so much judging [*Beurtheilung*] as sensation [*Empfindung*] or well-being [*Wohlbefinden*]. This delicacy of taste is a fruit of sociability.",["The second sentence is restated slightly for English idiom (\"kommt es nicht so sehr an auf\")."]],"philippi-s08-065":["II. Sociability is the cause and motive of taste. Sociable men acquire so refined a feeling that they experience a far greater pleasure when something pleases others than when they do themselves some good. They are more concerned for the well-being of others than for their own. So-called vanity is inseparable from taste, and this name is commonly given, with malicious mockery, to the conduct of sociable men. But is he then deserving of hatred who seeks to make himself agreeable to others, and feels pleasure when he can show others a satisfaction? Vanity, rather, so long as it remains within the limits which virtue sets for it, is praiseworthy.",["\"IL\" in the OCR is read as \"II.\" (the second point of an enumeration); the final \"1\" is read as \"?\".","\"Wohlgefallen erzeigen\" rendered \"show a satisfaction\"; the phrase is slightly odd in the German."]],"philippi-s08-066":["The agreeable concerns the well-being of a private person; the beautiful concerns the appearance [*Erscheinung*] of the thing and is the proper object of taste. Yet taste is often employed also in regard to what pleases in enjoyment and in sensation; but this must be judged empirically. One has, then, a taste not only of judgment but also of feeling, if one can hit upon, from one's own feeling, what pleases universally, or at least pleases many, in sensation. Many a man knows well, for example, what dish pleases many.",["\"Erscheinung\" rendered \"appearance\" per the fixed terms."]],"philippi-s08-067":["It is then a mark of a refined taste when one can, by feeling, even distinguish what pleases universally. The sociable man, who has sympathetic passions, has such a refined taste.",[]],"philippi-s08-068":["If one wishes to be agreeable, one must choose what pleases others, III. Young people have more feeling than taste, and the old more taste than feeling.",["\"III.\" is the transcript's enumeration marker, run on after a comma; kept where it stands."]],"philippi-s08-069":["The causes of this are as follows. (1) Taste is acquired through experience. (2) Where there is much feeling, taste is lacking. For what stands in relation to taste pleases in universal relations and concerns the understanding; what stands in relation to feeling pleases in a private relation. He who seeks what pleases in a private relation will not find what pleases universally; and he whose organs are strongly affected, as happens in constant feeling, has a blunt taste.",["\"ün\" in the OCR read as \"in\".","The sentence beginning \"Denn\" is divided at the semicolon for clarity; the German is a single run of clauses."]],"philippi-s08-070":["The endeavour of some authors is directed merely to feeling, not to taste. It is certainly also easier to depict objects in strongly sensuous fashion than to order a plan of ideas so that one can survey them almost at a glance. The former requires only imagination, only sensation; the latter, understanding and judgment. One must have experience and know how to compare things with one another, if one wishes to have taste.",["\"Gegenstände\" rendered \"objects\"; \"zu diesem\" resolved as \"the latter\" (the ordering of a plan of ideas)."]],"philippi-s08-071":["An object is beautiful in so far as order, unity, symmetry and proportion are to be met with in it and it pleases in the appearance [*Erscheinung*], not in so far as it has an influence upon our sensibility [*Sinnlichkeit*].",["\"Ebenmaaß\" rendered \"symmetry\"; this is a choice."]],"philippi-s08-072":["Besides what pleases us in objects there is yet another ground of satisfaction, namely whether the object also belongs to us, even remotely. That is charm [*Reiz*], and it begets vanity. Reflection upon the appropriation of the object is already present in charm. When a man has great merits, we always recognise some connections in which he stands with us, in order that, besides the satisfaction in his merits, we may also feel a charm which in some measure belongs to us. Those seven cities which contended over Homer's fatherland had this in view. We always like to boast of that to which we have a claim; that is vanity. The essential beauty is the form; from this must be distinguished the charm, which accords with the laws of the inclinations and passions.",["\"Reiz\" rendered \"charm\" throughout; \"Wohlgefallen\" as \"satisfaction\".","\"Die um Homers Vaterland stritten\" is placed after \"cities\" in English; the German order is awkward.","The full stop after \"Eitelkeit\" is supplied; the German has none."]],"philippi-s08-073":["If someone inherits a beautiful palace in a foreign land, and comes to the place and beholds it for the first time, he looks at it (1) as a passing traveller, without charm; then it pleases him in the appearance, and he then sees the true beauty alone; (2) he regards it as the property of his kinsman; that already belongs to charm; (3) he regards it as his own property, which he will now take into possession; here there is feeling.",["\"siebet\" read as \"sieht\", \"dami\" as \"dann\" (OCR).","\"Anverwandten\" rendered \"kinsman\"; presumably the one from whom he inherits, though the text does not say."]],"philippi-s08-074":["There is a difference between beautiful objects and beautiful modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*] or representations [*Vorstellungen*].",[]],"philippi-s08-075":["The representation may be beautiful although the object is ugly. The object, again, may be beautiful, and the representation of it ugly.",[]],"philippi-s08-076":["In Holland people love grotesque pictures. The representations are odd, the objects are beautiful.",["\"Avunderlich\" in the OCR read as \"wunderlich\". The sense of the last sentence is doubtful: the text appears to say that the representations are odd although the objects are beautiful."]],"philippi-s08-077":["The representation is entitled a depiction [*Abbildung*], in so far as it is presented to the senses.",[]],"philippi-s08-078":["Just as representations may displease us whose object in itself pleases us, so descriptions and representations may please us although their object displeases us. One can make the knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] of an ugly object quite charming. Thus, for example, agreeable and pleasing representations can be made of the Dead Sea, of the frozen zones, and of desolate nature, objects which in themselves displease us; that is to say, representations which stir and move us in an agreeable way. One can even represent very agreeably what is abominable; indeed, one can even mingle charm into representations of such a kind.",["\"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\" per the fixed term, though here it comes close to \"representation\"."]],"philippi-s08-079":["Poets take the trouble, the easy trouble, of representing charmingly things which are in any case all too moving for us, and whose charm they ought rather to damp down. An easy trouble, certainly! If only they would try to clothe in charm that which is true, essential beauty, and which has now lost all charm in our eyes, I mean virtue.",["\"gegen uns\" rendered \"in our eyes\"; the construction is loose. The exclamatory second half is restated as a wish."]],"philippi-s08-080":["We can mingle a charm into descriptions of the abominable, not only when we depict it quite true to life and represent it movingly, but also through smiling and beautiful descriptions.",["\"sondern\" is rendered \"but also\" to bring out the contrast with \"nicht nur\"."]],"philippi-s08-081":["I regard with a cheerful countenance the sublime, splendid mockery with which Wieland praises vice. No abuse, no cursing is more cutting than mockery, especially mockery of a kind over which one still makes merry; and so too an object never becomes more abominable than when one represents it in this way. Instead of scolding and cursing vice, as is usual, and thereby putting the reader or hearer into a melancholy, sorrowful agitation, one should represent it in beautiful ironical descriptions and sublime narratives, and then all at once show virtue in its sublimity, beauty and fullest splendour. That would make an uncommon impression. What should disturb us? The ill of another, when cheerfulness can be brought to us by it?",["\"ungeineinen\" in the OCR read as \"ungemeinen\".","The first sentence of the German after \"Wieland\" is restated: the \"So wie ... so\" comparison is divided into two sentences.","The last sentence is obscure in the German and may be a fragmentary note; I have rendered it literally, supplying the question mark at the end only."]],"philippi-s08-082":["Music furnishes no objects, nor descriptions of them. It is proportion that constitutes its beauty, and in no kind of beauty is proportion so exact and so manifold as in music. Yet in music there is not so much appearance [*Erscheinung*] as a multitude of sensations and of charm [*Reiz*]. Every tone is similar to certain expressions of the passions. Perhaps the pleasure we take in music arises from the similarity which the tones have to sensations that we like to recall. The proportion of music lies partly in the tones, partly in the time [*Tact*]; the unity lies in the theme of the music, or in the principal tone, which is carried through.",["Stray footnote numeral '1' after 'die' in the OCR omitted.","'Tact' rendered 'time' in the sense of musical measure or beat; 'metre' would also serve.","'Reiz' rendered 'charm'; the lectures contrast it with beauty proper (see s10-009).","The original has 'ist es die die Schönheit … ausmacht'; I restated it as a cleft sentence, 'It is proportion that constitutes…', and divided the opening sentence in two."]],"philippi-s09-001":["Of the Good in Concepts.",[]],"philippi-s09-002":["Our judgments of taste always concern the subject. In the case of the beautiful we looked, that is, to the relation of the thing to the subject. We considered not what the thing is, but only how it appears. In the judging of the good it is quite otherwise; there we look to the true constitution of the object, and this is a matter for the understanding. That is good which accords with the laws of the understanding; hence whoever wishes to judge the good must know the laws of his understanding.",["'Vorwurf des Verstandes' rendered 'a matter for the understanding'; the older sense of Vorwurf is 'object, subject-matter'.","'Subject' and 'Object' kept close to the German forms, as in Kemp Smith's usage."]],"philippi-s09-003":["In logic we do not speak of objects [*Gegenstände*], whether they are good or evil, beautiful or ugly, but of concepts.",[]],"philippi-s09-004":["The agreement of our representation with the object is otherwise entitled truth (we shall speak of it below); this is the essential logical perfection. But truth is not the ground of aesthetic perfection. Something can be beautiful even though it is nowhere to be met with in the whole of nature; aesthetic perfection consists rather in the highest degree of universal pleasing. Here we represent things not as they are, but as they ought to be if they are to please in a fully complete manner. If I concede too much to logical perfection, I become dry; if I concede too much to the aesthetic, I become a poet in the rational, or a romance-writer in the historical, as Voltaire in his *Charles the Twelfth*.",["'Man steht hier die Sachen vor' read as 'stellt … vor' (OCR/transcript slip).","Voltaire's *Histoire de Charles XII*; 'Carl dem Zwölften' translated as the title.","'Dichter im rationalen … Romanschreiber im historischen' taken to mean in the sphere of rational or of historical knowledge; 'sphere' not added to the text."]],"philippi-s09-005":["In § 21 the author speaks of learned knowledge [*gelehrte Erkenntniß*].",["'Autor' is Meier, whose compendium Kant's lectures follow."]],"philippi-s09-006":["Philosophy itself is not learned knowledge. The philosopher works; the scholar writes.",[]],"philippi-s09-007":["Learning [*Gelehrsamkeit*] is distinguished from common knowledge either materially or formally. Materially, by the object; formally, by the *modus cognoscendi*. Materially it is not different, since it can have an object in common with common knowledge; but formally it is different, in that the scholar knows the object better. All learning is either historical or rational; it has either objects of history or objects which are thinkable only through reason. These two kinds of learning are distinguished only in form. A historical object can be known rationally, and a rational object historically. Rational science which does not relate to objects of the senses is either mathematics or philosophy. For its object is either quantities, and that is mathematics, or realities, and that is philosophy. (The word *Gelehrsamkeit* in the strict sense comprehends only historical knowledge.) To what, then, do the belles-lettres [*schönen Wissenschaften*] belong? Do they belong to learning or not?",["'Gegenstand' and 'Object' both rendered 'object'.","'Wissenschaften' in 'schöne Wissenschaften' rendered by the conventional 'belles-lettres'."]],"philippi-s09-008":["There is no science of the beautiful at all, because no first rules can be given, as must be the case in a science. The doctrine of taste is no doctrine, no *doctrina*, but only a critique [*Critic*]. To criticise is to compare a particular case with the whole extent of sensibility. The rules of criticism are more explanations of the *phaenomena* than precepts for actual objects. It is the same in medicine. In aesthetics one learns to criticise, and this indirectly assists also in the production of the beautiful. In the rational sciences the mathematician, and in the common sciences the historian, are alone scholars. The philosopher is no scholar; for philosophy cannot be taught, and a scholar is one to whom something has been taught. An account of the endeavours of human reason may indeed be imparted to someone; but that is not philosophy.",["Doubtful OCR: 'Venranfft' read as 'Vernunfft'; 'Philo-Bophie' as 'Philosophie'.","'Doctrin' rendered *doctrina* in italics for the Latin sense; the text prints 'Doctrin'.","'Phänomenorum' kept in Latin form as *phaenomena*."]],"philippi-s10-001":["Of Perfection, § 22",[]],"philippi-s10-002":["Theoretical perfection, in respect of quantity, is extensiveness [*Weitläufigkeit*] and fruitfulness. In respect of quality, perfection is either logical or aesthetic.",["'quantitaet' and 'Qualitaet' given as 'quantity' and 'quality'."]],"philippi-s10-003":["Logical perfection has a relation to other modes of knowledge; practical perfection, to the will.",["'practische' read as qualifying 'Vollkommenheit', supplied in the English for clarity; no word is added to the German beyond that ellipsis."]],"philippi-s10-004":["The harmfulness which a truth may have is always contingent.",[]],"philippi-s10-005":["Aesthetic perfection has a relation solely to my taste.",[]],"philippi-s10-006":["Objective logical perfection is the agreement of a mode of knowledge with the laws of the understanding. Subjective aesthetic perfection is the agreement of the mode of knowledge with the laws of sensibility.",["'Erkenntniß' rendered 'mode of knowledge' where it is a particular instance."]],"philippi-s10-007":["We have two faculties, understanding and sensibility. The latter concerns only the manner in which the subject is affected by the object. It is the only power of the soul which goes immediately to the object.",["'Fähigkeiten' rendered 'faculties'; 'Krafft der Seelen' rendered 'power of the soul'.","'Diese' taken to refer to Sinnlichkeit, the nearer antecedent and the one the argument requires."]],"philippi-s10-008":["Aesthetic perfection is also entitled beautiful perfection.",[]],"philippi-s10-009":["We have already remarked that the beautiful is to be separated from charm [*Reiz*]. The beautiful concerns only the form, the contemplation; charm, however, concerns sensation and the alteration of our state. Beauty is to be preferred; for with it everything depends on judging, which is not required for charm. Further, beauty pleases every time; charm, however, wears out. The laws of sensibility remain always the same and are universal; in respect of charm men are not to be compared at all. Many strive after constant emotion [*Rührung*]; they want always to have a startling beauty; they want their nerves always stimulated. This kind of motion belongs to coarse senses, insensible to what is essentially beautiful. A man of refined taste demands true beauty, stripped of charm; that is what pleases him.",["Doubtful OCR: 'aUemal' read as 'allemal'.","'Beschauung' rendered 'contemplation'.","'Rührung' rendered 'emotion'; at s10-010 'rühren' is rendered 'affect'/'move' as context requires."]],"philippi-s10-010":["The agreement of knowledge with the object was logical perfection, and the means to it, truth. The agreement with the subject is aesthetic perfection, and the means to it, a diligent observation of how an object, or the knowledge of it, affects [*rühret*] our own and other subjects.",["The sentence is elliptical ('Diejenige mit dem Subject'); I supplied 'The agreement' and 'is' to complete the sense.","'Subjekt' rendered 'subject'."]],"philippi-s10-011":["Facility [*Leichtigkeit*] accords with the subjective laws, but not with the objective laws or laws of the understanding; hence it is not a logical perfection. The objects of knowledge are often very difficult, so that much exertion is needed to comprehend them properly. Thus all metaphysical, abstract truths are very hard to understand, because they please only in concepts.",["The German 'daß man viele Anstrengung braucht' is a consecutive clause; rendered 'so that'."]],"philippi-s10-012":["Novelty gives nourishment to the mind [*Gemüth*], which strives to extend itself and to be always occupied. It is a property not of the object but of the subject. The softness of the green colour, the brilliance of the red, the prominence of the violet, lie not in the colours but in our eye, which, through the impressions which the colours make, is capable of this or that modification.",["'hegen' read as 'liegen'.","'das scheinende der rothen' rendered 'the brilliance of the red'; 'scheinend' is a little uncertain (possibly 'glaring')."]],"philippi-s10-013":["We often take the relation of an object to our subject for a certain property of the object. So it is with colours and many other objects of the senses.",[]],"philippi-s10-014":["In logical perfection we shall bring everything to concepts; in aesthetic perfection, everything to appearances [*Erscheinungen*]. If we wish to give a logical perfection, we consider everything in the universal, *in abstracto*. If we wish to give an aesthetic perfection, we consider everything as it were present, *in concreto*. Logical perfection rests upon the understanding and is determined by it; aesthetic perfection rests upon sensibility and determines it. Because of our weakness, in that we are so much bound to the sensible, the latter must often come to the help of the former in making the matter clear. In morals, for example, we seek to show the universal in examples. Virtue pleases in concepts, but in order that it may please also in appearance it must be made sensible through examples. For this history is required, and we must therefore apply ourselves to it.",["'Diese muß jener … aushelfen': 'Diese' taken as the aesthetic, 'jener' as the logical; the sense requires it.","'bestimmt'/'bestimmet' rendered 'determined'/'determines' (*Bestimmung*).","'Sache deutlich zu machen' rendered 'making the matter clear'."]],"philippi-s10-015":["Aesthetic perfection is, then, as it were the vehicle of logical perfection. I realise, so to speak, the universal concepts for myself. All intuition, all sensibility, constitutes the true perfection of aesthetics; but truth is the end of logical perfection.",["'Volllvommenheit' (OCR) silently read as 'Vollkommenheit'; 'Aesthetick' rendered 'aesthetics'.","'Ich realisiere mir ... die allgemeinen Begriffe': 'realise' kept literal in the sense of making them real or concrete to myself."]],"philippi-s10-016":["Our intuitions are all sensible. When we are not affected [gerührt] by an object, we know it only through the understanding, *in abstracto*. God's understanding is intuitive, not affected by objects; His intuitions are all intellectual, through the understanding. They are immediate intuitions through it. Our understanding can attain to no evidence regarding an object unless it is made sensible, unless the universal (abstract) is exhibited in the particular case (*in concreto*). We arrive at the universal through the particular; God does not.",["'shid' read as 'sind'. 'gerührt' rendered 'affected' (literally 'touched, moved'); the context is the affection of sensibility.","'anschauend' rendered 'intuitive'; 'Evidentz' kept as 'evidence'.","'Sie sind unmittelbare Anschauungen durch denselben': 'through it' refers to the understanding."]],"philippi-s10-017":["Aesthetic perfection is often conducive to the logical, [though in a sense] opposed to it; for the aesthetic rests on sensibility, the logical on abstraction from sensibility.",["'entgegen öfters beförderlich' is awkward; I have supplied 'though in a sense' in brackets to make the paradox plain. Alternatively, 'often conducive, contrary to the logical'? The German construction is doubtful.","'Smnlichkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit'."]],"philippi-s10-018":["What serves sensible intuition promotes logical perfection; but what aims at [mere] impression does it harm.",["'diendt' read as 'dient'. 'Eindruck' rendered 'impression'; 'mere' is supplied in brackets."]],"philippi-s10-019":["The aesthetic combines many modes of knowledge; the logical separates them.",["'Erkenntniße' (plural) rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s10-020":["Authors who aim solely at impressions hold the lowest rank.",[]],"philippi-s10-021":["Whoever wishes to convince another of a proposition must take great care not to arouse the feelings [Empfindungen] and not to strew beauties in his way, since he thereby awakens distrust that he wishes only to persuade us.",["'überzeugen' and 'überreden' rendered 'convince' and 'persuade', in the Kantian sense of the distinction.","'Empfindungen' rendered 'feelings' here, as the context is rhetorical, not the technical 'sensation'."]],"philippi-s10-022":["The sciences have clearness, but not intuition.",["'Klarheit' rendered 'clearness'."]],"philippi-s10-023":["There is a great harmony between the intuition of the understanding and that of sensibility. For example, there is a great harmony between the proof of the theorem of Pythagoras, which the geometer beholds with the understanding, and the figure of it, which he beholds sensibly. The only difference is that in the figure one surveys at a glance what one has clearly known through a series of thoughts or words.",["'denn»' (OCR) read as 'den' (relative pronoun, 'which'), taking 'Beweise' as antecedent. 'Sinnhchkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit'.","'anschaut' rendered 'beholds' to preserve the sense of intuition without repeating 'intuits'."]],"philippi-s10-024":["Some understandings cannot grasp the universal except in particular cases; that is the common understanding, which knows the abstract *in concreto*. In moral judgments, however, even the most unlearned understanding has abstract concepts.",["'abstracte' and 'ungelehrteste' rendered literally."]],"philippi-s10-025":["If we lack the skill to exhibit the universal *in concreto*, and do not know how to combine aesthetic sensibility with intellectual concepts, we lack that evidentness and intelligibility the want of which makes so many writers useless and unintelligible.",["'Augenscheinlichkeit' rendered 'evidentness' (literally 'obviousness to the eye')."]],"philippi-s10-026":["Skill contains the possibility of producing various products.",[]],"philippi-s10-027":["The sensible form is also a true perfection, and logical perfection goes along in its company. Sensibility does not consist in confusion; that is, the understanding can judge things even though the sensibility is very great.",["'Sinnhchkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit'. 'Verwirrung' rendered 'confusion'.","The last clause ('wenn gleich die Sinnlichkeit sehr groß ist') could also mean 'even though the sensible element is very great'; I have kept 'sensibility'."]],"philippi-s10-028":["All distinctness comes from the working-over [Bearbeitung] by the understanding. When we consider propositions *in concreto*, they are indeed confused, but this is not due to sensibility, and the understanding soon knows how to order them and bring them into a fitting form. It takes only the materials from sensibility; the form it gives them itself. The reading of a Fontenelle, and intercourse with the unlearned and the eager to learn, will teach us to make universal propositions sensible and to clothe all concepts in sheer appearances. And if the form of sensibility agrees with the form of the understanding, or with logical perfection, then this is genuine beauty, the essential aesthetic perfection. Here no charm and no feeling comes into consideration, no matter, only form. When the form of intuition agrees with that of the understanding; when the former helps to express the perfection of the understanding; when the appearance of the thing makes the concept of the understanding easier: this is essential beauty. A sensible mode of knowledge pleases when it has manifoldness and unity, when the representations are so ordered that one does not hinder the other; but its greatest perfection is when it is in conformity with the understanding.",["'lecture' kept as in the source (French), rendered 'reading'. 'anpassende' rendered 'fitting'.","I have divided no sentences beyond the original's; the passage already runs in short clauses. 'Erscheinungen' rendered 'appearances' per the fixed terms, though here the sense is nearly 'sensible guise'.","'Reitz' rendered 'charm'; 'Gefühl' rendered 'feeling'."]],"philippi-s10-029":["Sensible representations used to be regarded as a hindrance to the understanding and, as such, rejected. But although sensible knowledge is at first confused and can never have the perfection of the understanding, it can nevertheless be said that all concepts of the understanding first attain their perfection when one makes them sensible, and that sensible knowledge is therefore a necessary supplement of the understanding.",["'Ergänzung' rendered 'supplement'; 'sey' read as subjunctive of indirect speech."]],"philippi-s10-030":["It is a defect of our understanding that it does not see an abstract concept distinctly, and must therefore go back to concrete propositions. Were it perfect, it would have no need of this detour and would know things immediately. Through the understanding one knows mediately, by means of universal marks. But our understanding is not intuitive, only discursive.",["'Merkmale' rendered 'marks'; 'anschauend' rendered 'intuitive'; 'Umweg' rendered 'detour'."]],"philippi-s10-031":["It is a real feat, and takes great skill, to make concepts sensible. In philosophy one must first think a proposition through in the abstract, and afterwards make it sensible. In this way universal maxims can be changed into sentiments, if one applies to a single case what they say *in abstracto*. This serves to make the truth more comprehensible and to present it more impressively.",["'abstracte' (adverbial) rendered 'in the abstract'. 'Sentiments' kept as in the source."]],"philippi-s10-032":["The perfection of the magnitude [Größe] of knowledge consists in the multitude of what I know.",["'derMenge' (OCR) read as 'der Menge'. The sentence is as it stands; the passage appears to continue elsewhere."]],"philippi-s10-033":["With regard to quality, modes of knowledge are either true or false.",["'Qualitaet' rendered 'quality'; 'Erkenntniße' as 'modes of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s11-001":["On imperfection, § 23",["Heading; the section number refers to Meier's *Auszug*."]],"philippi-s11-002":["Imperfection arises either from lack, or from conflict, or from privation.",["'Widerstreit' rendered 'conflict'; 'Beraubung' rendered 'privation' (Latin *privatio*). The three terms do not match the two-fold division in the next passage, which is Kant's own looseness (or the student's)."]],"philippi-s11-003":["Ignorance is an imperfection of lack, error an imperfection of conflict. In ignorance I perceive what is lacking in knowledge; in error, what is false in it. Ignorance is a lack, error a disease. Error not only procures nothing, but also robs.",["'Unwissenheit' rendered 'ignorance'; 'Irrthum' rendered 'error'."]],"philippi-s11-004":["Imperfection is therefore: 1. *imperfectio deficiendi*, 2. *imperfectio privandi*.",["'priuandi' in the source read as *privandi*."]],"philippi-s11-005":["The logical imperfection of lack is ignorance; that of privation consists in errors.",[]],"philippi-s11-006":["The former is *defectus*; the latter *vitium*.",[]],"philippi-s11-007":["Aesthetic imperfection of lack, where something is wanting, is dryness. That of privation is ugliness. Ugliness is therefore something positive, not a mere absence of beauty but also the presence of what is contrary to beauty. Vice is not only a lack of virtue but also something positive. What diminishes money is just as positive as what increases it; the former carries money from us to others, the latter from others to us. Something can be not beautiful and yet also not ugly, for example the artificial [das Gekünstelte]. It does not please us when we see that someone has laboured with great trouble over something that he could very easily have made in another way. We pity him that his art has miscarried.",["'ims' (OCR) read as 'uns'. 'Gekünstelte' rendered 'the artificial' (in the sense of the laboured or affected).","'Trockenheit' rendered 'dryness'; 'Häßlichkeit' rendered 'ugliness'."]],"philippi-s11-008":["The lack of every kind of logical perfection is shallowness. The degree of logical perfection is determined by thoroughness.",["'Seichtigkeit' rendered 'shallowness'; 'Gründlichkeit' rendered 'thoroughness'."]],"philippi-s11-009":["Ignorance is the lack of knowledge in general.",[]],"philippi-s11-010":["It is no reproach to knowledge that it is merely learned and has merely logical perfection. For it is sometimes expressly required that all aesthetic perfection be banished, as for example in algebra.",["'exulire' (Latin *exulare*) rendered 'be banished' (literally 'go into exile')."]],"philippi-s11-011":["If, however, logical perfection is allied in agreement with that of aesthetics, the latter considerably raises the former, in that it makes the concepts of the understanding intuitive and easier. In the sensible a great manifoldness is encountered; through this the intuition is extended, and confusion arises from it. If, then, we concede too much to aesthetic perfection, we become shallow, since we wish to be agreeable.",["'entstellt' (OCR) read as 'entsteht'; 'aesthetiscken' read as 'aesthetischen'.","'anschauend macht' rendered 'makes intuitive'; the sentence beginning 'Im Sinnlichen' is somewhat abrupt in the transcript."]],"philippi-s11-012":["If we take away all sensibility and aesthetic perfection from a mode of knowledge, the soul remains empty of representations and taste is wanting. Thus, since we wish to be thorough, we become dry! *Si brevis esse volo obscurus fio.* (Here it goes soon as with politeness or polish.)",["'Seele' rendered 'soul'. The Latin is Horace, *Ars poetica* 25 ('brevis esse laboro, obscurus fio'), quoted loosely by the student.","The final parenthesis is cryptic; it presumably compares the loss of aesthetic perfection to the over-refinement of manners, and is rendered literally."]],"philippi-s11-013":["How, then, are we to apportion it here? There are sciences whose final end is merely pure reason. In these thoroughness is the chief aim, and all beauty drops away. Yet there must be no ugliness. Of this character are metaphysics and pure morals. Practical morals is capable of every beauty. We must first learn abstract truths, and afterwards gather all the beauties by reading the authors. These latter must be the imitators [*Nachtreterinnen*] of the former.",["\"Nachtret erinnen\" in the OCR is read as *Nachtreterinnen* (female followers, imitators); the feminine form is odd, but the reading is the most plausible one.","\"Vertheilt\" is rendered \"apportion\"; the object \"es\" is unclear without the preceding context (presumably the claims of beauty and of thoroughness)."]],"philippi-s11-014":["Beauty is manifold, if we take it generally to be the sensible. Some of it benefits logical perfection, some harms it. The beauties of form accord with the perfection of the understanding and are of great benefit to it. The beauties of feeling, of the matter of sensibility, the tender and the laughing, are directly opposed to it and harmful. Whoever in a mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] strives much after emotion cannot introduce much perfection of understanding into it. In brief, then: what is directed to feeling hinders logical perfection, whereas the beauty of form benefits it; yet even this is sometimes not advantageous.",["\"die\" in \"wenn wir die überhaupt\" is taken as referring to Schönheit; \"ihr\" in \"nützet ihr\" is logical perfection.","\"doch ist auch die\": \"die\" taken as the beauty of form."]],"philippi-s11-015":["In order to grasp something through the understanding I must abstract. In order to learn what is beautiful I must show the abstract in concrete cases. These conflict. The abstract, in which we separate something from many other modes of knowledge and isolate it, is dry. It is as when through the microscope one seeks to separate everything else from an object, so that one may know the one thing the more distinctly. Through this dryness, then, I deprive the soul of certain objects. It is bound and suffers a kind of self-denial. But it is a property of all finite beings that they have a desire always to be active.",["\"Erkenntnissen\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\" (plural).","\"Seele\" is rendered \"soul\", not \"mind\" (which is reserved for Gemüt)."]],"philippi-s11-016":["If one gains in intuition, one loses this again in rational judgement [*Vernunfftbeurtheilung*]. Liveliness and the heaping together of objects yield intuition. But just this is prejudicial to the understanding. Of the many objects it then knows only very little, and that confusedly.",["\"Anschauung\" is here the aesthetic, sensible vivid presentation; kept as \"intuition\" per the fixed vocabulary.","\"dieses\" (what is lost) is left as \"this\", the antecedent being the clarity or gain just mentioned; the sense is somewhat elliptical."]],"philippi-s11-017":["Logical perfection demands abstraction: I am to separate. Aesthetic association demands that I heap objects together in due proportion. The former aims at knowing distinctly, the latter at knowing intuitively. The poet becomes the more moving, the more objects he heaps together in proportion. A concert is the more agreeable, the more instruments sound together.",["\"Aßociation\" rendered \"association\"; \"Ebenmaaß\" as \"due proportion\".","\"anschauend\" rendered \"intuitively\"."]],"philippi-s11-018":["In uniting these two perfections one should be cautious. There are modes of knowledge that do not belong to taste, although we reckon everything to taste. A mathematical demonstration of a mechanical theorem can have beauty; but the beauty lies in simplicity. One is pleased that a truth which one held to be so involved and difficult is all at once seen so distinctly. There are beauties which can be seen only through the understanding, although the understanding does not otherwise judge the beautiful. There are beauties which it alone sees, which depend neither on taste nor on fashion: and these are the eternal beauties.",["\"einsiehet\" rendered \"is seen\" / \"see\" (insight); \"Mode\" as \"fashion\"."]],"philippi-s12-001":["Perfections of Knowledge",["Heading; \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"philippi-s12-002":["Truth is the general perfection. No knowledge can be knowledge if it is not true.",[]],"philippi-s12-003":["Truth can relate",["\"kann gehen\" literally \"can go\"; rendered \"can relate\" and continued by the next two items."]],"philippi-s12-004":["1. to the object.",[]],"philippi-s12-005":["2. to the subject.",[]],"philippi-s12-006":["In the fable truth lies not in so far as the narrative is actual, but only in relation to the subject, in so far as it is most easily and most exactly grasped by that subject. Subjective Wahr-[truth] — There is a real difference between perfection in respect of quantity, which rests on the greatness and extent of the knowledge, and perfection of quality, which rests on the exactness of the knowledge. The former, great knowledge, the polyhistor seeks; the thorough mind, on the other hand, seeks to make knowledge perfect in respect of quality.",["\"Eabel\" is read as \"Fabel\". The fragment \"Subjektive Wahr-\" is a stray break (a marginal heading, perhaps \"Subjektive Wahrheit\") that has run into the text; kept as it stands and bracketed completion avoided beyond the visible word.","\"derQuantität\" and \"Weitläuf tigkeit\" corrected silently; \"nach vollkommen zu machen\" is read as \"nach der Qualität vollkommen zu machen\" order slightly garbled, translated by sense.","\"Polyhistor\" kept as in German."]],"philippi-s12-007":["The means of logical perfection is distinctness; that of aesthetic perfection is whatever makes intuition easier and more manifold.",[]],"philippi-s13-001":["§ 29",[]],"philippi-s13-002":["Certainty is the degree of truth and is found in both. In the logical I look to the certainty of the object, in the aesthetic to that of the subject. How are these two to be reconciled? What are the means? Which must come first, logical or aesthetic perfection, taste or understanding? Everything is subject to the understanding. It judges the thing according to its constitution, what it is; taste, however, judges according to the appearance, how the thing affects the subject. Logical perfection is therefore the essential, and is to be preferred. In history, for example, logical perfection consists in correct arrangement, in distinctness and certainty; distinctness is a means to truth. The aesthetic, on the other hand, consists in my embellishing, as Voltaire does in his *Charles XII*; it serves to make the work agreeable, but teaches nothing.",["\"Erscheinung\" rendered \"appearance\".","\"Karl XII\" is rendered *Charles XII* (Voltaire's history).","\"beurt heilt\" corrected to \"beurtheilt\"."]],"philippi-s13-003":["Logical perfection is the only one under which it is permitted to make a mode of knowledge aesthetically perfect.",["\"eine Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"a mode of knowledge\"; the sense is that aesthetic perfection may be added only on condition of logical perfection."]],"philippi-s13-004":["Here there are two wrong paths.",["The two paths are presumably those named in the following items, but the text does not say so; none supplied."]],"philippi-s13-005":["The rules of sensibility consist",[]],"philippi-s13-006":["1. in sensation.",[]],"philippi-s13-007":["2. in intuition.",[]],"philippi-s13-008":["Any man whose mind is moved, or whose feeling is touched or stirred, is no longer capable of reflecting. From this it is clear that emotion is contrary to logical perfection. For one must be quite calm if one is to judge an object correctly. Logical perfection may, however, make use of beautiful form in order to awaken attention, that is, of expressions, dress [*Einkleidung*], and simplicity in sublime objects. Nothing is accomplished through emotion. The understanding remains empty and gets nothing to think. The nerves are merely agreeably shaken, and when this is over one knows nothing. It is just as when violent laughter arises in a company: the next moment it passes off, and one looks at another and does not know what he laughed at.",["\"Gemüth\" rendered \"mind\"; \"Rührung\" as \"emotion\"; \"capable\" kept in sense of Latin-derived \"capable\".","\"Einkleidung\" rendered \"dress\" (clothing of thought in apt garb)."]],"philippi-s13-009":["Truth can be",[]],"philippi-s13-010":["1. aesthetic in appearance, and the means thereto is liveliness;",["OCR \"Erscheinungund\" read as \"Erscheinung und\"."]],"philippi-s13-011":["2. logical in concepts, and the condition is distinctness.",[]],"philippi-s13-012":["Logical distinctness arises from abstraction, aesthetic liveliness from association.",[]],"philippi-s14-001":["§ 30",[]],"philippi-s14-002":["The perfection of a mode of knowledge, in so far as it relates to the will of man and stands in relation to it, is practical perfection. Those perfections were theoretical: the logical speculative, the aesthetic not speculative, but both theoretical and without relation. This is practical in so far as it bears on the free choice [*Willkühr*] of the will. Without this perfection knowledge is idle; it may indeed have consequences, but without relation to the will. A knowledge that is logically perfect but not practical is nothing but speculation. An aesthetic perfection of knowledge that is not practical merely tickles the understanding. Modes of knowledge are idle (sterile) not because they furnish us with no thoughts, but because they have no influence upon our activity. Practical perfection is the most important; those that are not practical we can do without.",["\"ihrn\" read as \"ihm\" (the will); \"Hefern\" as \"liefern\"; \"kützelt\" as \"kitzelt\" (tickles).","\"Willkühr\" rendered \"free choice\" in brackets with German, since the sense turns on it; \"Erkenntnisse\" as \"modes of knowledge\".","\"Diese\" in \"Diese ist praktisch\" is taken to be practical perfection (the third); the preceding sentence is elliptical and was smoothed."]],"philippi-s14-003":["Logical perfection in and for itself is separated from practical perfection; hence one cannot ask in the former how this stands to my conduct. I ask only after the rules of insight. I wish only to learn insights, whatever the things may be.",["\"Thun\" rendered \"conduct\"."]],"philippi-s14-004":["A mode of knowledge is practical if it has an influence on our free choice, and indeed",["\"Willkühr\" here \"free choice\"; the sentence is continued by the two numbered items that follow."]],"philippi-s14-005":["1. subjectively, in so far as it has incentives of sensibility, in so far as it actually moves us.",["\"Sinnhchkeit\" read as \"Sinnlichkeit\"; \"Triebfedern\" rendered \"incentives\"."]],"philippi-s14-006":["2. objectively, in so far as it has grounds by which we recognize how it ought to occur. Objective practical knowledge shows how we ought to proceed; subjective practical knowledge indicates the incentives according to which we perform actual actions. To subjective practical knowledge, therefore, belongs the doctrine of the affects. Morals has to do with objective practical grounds. The orator must act according to subjective practical rules. Often one must deceive.",["\"Affekten\" rendered \"affects\"; \"täuschen\" rendered \"deceive\"."]],"philippi-s14-007":["All these perfections hitherto considered are now found together in the author.",["Restated the sense of 'zusammengenommen im Autor' as 'found together in the author'; the German is more compressed."]],"philippi-s14-008":["Logical perfection is seldom combined with aesthetic perfection, because they impair one another. Probability is put forward because it is easy for us. On the other hand, something can be thorough and convincing and yet not aesthetically perfect, as, for example, in metaphysics.",["'Wahrscheinlich -keit' (OCR line-break) silently rejoined as Wahrscheinlichkeit.","'Man bringt Wahrscheinlichkeit vor weil es uns leicht ist' is elliptical; I render it literally and do not supply the connexion with the preceding sentence. The transcript may be defective here.","'vermengt' rendered 'combined' to suit English idiom; 'mixed' is the literal sense."]],"philippi-s14-009":["A mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] can be practically perfect and yet logically imperfect, for example, a proof of the existence of God.",["'Erkenntniß' in the singular here is rendered 'mode of knowledge' to match the plural usage elsewhere and to suit the sense of a particular cognition; 'knowledge' would also serve."]],"philippi-s14-010":["A mode of knowledge can be objectively practical and not subjectively so. Most sermons are of this character. They always show what one ought to do, and even how one ought to do it with pleasure; yet it remains without profit. [The hearer says:] I cannot do it; I do not see it sensibly. I should gladly do it; indeed, I wish to have pleasure in it.",["'sinnlieh' is OCR for 'sinnlich'; rendered 'sensibly' (i.e. through sensibility, sensuously).","The words 'The hearer says' are supplied in brackets to mark the shift of speaker in the last sentences; the German gives no such marker."]],"philippi-s14-011":["Exhortation is carried on without end. One always says that one ought to be thus and so. The hearer also wills it and wishes it, but can bring nothing to actuality. Attention should therefore be given to how, from grounds of the understanding, incentives [*Triebfedern*] of sensibility might also be produced; how the discourse might be clothed in pleasure, and subjective practical grounds be added to the objective practical grounds. Logical knowledge gives insight; aesthetic knowledge gives none at all. Logical knowledge must be laid at the foundation. One must first learn something real, and then know how to clothe beautifully what has been learned. The modes of knowledge which we need are so easy to grasp that the learned man has no greater insight into them than the common understanding, so far as practice is concerned; for example, the knowledge of God and all practical knowledge. Practical perfection is very brief, and all our practical moral knowledge can be reduced to very few formulae. The plan of speculative knowledge, on the other hand, is immeasurable. These speculative modes of knowledge have only a conventional value, for example in society. There is an imitation according to universal laws. The customary [*das Gebräuchliche*] is what agrees with something universal yet contingent, or what agrees in this, that we accommodate ourselves to one another. When one imitates the customary, this is not beautiful; it serves only to prevent offence. The customary does not belong to the beautiful. The beautiful ought always to be uncustomary; see Thomas Abbt.",["Divided no sentences but kept the long run as in the German; 'conuentionalen' is OCR for 'conventionalen'.","'Praxin' (accusative of Latin 'praxis') rendered 'practice'.","'Gebräuchliche' rendered 'the customary', contrasted in the next passage with 'gewöhnlich' (usual, ordinary).","'Es giebt ein Nachgeahmtes nach allgemeinen Gesetzen' is obscure and possibly corrupt; rendered literally. 'Widerwillen' rendered 'offence' by judgment; 'aversion' is literal.","'vid. Thomas Abbt' is Kant's (or the transcriber's) reference; kept as 'see'."]],"philippi-s14-012":["Something can be usual [*gewöhnlich*] and yet not customary [*gebräuchlich*].",["The distinction between gewöhnlich and gebräuchlich is not explained in the passage; the German is kept in brackets since the sense turns on it."]],"philippi-s14-013":["The beautiful is a matter of genius and by no means a matter of imitation. It is precisely the opposite of all imitation. Imitation can serve as no ground of the beautiful. The authors who have written according to independent laws, and not laws of fashion, serve only to test one's taste by them. Authors often followed laws of fashion, as, for example, Lohenstein and Voiture, and these are wholly opposed to the laws of beauty. They aim constantly at nothing but stimulating feeling and making a stir in the mind. But feeling requires no understanding; taste requires understanding, and is therefore an analogue of the understanding [*Analogon des Verstandes*]. Animals also have feeling, but not taste, for they have no understanding. In short, what is contemptible and what is disgusting is imitated. By imitating one finally becomes so accustomed that one can work only after models and can produce nothing out of oneself.",["'seinen Geschmack' ('his taste') is rendered 'one's taste'; the pronoun is indefinite.","'dabin' is OCR for 'dahin'; 'eckelhafftist' separated as 'eckelhafft ist'.","'Kurz was geringschätzig was eckelhafft ist, ist nachgeahmt' is rendered literally; the sense (that what is contemptible and disgusting is what is imitated, or is of the nature of imitation) is somewhat doubtful.","'Gemüth' rendered 'mind'."]],"philippi-s14-014":["Nothing is more repugnant, nothing more contemptible, than what has been imitated. What does not arise from myself cannot be imputed to me. The chief condition of all beauties is ease [*Gemächlichkeit*]; according to the rules of sensibility it is a ground of satisfaction. Everything must present itself, as it were, of its own accord and quite easily, to our sensible apprehension. But how anxious, how painfully laboured do the beauties of imitation appear! One can see at once, from the work itself, with what anxiety and constraint the writer seeks to please, and one pities him that he displeases precisely by this means.",["'Gemächlichkeit' is rendered 'ease'; 'comfortableness' is also possible.","'ängstüch' read as 'ängstlich' (OCR); 'Faßlichkeit' rendered 'apprehension' (capacity to grasp)."]],"philippi-s14-015":["The good is the essential ground of the beautiful. Satisfaction [*Wohlgefallen*] is only an accidental one, and its grounds are contingent.",["'einen ... Grund' is understood after 'accidentaler'; the word 'one' stands for 'Grund'."]],"philippi-s14-016":["If, then, the beautiful is long and artificially contrived in order that it may please, this is ridiculous, because it is contingent. The effort ought to have been devoted to the good. The beautiful must be unaffected, or at least it must appear not to be artificial.",["'gekünstelt' rendered 'artificially contrived', 'ungekünstelt' as 'unaffected'.","'lang gekünstelt' is somewhat doubtful; I read it as 'long (laboriously) contrived'."]],"philippi-s14-017":["The petit-maître, a master in trifles, does not keep to what is customary. Yet everything in him is in harmony with what pleases.",["'Petitmaitre' left as a French loan-word."]],"philippi-s14-018":["Nothing causes more vexation than the studied, where the painful sweat of anxiety and the gloomy rubbing of the brow show through. But where a beauty seems to have arisen of itself, without the slightest effort, that pleases. Ease is always very dear to us. Painful order is not easy but hard. He who writes in a studied manner writes as little according to taste as he who dresses and adorns himself anxiously does so according to taste. Ease, loftiness, self-command, a certain sort of disorder and negligence, in that one pays no heed to trifles in order to be the more exact in what is of greater importance: that is pleasing. When one hears a sermon, or is present at a play, and notices that something is studied, it at once ceases to please us. An anxious rhymester displeases in the highest degree; but with what pleasure one reads the verses of Pope, where the rhymes seem to have flowed naturally and from the very nature of the subject. Simplicity [*Einfalt*] is opposed to the studied and artificially contrived. All beauty must have simplicity; without it there can be no true beauty. Everything is agreeable which seems to have cost no effort and to have arisen of itself. Simplicity is, however, not opposed to amplitude [*Weitläufigkeit*] and manifoldness. These too belong to true beauty.",["'Nachlaß igkeit' (OCR break) read as 'Nachlässigkeit'; 'Schau spiel' as 'Schauspiel'.","'Weitläufigkeit' rendered 'amplitude'; 'extensiveness' is possible.","'Hoheit' rendered 'loftiness'; 'dignity' is also possible.","The sentence 'Wer auf eine gesuchte Weise schreibt ...' was kept as one sentence with the parallel clause smoothed."]],"philippi-s14-019":["Naïveté [*Naivität*] is, however, something other than simplicity [*Einfalt*]. It consists in simplicity [*Einfältigkeit*], so far as this is characteristic and has arisen from the object, and cannot be taught. Thus unvarnished honesty in a man is naïveté. We always rejoice when we catch sight of nature. Art is always suspect, in that it deceives and misleads us.",["Kant here distinguishes Einfalt and Einfältigkeit only by form; both are rendered 'simplicity' with the German shown.","'Objekt' rendered 'object'; the sense (the subject or person who displays the quality) is doubtful."]],"philippi-s14-020":["It is disagreeable when one seeks to spread still more charm over objects which already of themselves have by nature far too much charm; when one burdens simple nature, which has the greatest attractions quite easily, with foreign beauties collected through art, in order to make it still more agreeable and charming. Into this fault, into this bombast of beauties, the present age of German poets has fallen. Meadows, gardens, voluptuousness itself, things which in nature have all too much charm, they strive in rivalry to make still more charming. Charm should rather be given to things which are wholly deprived of all charm, like virtue, the essential beauty. That would be useful. But this is not so easy as it is to stupefy the grounds of approval by cold reason, and to stifle them by titillating charm. Let them give charm to the contempt of all charm. Everything seeks to destroy the charm of human society. Now come forward, poets, and restore the half-lost charm. But just as it is easy to flatter a tyrant, but great art is required to tell him the truth in such a way that he does not take it ill; so it is also easy to flatter sensibility with charm, but very hard to present to it the reflections [*Reflectiones*] which the calm understanding makes upon it, in such a way that it does not take them ill. Certainly great art is needed even to present to sensibility the true judgments of the understanding so that it does not become vexed, to say nothing of making them charming.",["'Reiz' rendered 'charm' throughout; 'stimulus' or 'attraction' are alternatives.","'wesentliehe' read as 'wesentliche' (OCR); 'bemüht man sich um die Wette' rendered 'they strive in rivalry'.","'Laßt sie Reiz geben, der Verachtung alles Reizes' is obscure, possibly corrupt; rendered literally. 'Alles sucht den Reiz der menschlichen Gesellschaft zu zerstören' is also doubtful: perhaps 'everything' is the transcriber's slip.","'Gründe der Billigung' rendered 'grounds of approval'; 'Sinnlichkeit' 'sensibility'; 'Verstand' 'understanding'."]],"philippi-s14-021":["Of the second section.",["A heading; translated as it stands. 'Abschnitt' rendered 'section'."]],"philippi-s14-022":["On the extent of learned knowledge",[]],"philippi-s14-023":["He whose possessions do not suffice for his needs is poor. Among the learned there is found poverty, for the desire for knowledge [Wißbegierde] is not satisfied.",["'Der dessen Güter' read as 'Der, dessen Güter'; a relative pronoun appears to be missing a comma or a word in the transcript.","'Armseeligkeit' rendered 'poverty' here; it carries the sense of wretchedness or paltriness, which the next passage brings out ('paltriness')."]],"philippi-s14-024":["The paltriness [Armseeligkeit] of our philosophical knowledge comes from this, that men are proud of their wisdom.",["'thut stolz auf' rendered as 'are proud of'; 'man' rendered 'men' for English idiom."]],"philippi-s14-025":["A man is entitled poor in proportion to his station. A prince may possess many goods and yet, as a prince, be poor. He who has as much knowledge as he requires to attain his end is not poor. But suppose a knowledge which is possible to the whole capacity of man, and that the man who has a vocation for it does not seek to acquire as much as is possible to him; then his knowledge is paltry, because it does not reach the degree which it could and should reach.",["'armseehg' is an OCR slip for 'armseelig'; 'wird vorausgesetzt' restated as 'suppose'.","'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity' as the sense requires."]],"philippi-s14-026":["Ignorance is",["Breaks off; the enumeration follows in the next passages."]],"philippi-s14-027":["1. Ignorance of things [der Sachen]. When the objects are unknown, we cannot judge them. All logical, practical and aesthetic knowledge is lacking. That is *ignorantia quoad materiam*.",["'Objecte' rendered 'objects'; 'Sachen' rendered 'things'."]],"philippi-s14-028":["2. Ignorance of the form, of the connection of the modes of knowledge [Erkenntnisse] with the understanding, with taste and with practice [*Praxi*]. When one does not know the grounds or the connection, that is *ignorantia quoad formam*.",["'Praxi' left as the Latin ablative-looking form, in italics."]],"philippi-s14-029":["Formal ignorance is either",[]],"philippi-s14-030":["1. Ignorance of the grounds of the modes of knowledge [Erkenntnisse].",["'Gründe der Erkenntnisse' is rendered literally; contrast 'Gründe der Sachen' (grounds of things) in the next item."]],"philippi-s14-031":["2. Ignorance of the grounds of things. For example, in history, to prove that something is, one must know grounds of knowledge [Gründe der Erkenntniß]; and to prove why something is, one must know grounds of things [Gründe der Dinge].",["The sentence is slightly irregular in the German; I have supplied 'one must know' for both clauses."]],"philippi-s14-032":["There are modes of knowledge with the grounds of knowledge. Thus Copernicus knew our solar world and had grounds of knowledge. He showed what happens there.",["'Gründe des Erkenntnisses' taken as equivalent to 'Gründe der Erkenntniß'."]],"philippi-s14-033":["But there are also modes of knowledge with the grounds of things. Thus Newton knew the Copernican system, but at the same time showed the grounds of things, namely why the heavenly bodies move as they do, through his system of gravitation; see Euler.",["'tue' is an OCR slip for 'die'. 'vid. Eulerum' rendered 'see Euler'."]],"philippi-s14-034":["In order, then, to dispel formal ignorance, one must",["Breaks off; continued in the next items."]],"philippi-s14-035":["1. Seek out the grounds of knowledge.",[]],"philippi-s14-036":["2. [Seek out] the grounds of things. These latter are often unknown to us. Philosophy occupies itself with them; but grounds of knowledge are demanded even of common knowledge. In all knowledge one first asks whether it is also true, and then what ground it has. The geniuses of men differ, and one can judge them by this. Some ask after the grounds of things. The greater part of mankind is very indifferent to the grounds of knowledge; they do not inquire whether the thing is really true. Hence arises general credulity. One ponders and investigates how this or that which one finds reported may have been possible, and it does not occur to one to ask whether it really happened. Against this one must take good care. One should first ask what ground the report has, and then seek to track down the grounds of things.",["'Seek out' supplied in brackets from the preceding item.","'wras' is an OCR slip for 'was'; 'hier bey' taken as 'hierbei' ('by this'); 'Erzählung' rendered 'report'.","'Die Genies der Menschen' rendered literally as 'the geniuses of men', i.e. their native talents."]],"philippi-s15-001":["§43",[]],"philippi-s15-002":["Ignorance may be unavoidable and necessary. Physically unavoidable is ignorance of what we do not know through our senses, for example what creatures there are in the moon, or what the state after death, or that after the world, will be. And just this is what men ask after most eagerly and most frequently. Such questions about impossible modes of knowledge are a business in which mediocre scholars amuse themselves, who by that very thing only show how much they do not know.",["'wie derjenige nach der Welt seyn werde' is obscure; translated as it stands ('that after the world'), possibly a transcription slip.","'Geschaffte' read as 'Geschäft', 'business'."]],"philippi-s15-003":["But to determine the limits of the knowledge of the understanding and of reason, not by trial [*tentiren*], not *per principia empirica*, but from universal grounds, and to see clearly in what cases our understanding must necessarily be ignorant: that is true philosophy. This is what metaphysics does, which has for its object the pure understanding and its limits. It is a study of the subject; hence it fully attains its end if it can show by rules of the understanding how far the pure understanding extends, and if it explains the laws of the same. In it we learn to see our necessary ignorance from the nature of reason. O an excellent science! This is indeed true wisdom, that we learn our vocation [Bestimmung] and the degree of perfection which is suited to us and of which we are capable. The former is taught by morals, the latter by metaphysics. These sciences too will be brought to perfection; through metaphysics nothing at all will be discovered.",["'0' is an OCR slip for 'O'; 'Subjekt ;' spacing corrected.","'tentiren' kept in italics as a Latinate German verb ('by trying').","'Jenes ... dies' taken as 'the former [our vocation] ... the latter [the degree of perfection]' — but the text may intend morals for the vocation and metaphysics for the limits; the allocation is a judgment.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'vocation' here (contrast the 'determination' sense elsewhere)."]],"philippi-s16-001":["§44",[]],"philippi-s16-002":["On the horizon of learned knowledge.",[]],"philippi-s16-003":["In every kind of knowledge one must begin with the large; thereby arises connection with the small and with all the parts. When one learns geography, one begins with the globe. We shall mark the limits logically by the standard of reason, practically by the end [Zweck] of the will.",[]],"philippi-s16-004":["This belongs pre-eminently to logic.",[]],"philippi-s16-005":["The horizon of human knowledge is the compass of the modes of knowledge which are suited to human perfection. If modes of knowledge agree, in magnitude and quality, with the rules of perfection and are at the same time suited to the limits of the understanding, then it is these that constitute the horizon of human knowledge, beyond which one cannot and ought not to go. The sciences are bounded either by the capacity [Vermögen] of man, so that some cannot be known by him because they exceed his capacity; or by the vocation [Bestimmung] and end of man, so that some may not be known by him because they do not belong to his end.",["'Grösse' rendered 'magnitude'."]],"philippi-s16-006":["The sphere of man's knowledge, determined aesthetically, accords with illusion [Wahn], but not with the end of man.",["'Wahn' may be an error in the transcript (perhaps 'Wunsch' or 'Wahl'); translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s16-007":["When the limits of knowledge coincide with the limits of perfection, the region which they enclose is the horizon determined for man. This horizon of human knowledge will therefore be",["'Vollkonimenheit' corrected from OCR. Breaks off; continued in the next items."]],"philippi-s16-008":["1. Logical, in so far as it is determined by the relation of knowledge to capacity [Vermögen].",[]],"philippi-s16-009":["2. Practical, in so far as it is determined by the relation of knowledge to the end, to the will, to moral perfection. Logical, when I take the compass of man's capacity, that is, his ability, as the standard. But since not everything belongs to our end that it would be possible to acquire by our capacity, there must be added the determination of the horizon according to worth, goodness [Bonität], utility, and the agreement of knowledge with the end; that is the practical determination of the horizon. The wise man sets the limits of his horizon not by his capacity but by his rational end, not dynamically but teleologically. He consults his perfection and draws his horizon only around the modes of knowledge that accord with it.",["'Bonität' retained in brackets with the German; 'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s16-010":["Perfection is theoretical according to the magnitude of the capacity, practical according to the degree of perfection.",["The sentence reads oddly (perfection defined by perfection); translated as it stands, possibly a transcription fault."]],"philippi-s16-011":["There are degrees of knowledge beyond which we can do no more: 1. to understand [verstehen], 2. to have insight [einsehn], 3. to comprehend [begreifen].",["'Erkenntniße' as 'knowledge' in the plural sense; the list is given as in the source, and the order is Kant's own."]],"philippi-s16-012":["The circle of the objects which I can know is entitled the logical horizon of knowledge. *Congruentia cognitionis cum perfectione humana* [congruence of knowledge with human perfection].",["'Obiecte' rendered 'objects'; the Latin phrase is kept in italics with a bracketed English gloss."]],"philippi-s16-013":["When we speak of the horizon of human knowledge, and I am to determine it, I must distinguish the horizon of the knowledge of the whole human race from that of the knowledge of a single man. When I speak of the horizon of knowledge of the human race, I understand by it the extent of the modes of knowledge that are possible to man, and indeed possible *alternatively*, not *collectively*. The horizon is then very great; one man, for example, may be great in history, another in mathematics, and so on. If I now sum all these modes of knowledge of different subjects, a true sum results, but not a whole, since the parts are not connected. As with the countries of the earth, so the understanding of men bears different fruits. Provision has been made for men in respect of understanding as in respect of inclinations. Providence has carefully and kindly distributed the abilities as well as the inclinations in such a way that all kinds of sciences and arts are well supplied. When all this is added together, what a multitude! Those who gather together what many others understand, and unite it in themselves, are entitled polyhistors. Such a one was Leibniz. It is not possible to determine the horizon of the human race.",["'alternative nicht kollective': rendered 'alternatively, not collectively', i.e. each man knows some part, but no one knows the sum.","'Vorsicht' rendered 'Providence'; 'Fähigkeiten' rendered 'abilities'; 'Polyhistores' anglicised as 'polyhistors'.","'Vor die Menschen ist gesorgt' read as 'für die Menschen'; 'vertheüt' silently corrected to 'vertheilt'."]],"philippi-s16-014":["But how is the horizon of a single man to be determined? To be able to do this we must see of what 1. degree and 2. kind of knowledge he is capable; that is, we must look to his capacity and to his end. Men do not at all acquire modes of knowledge in accordance with their horizon; for they do not know their capacity, nor their future inclinations, and therefore also not the use which they will derive from this or that knowledge. We see here, then, that we can know the horizon in general well enough, but that of a single man only with difficulty.",["OCR 'l' after 'bestimmen' read as a question mark.","'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity'; 'Zweck' rendered 'end'."]],"philippi-s16-015":["I. The horizon in general is to be determined logically, that is, in terms of what man can know. Nothing is more ridiculous than to exert oneself to do something when the end is not possible to our capacity. When we have repeatedly tested our powers by trials, we then see that it was not possible for us. [But] then we trust our powers too far, supposing that what they do not reach must also lie beyond the powers of men; for we often see that much that was impossible for us has been discovered by others. A science which would mark out for us with certainty the limits of knowledge is difficult; for this cannot be determined on empirical grounds, since more is always being discovered. It must therefore be done on grounds *a priori*. To determine the limits of historical knowledge is not possible, nor those of any sensible modes of knowledge; but what degree of knowledge is possible, and what objects belong to it, can be determined. Metaphysics, namely, shows which objects belong to the intellectual and what degree of knowledge can be attained.",["The sentence 'Dann trauen wir unsern Kräfften zuviel daß...' is loosely constructed in the transcript; I restated it and supplied '[But]' to bring out the sense, which seems to be a warning against over-hasty inference from failed trials.","'Dann sehen wir daß es uns nicht möglich war' is also loose; the sense of the first trial-clause is rendered as it stands.","OCR 'möglieh' and 'Mensehen' silently corrected."]],"philippi-s16-016":["Metaphysical windbags make use of this science so as never to remain without an answer to endless questions. To them it is a treasury from which they fetch decisive answers to all questions. But it is philosophy when, through a correct knowledge of the limits of reason, I prove that these and other questions cannot be solved at all; it teaches, indeed, nothing positive, but it proves that [the question] is no problem of reason. It pursues the question back to its origin, and then sees how it arose, and whether it is not inapplicable to our natural powers. That is the most sublime of speculations. The circle of knowledge of a man can therefore be drawn, so that he sees that, given the constitution of his understanding, it is impossible for him to know this or that. Such a metaphysics keeps us back not only from what is useless but also from error. The greatest errors of theologians and moralists arise from the fact that metaphysics was taken for a dogmatics of all propositions raised above sensibility, of everything purely intellectual. Spinoza was not in his conduct what he was in his theory. Merely his perverted opinion of the capacity of reason brought him to such strange chimeras.",["'Sie verfolget die Frage...': 'sie' taken as philosophy (metaphysics), and the later 'sie ... sey' as the question; supplied '[the question]' accordingly.","'ob sie auf die natürlichen Kräfte nicht applicable sey' rendered 'whether it is not inapplicable to our natural powers' is a judgment; the German is literally 'whether it is not applicable', which in context must mean 'whether it is applicable'. Please check: the sense required is 'whether it is applicable to our natural powers'.","'Schwätzer' rendered 'windbags'; 'Hirngespinste' rendered 'chimeras'. OCR 'aufgelöset', 'Irrthüiner' silently corrected."]],"philippi-s16-017":["Logically we determine the horizon by capacity.",["'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity'."]],"philippi-s16-018":["Practically, by ends.",[]],"philippi-s16-019":["II. The practical horizon can be determined",["Breaks off as a heading; the list follows in the next passages."]],"philippi-s16-020":["1. pragmatically, according to utility.",[]],"philippi-s16-021":["2. morally, according to wisdom.",[]],"philippi-s16-022":["The practical horizon can therefore be determined 1. according to rules of prudence, 2. according to rules of wisdom. It was by these that Rousseau endeavoured to determine it.",["'Klugheit' rendered 'prudence'."]],"philippi-s16-023":["The horizon of knowledge is like the optical field of vision. Every man has a portion which he surveys. All men on the earth together have a very great horizon in the sciences. Taken together, men know a great deal, but this can produce no particular benefit; if one man had these scattered sciences together, he could produce benefit.",["The last sentence is elliptical; 'der kann Nutzen schaffen' is rendered as 'he could produce benefit', the conditional being required by the sense."]],"philippi-s16-024":["I. The horizon of historical modes of knowledge cannot be determined exactly, though it can to a certain extent. As new matter is added, part of the old is forgotten. The old falls more and more into oblivion. A Nabopolassar and the like are known only by name. Compendia are made out of the great histories; these gradually swell up again so high that our grandchildren will have to make compendia [of the compendia]. Thus the old is continually contracted and cut out. Man cannot take in everything. History furnishes only objects for rational knowledge.",["'Was von neuem hinzukommt, das vergißt sich vom Alten' rendered freely as 'part of the old is forgotten'.","'[of the compendia]' is supplied to make explicit what the transcript leaves implied."]],"philippi-s16-025":["2. The horizon of rational knowledge",["Heading; the subdivisions a and b follow."]],"philippi-s16-026":["a. That of mathematics, which is determined by the shortness of human life. It is therefore reasonably believed that not much more will be discovered. For one spends one's lifetime before one knows what has already been discovered, before one reaches the point from which one sets out [to discover]. A man, therefore, who lived some 200 years and had mastered everything in mathematics would be able to make more discoveries in it than others who live the ordinary span could make in 12,000 years. Commonly, just as a man arrives at the standpoint from which he can go on to discoveries, he dies; the next must begin again from the beginning, and cannot proceed from where the other left off.",["'[to discover]' supplied to complete the sense of 'von wo man ausgehet'.","'Standpunct' rendered 'standpoint'."]],"philippi-s16-027":["b. The horizon of philosophy is determined by metaphysics.",["OCR 'Metaplrysik' corrected to 'Metaphysik'."]],"philippi-s16-028":["This would be a consideration of the logical horizon; but the practical horizon is much smaller. Rousseau sought to determine it. There is much that we need not know, indeed that we must not know. Why, then, do we have faculties for it? Rousseau says: Nature has given it to us so that we should have no need of it; but if we go astray, it is to serve us as a means of helping ourselves back again, like an emetic and all the medicinal plants which nature has given us so that we should have no need to use them.",["'dürfen' ... 'müssen' in 'was wir nicht wissen dürfen, ja was wir nicht wissen müssen' is rendered 'need not ... must not' on the reading 'nicht brauchen'/'nicht sollen'; 'dürfen' here is probably 'brauchen' (need), and the second clause an intensification. Doubtful.","'Vermögen' rendered 'faculties' here; 'Vomitiv' rendered 'emetic'; OCR '?' misread as 1 corrected."]],"philippi-s16-029":["To the rational horizon belong only those objects which yield concepts of themselves and occasion reflection.",["'Begriffe von sich geben' rendered 'yield concepts of themselves'."]],"philippi-s16-030":["The more the sciences are restricted to a small number, the greater the perfection to which they can attain. The sciences find a rich value the more they become general and can be grasped by a playful wit.",["'Wissen-Schäften' (OCR) corrected to 'Wissenschaften'.","The second sentence is somewhat obscure and may be ironical; rendered literally."]],"philippi-s17-001":["§45",["Section number of Meier's Auszug; 'Der Autor' in the next passage refers to Meier."]],"philippi-s17-002":["The author says of a mode of knowledge that it is above our horizon only in so far as the horizon is determined logically, that is, in so far as the knowledge exceeds our capacity; for example, what our soul was before birth, and where it was, is above the horizon. What sort of fashions are there in Saturn? As our eye here has certain limits, and as astronomy has them, since the senses do not reach further, so the understanding also has them. Milton seems to have entered the *Limbus vanitatis* (the border of vanity).",["OCR '1' after 'Saturn für Moden' read as '?'.","'Fähigkeit' rendered 'capacity'. 'Limbus vanitatis' kept in Latin, with Kant's German gloss translated."]],"philippi-s17-003":["When we see how slight our knowledge is here, we feel uneasy about remaining in our true world, and we soar into ideal worlds. It is good to keep to the horizon, and it is also possible to determine it exactly. There are not many faculties that do not conduce to our happiness and benefit. Where something has no bearing on our happiness, we have little faculty for it. When man departs from his vocation [*Bestimmung*], he wavers like the ass in Aesop standing between two bundles of hay. What need has he to roam with a wanton eye over what cannot be known by him?",["'Bestimmung' is rendered 'vocation' here, since the sense is man's appointed destiny, not a logical determination; 'determiniren' is rendered 'determine'.","'Fähigkeiten' and 'Fähigkeit' are rendered 'faculties' and 'faculty'.","'Heubünden' is read as 'bundles of hay'; the fable is of the ass between two hay-bundles, attributed here to Aesop.","The sentence 'Gut ist es sich am Horizont zu halten' is kept close to the German; 'horizon' is Kant's technical term for the bounds of knowledge."]],"philippi-s18-001":["§46 §47",[]],"philippi-s18-002":["The author speaks only of the practical determination of the horizon when he explains what lies outside the horizon. What is harmful and useless lies, in the author's terminology, beneath [*unter*] one's horizon. When man, who is under obligation to so many things, does what is useless, he neglects what is his own. But what the usefulness of a mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] is, is hard to determine. It must be determined (1) morally, according to rules of the understanding, and (2) pragmatically, according to those of prudence.",["'Der Autor' is Meier, whose Auszug Kant is following.","'Benennung' rendered 'terminology'; 'das Seine' rendered 'what is his own'.","'Erkenntniß' in the singular with 'eine' is rendered 'a mode of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s18-003":["Beginners cannot foresee the remote usefulness [of a science]. How could Archimedes and Euclid know that the conic sections would serve to explain the course of the heavenly bodies? Only experience makes it clear.",["'[of a science]' is supplied to complete the sense."]],"philippi-s18-004":["A mode of knowledge that lets us feel the acuteness of the understanding: that is mathematics. It does not serve civil life. That sounds unworthy and niggardly, for one would then have to do without it; [but] it serves in respect of evidence, since it shows us the acuteness of the understanding.",["The sentence 'das klingt unwürdig und geizig, denn müßte man ihrer entbehren' is elliptical and its logic is doubtful; I have supplied '[but]' and kept the clause close to the German.","'Evidenz' is rendered 'evidence', in the sense of self-evidence or certainty."]],"philippi-s18-005":["Knowledge of morality has the most exact universal grounds. Here the understanding has a special insight. It is an honour and a happiness of mankind, but also a duty of it, to strive after knowledge of moral laws. The usefulness of knowledge is hard to establish. A mathematician invents at random, and often his inventions bring inexpressible benefit.",["'Sittlichkeit und Moral' is rendered 'morality', the two words being treated as a doublet.","'aufs Gerathe wohl' is rendered 'at random'."]],"philippi-s18-006":["Beneath the horizon. Knowledge of nature can be gained in every region. Indeed, if only the other necessary modes of knowledge would permit it, one would find even the text for morals in it. The effects of human folly and of human vices are not worth being known by us. What use, then, is the history of heresies? Insects at least adorn the cabinet even when dried; but how are Averden's chimeras adorned, even in French bindings? Indeed, they serve only to lead some into imitative reveries and others into a passion for mockery. What? Are we to learn to cultivate our hatred? Many remnants of the old philosophy are dreams that the dreaming ancients fashioned. They would laugh, the ancients, if they saw that we wished to learn from them.",["'Averdens' is doubtful; perhaps a proper name (Arnold? Averroes?) garbled in OCR or in the transcript. I have kept it as 'Averden'.","'exkoliren' is read as 'excoliren', 'to cultivate'.","'Franz-bänden' is rendered 'French bindings'.","'Naturerkennt niß' silently corrected to 'Naturerkenntniß'; 'Thorheit' and 'Laster' rendered 'folly' and 'vices'."]],"philippi-s18-007":["So far of the horizon with regard to the human race in general; now of the same with a view to age, sex, and station in life.",[]],"philippi-s18-008":["I. A child has a different horizon from a man. After about forty years, if a man continues to think, he sees things quite differently from before. He is then no longer so sensitive to charm. His inclinations vanish. Before, he decided what is good according to inclinations, but now he decides it by other marks; he recognises it in itself. He no longer has new insights, but more application of insights. Now the question arises: are modes of knowledge to be arranged according to the horizon of the age in which the man now finds himself, or according to that of the future age? For example, how much knowledge, and which, is appropriate for a child of six years? Is its horizon to be arranged according to the future horizon of the man, or according to the age in which it is, according to what it is now capable of knowing and to what its taste now inclines? Rousseau maintains the latter: (1) the child then enjoys its life in accordance with its condition, and (2) it is also prepared for the future horizon; (3) it passes its time in contentment and without fear of the future. Such a child may die when it will; it has enjoyed its life. If a man fills well the post he now has, he may by that very means be well prepared for the future. But if he strives only after the future, he fills his post badly and passes his life in unrest. So it is also with children. But what kinds of knowledge are suited to the child's taste and benefit is hard to say.",["'Erkenntnisse' (plural) rendered 'modes of knowledge'; 'Kenntnisse' rendered 'knowledge'.","'Verhältniß' rendered 'condition', i.e. its relation to its stage of life; 'Applikation' rendered 'application'.","The sentence on Rousseau's three points is kept as a list with numerals as in the text.","OCR 'empfindlieh' silently corrected to 'empfindlich'."]],"philippi-s18-009":["II. The horizon of sex. Here it is evident that that of the other sex encompasses only such knowledge as accords with its taste and calling. A calling suited to the female sex is its honour. The sexes have two different departments; to each belongs what is its own. Charm is lost when one occupies itself with what belongs to the department of the other.",["'IL' silently corrected to 'II.'","'Ehre desselben' is rendered 'its honour'; the sense of the first sentence ('des andern Geschlechts') is slightly obscure, and I have kept it close to the German."]],"philippi-s18-010":["III. The horizon according to difference of rank and mode of life. It is lamentable that a man of high birth should believe himself entitled to only a small horizon. Persons of distinction possess country estates; since they thus have such desirable time and opportunity, they ought to cultivate the sciences in preference, and not spend their lives feeding cattle and catching hares. In France and England nobility is placed not so much in birth as in the manner of thinking and in the soul. Mode of life is a true ground for determining the horizon.",["'Stände' rendered 'rank'; 'Lebensarten' rendered 'mode of life'; 'Denkungsart' rendered 'manner of thinking'.","'des Hasens' is taken as 'of hares' (a singular used generically)."]],"philippi-s18-011":["Sciences of skill in conducting oneself, and others, serve only for insinuation and recommendation. The principal sciences are those which, through their connection, can benefit the state.",["The first sentence is elliptical ('und andere'); I have kept it close to the German.","'durch den Zusammenhang' is rendered 'through their connection'."]],"philippi-s18-012":["Remarks. What is above our horizon is not just above the horizon of all. Hence one should study the history of the learned. What is within my horizon is not therefore within the horizon of all. From this arises faulty instruction, in that a teacher thinks the pupil must have the horizon that he himself has. Great acuteness is required to be able to put oneself at another's standpoint. Something may be outside the horizon of others and yet beneath our own.",["'Daher lerne man die Historie der Gelehrten' is kept as it stands; its connection with what precedes is not clear.","'Lehrling' rendered 'pupil'."]],"philippi-s18-013":["Much knowledge of the world is required if one wishes to know the proper application of the sciences and to know how far a given piece of knowledge befits this or that station. Is it really becoming for the Turkish emperors to learn handicrafts? Could they not have a nobler pastime? Knowledge of the worth of the sciences is necessary for a prince to have. It is a misfortune that man in his youth cannot choose what is suited to him, and that other men cannot hit upon it [for him].",["'[for him]' is supplied to complete the sense.","'Zeitvertreib' is rendered 'pastime'; 'türkischen Kaysern' rendered 'Turkish emperors'.","'Stand' rendered 'station'."]],"philippi-s18-014":["The evil for the horizon of man comes partly from the difficulty in the choice, partly from that in the profound final end.",["'bey dem tiefen Endzweck' is obscure, perhaps corrupt; I have kept it literal.","'Endzweck' rendered 'final end'."]],"philippi-s18-015":["A theoretical teacher must direct himself by the most capable; but a practical teacher also by the least capable of all.",[]],"philippi-s18-016":["Just as we do not make our horizon the measure of others, so we must always seek to determine our own more closely. Man must not sketch out his horizon at once; for he does not know his future faculties and inclinations, nor yet the calling to which they will draw him. In the end he must fix it, and then not alter it without necessity. Those who apply themselves now to this, now to that, become perfect in none and are bunglers in everything.",["The sentence 'denn er weiß ... nicht auch den Beruf nicht' is slightly irregular in the German; I have smoothed it.","'fuscherhafft' is rendered 'bunglers'."]],"philippi-s19-001":["§50",[]],"philippi-s19-002":["Here the author speaks of ignorance that is blameless. This relates to things for the knowledge of which we have no faculty, which do not belong to our calling, and which also afford us no real benefit. Such ignorance is, in respect of the knowledge that is properly our own, its very soul. Why should one trouble oneself about modes of knowledge that do not belong to us and are of no use to us? The ignorance of a scholar in the science of the kitchen, or in the methods by which merchants deceive, will, for example, be blameless. Ignorance becomes praiseworthy when one rejects unnecessary, or harmful, unworthy and pernicious modes of knowledge, with the intention that one may occupy oneself instead with useful ones. But ignorance may also be praiseworthy *respective*, that is, in view of certain circumstances, ages and so forth, in which it deserves not merely no blame but even praise.",["'Erkenntnisse' (plural) rendered 'modes of knowledge' where the plural is needed; 'Vermögen' as 'faculty'.","'sejm' read as 'seyn' (OCR slip); 'Betrugmethode' rendered 'methods by which merchants deceive'.","'respectiue' kept in Latin and glossed in the English, since the sense is 'relatively, in a certain respect'.","'etc. etc.' rendered 'and so forth'; 'Zeitalter' taken as 'ages'."]],"philippi-s19-003":["I § 52",["Leading 'I' left as printed; it may be an OCR artefact."]],"philippi-s19-004":["Socrates said that through much inquiry he had found that he still knows nothing. This profound saying has been bandied about by shallow minds, without their having understood it. It takes a great deal to know one's ignorance. There are two kinds of consciousness of ignorance.",["'Bewußt -seyn' joined as 'Bewußtseyn' (broken word)."]],"philippi-s19-005":["1. When one measures oneself against the objects and thereby sees",["Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s19-006":["that one does not know them.",[]],"philippi-s19-007":["2. Or, more materially, when one does not know the objects themselves.",["'materiahter' read as 'materialiter' (OCR slip), rendered 'more materially'."]],"philippi-s19-008":["But to see that our knowledge is nothing is scientific. To see the limits of one's knowledge, the extent of it, and thereby to recognize that I do not know this: that is very profound philosophy. Not-knowing is here scientific in respect of the limits of knowledge, which one must know in order to see it. To see one's ignorance systematically and with acuteness, to what degree one can go: oh, for this a great deal is required. We can see a lack only by having a knowledge of the opposite positives. For example, one cannot know darkness except through light. Blind people who have been operated on have been afraid of black night, though before they were not afraid of it, because they did not know it.",["'Wissen' and 'Erkenntniß' both rendered 'knowledge'; 'wissenschaftlich' kept as 'scientific' to match 'scientifisch'.","'Positionen' rendered 'positives' by the sense of the argument (what is posited); 'gegenseitig' taken as 'opposite'.","The sentence 'Not-knowing is here scientific...' is awkward in the German; kept close, since the transcript is rough."]],"philippi-s19-009":["Non-being is no object; but the removal [*Aufhebung*] of the positive is something objective. The *remotion* of light is darkness; so I must have known the light if I am to know that it has been removed and that darkness is thereby present. The savage does not know his lack, for he has never had the abundance. In the same way one cannot know the limits of knowledge without knowing all knowledge [i.e. the whole of it]. Thus the astronomer will recognize his ignorance far more than the common man. Among all the theoretical sciences none can humble us more than astronomy.",["'Remotion' (Remotion/removiret) kept in Latinate form as Kant's technical term; the German 'removiret' rendered 'removed'.","'als daß man alles Wissen kennt' is elliptical; I supplied the bracketed gloss and take it as 'unless one knows ...'.","'Erkenntnisses' (genitive singular) rendered 'knowledge'."]],"philippi-s19-010":["First it exalts us, then it abases us to the very depths.",[]],"philippi-s19-011":["What we have to esteem highly in ourselves is the ability to distinguish the moral and to determine the limits of our wishes.",["'das Sittliche' rendered 'the moral'."]],"philippi-s19-012":["It is a great benefit of the sciences that they show us our ignorance.",[]],"philippi-s20-001":["§ 54",[]],"philippi-s20-002":["One can choose sciences for one's horizon according to skill, prudence, or wisdom. He possesses skill who has good means for attaining any ends whatever. Prudence is the capacity to choose for oneself ends that are suited merely to the inclinations. Wisdom is the capacity to choose for oneself ends of perfection.",["'wählen zu seinem Horizont Wissenschaften' is loose; rendered 'choose sciences for one's horizon'.","'behebigen' read as 'beliebigen' (OCR slip).","'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity'."]],"philippi-s21-001":["§53",["Paragraph numbering is out of sequence in the source (§54 precedes §53); kept as printed."]],"philippi-s21-002":["Plato distinguished philosophy and philodoxy. A philodox is one who acquires every skill in answering all possible questions, however absurd the ends they may have. The philosopher in the academic sense is such a philodox; but the true philosopher is he who seeks to make himself capable of knowing only what is suited to true perfection and to the highest determination of man.",["'Pliilosoph' read as 'Philosoph' (OCR slip). 'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination' per the term list; 'vocation' might suit better.","'Philodoxie' kept as a transliteration (lover of opinion)."]],"philippi-s21-003":["Polyhistory is a historical knowledge of the whole horizon of mankind. Vanity is a great incentive to polyhistory, because historical knowledge is the easiest. He discovers what has been thought without himself thinking.",["'Er' has no clear antecedent; I take it as the polyhistor and leave it as 'He'."]],"philippi-s22-001":["§ 55",[]],"philippi-s22-002":["If a mode of knowledge contains nothing that does not belong to the purpose, it is commensurate (*adaequat*).",["'Erkennt niß' rejoined (broken word); 'abgemessen' rendered 'commensurate'."]],"philippi-s23-001":["§56",[]],"philippi-s23-002":["We can study 1. for the sake of taste, when one is captivated by the charm of the sciences; 2. for the sake of vanity, merely that one may come into repute. In and by itself cursory study is not wholly to be rejected. One should first run cursorily over the field of the sciences before learning them thoroughly. If we study synthetically, that is, from the parts to the knowledge of the whole, we shall not get on well; we shall be more fortunate in study if we proceed analytically, going to the parts after considering the whole. Hence, when we read through a book, it is necessary that we first represent to ourselves the whole outline, then proceed to the principal parts, and then to the subordinate parts.",["The stray apostrophe before '2.' is an OCR mark and is omitted; 'Theüen' read as 'Theilen'.","The long sentences are kept largely intact; 'Geschmak' rendered 'taste'."]],"philippi-s24-001":["§62",[]],"philippi-s24-002":["The extent of learned knowledge rests",["'Weitläufftigkeit' rendered 'extent'."]],"philippi-s24-003":["1. on the multitude of objects that I know, that is, *quantitas extensiua*.",["'extensiua' left as in the source (for *extensiva*)."]],"philippi-s24-004":["2. on the degree to which I know each of these objects: that is, *quantitas intensiua*.",[]],"philippi-s25-001":["§63",[]],"philippi-s25-002":["The principal science must have a relation",["'Hauptwissenschafft' rendered 'principal science'."]],"philippi-s25-003":["α. to the faculties [*Fähigkeiten*].",["'a.' read as Greek alpha; 'Fähigkeiten' rendered 'faculties' (abilities)."]],"philippi-s25-004":["β. to the inclinations.",["'ß.' read as beta."]],"philippi-s25-005":["γ. to the duties and determination of the subject, and to the place one occupies in the world. By these rules one should choose one's principal science; and this one must do, for among all modes of knowledge there ought to be unity and one ground to which I relate them all.",["'y.' read as gamma; 'die\" Stelle ... eiiinimmt' corrected to 'die Stelle ... einnimmt'; 'Kegeln Avähle' read as 'Regeln wähle'."]],"philippi-s26-001":["§65",[]],"philippi-s26-002":["A pedant is one who may indeed possess skill and knowledge but does not know how to apply them, because he does not rightly know the world. There are therefore pedants in all estates: among courtiers, soldiers, pedants of dress; there are pedants of taste, and even pedants among the learned in metaphysics, when one applies a subtlety to an object that is nevertheless of no use. The learned pedant is the most tolerable, because one can at least learn something from him, even though it is brought forward at the wrong time. Hence, in order to be rid of pedantry, one must be in a position to form a sufficient judgment in what cases the judgments fit. Cicero's *esse videatur*, the so-called *fallacia causae non causae*, almost the whole of syllogistic, and the multitude of terminologies belong to pedantry. Pedantry is opposed to *petitmaitrerey* [petit-maîtrerie, foppery], which is full of shallowness. This is found among peoples in which the womenfolk set the tone, for example among the French. But charlatanry is quite different. The pedant errs from ignorance, because he lacks the insight to display his accomplishments properly. The charlatan, however, errs on purpose; he intends, in the presence of others, to parade his insight, whether it fits or not, in order to win fame.",["'Metaplrysik', 'Pedantere}T', 'loß' corrected silently (OCR); 'Frauenzinimer' read as 'Frauenzimmer'.","'Cicerons esse videatur' is a garbled or cut allusion (to Cicero's stylistic ideal); kept as it stands and not explained.","'caussae non caussae' modernised to 'causae non causae'.","'Schicklichkeiten' is taken as 'accomplishments', but 'proprieties' is possible; doubtful."]],"philippi-s27-001":["Third Section. §66",["'Der III. Abschnitt' rendered 'Third Section'."]],"philippi-s27-002":["Extent is an extensive magnitude, when I know how much is posited; this magnitude is regarded as a whole and is counted. Intensive magnitude is the degree of the modes of knowledge; it is regarded as a ground, when the parts have many consequences, and I ask how much is posited through the thing. This magnitude is entitled fertility and importance. Subjective modes of knowledge consist in the degree of distinctness.",["'Gröse' read as 'Grösse' (magnitude); 'wie viel gesetzt' is elliptical and I supplied 'is posited' from the following clause.","'Deutlichkeit' rendered 'distinctness'.","The sentence on intensive magnitude is obscure in the German; kept close to the text."]],"philippi-s28-001":["§ 74",[]],"philippi-s28-002":["The author speaks of the vulgar [*Pöbelhafte*]. In and by itself nothing is vulgar or base; it is the actions of men, the misuse of man's powers, that make it vulgar or base. For one cannot say that a worm in nature is contemptible, but in the actions of men something becomes base. There are many indecorous things. For example, the casuistic moral theology written by a Jesuit, in which the author treats of how a clergyman is to conduct himself in all cases of married life, is an indecorous book. Rules are laid down in it for cases which will never occur.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'pöbelhaff t', 'emem' (einem), 'Z. B.' retained as 'For example'.","'casuistische Moral' rendered 'casuistic moral theology' by judgment, since the work is by a Jesuit and concerns a clergyman's conduct; the German says only 'Moral'.","'Pöbelhafft' rendered 'vulgar'; 'niederträchtig' as 'base'; 'unanständig' as 'indecorous'. These are choices."]],"philippi-s29-001":["§91",[]],"philippi-s29-002":["The faculty of judgment, of seeing the true worth of things, we attain late, when experience, wisdom and prudence are sufficiently present in us. The faculty of speculation, on the other hand, we attain sooner.",["'Vermögen' rendered 'faculty' in both occurrences; 'Beurtheilung' as 'judgment' (in the sense of appraisal)."]],"philippi-s30-001":["Section IV. On the Truth of Learned Knowledge.",["'gelehrte Erkenntniß' rendered 'learned knowledge'."]],"philippi-s30-002":["The first of all logical questions is this: What is truth? Yet it is so hard to give an answer to it that does not run back in a circle that this remains to the present day one of the most difficult matters in philosophy. The rules about which a judgment is to be passed must already have been laid down as a ground. I am to investigate the rules of truth, and in doing so I must myself proceed according to the rules of truth, etc. Jacobi, in his *Vermischte Schriften*.",["'viel.' read as an abbreviation for 'vielleicht' or 'etc.'; I render 'etc.', but the reading is doubtful.","The reference to Jacobi's 'vermischte Schriften' is left in German as a title.","The sentence 'Die Regeln müssen ... soll' restated more plainly; the German is elliptical."]],"philippi-s30-003":["We are to explain truth. We do not wish to do this directly, but by comparing it with falsity. Among the ancients the sceptics also asked after truth, in order to throw the dogmatists into confusion, and refuted them by their dialectic; of this more below. Here I shall oppose truth not to error [*Irrthum*] but to falsity [*Falschheit*]. The theme of truth is the object. I here compare truth and falsity, and I establish the following:",["OCR 'f rügen' read as 'fragten' (asked); 'gerade zu' as 'directly'.","'Das Thema der Wahrheit ist das Object' is rendered literally; the sense is obscure and may be a transcriber's slip."]],"philippi-s30-004":["I. Truth and falsity are found only in judgments, not in concepts. If someone does not judge, I can call his knowledge neither true nor false; only through judging do truth and falsity manifest themselves. But if a concept contains a contradiction in itself, then surely it must be false? No, not even then. Falsity is when one is deceived. Concepts cannot deceive me, unless judgments are added to them.",["OCR 'hinzulvommen' read as 'hinzukommen'.","'hintergangen' rendered 'deceived'; 'Wiederspruch' as 'contradiction'."]],"philippi-s30-005":["The concept of a corporeal spirit is not false. An impossible or self-contradictory concept can itself have true consequences. For example, a corporeal thinking being would be divisible. And by drawing true consequences from the impossible I afterwards perceive the impossibility. If the concepts in the inferences were false, one could perceive no impossibility. Spinoza's error consisted in judgment, not in concepts. In short: falsity is found only where one judges, and not where one does not judge.",["'Schlüsse' rendered 'inferences'; the clause 'Wären die Begriffe der Schlüsse falsch' is kept close to the German."]],"philippi-s30-006":["II. Upon what does truth and falsity rest in every judgment? Upon the agreement of the knowledge with the object. Good; but how can I see that my knowledge agrees with the object? I can compare the object only in so far as I know it. I cannot compare the object itself with my knowledge, but only the knowledge of the object with the knowledge of the same object; and if they agree, I say the knowledge is true. Three sides have three angles. Against this the ancients set up the dialectic, in which they sought to discredit all marks of truth. It runs thus: If someone whom you do not know puts forward something as true, and in order to confirm his assertion appeals to another whom you likewise do not know, the question is whether I can be certain that what he asserts is true. No; for I can accept something as true only in so far as it agrees with the object. Here, however, I do not know the objects, or those people. It is just so with knowledge. One knowledge of the object, of which I do not know whether it is true, appeals to another knowledge of the object, of which I likewise do not know whether it is true, and yet if they agree I say that it is true. This dialectic is no mere objection but an argument, and serves to make known the proper nature of truth. Truth is the agreement of the modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*] of the object with one another. For what we call objects are only our modes of knowledge. Have we, then, general rules of agreement? Yes, and we shall go through them.",["'verachten' in 'alle Merkmale der Wahrheit verachten wollten' is probably a transcriber's slip or OCR error for 'verdächtig machen' or similar; rendered 'discredit' by the sense of the argument. Doubtful.","'Drey Seiten haben drey Winkel' (OCR 'clrey') is given as the example of a judgment; it is a triangle's property.","'Erkenntnisse' in the plural rendered 'modes of knowledge'; 'Gegenstände' as 'objects'.","The long sentence on the sceptical dialectic is divided only where the German allows; 'auf -nehmen' read as 'aufnehmen'."]],"philippi-s30-007":["All judgments spring from understanding and reason. If we wish to judge what is semblance [*Schein*] or truth, we must do so according to the laws of reason. Sensibility can do nothing here. The judgment which has private validity indicates only the particular relation of the object to my subject; and that is semblance. The judgment which is universally valid concerns truth. For a judgment which necessarily holds for all agrees with the object. And the universal validity of knowledge is just what we are seeking; and by what power of the soul? By the understanding. Sensibility, the manner in which we are affected, shows us only semblance; but the understanding, when it judges unmixed with sensibility, shows us truth unmixed with semblance. The understanding is therefore alone the power to find what is universally valid, what agrees with all judgments.",["OCR 'Mir afficirt' corrected to 'wir afficirt'; 'Kraff t' to 'Kraft'.","'Verstand und Vernunfft' rendered 'understanding and reason'; 'Seele' by 'soul', as in the text, not 'Gemüt'.","'Schein' rendered 'semblance' and kept distinct from 'Erscheinung' (appearance)."]],"philippi-s30-008":["Truth is the agreement of knowledge with the common laws of reason.",["'gemeinen Gesetzen' rendered 'common laws', as the same phrase recurs below with 'general'."]],"philippi-s30-009":["If we are to know truth, we must have certain marks of truth, and these are:",[]],"philippi-s30-010":["1. Agreement with the supreme laws of the understanding in general.",["'Merkmale' rendered 'marks'."]],"philippi-s30-011":["2. That which accords with general laws *a posteriori*, in that I observe whether human understanding actually agrees.",["The elliptical 'diejenige' is expanded as 'that which accords', supplying the sense of 'Zusammenstimmung' from the previous item."]],"philippi-s30-012":["According to the first I say: what is true according to the general laws of the understanding is good for everyone; and according to the second: what agrees with everyone's judgment of the understanding is true. There are thus two ways of knowing truth. The mathematician proceeds according to the first, and seeks to make all propositions agree with the common rules of reason. Then he proves his proposition and boldly defies anyone to withhold assent. The ground on which all must give assent is the agreement of the knowledge with the fundamental laws of the understanding. If we saw that some doubted, we should doubt too. We have, then, a double mark of truth, the one *a priori*, the other *a posteriori*. The latter rests on the principles: *Consentientia uni tertio consentiunt inter se*; and *Consentientia inter se consentiunt uni tertio*. The test *a posteriori*, whether a proposition which one believes oneself to have framed according to general rules of the understanding also finds assent in everyone's understanding, cannot be dispensed with. For example, the doctrine of monads is not accepted by the English. If this doctrine accords with the common laws of the understanding, it must also be convincing to the English. The application of a rational proposition *a posteriori* is therefore highly necessary. It is the test whether the proposition accords with the common laws of the understanding or not. Even if I should err, others will not, and will thereby reveal my error to me.",["OCR corrected: 'Ueberemstimmung' to 'Uebereinstimmung', 'Wü' to 'Wir', 'vni' to 'uni'.","'gut für jedermann' is rendered 'good for everyone'; the sense may be 'valid for everyone' but the German is kept.","The Latin principles are left in Latin, in italics, as technical terms; the transcriber's 'vni' is emended to 'uni'.","'Wenn wir sehen würden daß einige zweifeln, so würden wir es auch thun' translated literally; the sense is that doubt by others would produce doubt in us."]],"philippi-s30-013":["The diameter stands to the circumference as 100 to 314. Since all geometers accept this proposition, it must be true, and it is so. The proof can be given through geometry. There are",["OCR 'Weü' read as 'Weil'. The passage breaks off at 'Es giebt'; the next passage continues it."]],"philippi-s30-014":["1. Objective criteria of truth; these are *a priori cognoscibile* [knowable *a priori*].",["The Latin 'cognoscibile' is kept as written (the neuter singular is irregular, perhaps the transcriber's). The bracketed gloss is mine."]],"philippi-s30-015":["2. Subjective criteria; these are known *a posteriori*.",[]],"philippi-s30-016":["The objective criteria consist in the universal laws of reason, in accordance with which the agreement of modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*] is judged. Agreement in accordance with the laws of the understanding consists in propositions of identity. Conflict between modes of knowledge, in accordance with the laws of the understanding, is the principle of contradiction. These are the objective criteria of truth, and they alone are such; but they are not sufficient, that is to say [what is further required is] the agreement of the subject's knowledge with the knowledge of others, or the agreement of the subject's knowledge in one state with that in another. Even when we have satisfied all the objective marks, we are still uncertain in the application, whether the knowledge also agrees with the universal laws of the subject. It is so in reckoning. Universal marks cannot be given for the application. Even if I give a rule of application, there must again be added an instruction how I am to apply this rule.",["Sentence divided. The 'd. i.' clause is incomplete in the German; I supply 'what is further required is' in brackets, as the sense seems to demand. Doubtful.","'Erkenntnisse' is rendered 'modes of knowledge' in the plural. 'Kennzeichen' is rendered 'marks'."]],"philippi-s30-017":["Of correctness in application there are no universal rules. It can therefore be had from nothing but subjective grounds. The subjective grounds serve to verify the exercise of the universal rules. I must accordingly make tests:",[]],"philippi-s30-018":["I. I compare my knowledge, which I have of the object, with the knowledge of other objects that has already been accepted as true and convincing. In this way we have a clue for applying the universal rule. The subject compares the judgments which it passes with those it passes at another time and in another state. To guard against falsity we must take time, so as to be able to consider the judgments from another side, from another point of view, at different times. Falsity does not show itself at once. The many errors arise from haste in judging. It makes a great difference in the application of the common rules of understanding and of reason if we look at something at different times and from different points of view: we shall thereby often discover errors which were at first quite hidden from us.",["'die es gefällt' read as 'fällt' (passes, pronounces judgments). Restated in shorter sentences."]],"philippi-s30-019":["II. The second criterion is the agreement of the knowledge of our subject with that of other subjects. In general, that is true which is valid for everyone; what has only a private validity is illusion [*Schein*]. When I have proceeded in knowledge according to universal rules, it is still necessary that I test whether this knowledge of mine fully agrees with the judgment of others. For if it does not agree, I may be assured that my knowledge has not been framed in agreement with the universal rules of the understanding. Mathematics itself must be tested in this way. And when the mathematician proves newly discovered propositions without first ascertaining whether others also accept them as true, this assurance and confidence arise from the observation that the reason of every man agrees with the reason of the mathematician, and that mathematics conforms to the rules of the common understanding.",["'Schein' is rendered 'illusion'; 'semblance' would also serve."]],"philippi-s30-020":["From this it follows that the subjective grounds must be added to the objective for their confirmation.",[]],"philippi-s30-021":["Universal validity is required for truth.",[]],"philippi-s30-022":["We ought not to be indifferent when others do not give us their assent. It is a logical experiment when I test my judgments by the minds of others. If someone does not believe my propositions, I may suppose that there is somewhere in them an error, since they are not universally valid. The point of distinction of truth is the universal understanding, and not mine alone.",["'Unterscheidungspunkt' rendered 'point of distinction'."]],"philippi-s30-023":["Whence does it come that all men who are given to reflection are ready and inclined to communicate their insights to others? Is it the vanity of being praised? No; indeed a scholar is even blamed if he does not make his knowledge known, and this vanity will never be suppressed. The cause is that the human understanding finds itself, in its insights, in need of testing its knowledge on other minds. Without this experience it does not venture to hold its knowledge true. It is conscious that it is not itself capable of seeing its errors; hence it leaves this to others. Thus the human understanding is to be regarded as by nature participating [*theilnehmend*]. It has the impulse to hold its judgments against the judgments of others, and not merely to stop short at the supreme principles of reason.",["OCR 'comniuniciren' read as 'communiciren'; 'be}?\"' read as 'bei' (conjectural). The last clause is therefore somewhat uncertain.","'theilnehmend' is literally 'sharing' or 'taking part'; I keep the German beside the English."]],"philippi-s30-024":["Objections raised by others, or the poor effect which my proposition has upon conviction, ought to make me attentive. If objections are made to our judgments, we shall test our own propositions accordingly and seek to bring them into agreement. If our propositions are not convincing, we shall soon see that something is lacking in them, at least in distinctness, and we shall improve them.",[]],"philippi-s30-025":["We must therefore be glad to submit our judgments to others for sifting. It belongs to the eternal laws that a man should have freedom to make known to others the propositions on which he speculates. It is otherwise [?] when one is to produce something of oneself; that can have only a private validity. A man on a desert island will certainly give up all speculation.",["'Es sey dann wenn man von selbst etwas machen soll' is obscure; my rendering is tentative and the '[?]' marks the doubt."]],"philippi-s30-026":["Falsity is either",["Breaks off; the following passages continue it."]],"philippi-s30-027":["1. *materialis* or",[]],"philippi-s30-028":["2. *formalis*.",[]],"philippi-s30-029":["Formal falsity is when a mode of knowledge contradicts itself or does not fit [together].",["'sich nicht paßt': the bracketed 'together' is supplied."]],"philippi-s30-030":["Material falsity occurs most frequently, so that even true propositions may have it. It occurs when a common piece of knowledge, which everyone knows, is presented and stated as if it had been discovered by our understanding. One is deceived in one's hope. One hopes to receive a learned and profound answer, and gets back only what one already knows. What is the cause of the generation of plants? *Vis plastica*. Here I am deceived. It is just as if someone paid me with a Polish copper groschen boiled in tartar, when I had lent him an ordinary groschen. I get my own back, but am deceived in my hope. I ask the scholar, and he gives me back what I asked, in a new dress. In explanations of causes and in solutions of problems there is so much word-mongering and tautology that one must be astonished. How am I to make a definition that sets everything before me, in which everything is united as in a quintessence? Seek so many marks until, taken together, they make up the whole concept. But that is just what I asked.",["Sentences divided. The groschen was boiled in tartar to give it a silvery look. 'Vis plastica' is kept in Latin.","'Wortkrämerey' (OCR 'Wortkrärnerey') rendered 'word-mongering'."]],"philippi-s30-031":["One should be on one's guard against such tautologies. The beginning of wisdom is *stultitia caruisse* [to be free from folly].",["The quotation is Horace's 'sapientia prima stultitia caruisse'; the transcript's wording is kept and the gloss is bracketed."]],"philippi-s30-032":["No science is so full of tautologies as morals. The greatest men have this fault. 'If you would be virtuous, tame your affects.' But how am I to change my taste and tame the affects? It is just as if a watchmaker, when his apprentice asks him how watches are to be made, answers: 'You must see to it that the wheels engage one another with their teeth.'",["OCR 'Kerne' read as 'Keine'; '\\\\ ie' read as 'wie'. 'Affecten' is rendered 'affects'. 'Geschmack' is rendered 'taste'."]],"philippi-s30-033":["The many tautologies in morals come from this, that we at once give assent to the laws of virtue and morality, and at once concede them, even when the philosopher states them to us only by halves and without proof.",[]],"philippi-s30-034":["One must attend to falsity of form even when the propositions are true.",[]],"philippi-s30-035":["If someone proves the existence of God or a moral proposition, everyone is already inclined to give him his assent, even if the proof is a poor one, and as it were to make him a present of the proof.",[]],"philippi-s30-036":["Doubt about the form of knowledge is distinct from doubt about its matter, and falsity of form can not and must not be regarded as falsity of matter. There are some sciences which are raised above the common understanding; but there are others in which the philosopher can prove his point with the common understanding, and in which his scientific grounds are very frail. Yet he chooses the latter grounds in order to give the appearance of having an insight into the matter quite different from that of the common understanding.",["\"die letzten\" is taken to refer to the \"scientific grounds\" (wissenschaftliche Gründe), not to the common understanding; the sense requires this, and I have made it explicit as \"the latter grounds\".","The original has a stray space before the semicolon (OCR/print); silently normalised."]],"philippi-s30-037":["The marks and criteria of distinction of the true and the false. A mode of knowledge is false if it contradicts itself. Is it also false if it contradicts other modes of knowledge? Yes: if it contradicts those that are true, it is false. But that cannot be the supreme mark of the false, since here I presuppose that I already have a mark of the true and the false.",["\"Erkenntnis\" is rendered \"mode of knowledge\" where the plural or the individual item is meant (\"andern Erkenntnissen\", \"den wahren\").","\"den wahren\" is taken as an elided \"wahren Erkenntnissen\"; supplied in the sense, not bracketed in the English for readability."]],"philippi-s30-038":["The matter is very subtle. Meanwhile we will accept it. For example, whatever contradicts the principles of experience is false.",["\"Die Materie\" here means the subject-matter under discussion, not \"matter\" as opposed to form; \"Indessen\" is taken as \"meanwhile\" (provisionally)."]],"philippi-s31-001":["§96",[]],"philippi-s31-002":["If all the consequences of a mode of knowledge are true, the knowledge itself is true. But if only a single consequence is false, the knowledge is false. If many consequences of a mode of knowledge are true, it is probable; but to see all the consequences is very difficult. From the truth of the consequences the truth of the knowledge can be inferred only by approximation; it does not possess logical evidence. Such is the Copernican system, and all other hypotheses whose correctness is inferred from the multitude of true consequences.",["OCR \"Kopernikamsche\" corrected to \"Kopernikanische\".","\"So ist\" is rendered as \"Such is\" (i.e. the Copernican system is a case in point)."]],"philippi-s31-003":["Many physical theorems held good for a long time because all the consequences that were known were true, until a single experiment refuted them.",[]],"philippi-s31-004":["These are the two ways of inferring a posteriori, from the truth or falsity of the consequences, to that of the grounds. To know the truth one must drive out the falsity. If we wish to infer from the truth of the consequences to that of the knowledge, this is difficult, for we should then have to know all the consequences. Suppose that for the present all the consequences known to me are true: the knowledge is still not completely certain, by the strictness of logical form. The proof and inference is entitled apagogic when I infer from the consequences of a mode of knowledge to its truth [sic: see note]. I take the opposite and refute it by seeking out a single false consequence; then my contrary proposition is proved. This apagogic mode of proof is customary in mathematics and is very evident. It is remarkable that we have more conviction in seeing the false than in seeing the truth. Throughout the whole of nature we perceive conflict (*Widerstreit*) sooner than truth. There is thereby, as it were, a hiatus and a gap in the order of things. Hence apagogic proofs are preferred to direct ones.",["OCR \"be wüste\" read as \"bewusste\" (\"known to me\"); \"BeAveiß\" read as \"Beweiß\"; \"vorjetzt\" taken as \"for the present\".","The text says apagogic proof infers \"auf die Wahrheit derselben\"; the following sentences show it proceeds from a false consequence to the falsity of the ground, hence the contrary's truth. I translate as it stands and flag it with [sic]; possibly a slip in the transcript.","\"Der Beweiß und Schluß heißt\" rendered \"is entitled\" per the method.","\"Widerstreit\" is kept in German beside \"conflict\" because the sense turns on it; the sentence on the \"hiatus\" is obscure in the transcript."]],"philippi-s31-005":["Since logical perfection consists in the agreement of modes of knowledge with the object, aesthetic perfection in the agreement of the knowledge with the subject, truth is the essential perfection and the necessary essential condition under which a mode of knowledge can be perfect. In regard to truth we have to look",["The passage breaks off at \"zu sehen\", introducing the two points that follow in the next passages; translated as it stands.","\"Erkenntnisse\" (plural) in the first clause is rendered \"modes of knowledge\"."]],"philippi-s31-006":["1. to the agreement of the knowledge with the object;",["Semicolon added for the run-on list; the grammatical continuation is from the preceding passage."]],"philippi-s31-007":["2. to the means of arriving at it; to this belongs distinctness, etc., etc.",[]],"philippi-s31-008":["If we seek the degree of truth that is compatible with the subjective laws, this is indeed not in accordance with the requirement of the understanding but is in accordance with the requirement of wit (*Witz*). This is aesthetic truth, truth according to the laws of sensibility. For example, when the sky is represented as a blue canopy, or God as a majestic monarch, this is aesthetically true but not logically. Yet even aesthetic truth must be in conformity with the logical. Untruth destroys all charm; one sees this in false similes. In poets one finds such aesthetic truth, and they must be judged accordingly. Haller and Lucretius, therefore, must be judged not so much logically as aesthetically. The poet expresses universally what is frequent. He expresses himself *hardi* (boldly). The painfulness of confining oneself to a particular proposition does not suit taste. What everyone holds to be true is true according to the aesthetic rules. In these there is nothing constant; they change and are governed by the judgments of men.",["OCR \"-\\\\ ird\" read as \"wird\"; \"Erf orderung\" as \"Erforderung\". The apparatus mark and the pages (393–394) are omitted.","\"hardi\" is the French word (hardi) in the German text; kept in italics with an English gloss.","\"Witz\" is kept beside \"wit\" because the eighteenth-century sense (ingenuity, combining faculty) is wider than the modern.","\"Peinlichkeit\" rendered \"painfulness\"; \"scrupulous strictness\" might also be defensible."]],"philippi-s31-009":["Objectively every mode of knowledge is either false or not; there is no third possibility. But subjective knowledge can be either true or false, or neither true nor false. It can have neither certain marks of truth nor of falsity, that is, it can be groundless. A mode of knowledge which is uncertain or groundless according to logical laws can be sufficiently grounded according to practical laws; that is, it can be capable of guiding man as if it were true.",["\"entweder falsch oder nicht\" is probably a slip for \"entweder wahr oder falsch\"; translated as it stands.","Final sentence lacks a full stop in the source."]],"philippi-s31-010":["§ 100",[]],"philippi-s31-011":["The author speaks of total truth and falsity.",["\"Der Autor\" is Meier, whose text Kant is lecturing on."]],"philippi-s31-012":["All our modes of knowledge are either wholly true or partly true, or wholly false or partly false. A mode of knowledge may contain nothing but truth, and yet I may not have the knowledge complete; something is still lacking in it. In that case I must not say that it is practically true. For example, when I extract the square root of 2, I may approximate as long as I like, but I never get it out completely; something is always still lacking. Yet what I do get out is true. It is the same with the ratio of the diameter to the circumference. There is nothing false in it, but some small particles are always still lacking. Yet the difference is to be reckoned as nothing. The moon is 59 [?] earth-diameters distant from the earth. Something is still lacking here. What is thought with less than the total truth is true; what is thought beyond the total truth of the knowledge is false. If I give the true knowledge not wholly but only a part of it, this is no falsity contradicting the remaining modes of knowledge, but a defect, or a missing piece, of the measured true knowledge.",["\"Dann muß ich nicht sagen, daß sie praktisch wahr sey\" is obscure; translated literally. \"se}^\" read as \"sey\".","\"59 l Erddiameter\" is garbled OCR (perhaps 59 and a fraction); I give \"59 [?]\" and do not supply the fraction.","\"Dann\" rendered \"In that case\" and \"Verhältniß des Diameters zur Peripherie\" as \"ratio of the diameter to the circumference\"."]],"philippi-s31-013":["We now come to total falsity, and here we may fairly ask: Can a human judgment ever be wholly false? No. For it is only the understanding that judges, even when it judges falsely. Falsity arises from the busy activity of sensibility and of other powers of knowledge, which couple themselves with the understanding that judges and mingle in its judgments. If it were possible for sensibility at times to judge, then totally false judgments would also be possible. But since nothing in man can judge except one and the same understanding, all judgments are in part true. There is no judgment that would not be true in some particular respect. There must always be a ground why the understanding has judged as it did. For example, someone said there were many gods. This is false, but yet not wholly false: there is at least one God. And if someone said that there is no God, we must consider the judgment in its connection. The judgment is false, but in its form it may be in part true and may have specious grounds. A man can never really make his own understanding believe such a thing. Because no sensible proofs can be given him that would satisfy all his objections, or because he finds insufficient evidence in those proofs which he knows, he begins to doubt, and afterwards to deny; but he will never prove that there is no God.",["Restated the closing sentences more smoothly; \"Er kann niemals seinem Verstände etwas weiß machen\" is idiomatic (\"he can never impose upon his own understanding\"), and I have rendered it by sense, supplying \"such a thing\".","\"begatten\" (literally \"copulate\") rendered \"couple themselves\"; \"Erkenntnißkräffte\" = powers of knowledge.","OCR \"Aväre\" corrected to \"wäre\". \"in der Verbindung\" / \"in der Form\" are obscure; rendered literally, sense doubtful.","\"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility; \"Verstand\" = understanding."]],"philippi-s31-014":["Suppose that man judged wholly falsely: then the understanding would depart entirely from its laws. In that case we could never place any trust in the understanding or appeal to it. In every case where there is error, something special and extraordinary occurs. If we were to suppose that a body denser than air could rise into the air, we should be uncertain in our whole physics. So it would be with all knowledge if we assumed that the understanding could deviate from its laws.",["\"dichterer\" (modern \"dichter\") = denser."]],"philippi-s31-015":["I briefly repeat:",[]],"philippi-s31-016":["A mode of knowledge is wholly true if there is nothing false in it.",["\"Erkenntniß\" here rendered \"mode of knowledge\" since it is a countable item."]],"philippi-s31-017":["Partly true: (1) when not all the truth of the question is to be found in it; for example, someone who measures the height of a mountain and says that the mountain cannot be under 100 rods. His statement is true, but not in respect of the whole question. Arithmetical and geometrical approximations are of the same kind.",["\"Quaestion\" kept as \"question\"; \"Ruthen\" = rods (a unit of length)."]],"philippi-s31-018":["(2) Because falsity is to be found in it.",[]],"philippi-s31-019":["In the first kind of partly true modes of knowledge there is nothing false, but only a *defectus* [deficiency] of the required quantity of truth; in the other kind there is something actually false. Every judgment is an act of the understanding; hence it is impossible that a judgment should not take place according to the laws of the understanding.",["\"wireklich\" corrected to \"wirklich\"."]],"philippi-s31-020":["Error is a mixed effect of the understanding and of sensibility. If there were no sensibility, the understanding would not err. No human power, and therefore the understanding also, can deviate from its laws; otherwise it would not act. No judgment can be wholly false; indeed nothing can be. For [if it were,] the understanding would have judged or acted, and yet not have acted according to its laws, that is, it would not have acted at all. If we therefore know that no judgment can be wholly false, we must search out the gold of truth from beneath the sweepings of what is false and separate it, and not throw away both together.",["Supplied \"[if it were,]\" to clarify the argument.","OCR \"Kraff t\" corrected to \"Kraft\" (power)."]],"philippi-s31-021":["The growth of truth has been greatly hindered by throwing out, all together, the truth that was mingled with errors. The participating understanding [*der theilnehmende Verstand*] is necessary in logic also, which holds that the criterion of truth and falsity is decided and determined not by the particular (private) understanding but by the universal understanding.",["The relative clause \"der da glaubt\" is awkward; I have made \"which holds\" refer to the participating understanding's principle. Sense somewhat uncertain.","\"priuat\" printed so in the source; rendered \"private\"."]],"philippi-s31-022":["If one man contradicts the judgment of another, the latter should not at once contradict him in turn; they should not both look only to their own grounds and quarrel. Both may be right, for neither has judged wholly falsely. One should therefore put oneself in the standpoint of the other and examine the proposition from the point of view from which the other regards it. If, then, someone contradicts me, I shall put myself in his position and no longer be obstinate about my proposition. For if another believes that I err, I too must be uncertain of it and doubt. When a man sees the judgments of others in common agreement against him in a judgment of the senses, he himself doubts his senses. For example: when a crowd of people are looking at an aurora borealis, and I come up too but can perceive nothing, I shall sooner doubt my eyesight than the judgment of so many who oppose me.",["\"trozen\" (trotzen) rendered \"be obstinate\"; \"Gesicht\" = eyesight."]],"philippi-s31-023":["All conflict is a phenomenon [*Phänomenon*]. When I become aware that another's judgment contradicts mine, I must look to the ground of how it comes about that men, who all conform to the universal laws of the understanding, contradict one another.",["OCR \"WTenn\" corrected to \"Wenn\"."]],"philippi-s31-024":["Here I must not be guided by my own judgment, for truth is not decided by a private judgment. It takes great acumen to put oneself in another's place. If, then, one gets into a dispute with someone, one should concede to him who opposes our judgments; thereby one makes him well disposed, and puts oneself in a position to seek the truth with common effort. This is really the only logical means of seeking the truth: to hold one's own judgment together with the judgments of others and then bring them into agreement. One must never hold another's judgment to be wholly false. Scholars who dispute with one another and at times contradict each other do so not with the intention of annihilating the other's judgment and exalting their own, but both wish to bring their understanding into agreement. If someone believes the other to be wholly in the wrong, why does he dispute with him? He is mad who reasons with the mad. [There is also] the appraisal of a writing, in which one places oneself in the author's standpoint, separates the degree of truth that is there from the falsity, and seeks to increase the truth. That is the logical and moral duty, and it is useful. In this way one can read writings with profit. Isolated modes of knowledge can be wholly true, for example in higher geometry. But systems, for example philosophical ones, are not at all wholly true. A hard book, a hard evil; and there it takes effort to sift out what is true.",["The sentence \"Die Beurtheilung der Schrifft, indem ...\" has no finite verb; I supplied \"[There is also]\" in brackets.","\"Ein schwer Buch, ein schwer Uebel\" is a proverbial play on the old saying \"ein großes Buch, ein großes Übel\"; rendered literally.","OCR \"Kraff t\" corrected to \"Kraft\"; \"auszusichten\" read as \"auszusichten\" (sift out)."]],"philippi-s31-025":["In philosophical matters one must always make improvements. Wolff wrote too much. Crusius is so stubborn as to be unwilling to improve what he wrote in his youth. Oh, we all err, and is it not more praiseworthy when, after attaining better insight, one changes and corrects one's opinion? Resignation of one's own honour is a great touchstone of a lover of truth.",["OCR \"0\" read as \"O\"; \"Wolf\" is Christian Wolff."]],"philippi-s32-001":["On the Exactness of Knowledge.",["\"Genauigkeit\" rendered \"exactness\"; the sense is that of adequacy to purpose, as the next passage shows."]],"philippi-s32-002":["A mode of knowledge is exact in so far as the degree of truth is sufficient for the purpose. Even if not all the truth that is required for the object is contained in a mode of knowledge, there may nevertheless be in it as much truth as the purpose requires. Thus, for example, a man may have an exact knowledge of his duties, although not everything is to be found in it that would be required of the moral propositions. The survey of an estate is exact even if a rod is missing.",["OCR \"Objeet\" read \"Objekt\", \"rnoralischen\" \"moralischen\", \"Avürde\" \"würde\", \"eüie\" \"eine\".","\"Landguts\" rendered \"estate\"."]],"philippi-s32-003":["The Dutch are too scrupulous in drawing up plans of fortifications. They go down to cubic inches, although such accuracy does not belong to the purpose at all.",["\"Accuratesse\" rendered \"accuracy\"."]],"philippi-s32-004":["Modes of knowledge can be exact *comparative* [comparatively]. A mode of knowledge is entitled exact chiefly with respect to the degree of truth.",["\"comparatiue\" is Latin adverb in German text; rendered \"comparatively\" with the Latin kept in italics."]],"philippi-s32-005":["Exactness [*Genauigkeit*] is opposed to incorrectness [*Unrichtigkeit*]. A mode of knowledge is incorrect not in so far as there is no correctness in it at all, but in so far as it contains untruth.",["OCR \"ahe Richtigkeit\" silently read as \"alle Richtigkeit\"; the sentence is rendered as \"no correctness at all\".","Richtigkeit = correctness, to keep it distinct from Genauigkeit = exactness."]],"philippi-s32-006":["In many cases we must be content with the *prope vero* [what comes near to the true], on which Hanau, Professor in Danzig, has written.",["The gloss in square brackets is mine, supplied to give the sense of the Latin.","\"Hanau\" is as printed; the Danzig professor is presumably Michael Christoph Hanow (Hanov). The name is not corrected here."]],"philippi-s32-007":["Coarse knowledge is opposed to fine knowledge, not to exact. Exactness concerns correctness, that is, the degree of truth required for the purpose; coarseness, however, concerns not the object, [that is,] the truth, but the form. A mode of knowledge is coarse in so far as it is very sensible [*sinnlich*]. What the senses teach us is grasped quite comfortably [*gemächlich*]. The more a mode of knowledge contains truths of reason, the finer it is. The common man has coarse knowledge of God and divine things. Coarse or sensible modes of knowledge may nevertheless be useful, and also true. Sensible modes of knowledge may be true *quoad materiam* and coarse *quoad formam*. Just because modes of knowledge are coarse, they are intuitive [*anschauend*] and impressive, but empty of all concepts of the understanding.",["Supplied \"that is\" in brackets to make plain that \"die Wahrheit\" stands in apposition to \"Object\".","*gemächlich* (comfortably, with ease) is a choice; it could also be \"conveniently\".","*quoad materiam* / *quoad formam* = as to matter / as to form; kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s33-001":["§104",[]],"philippi-s33-002":["Dogmatic and historical truths are distinguished. [We here understand by truths, with the author, true modes of knowledge.] Modes of knowledge, in so far as they do not arise in us through the understanding and reason, nor according to the laws of the understanding, are historical: for example, natural description and geography; and philosophy itself can be known historically, if one does not proceed according to the laws of the understanding. We here consider history in respect of matter, that is, of the object, and not in respect of form, or of the *modus cognoscendi* [mode of knowing]. Historical modes of knowledge are distinguished from the intellectual only by their form. Socrates accordingly said that he was only the *obstetrix auditorum suorum* [midwife of his hearers]. Historical modes of knowledge, however, are distinguished from dogmatic in respect of matter. Dogmatic knowledge of truth has for its object the universal character of things; historical truth has for its object *actualia quae sunt obiecta sensuum* [actual things, which are objects of the senses]. Every event is historical; all propositions are dogmatic. Theoretical physics is dogmatic; the description of natural events is historical. Here \"dogmatic\" is contradistinguished from the historical; otherwise it is also opposed to the sceptical. In that [latter] sense a dogmatic mode of knowledge means a universal and decisive one, and a sceptical, on the other hand, a universal mode of knowledge that is problematic. Every universal mode of knowledge by reason is entitled a *dogma*. To judge dogmatically is to judge decisively; to judge problematically is [to judge] where one sets problems [*Aufgaben*]. Dogmatic propositions are found in morals, religion, etc., etc. Metaphysics, as the organon of pure modes of knowledge by reason, and logic, as the organon of modes of knowledge in general, are not supposed to contain dogmatic truths. The greatest dispute among the ancients was that concerning dogmatic propositions, partly as to what they are, partly as to whether they are certain. The old Platonic school contended for the certainty of the *dogmata*; the more recent Academics maintained that *dogmata* have no certainty.",["The square-bracketed sentence is in brackets in the source; carried over as printed.","Restated a few clauses for clarity; \"in that [latter] sense\" is supplied for \"Da\". \"Dogma heißt\" rendered \"is entitled a *dogma*\".","\"Aufgaben\" (tasks, problems) rendered \"problems\"; the clause \"heißt da man Aufgaben giebt\" is elliptical and the supplied \"[to judge]\" is mine.","The statement that metaphysics and logic \"sollen nicht dogmatische Wahrheiten enthalten\" looks odd in the context (perhaps a slip by the note-taker); translated as it stands.","\"Erkenntnisse ... entspringen sind\": the transcript omits a finite verb after \"entspringen\"; I treat it as a participle with \"sind\"."]],"philippi-s33-003":["The author entitles a multitude of connected dogmatic propositions a doctrinal edifice [*Lehrgebäude*], or system; but there are also other systems that do not consist of *dogmata*. For all systems of knowledge are either rational or historical. Hübner's geography is the first geographical system. Wolff made the first complete mathematical system. A dogmatic system, then, is a connection of various truths of reason that cohere through one ground. Thus there are doctrinal edifices of philosophy, of morals, of the influence of the soul upon the body, etc., etc. But besides the dogmatic systems there are still others. The question is: are systems necessary in philosophical knowledge? Maupertuis, an author, says that it is a madness to seek systems. For such a man contrives to derive everything from one ground, and afterwards looks not at nature but at his own chimera. And however many false consequences he may afterwards see, he still will not overturn the system he has laboriously built. Yet although doctrinal edifices do harm in this way, they are nevertheless serviceable for distinctness and for correct insight into the connection of truths. Whoever believes that he can know truths singly deceives himself. In a doctrinal edifice I must begin with the whole, with the principal concept with which the extension of knowledge is connected, and not with the parts. For example, in geography everything comes down to knowing the globe. The plan for the whole must be drawn up beforehand; afterwards it can be filled in with the parts. Man strives by nature after doctrinal edifices. The ideal, or the whole, comes first, and only in the whole can the parts be thought. Encyclopaedias are such systems or outlines, from which we learn how modes of knowledge cohere and how they support one another in making a whole. If someone had known how to draw the circumference and outline of metaphysics, it would already have been very perfect. But that is what has been lacking; it has not been surveyed as a whole. Systems are nothing contemptible. But not every structure is a system, nor every heaping together in which an author begins with particular propositions that seem to him brilliant, and then, suppressing all the doubts that arise in him, so as not to have laboured in vain, goes on and glues a whole together; not every such patchwork is a system. That is not the way to ideas. The plan must come from a distinct principal idea, not from the parts. Gaps and empty compartments will indeed remain, but that does no harm. It seems as if there were now systems of almost all the sciences; but doctrinal edifices are still very much wanting, such as would contain merely the method by which truth can be discovered and tested. And that is what would be most necessary.",["Restated the first sentence (\"nennt ein Lehrgebäude, System eine Menge …\") as \"entitles a multitude … a doctrinal edifice, or system\"; *Lehrgebäude* is kept in brackets at first mention.","The long sentence on \"nicht jede Zusammenhäufung …\" is irregular in the German; I kept its content but recast it as two clauses joined by a semicolon.","OCR slips silently corrected: \"Sj'stemen\", \"S3^steme\" (Systeme), \"wül\" (will), \"allem\" (allein), \"Begrif\" (Begriff).","\"Wolf\" is rendered \"Wolff\" (Christian Wolff); \"Hübner\" is Johann Hübner. \"Hirngespinst\" = chimera.","\"aparten Hauptidee\" rendered \"distinct principal idea\"; \"aparte\" could also be \"separate\".","\"Dogmatibus\" rendered by the English plural *dogmata*; \"Lehrgebäude welche nur bloß die Methode enthalten\" is a little compressed; I took \"Lehrgebäude\" as the subject of \"fehlen\"."]],"philippi-s34-001":["§106",[]],"philippi-s34-002":["The author distinguishes aesthetic truth from logical. Something is aesthetically true and yet not logically true, namely when it is true in the appearance [*Erscheinung*].",[]],"philippi-s34-003":["\"The sun dips into the ocean\" is true according to laws of sensibility, but not logically, not objectively. According to aesthetic rules that is universal which one sees only in many [cases]. According to aesthetics that is not true which is remote from sensibility.",["\"[cases]\" is supplied: the German has only \"in vielen\" (in many)."]],"philippi-s34-004":["Something is logically true and yet not aesthetically. It is especially to be noted that there are beauties that are greater than nature gives, so that one can think an object, in every kind, as perfect as it never is in nature, and perhaps cannot be. These beauties arise, then, precisely through one's departing from what is logically true. The painters Michael Angelo and Raphael render the bones undulating, with gentle curves, though bones must be pointed. Such things are ideal beauties, and the ideal is not drawn according to truth but according to taste. Taste aims at comfort [*Gemächlichkeit*] and does not regulate itself by the consequences that will follow upon it. The beauties of nature are bound up with essential usefulness and have regard to the purpose. Taste, when it draws the ideal, has no regard to usefulness, to the purpose. The image of the Apollo at Rome is drawn contrary to nature, which looks to the purpose and use of bodies, but it is drawn perfectly according to taste.",["*Gemächlichkeit* (ease, comfort) is a choice; \"agreeableness\" is also possible.","\"Bild des Apollo\" rendered \"image\" (the Apollo Belvedere, evidently).","\"Michel Angelo\" kept in the transcript's spelling, in the English form \"Michael Angelo\"."]],"philippi-s34-005":["As the thing actually is, so it seems to be best of all. Our taste is formed according to objects and takes them as models. *Nihil materiale est in intellectu quod non antea fuerat in sensu* [nothing material is in the understanding which was not previously in sense], says Aristotle. But when artists of taste have already brought in all the conditions of nature, they then range beyond them with their imagination [*Einbildungskraft*]. What promotes the extravagance of the imagination is beautiful. So Hogarth has remarked that the Apollo in Rome excites such admiration for the reason that he has long legs. The artist has happily ventured to go beyond nature.",["OCR \"bat Hogarth\" silently read as \"hat Hogarth\".","The Latin is as the transcript has it (with *materiale*, not the usual scholastic \"nihil est in intellectu …\"); the bracketed English gloss is mine.","\"Geschmackskünstler\" rendered \"artists of taste\"; \"Ausschweifung\" as \"extravagance\"."]],"philippi-s35-001":["§109 On Error.",[]],"philippi-s35-002":["That something is false is nothing but a [Vermengung] confusion, that is, that the judgment contradicts the object. But the semblance of false knowledge, by means of which it resembles truth and is easily mistaken for it, is error. It is an analogue of truth. The human understanding can often hold false modes of knowledge to be true, and that is an erroneous judgment.",["'Vermengung' (mixing, confusion) is doubtful in sense here, since the context speaks of contradiction with the object; I render it as 'confusion' and give the German in brackets. The transcript may be corrupt or the word miswritten.","OCR 'Eikcnntniß' silently read as 'Erkenntniß'; 'falsche Erkenntnisse' (plural) rendered 'false modes of knowledge'.","Restated the second sentence slightly: 'vermittelst dessen' clause kept, but 'Es ist ein Analogon' retained as a separate sentence."]],"philippi-s35-003":["We wish here to consider",["Fragment; continues in the following items."]],"philippi-s35-004":["1. how an error is possible.",[]],"philippi-s35-005":["2. how it actually arises, and",[]],"philippi-s35-006":["3. how it is to be prevented.",[]],"philippi-s36-001":["First Consideration.",["OCR 'Bet rachtung' corrected to 'Betrachtung'."]],"philippi-s36-002":["Every force in nature acts according to its own laws. There is no substance whose effect would not accord with its force. It is contradictory that the effect should conflict with the laws of an acting force, since otherwise it could not arise at all. Now if the understanding acts and itself never deviates from its laws, it does not deviate at all; and yet an erroneous judgment is precisely that which deviates from the laws of the understanding.",["'kerne' read as 'keine'; 'Krafft' rendered 'force' here (Kraft), the same word as 'power' in later items, kept as 'force' for the physical analogy.","The final sentence is the paradox the next item resolves; I have joined it with a semicolon for clarity."]],"philippi-s36-003":["Error does not arise from the understanding; the understanding, when it judges alone, cannot err. If it errs, it must have paired itself with some other, distinct force and so have brought forth such a product. Besides the understanding, the soul has still other active forces. Our imagination readily combines with a representation the images related to it; for example, with the representation of virtue the imagination brings forth honour and whatever else accompanies virtue. It works restlessly; it stupefies the understanding and furnishes it with all sorts of works of its own which the understanding does not want. In this way the understanding produces something that departs from its own rules; but it is not the understanding that departs from them, it is merely deceived by the resemblance. For example, shape and figure are found only in intuition; yet the imagination presents to us God as an old man. The understanding becomes a plaything of the imagination, and this then combines concepts according to its laws of sensibility, which are directly opposed to the laws of the understanding. Wit acts according to rules other than those of the understanding. By wit a thing is brought under a concept with which it has only a resemblance. The resemblance must guide us in inventing. Wit supplies us with materials.",["OCR 'Kniffte' read as 'Kräfte'; 'Verstände' as dative of 'Verstand'; 'Begrif' modernised to 'Begriff'.","'Einbildung'/'Einbildungskraft'/'Imagination' all rendered 'imagination'.","'Bilder' rendered 'images'. 'Witz' rendered 'wit' (a faculty of discovering resemblances).","Divided a few long sentences; 'Z.E.' expanded as 'for example'. The sentence 'Dadurch bringt der Verstand ...' is slightly irregular; smoothed."]],"philippi-s36-004":["Inclination and aversion deal with concepts partially, not as the cold understanding combines them according to its laws; rather they combine concepts according to the feeling of the agreeable and disagreeable. Error, therefore, does not arise from the understanding, for the understanding cannot depart from its laws. Truth is the agreement of knowledge with the laws of the understanding. Bodies fall according to the *leges lapsus gravitatis* [laws of the fall of gravity] which Galileo found in empty space. A raindrop therefore falls almost always uniformly: as much as gravity presses it downward, so much does the air resist. No body, then, deviates of itself from its laws, but only through the admixture of other forces; and so it is with the understanding. And as a body that is driven by two opposed forces takes the path of neither force, but moves at an angle along the diagonal, so too the understanding then takes a middle course between its judgments and inclination. Error is an *effectus hybridus* [hybrid effect], arising partly from pure sensibility, partly from the understanding mixed with sensibility. To regard mountain-tops as clouds is a judgment of pure sensibility. If we had a pure understanding unmixed with sensibility, we should not err.",["OCR 'Kläfften', 'Kräff te', 'Kraff t' read as 'Kräfte/Kraft'; 'lufftleeren' as 'luftleeren'.","'Partheilichkeit' rendered 'partiality'; 'schalten' as 'deal with'.","'Directum' (as a noun, direct course) is not rendered separately here; see the next item.","Gloss in brackets on the Latin terms is mine."]],"philippi-s36-005":["Second Consideration.",[]],"philippi-s36-006":["Errors do not arise from the limits and weakness of the understanding. From these arises only ignorance, not error. If a force that moves a body diminishes, the body will not on that account deviate from its *directum* [direct course]; it will merely move slowly. Error requires something positive, not a deficiency. With a small understanding one can know little, but what one knows is good. If it is possible to prevent error, it is to be done by choosing for oneself few modes of knowledge, but those quite correct. That is better than having a great but uncertain knowledge. How is the positive ground of error to be discovered? In every judgment [for only there are truth and error] we must consider our understanding in isolation, remove all other powers of the mind, such as habit, imagination, inclination, and then judge the matter through the pure understanding. For it is only these powers of the mind, which creep into our judgments and guide the understanding by their laws, that bring it about that we do not know the truth. If a concept occurs which the understanding needs, the imagination brings forward another, and the understanding thinks that it belongs with it. I must therefore see whether in my judgment I have not combined concepts merely because they were formerly more often coupled together. I must see whether a concept in my soul arises first from experience or from the pure understanding. I am conscious only of my own experience. I, as I know myself through inner sense alone, am my soul. I, as I know myself through outer and inner senses, am a human being. I, as I know myself merely through the outer senses, am my body. I must inquire where my understanding has taken the concept of the soul, and generally where the soul gets the concept. For example, one speaks of the immortality of the soul. Much may have entered into the concept from habit, because we accept much on credit, and because people who doubt it are described to us in very unfavourable terms. But in judgments all partiality must be set aside. Both sides are indeed equally important; yet when we investigate philosophically we must act as if it did not concern us at all, without any partiality. In actions we may take sides; but speculation must always be free. Understanding and the senses, by themselves alone, never err.",["OCR 'wemg' read as 'wenig'; 'allem' (twice) read as 'allein'; 'Directum' left in Latin as a noun, doubtful in sense.","'Ich wie ich mich ...': rendered 'I, as I know myself'. The sentence 'Ich bin mir nur meiner eigenen Erfahrung bewust' is left as written.","'zwar sind sie gleich wichtig' is obscure (antecedent unclear: probably the two opposing opinions); I supplied 'Both sides' in a resolution of judgment, and 'indessen' as 'yet'.","'Gemüthskräffte' rendered 'powers of the mind'; the bracketed remark on truth and error is Kant's own (the brackets are in the text).","Divided several long sentences."]],"philippi-s36-007":["But I also never judge through the understanding alone, or through the senses alone, but always through both mixed together.",["OCR 'allem' read as 'allein'."]],"philippi-s36-008":["The horizon of our understanding seems at times to be clouded over, which arises from the state in which I then find myself.",["OCR 'bisweiien' read as 'bisweilen'."]],"philippi-s36-009":["The subjective conditions which promote our thinking are reckoned to sensibility.",["'subiective' modernised."]],"philippi-s36-010":["An inner sense always belongs to every distinction of the true from the false.",["Initial 'j' is an OCR stray mark, omitted."]],"philippi-s36-011":["When the effect of sensibility is held to be an effect of the understanding, error arises.",[]],"philippi-s36-012":["Much harm has arisen from the practice of frightening people and threatening them into accepting propositions without speculating about them, and without being convinced of them through the understanding. This is done especially with propositions that bear upon religion, because it is feared that speculation will exert an influence upon practice. A philosophy of this kind, in which one seeks to stun another into giving blind assent, is of no use either to the understanding, or to practice, or to religion. We must judge calmly and in cold blood, and inquire into the truth of the grounds. It is a source of many errors that we do not speculate about propositions of which we could convince ourselves through the understanding. Suppose I find no decision in the matter; even so, that will not flow into my actions. It is not possible for anyone to live according to metaphysical speculations. Speculation can never do harm. For if one finds the truth, that is uncommonly convincing; if one does not find it, speculation will not govern our actions. On the other hand, the terror and the ban by which the understanding is forced to turn back on the spot is a great fault, since one is compelled by servitude to arrive at what one might have reached through the noble impulse of the understanding. If a man never knows [erkennt] that a God is possible, all his rules and duties are already in order. For in the practical sphere the understanding already has the possibility as a great incentive. Man will act not according to speculations but according to prudence.",["OCR corrected silently: 'Nachtheü' = Nachtheil; 'Phüosophie' = Philosophie; 'einf Hessen' = einfließen; 'Sclaverey' kept as servitude.","'Solche Philosophie da man ...' restated: 'da' read as 'in which'; the long sentence is kept but smoothed.","'auf dem Fleck wieder zurückzukommen' rendered 'to turn back on the spot'; the sense is doubtful (possibly 'to return to the same spot').","'Praxin/Praxi' (Latin forms) rendered simply 'practice'.","'in einer Reihe' rendered 'in order' (sc. in a series, i.e. already settled); the sense is somewhat uncertain.","'Klugheit' = prudence."]],"philippi-s37-001":["§ 111",["OCR reads 'Hl'; the number 111 is a conjecture from the sequence (§ 113 follows) and is doubtful."]],"philippi-s37-002":["There is no unavoidable error at all. He who does not judge cannot err. He who treats matters problematically, which is common and no art at all, likewise cannot err. It is not good to avoid the most agreeable sciences for that very reason. Nevertheless the wise man must leave some matters *in suspenso*. If my actions stand in no relation to a question, then one sets speculation aside; for example, whether the final end of creation is happiness. On the condition that one is required to judge, an error can become unavoidable. Such hypothetical error occurs in England in law, where the judge must pronounce and the defendant must confess or not.",["'artigsten' rendered 'most agreeable' (literally 'nicest, most charming'); a choice.","*in suspenso*: 'in suspense', left in Latin.","'im Rechten' rendered 'in law' (in legal proceedings)."]],"philippi-s37-003":["All criteria of truth are either objective or subjective; [the latter] contain, in and for themselves, no ground why something is true or not true.",["The relative 'welche' has an unclear antecedent; I take it to refer to the subjective criteria and supply 'the latter' in brackets. The sentence may be corrupt or abbreviated.","Semicolon introduced to divide the sentence."]],"philippi-s38-001":["§ 113",[]],"philippi-s38-002":["A mode of knowledge is entitled manifestly false if its falsity is manifest to others, though it is hidden from me. If one believes that the falsity of a mode of knowledge must be manifest to the average degree of reason, it is manifestly false.",["'Erkenntniß' rendered 'mode of knowledge' as the method prescribes. The second sentence is awkward; I keep its sense without supplying more."]],"philippi-s38-003":["In objects of reason and morals we have a highest model, the ideal of the greatest perfection, by which we judge them all. Objects of experience are estimated by a mean measure drawn up in thought. Man and his beauty are estimated by the mean magnitude. *Aequatio temporis*: one takes the day on which, if all days are assumed equal, just as much time results as when they are assumed as unequal as they are and are added together. Prosperity [Wohlstand] is the mean between rich and poor. The mean understanding is as follows: if one assumed all men equal in understanding and added them together, the sum would be equal to the sum of the understanding unequally distributed among men, since what one lacks the other has in excess. No one can describe the middle path. Cicero says that one who has 100,000 sesterces is rich. If he has less, is he still rich? In the case of mean understanding, he who has less is entitled stupid, and he who has more deserves praise. It is a condition that can be neither praised nor blamed. Where else should man get the measure of beauty? Only from the mean measure.",["'Model' = Modell, rendered 'model'; 'Mittelmaaß' = 'mean measure'; 'Mittelverstand' = 'mean understanding'.","'Wohlstand' rendered 'prosperity'; the German is kept in brackets.","The sentence on the Mittelverstand is restated slightly for clarity; 'ungleich unter die Menschen vertheilt ist' kept as 'unequally distributed'.","'Mittelstrasse kann keiner beschreiben' rendered literally 'no one can describe the middle path'; 'Mittel verstand' joined.","'heißt' rendered 'is entitled'."]],"philippi-s38-004":["A judgment is absurd [ungereimt] if it falls grievously below the mean measure. The mean measure one must already know from experience. Not every error that is impossible for the mean understanding, but only an error of an understanding that has been lowered beneath the mean measure, is entitled an absurdity. Hence not every error is an absurdity. But it is very bad to say at once that the other is stupid and has erred absurdly. Every error is a phenomenon, and the gross error, in which one departs altogether from the ordinary laws of the understanding, is to be regarded as a paradox. It is something unnatural, just as everything is unnatural that departs from the ordinary laws of the understanding. And so one must investigate attentively how the error could have arisen, just as in morals one must inquire how vice, which is precisely such a paradox of the will, could have come about. An absurd error is as much a prodigy as a miracle in nature. One can make a man prudent only through the understanding that is in him, and improve him only through the degree of good will that is in him. I must therefore call no one absurd whom I wish to instruct, and no one malicious whom I wish to improve. If Crusius and Wolff seem to me to depart from all ordinary rules and laws, I must examine this as a phenomenon very carefully. If I wish to explain the error, this leads me to modesty; for I must cite how many grounds and how much truth, besides, are true in them.",["'erniedri get' corrected to 'erniedrigt'. 'boßhalft' read as 'boshaft' (malicious).","'Wolf' given as Wolff. 'klug' rendered 'prudent'.","'Nicht jeder Irrthum der durch den Mittelverstand unmöglich ist' is awkward; rendered 'impossible for the mean understanding'.","The final clause ('wie viel Gründe und Wahrheit sonst von ihnen wahr sind') is obscure in the German; rendered closely and left as it is.","*Phänomenon, Paradoxon, Prodigium* rendered as English equivalents."]],"philippi-s38-005":["The way to refute another is to enter into his thoughts, to augment his grounds still further, and then to bring our judgment into agreement with the universal human understanding. This can be done by bringing my judgment into agreement with the particular judgment of each. Then the mode of knowledge becomes universally valid.",["'Erkenntniß' rendered 'mode of knowledge'; the sentence division after 'Verstand' follows the method's allowance."]],"philippi-s39-001":["The fifth section. On learned knowledge and its clearness.",["'Erkenntniß' here rendered 'knowledge' (a singular, collective use). 'Klarheit' = clearness."]],"philippi-s39-002":["§ 115",[]],"philippi-s39-003":["On marks.",["'Merkmale' rendered 'marks' (Latin *notae*)."]],"philippi-s39-004":["A mark is a ground of knowledge. All grounds of knowledge are either 1. external, that is, the grounds of comparing the thing with others; or 2. internal, that is, the grounds of what I know in the things themselves. We can never know external marks with certainty unless we have first known the internal.",["'Erkenntnissgrund' = 'ground of knowledge'. 'erkennen' kept as 'know'."]],"philippi-s39-005":["When I compare things, I compare them either *quoad identitatem* or *quoad diuersitatem* [as to identity or as to diversity].",["English gloss in brackets supplied by me."]],"philippi-s39-006":["That is now entitled a mark which contains the ground of comparison with others. Thus not every determination (*determinatio*) is regarded as a mark, though it may well be one.",["'Bestimmung' = determination, with Latin kept."]],"philippi-s39-007":["Marks are:",["The sentence breaks off; continued in the next passage."]],"philippi-s39-008":["immediate, if in the series of marks subordinated to one another it borders upon another *sine notis intermediis* [without intermediate marks]; mediate, if in the series it borders upon a mark but *cum notis intermediis* [with intermediate marks]. The former are entitled *notae propiores*, the latter *remotiores*. What is not a mark of another mark is *nota proxima* in the regress, or in ascending from the consequence to the ground.",["'cum notis intermediis': the text has 'mit notis intermediis'; I render the Latin 'cum' in italics for consistency, but this is a slight normalisation.","The first clause continues from the preceding fragment (the 'Merkmale sind'); 'es' taken to refer to the mark. The last sentence is obscure; rendered closely."]],"philippi-s39-009":["In the progress [*in progressu*] there is a *terminus a priori*, that is, the concept of the thing, and one *a posteriori*, that is, the thing itself. But marks can also stand coordinated in a series. *Plures notae vnius eiusdemque, quarum vna non est nota alterius sunt coordinatae.* Many children are coordinated. Gold, body, composite, divisible are *notae subordinatae*. But yellow, heavy, hard, fixed (in that it does not evaporate) are coordinated marks of gold.",["The Latin sentence is left untranslated, as in the source; roughly: several marks of one and the same thing, of which one is not a mark of the other, are coordinate.","'Begrif' corrected to 'Begriff'; 'fix da es nicht verfliegt' rendered 'fixed (in that it does not evaporate)'."]],"philippi-s40-001":["§ 117 On negation and affirmation.",[]],"philippi-s40-002":["Every affirmation can be thought by itself; but no negation can be thought except by means of affirmations, for a negation is nothing other than the removal of the positive. It is a pure act [of the mind] that something is not to be posited. The purpose of all negation is the hindrance of position. Not knowing and abstracting are analogues of negation.",["\"Handlung\" is rendered \"act\"; the bracketed \"[of the mind]\" is supplied to complete the sense.","\"Nicht wissen abstrahiren\" is elliptical in the transcript; I read it as \"Nicht wissen [und] abstrahiren\" and have supplied \"and\" silently. The reading is uncertain.","\"Position\" (Position, Setzen) is kept close to the Latin *positio*, in contrast to negation."]],"philippi-s40-003":["The series of *notae sibi subordinatae* has limits. That of the *notae coordinatae* also has limits, but we cannot see all these limits; for otherwise we should have to know all the concepts of the thing, as here, for example, of gold. Leibniz seemed to maintain that between two subordinate marks there is always a third, in accordance with the law of continuity. Cf. Metaphysics.",["OCR \"subordinirtenMerkmalen istioi in dem lege\" is garbled; I read \"subordinirten Merkmalen ... ist, in dem lege [= Gesetz] der Continuität\". The word \"istioi\" is doubtful and I have translated only what the sense allows.","\"Merkmal\" is rendered \"mark\", with *nota* kept in Latin as in the text.","\"vid. Metaph.\" is rendered \"Cf. Metaphysics\" (probably Baumgarten's Metaphysica)."]],"philippi-s40-004":["The genus is a remote mark, the species a proximate mark.",["\"propinr.\" is expanded as *propinqua* (proximate), an abbreviation resolved by judgment. The Latin is otherwise rendered as it stands."]],"philippi-s41-001":["§121",[]],"philippi-s41-002":["The sufficient ground, in the internal [determinations], of the predicates of an absolute being is the *essentia* [essence]. All necessary determinations belong to the essence. The necessary determinations belong to the essence either as grounds (*rationes*), as soul and body, or as consequences, and these are *accidentalia*; for example, in man mortality. It is a necessary determination, but a consequence. Learning is a contingent but still internal determination, and so a *modus*. That man is lord of the animals is a *relatio extraessentialis*. Everything that belongs necessarily to a thing is *ad essentiam pertinens*. What belongs to the essence as a necessary ground is an *attributum*.",["The first Latin sentence is garbled by OCR (\"praedicatorum entis absoluter um\"); I read it as *praedicatorum entis absolute internorum*, bracketing \"[determinations]\" as supplied. The reading is doubtful.","\"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Wesen\" = essence, with *essentia* kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s41-003":["Metaphysics distinguishes the real from [what pertains to] concepts. That which contains the first ground of everything necessary in the concepts we have of the thing is the logical essence. The first ground of what is in the thing itself, of which I have a concept, is the real essence. We know the logical essence in respect of all things, for it is the first fundamental concept. But of the real essence we can know nothing at all. Even if we know what is contained in the concept, we cannot say that it is so also in the thing. This, however, is to be noted:",["\"das reale von Begriffen\" is elliptical; I supplied \"[what pertains to]\" to make the contrast with the logical essence plain.","\"Begrif\" (OCR spelling) rendered \"concept\"; \"Grundbegrif\" as \"fundamental concept\"."]],"philippi-s41-004":["1. Of a thing which is given to us through experience as an object we can never know the essence. For the thing is given outside us.",[]],"philippi-s41-005":["2. But if something is given to us through pure reason, we can know the real essence.",[]],"philippi-s41-006":["3. If I myself invent essences, as Leibniz did the monads, then I can know such essences; for they are indeed my own work.",["\"erkennen\" here rendered \"know\", in keeping with the method's \"knowledge\" for *Erkenntnis*."]],"philippi-s41-007":["Thus only in arbitrary and pure concepts do I know the real essence, not in objects of experience. Not a single atom, not a single drop do I there know according to its essence.",["\"willkührlich\" rendered \"arbitrary\" (concepts made at will)."]],"philippi-s41-008":["In empirical concepts we shall never find even the logical essence. We cannot know things according to the essence of things, but only through concepts.",[]],"philippi-s41-009":["Why do we seek the logical essence? When I receive a concept, I go back in the understanding to the first grounds. That is the law of reason. I look for the essential constituents of the concept. Then I reduce the concept, as it were, *ad minores terminos*, like a fraction. Why? So that my concept may be brought wholly to the smallest number, to the elements of the grounds. Reason seeks simplicity and proportion. I look for the grounds, and subordinate their consequences to them. This series of subordinated elements is the essence. Into the inner essence of nature no human understanding can penetrate.\n\nIf I wish to know the essence of a thing in nature, it is not enough that I reduce the concept *ad minores terminos*, in order to give the soul power of comprehension and make room for it (for the simpler the concept, the more easily the soul grasps it, and for that reason we seek to derive the phenomena from the smallest grounds); I must also seek, by repeated experiences, to know everything in the thing. But since experiences of a thing never come to an end, because my limits hinder me in this, no single essence of a thing can be known. Indeed, of countless things we do not know even the logical essence. We also seek only the logical essence. Newton too wanted to spy out the real essence; but a scholar has shown that this was impossible. Because the real essence is the ground of all the appearances that one sees, it cannot be understood; for from the effect I cannot comprehend the ground.",["I divided the long second sentence and placed the parenthesis (\"Denn je einfacher ...\") in brackets-free parentheses; the German's \"sondern\" is left hanging after it, and I resolved it as \"[it is not enough ...]; I must also\".","\"Phänomena\" is kept as \"phenomena\"; \"Erscheinungen\" is rendered \"appearances\".","\"Faßungskräfte\" rendered \"power of comprehension\"; \"Seele\" rendered \"soul\".","OCR \"kami\" read as \"kann\"; \"Vernunfft\" normalised."]],"philippi-s41-010":["In the following sections the author speaks of distinctness. Why did he put this off until now, and not introduce it at once at §10, after the example of Wolff? He has perfect reason. No distinctness arises without judgment; the treatment of judgment must therefore come first.",["\"Wolfen\" read as Wolff (genitive/dative form); \"§§.\" rendered \"sections\"."]],"philippi-s41-011":["Obscure modes of knowledge are those of which we are not conscious. Wolff says: either we are conscious of the modes of knowledge, or not conscious. How can Wolff say: we have modes of knowledge and know that we do not have them?",["\"Erkenntnisse\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\" per the method."]],"philippi-s41-012":["Sulzer has written about obscure representations.",[]],"philippi-s41-013":["Not all distinctness arises *per analysin*. Only in concepts of reason does it arise in this way; in concepts of experience it arises *per synthesin*, that is, through experience, for synthesis is possible only through it. In concepts of reason the whole is given to me, and I seek only *per analysin* to become conscious of the parts; in concepts of experience the parts are given to me. I must add more through experience, in order to obtain a distinct concept.",["\"Vernunftbegriffe\" rendered \"concepts of reason\"; \"Erfahrungsbegriffe\" \"concepts of experience\"."]],"philippi-s41-014":["All distinctness in arbitrary concepts, as in empirical ones, is synthetic. A manifold concept cannot be made distinct *per analysin*, but well may be *per synthesin*.",["\"vielfacher\" rendered \"manifold\"."]],"philippi-s41-015":["Distinctness must be present in a beautiful [*schöne*] mode of knowledge, and there it arises *per synthesin*.",["\"schöne Erkenntniß\" is rendered literally; the sense (aesthetic knowledge) is not supplied."]],"philippi-s41-016":["An obscure concept is not one of which we are not conscious, but one of which we are not immediately conscious, of which we can become conscious through inferences, though not through inner sense. A musician who is playing is not immediately conscious of the playing; he makes a thousand reflections and does not know it. That shows that we are not immediately conscious of many modes of knowledge. When I look at the Milky Way, I am not immediately conscious that these are stars. If a wooden box attracts iron, I can become conscious through inferences that a magnet is in it, but immediately I am not conscious of this. When I see a cheese-maggot, I see all the parts in it, only I am not immediately conscious of this. For suppose I had seen no eyes, no head, no rings; then I should have seen nothing at all.",["\"Reflexions\" rendered \"reflections\"; \"Käsemade\" \"cheese-maggot\"; \"Ringeln\" \"rings\" (segments)."]],"philippi-s41-017":["The microscope and telescope bring nothing new before our sight, but only present distinctly to us what we previously knew only obscurely. In the same way philosophy too is to teach us nothing new, but only to develop and make distinct what already lies in us. Metaphysics and morals lead us back to what we already have, and seek to make us conscious of it.",["\"Microscopium und Telescopium\" rendered \"microscope and telescope\"."]],"philippi-s42-001":["§131",[]],"philippi-s42-002":["The author treats of obscurity and clearness. The investigation of how obscure modes of knowledge arise in us, and the discovery of the means by which obscure modes of knowledge can be made clear, are simple acts which belong to psychology. Logic does not ask whence modes of knowledge become clear; it speaks of modes of knowledge already clear and indicates the procedure.",["\"Handelt der Autor\" is inverted in the German (\"The author treats\"); \"Handlungen\" rendered \"acts\"."]],"philippi-s43-001":["§§132–149. On comprehensiveness [*Ausführlichkeit*].",["\"Ausführlichkeit\" is rendered \"comprehensiveness\"; the word is a choice (alternatives: fullness, exhaustiveness)."]],"philippi-s43-002":["Every distinct concept is clear, but not conversely. Of the Milky Way I have, by the mere sight of unarmed eyes, a clear concept, but not a distinct one. Likewise of hairs. Through full detail [*Ausführlichkeit*] of clearness we learn to distinguish things from all others. Distinctness is presupposed by fullness of detail. In distinctness I cannot know whether my concept is sufficient to distinguish the thing from all others. We shall here make closer inquiry into this.",["'Ausführliche Klarheit' rendered 'full detail of clearness'; 'Ausführlichkeit' is kept as 'fullness of detail' (later also 'completeness', cf. *completudo*), since Kant treats the terms as parallel.","'unbewafneter' read as 'unbewaffneter' (unarmed, i.e. unaided by instruments).","'Deutlichkeit wird von der Ausführlichkeit supponirt' is rendered literally; the direction of the presupposition is as in the transcript and may reflect a slip by the student."]],"philippi-s43-003":["The sufficiency (*sufficientia*) of a distinct concept represents nothing but the sufficiency of the concept to serve as ground of knowledge.",["'Erkenntnißgrunde' rendered 'ground of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s43-004":["The sufficiency (*completudo*, *sufficientia*) of a distinct concept is",["The sentence breaks off; it is continued in the next passage."]],"philippi-s43-005":["1. *interna*, when the marks of a distinct concept, taken together, are sufficient for knowing from them everything that belongs to the thing.",["'Merkmale' rendered 'marks'. 'da' is taken as 'when' (in the sense of 'in so far as')."]],"philippi-s43-006":["2. *externa*, when the marks are sufficient to distinguish the thing from all other things with which it is compared.",[]],"philippi-s43-007":["If I cannot attain *notas internas* of a concept, I shall not attain *notas externas* either. If the concept is for me comparatively complete, we must be content. Absolute fullness of detail is never possible with empirical concepts. Hence all definitions of them are in vain.",["'wrerde' read as 'werde' (OCR slip).","'comparativ complet' rendered 'comparatively complete'."]],"philippi-s43-008":["Completeness [*Completudo*] will therefore rest on this, that the marks are sufficient for the whole concept of the thing and exhaust it. For example: *Materia est extensum impenetrabile iners*. The marks, namely the extended, the impenetrable and the lifeless, here make up the whole concept of matter and exhaust it; hence they are internally *sufficient*. The distinct concept is that whose marks are internally sufficient, that is, full in detail. In seeking here the distinct concept of matter, I have only compared the marks with my concept, to see whether they wholly *exhaust* it, and not whether they suffice to distinguish the concept from all others. Does the latter, then, already follow from the former? Yes, because every concept is distinguished from all others. A concept can never wholly agree with another; for otherwise it would not be another concept; it can, however, agree in part, as *man* and *animal*.",["'Leblose' for *iners* is Kant's student's gloss (literally 'lifeless'); *iners* is otherwise 'inert', cf. 'Trägheit' in the next passages. Kept as in the text.","'Ausführlich' rendered 'full in detail', 'exhauriren' as *exhaust*.","'Ja, weil ...' is a student's compression; translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s43-009":["Thus if marks taken together make up the whole concept, they also serve to distinguish the thing from all others. From *completudo interna* flows *completudo externa*.",[]],"philippi-s43-010":["But how do I find the concept by which I can distinguish the thing from all others? How do I assure myself that the marks of my distinct concept are externally sufficient to distinguish the thing from all others? I cannot know this immediately. Can I know *a posteriori* that my concept is externally complete? No one can do that except him who knows all things. It is therefore impossible for us, who are so greatly limited, to begin with external *completudo*. We cannot know it immediately; we must begin with *completudo interna*. External *completudo*, or the completeness of the concept in its external relation, depends upon that in its internal relation.",["The sentence 'Unmittelbar kann ich es nicht wissen Kann ...' lacks a stop in the OCR; I have supplied one.","'Verhältniß' rendered 'relation'."]],"philippi-s43-011":["The connection of several marks is twofold: they are either 1. coordinated or 2. subordinated. If, then, I see that all the marks in the series of marks can be completed in two ways, I shall also think a twofold fullness of detail.",["'Ausf ükrlichkeit' read as 'Ausführlichkeit' (OCR garble)."]],"philippi-s43-012":["1. The fullness of detail in the series of subordinated marks, and",[]],"philippi-s43-013":["2. that in the series of coordinated marks. We begin our consideration with the coordinated marks, and their fullness of detail is properly entitled *completudo*; that is, the totality of a possible representation of the thing. Suppose I wish to form a distinct concept of body; I must then coordinate as many marks as are needed until together they make up the whole concept. I take extension, impenetrability, inertia, and I see that these marks do not yet exhaust my concept; I add figure, and so they exhaust it. What have I done here? I have added the marks together, coordinated them. I could not take together more marks than these, because they already exhausted my concept. And all these were immediate marks of the thing.",["'heißt' rendered 'is entitled', following the method."]],"philippi-s43-014":["And the subordinated marks, which will they be in the case of body?",[]],"philippi-s43-015":["The mark of body is the composite; the mark of the composite is the divisible; that of the divisible is the contingent; and that of the contingent is that it has a cause distinct from itself. Here is a series of marks in which one is subordinated to another, or in which one is the mark of another. The first mark is an immediate one, but the others are not. Divisibility is not a mark of body, but the composite is. In the series of subordinated marks I can also have a sufficiency of marks, when the marks of a thing which are subordinated to one another are carried out as far as the first, which cannot be derived from anything further. The sufficiency of the subordinated marks, in that they are all not merely added together but completed, is *adaequatio*.",["'Theübare' read as 'Theilbare'.","The last sentence is clumsy in the German ('daß alle nicht zusammengezählt sondern vollendet seyn'); I restated it so that the sense (completed, not merely summed) is plain."]],"philippi-s43-016":["The more coordinated marks there are in a concept, the more is the concept *extensiue distinctior*. Poets and orators bring about distinctness through a multitude of coordinated marks.",["*extensiue distinctior* kept in Latin, as in the source."]],"philippi-s43-017":["The greater the series of subordinated marks, the more *intensive* distinctness there is in the concept.",["Kant's 'intensiue' is kept as 'intensive' in the English."]],"philippi-s43-018":["Every clear mark is a ground of distinctness; there are therefore degrees of extensive distinctness, which are determined by the multitude of coordinated marks. Intensive distinctness also has degrees. The greater the series of subordinated marks, the greater the intensive distinctness. If one wishes to make spring distinct to a Laplander, one coordinates many marks.",["'( iiade' is garbled OCR; read as 'Grade' (degrees), the sense being fairly secure from the context.","'Lappländer' rendered 'Laplander', as in period usage."]],"philippi-s43-019":["Vividness [*Lebhaftigkeit*] of knowledge is its subjective clearness; for example, I take a certain wrongful action. Let it have occurred as it may, it is equally intelligible; but if one says, 'How if it had happened to you?', then the knowledge becomes vivid.",["'Unrechte' read as 'unrechte' (wrongful)."]],"philippi-s43-020":["Objects of nature are never perfectly distinct, because we can never bring the series of coordinated marks to an end. But objects of reason we can know perfectly. There the series of subordinated marks can be brought to an end, and the knowledge is then adequate or complete. Fullness of detail is the sufficiency of the coordinated marks.",["'Gegenstände' rendered 'objects'; 'Vernunfft' as 'reason'."]],"philippi-s43-021":["We have, then, degrees of distinctness; only one must not crowd together too many coordinated marks, lest they hinder one another. To find the balance, taste is required. Poets and orators are extensively distinct. It is not indistinctness or confusion that is the mark of the sensible, as the author and Baumgarten and many others believe. They attain their end not by making things confused but by making them distinct. They need distinctness just as necessarily as the logician, but in another manner. In subordination metaphysics goes furthest, as poetry does in coordination. Distinctness through synthesis is extension; that through analysis is intensity. In the fine sciences, which are directed to the senses, the former, coordination, holds; in the higher sciences of the understanding, subordination holds.",["'the author' is Meier, whose compendium Kant used as his text; 'Autor' is left as in the source.","'noth wendig' read as 'nothwendig'; 'Synthesin' and 'Analysin' rendered 'synthesis' and 'analysis'.","'Ausbreitung' rendered 'extension'; 'Intensivität' as 'intensity'."]],"philippi-s43-022":["Fullness of detail is the totality of the coordinated marks. A concept is therefore complete, or full in detail, when it has all the necessary marks which in their connection make up a *totum* and exhaust the concept.",[]],"philippi-s43-023":["NB. Logic has to do only with necessary marks. For only these are objects of the understanding. Contingent marks, for example learning in man, are objects of experience. If in a concept contingent marks are added besides the necessary ones, the concept is *supercomplet*. It is incomplete when it does not have all the necessary marks.",["'In complet' read as 'Incomplet' (OCR break); *supercomplet* is kept in the source's Latinised form."]],"philippi-s43-024":["Completeness [*Completudo*] involves coordination and the connection of the parts in the whole. The connection between consequences and grounds is *subordinatio*. If I posit a ground, I posit at the same time all its consequences. In coordination, however, the positing of one [mark] does not involve the positing of the rest; these must all be added. For here all the marks must be immediate marks, and not mediate marks, that is, marks of marks.",["'Folgen und Gründe' is rendered 'consequences and grounds'; the order is as in the German.","'eines' and 'das übrige' are completed as '[mark]' and 'the rest' by judgment of the argument."]],"philippi-s43-025":["Extensive distinctness (*extensiue distinctior cognitio*) is entitled vividness [*Lebhaftigkeit*]. [This is so] when a mode of knowledge is, as it were, such as we have of things which we receive through the senses.",["The second sentence has no main clause in the German; the bracketed '[This is so]' is supplied to make it a sentence.","Latin kept in the transcript's spelling ('extensiue')."]],"philippi-s43-026":["But when the marks stand in a subordinated thing [*Sache*], the distinctness is entitled profound distinctness. The greater the series of subordinated marks, the more profound the distinctness. That is *cognitio intensiue distinctior*. The happiness of a country is *extensiue maior* if it has many inhabitants; it is *intensiue maior* if, without increasing the number of inhabitants, the degree of well-being increases in each subject.",["'in einer subordinirten Sache' is doubtful (perhaps 'Reihe' was meant); translated as it stands.","'wenn' in the last clause is supplied with 'it is' for symmetry with the first."]],"philippi-s43-027":["The more profound the distinctness, the further the subordinated marks extend, the drier is the knowledge; the more extensive and vivid it is, the more shallow it is. Toussaint, in his book *Les moeurs*, is vivid, and just on that account shallow.",["The relation between 'je tiefer…' and 'je weiter…' is loosely joined in the German; I have kept it as an apposition."]],"philippi-s43-028":["The senses give us the most extensive knowledge. The understanding gives us profound knowledge. Profound knowledge has the advantage of thoroughness [*Gründlichkeit*].",[]],"philippi-s43-029":["If, in the series of subordinated marks, the distinctness is carried up to the highest mark, it is consummated [*consummirt*].",[]],"philippi-s43-030":["Does completeness [*Completudo*] admit of degrees? Not in the logical sense, but certainly in the aesthetic. There modes of knowledge are made *supercomplet* through contingent marks. Bacon of Verulam has the habit in his writing of saying almost everything twice, in order to make a deeper impression.",["OCR 'Schlifft' corrected to 'Schrift'; 'logischen Verstände' read as 'in the logical sense' (the German 'im logischen Verstande')."]],"philippi-s44-001":["Of the adequation of modes of knowledge.",[]],"philippi-s44-002":["Wolff entitles a concept adequate when it has the second degree of intensified distinctness. If I know a thing through the first immediate mark, that is the first degree; of body, for example, that it is composite. If I obtain new marks from the immediate marks, that is the second degree, and the concept is then adequate. But this is not right. Adequation (completeness) requires the highest degree, and the second degree is not yet the highest.",["'Vollständigkeit' is given as the gloss on *adaequatio* in parenthesis, as in the text."]],"philippi-s44-003":["If I know the marks of a thing clearly, that is the first degree of distinctness; if I know clearly the marks of the marks, that is the second degree. If I know the marks of these marks, that is the third degree of distinctness, and so on, until the mark is the thing itself. Thus *notio consummata* is the highest degree of intensity.",["'Intensivität' rendered 'intensity'."]],"philippi-s44-004":["If in the series of coordinated marks there is completeness [*completudo*], that is, if a concept is exhaustive [*ausführlich*], something could still be added which goes beyond the completeness, namely the consequences that flow from the necessary marks of the thing. To exhaustive concepts that contain all the necessary marks there may still be added contingent marks which flow from those. But these marks are always dispensable. Consequences can always be left out when one has the ground; for if one has the latter, one has the former also.",["OCR 'abf liessende', 'f Hessen' corrected to 'abfliessende', 'fliessen'.","'zufällige Merkmale' is used here for derived consequences; kept as 'contingent marks'."]],"philippi-s44-005":["A is a figure enclosed by 3 straight lines and having 3 angles; this latter makes the concept overcomplete [*übercomplet*]. What is added to a concept beyond completeness is dispensable and to be left out. In the aesthetic realm one seeks to give ease to concepts, and for that reason seeks to take together as much as is only fitting.",["OCR 'üi' read as 'in' (enclosed *in* 3 straight lines, i.e. by them)."]],"philippi-s44-006":["Only the separation of all dispensable and tautological marks that already lie in those given is entitled precision [*Praecision*]. A concept is precise if it is complete and contains nothing superfluous. If a concept lacks nothing for the whole concept of the thing, it is entitled complete; if it has nothing too much, it is entitled precise. It is then commensurate with [*angemessen*] the thing. A garment that is neither too large nor too small is commensurate; it fits. *Conceptus rem adaequat* is what it is then called. Completeness [*Completudo*], exhaustiveness, is the perfection of a concept in which nothing is lacking to it; adequation, commensurateness, is that in which it has nothing too much. A concept that is at once complete and precise is *conceptus obiecto alio adaequatus*, an adequate concept. The adequation of a concept admits of no degrees. A concept cannot be more equal than equal. But according to Wolff adequation admits of degrees.",["OCR 'ldein' read as 'klein', 'derm' as 'denn'; 'nach dem Wolfen' read as 'nach Wolff'.","*obiecto alio adaequatus* is doubtful ('alio' is odd; perhaps 'obiecto adaequatus'); kept as printed."]],"philippi-s44-007":["This is the greatest perfection of concepts in the logical sphere, when they are adequate. Definitions differ from descriptions only in this, that the former have precision and the latter do not.",[]],"philippi-s44-008":["The degrees of distinctness are these:",[]],"philippi-s44-009":["1. That one have clear marks. 2. That they be complete, that there be enough of them. This is exhaustiveness [*Ausführlichkeit*].",[]],"philippi-s44-010":["3. That there be not too many of them. This is precision. The concept is then commensurate with the thing when it attains this kind of distinctness; then it is adequate. Wolff entitles adequation completeness [*Vollständigkeit*], but this does not express it well. Adequation indicates a preceding comparison, and that the concept has been found in it to be equal and commensurate. One might call it commensurateness [*Angemessenheit*]. That is the greatest logical perfection in comparison with the thing. The author speaks of vividness and strength. This belongs to sensible representations. The more abstract modes of knowledge are, the more universal they are, the less vivid they are. Through concrete cases they can become vivid. Thus when a mode of knowledge is considered in general, it has little vividness; but what it lacks in this respect is made good by thoroughness, which is logical perfection. The former [vividness] one also supplies by applying it to concrete cases. Strength belongs to a mode of knowledge in so far as it has a ground of influence upon the powers of the human mind; but abstract concepts have the least strength.",["OCR 'Lebhaff tigkeit', 'smd' corrected to 'Lebhafftigkeit', 'sind'.","'Jene daß man sie…anbringe' is elliptical; I take 'Jene' as vividness, supplied in brackets, and 'sie' as the knowledge. Doubtful.","'der Autor' is Meier, the textbook author."]],"philippi-s44-011":["The question is how distinct modes of knowledge arise. Metaphysics investigates the origin of clear modes of knowledge. Logic makes use of them without asking whence they come. But how do distinct concepts arise? This logic must know. All distinctness arises from analysis, says the author. I say it arises also *per synthesin*. All distinctness arises from marks in a twofold way:",["OCR 'entin springt' corrected to 'entspringt'."]],"philippi-s44-012":["1. By my seeking out, through dissection, the marks that lie in the concept, and making them clear.",[]],"philippi-s44-013":["2. By my adding, *per synthesin*, new marks that did not lie in the concept. If, for example, I wish to form a distinct concept of gold, it is not enough that I analyse the concept which I obtain by the sight of it; I must also search out and represent to myself several marks that did not lie in my concept. Through experiments I shall find that gold is ductile, that it does not evaporate in fire, and so on, which I should not know from the mere sight.",[]],"philippi-s44-014":["To make distinct concepts and to make concepts distinct are different acts. (Wolff in his philosophy makes distinct concepts, but he does not make concepts distinct.) To make distinct concepts is *per synthesin*. To make given concepts distinct is often *per analysin*, and also *per synthesin*.",["The parenthesis is as in the transcript."]],"philippi-s44-015":["Through experience I can make concepts distinct *per synthesin*. Concepts of reason are always made distinct *per analysin*; empirical concepts, *per synthesin*.",["'Vernunfftbegriffe' rendered 'concepts of reason'."]],"philippi-s44-016":["If, then, concepts are given, whether through reason or through experience, and they are to be made distinct, this can be done only *per analysin*. I shall not there make experiments; I merely compare my judgments with the judgments of universal reason.",["'Vernunfft' rendered 'reason'; 'deutlich machen' as 'made distinct' (distinctness, Deutlichkeit).","Latin *per analysin* kept in italics: 'by analysis'."]],"philippi-s44-017":["If I am myself to make distinct concepts, these are my empirical concepts. Suppose, then, that I wished to have a distinct concept of, for example, quicksilver. I should not obtain it by analysing my concept of it; I must institute experiments and seek new marks. I shall then find, for example, that quicksilver dissolves the metals, hardens in the cold, and so forth.",["The first sentence is awkward in the German: 'Wenn ich mir selbst deutliche Begriffe machen soll' is read as the making of concepts from experience, as the next sentence requires; the sense is somewhat smoothed.","'? vir.' is the OCR of the alchemical sign for quicksilver (probably *Mercurius vivus*); rendered 'quicksilver'.","'Merkmale' rendered 'marks'."]],"philippi-s44-018":["If, then, I am to make a distinct concept, I must produce the concept together with its distinctness, the matter together with the form. But if I make a given concept distinct, I make the form anew; I give it the form of distinctness.",["'Materie'/'Form' rendered 'matter'/'form'."]],"philippi-s44-019":["Mathematics never makes confused concepts distinct; it produces arbitrarily invented concepts together with their distinctness. The only concept that is given to it is the concept of space. But this it never defines, and it presupposes that everyone already knows it. So Augustine, when asked what time is, said: it is what you and I well know. All the concepts which mathematics makes distinct in its definitions did not previously dwell in us in a confused state; it invented them, and thus brought forth the concept, along with its distinctness, out of itself.",["Augustine's remark on time is given as in the transcript; the OCR 'wol' is read 'well' (wohl).","'willkürlich erdachte' rendered 'arbitrarily invented'."]],"philippi-s44-020":["If we make for ourselves concepts of the understanding which are drawn from no experience, from immediate intuition, but from reason itself, these are made distinct only *per analysin*. Concepts of the understanding, however, are produced *per synthesin*. Mathematics devises hypotheses for itself. Suppose we wished to draw a line around a point and to call the space which it encloses a circle. What mathematics now infers from this circle is, however, no fiction.",["Text as transcribed seems confused: 'Verstandesbegriffe' in the first sentence is probably meant to cover concepts given, not made; I translate as it stands.","OCR 'Vernunf ft' corrected to 'Vernunft'; 'per analysin / per synthesin' kept in Latin.","'Zirkel' rendered 'circle'; the last sentence is smoothed slightly."]],"philippi-s44-021":["We shall indicate the gradation of concepts. The first degree is to represent something to oneself. There is no lesser degree: *repraesentare*.",["'sich etwas vorstellen' rendered 'to represent something to oneself' (Vorstellung = representation)."]],"philippi-s44-022":["The second is to know [wissen] something, *noscere*: to represent it to oneself with consciousness.",["OCR 'noße' read as *nosse* / *noscere*; the reading is doubtful, and I keep it as *nosse*? No: rendered *nosse* is uncertain, so the transcript's 'noße' is left as *nosse*."]],"philippi-s44-023":["The third is to be acquainted with [kennen] something: to be able to distinguish it in comparison with others; to know with which other thing it is identical and from which it is different. For example, when one recognizes one's relative among a crowd of people. *Nosse*.",["'kennen' rendered 'to be acquainted with'; OCR 'Noße' read *Nosse*."]],"philippi-s44-024":["The fourth is to understand [verstehen] something, to know something distinctly through the understanding. *Intelligere*.",["'erkennen' rendered 'to know'."]],"philippi-s44-025":["The fifth, to have insight into [einsehen] something, is to know something through reason or through inferences. *Per rationem aliquid cognoscere*. For example, many can be acquainted with shells. But to have insight into what the ground is of the enamelling of the calcareous shell, and that, since art produces an enamelling only by means of glass-stones in the fire, the sea can produce this by means of calcareous stars: this insight not everyone has. All mysteries must be understood, otherwise they would not concern us at all, but they cannot be had insight into. Absolute necessity no one can have insight into. Freedom I understand well enough, but I do not have insight into how I act out of myself without anything determining me, when everything else is guided by nature. The sixth degree is to comprehend [begreifen], *comprehendere*: to know something through reason to the degree that is sufficient for the purpose. When I asked a blind man what he would most wish to see, he said a house; for a man, he said, I can already represent to myself, since I can embrace him, but a house I cannot. Light and colour we are acquainted with, but we do not understand them.",["'Emaillation' and 'kalkartige Sterne' are rendered literally; the argument about shells is obscure in the transcript and perhaps corrupt.","'Glaßsteine' rendered 'glass-stones'; 'Sterne' may be an error for something else (e.g. 'Steine'); doubtful.","'Einsicht/einsehen' rendered 'insight / have insight into'; 'begreifen' as 'comprehend'.","'Schlüsse' rendered 'inferences'; the sentence on freedom is lightly smoothed; 'wol' read 'wohl'."]],"philippi-s44-026":["5) When I have insight through reason, I subordinate the marks.",["'5)' stands for the fifth degree."]],"philippi-s44-027":["For example, gunpowder consists of saltpetre, sulphur and charcoal.",[]],"philippi-s44-028":["Whether there are many things we do not understand, through the understanding? Yes, especially in natural theology, for example the eternity of God, the beginning of the world. There we can make for ourselves no concept through the understanding. We proceed only along the thread of analogy. The concept of freedom, etc.",["'Theol. nat.' expanded to 'natural theology'; the question mark after 'Verstand' is in the source."]],"philippi-s44-029":["If one attends too much to the partial representations and accumulates them, the understanding becomes confused by their multitude. Hence the many distinctions contribute more to confusion than to distinctness.",["'Theilvorstellungen' rendered 'partial representations'."]],"philippi-s44-030":["Concepts are relatively sufficient, either",["Breaks off; continued in the next two passages."]],"philippi-s44-031":["1. in some respect, or",[]],"philippi-s44-032":["2. in every respect.",[]],"philippi-s44-033":["The author entitles this highest degree *concipere*, but that indicates only the lowest degree.",["'Der Autor' is Meier, the textbook author."]],"philippi-s44-034":["It comes from habit and practice that a head is inclined to clearness and distinctness. Mathematics contributes much to this.",[]],"philippi-s45-001":["On Certainty.",["'Gewißheit' rendered 'certainty'."]],"philippi-s45-002":["The author speaks of learned knowledge, but we wish to speak of the certainty of knowledge.",["'gelehrte Erkenntniß' rendered 'learned knowledge'."]],"philippi-s45-003":["Only in judgments is a distinction made between what is true and what is certain. Truth is what relates to the object. Certainty is in the subject.",[]],"philippi-s45-004":["There may be sure grounds of truth which convince us to hold our knowledge to be true. But if these grounds do not remove all the doubts and misgivings that might occur to us, our knowledge indeed remains true, but it is not certain. For certainty it is required that there be complete grounds to convince us wholly. Certainty is therefore in the subject; truth in the object. The two differ only in this, that for truth it is enough if grounds for it are present; for certainty, however, it is required that these grounds be capable of producing conviction in a subject.",["OCR 'Zweif el' and 'WTahrheit' silently corrected.","'Ueberzeugung' rendered 'conviction'."]],"philippi-s45-005":["If my knowledge is to be true, it must agree with the object. If I know something through reason and it agrees with the senses, it is certain. Subjective certainty arises from the observation that it agrees with my own and others' modes of knowledge. One is sensibly certain of many universal propositions",["Passage breaks off without a full stop; the following passage begins with '1.', so the sentence may be incomplete.","'Erkenntnissen' rendered 'modes of knowledge'; OCR 'Erkenntmssen' corrected."]],"philippi-s45-006":["1. The greatest certainty or conviction is in mathematics. This is merely a certainty of form. *In abstracto*, however, it is greater than *in concreto*. In every application of mathematical certainty there is still a great deal of sensibility.",["OCR 'derMathematick' separated; 'Sinnlichkeit' rendered 'sensibility'."]],"philippi-s45-007":["It is true that there are inhabitants in the moon, that is, we have grounds for asserting this, but it is not certain, that is, the grounds are not yet capable of convincing every subject. Difficulties can always be raised.",["Kant's example is as transcribed; the 'it is true' is meant in the sense of there being grounds."]],"philippi-s45-008":["Thus men often hold something to be true although it is not certain. If in a piece of knowledge there is not everything that confirms it, I am not certain. For example: no substance can be passive or suffer without at the same time being in activity. No one can show that in a sensible example. It lies in the concepts of suffering.",["OCR 'Hegt' corrected to 'liegt'; 'Leidens' rendered 'suffering'."]],"philippi-s45-009":["We must not hold everything to be evident. Men are inclined to this, especially in propositions which have great moral importance. For example, a proof of the existence of God seems to us evident although it is not. Much more belongs to evidence. Although in the certainty of moral truths there is much that is similar to the mathematical, there is nevertheless a difference.",["'Evidenz' rendered 'evidence'."]],"philippi-s45-010":["Certainty and truth are different with regard to subjective holding-to-be-true.",["'Vorwahrhalten' rendered 'holding-to-be-true'."]],"philippi-s45-011":["If there is a proof for a proposition, then, if it is sufficient, it makes certain. If there are many, it is to be presumed that singly none of them makes certain. If this is so, then taken together they will not produce a greater certainty either. For certainty cannot be increased; for it is the maximum of holding-to-be-true.",["The inference in the middle sentences is as transcribed and somewhat elliptical."]],"philippi-s45-012":["Certainty is a feeling of the human soul.",["'Seele' rendered 'soul' rather than 'mind' (Gemüt), since the German is Seele."]],"philippi-s45-013":["The degrees of holding-to-be-true, which are distinguished by the matter [Sache], are: 1. to know, 2. to believe, 3. to opine. Presumption [Vermuthung] rests on the diversity of space and time, to which our knowledge may be subject. Very much is required for certainty. Practical truths, even if we cannot declare them certain, can suffice to move us to act in accordance with them. Socrates believed in a future life and died in that belief. He himself says that he believes in a future life, not that he knows it.",["\"Sache\" is rendered \"matter\"; the sense may be the object or ground of the holding-to-be-true. Vorwahrhalten is rendered \"holding-to-be-true\".","The sentence on Vermuthung is obscure in the transcript; rendered literally, with \"[Vermuthung]\" shown as the German.","\"Erkenntniß\" in the singular is rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"philippi-s45-014":["There is a belief that is decisive, so that one does not inquire after the contrary and does not think it worth the trouble to examine it. Such a belief produces complete certainty, but, *NB*, practical certainty. Knowing is theoretical certainty.",["\"NB\" is the transcriber's own abbreviation (nota bene), kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s45-015":["Opining stands midway between knowing and believing. It is an incomplete theoretical holding-to-be-true, in so far as I am conscious that it is insufficient. If even the smallest degree of holding-to-be-true is lacking, it is already mere opinion. Belief is contradistinguished from certainty, not from rational knowledge. For otherwise belief alone could have a place in all irrational or historical modes of knowledge. But there is certainty in these. It is, for example, certain that Madrid is the capital of Spain. In belief, however, there is always a kind of uncertainty. If men knew that the rewards for their good conduct were quite certain, their morality would not be so pure. Interest, perhaps, and not the feeling of duty, would then alone move them to virtue.",["OCR \"Demi\" corrected to \"Denn\"; \"Wolverhalten\" read as \"Wohlverhalten\" (good conduct).","\"Erkenntniß der Vernunfft\" rendered \"rational knowledge\"; \"Erkenntnissen\" as \"modes of knowledge\".","\"contradistinguirt\" kept in close English form."]],"philippi-s45-016":["Between belief and knowledge the difference is not one of *completudo* [completeness]. Belief has practical, knowing has theoretical, *completudo*.",["OCR \"theoi'etische\" corrected to \"theoretische\"; \"completudinis\" and \"Completudinem\" are Latin inflected forms of *completudo*, kept in the nominative for clarity."]],"philippi-s45-017":["Belief, as is evident from all that has been said, is a complete practical certainty, though not a theoretical one. Opinion is compared with knowing.",["OCR \"Me^ynung\" corrected to \"Meynung\" (opinion)."]],"philippi-s45-018":["It is remarkable that some logicians have brought into logic an argument *a tuto* or *ab utili*, in which one holds a thing to be true on account of the great utility one has from it, and of the harm that would result if one did not hold it to be true. Such arguments are detrimental to philosophy; they destroy the authority of reason.",["*a tuto* (from safety) and *ab utili* (from utility) kept in Latin; \"es ist besonders\" rendered \"it is remarkable\"."]],"philippi-s45-019":["A mode of knowledge is undeniable if there are no grounds for the contrary opinion, and if it is impossible to contradict it. If a mode of knowledge is so convincing that the other merely will not admit that he is convinced, this is denying. Whoever denies something must have specious grounds [*Scheingründe*], otherwise he is ashamed.",["\"unleugbar\" rendered \"undeniable\"; \"Erkenntniß\" as \"mode of knowledge\" since the sense is of a particular item of cognition."]],"philippi-s45-020":["If I wish to convince someone of a proposition, I must not recite my proofs to him in dictatorial expressions and with an assumed superiority; his self-love is thereby aroused, he will not submit to my pride, and I do not attain my end. I must simply set out the grounds to him in a friendly manner. If he raises an objection, I must look at his objections from the side from which he regards them; I must concede that they are correct under the conditions which he assumes; I must then, however, show him that the conditions are otherwise. Such a procedure, in which one helps to explain the opinion of the other, is advantageous to the investigation of truth.",["OCR \"Selbsthebe\" read as \"Selbstliebe\" (self-love); \"vortheillrafft\" read as \"vortheilhafft\"; \"Yorwahrhalten\"-type slips corrected silently.","The long sentence on objections is kept as a series of clauses divided by semicolons."]],"philippi-s45-021":["My understanding will not submit to any understanding; it wills only to be grounded. Otherwise, in such an action, everything in the soul is resistant.",["\"Seele\" rendered \"soul\", not \"mind\" (Gemüt)."]],"philippi-s45-022":["A mode of knowledge is settled [*ausgemacht*] if it is so constituted that, having been contested, it is completely decided and freed from all further dispute. The circulation of the blood and the Copernican system are settled propositions. Recently Monatessa, a doctor in Padua, who was the last opponent of the Copernican system, has died. One must not be precipitate in this. Sometimes something is settled in one age which was not so in another. There are many things which have never been settled and yet are held to be true. Many propositions of religion are of this kind. In mathematics nothing is settled, for nothing is contested.",["The name \"Monatessa\" is as printed in the OCR and the Akademie text; the identification is doubtful, and I leave it unaltered.","\"ausgemacht\" rendered \"settled\"; \"Lehrgebäude\" as \"system\" (more literally, doctrinal edifice)."]],"philippi-s45-023":["A certain mode of knowledge is entitled thorough [*gründlich*] in so far as all the grounds that can produce certainty are distinctly known. Thoroughness is opposed to shallowness, where everything floats on the top and is not deep, where everything is merely superficial. Democritus says that truth is a deep well.",["OCR \"ohen\" read as \"oben\" (on top). \"Gewißheit\" is rendered \"certainty\"."]],"philippi-s45-024":["A mode of knowledge is deep if the marks in their subordination extend very far. It is extensive [*ausgebreitet*] if the series of coordinated marks extends far.",["OCR \"m ihrer\" read as \"in ihrer\"; \"Ausgehreitet\" read as \"Ausgebreitet\"."]],"philippi-s46-001":["On Assent.",["\"Beyfall\" rendered \"assent\" throughout."]],"philippi-s46-002":["Can one properly say: to give one's assent? Does it rest on our pleasure whether we will give it or not? Is assent, then, an *actus arbitrii* [act of choice]? If it is not, but is a state of holding-to-be-true to which I am constrained by grounds, without my will counting for anything, why then are these expressions used? In the force of the grounds lies the power to extort assent *obtorto collo* [by the scruff of the neck]. Assent is wrested, not given.",["OCR \"gehen\" read as \"geben\"; \"Yorwahrhaltens\" as \"Vorwahrhaltens\".","*actus arbitrii* and *obtorto collo* kept in Latin with English glosses supplied in brackets."]],"philippi-s46-003":["Assent is a positive [mode of] knowledge. Has freedom an influence on assent? Not immediately. Assent must be brought about by grounds which accord with the rules of knowledge and with the will. Mediately freedom has an influence on assent, in that we may be unwilling to examine the grounds. Accordingly, to withhold assent means as much as not to judge at all. Inclination and habit have an indirect influence on assent. Imitation also. The inclination to imitate is greatest in childhood, since a child is unable to use its understanding. The perfection of education also rests on this, that it should early break the habit of imitating.",["\"[mode of]\" supplied, since \"eine positive Erkenntniß\" is elliptical in the transcript; the sense is doubtful.","\"und dem Willen nach gemäß\" is obscure; rendered literally. \"Auch Nachahmung so\" is expanded to \"Imitation also\"."]],"philippi-s46-004":["If by assent the declaration of assent is understood, then it can be given and withdrawn. But here the question is not of the declaration but of the state of the mind, whether it is moved by the grounds to agree or not.",["\"Gemüth\" rendered \"mind\"."]],"philippi-s46-005":["There are certain grounds in respect of which we find ourselves compelled to assent. But in the case of many we can, on the contrary, postpone our assent; at least the withholding of assent is there within our will.",["\"unter unserm Willen\" rendered \"within our will\", i.e. subject to it."]],"philippi-s46-006":["For the examination by means of grounds depends on our will. Assent cannot be obtained otherwise than by insight into the grounds. A professor in Padua, who held stiffly to Aristotle, would not give his assent to the new discoveries of the moons of Saturn. He would not look through the telescope when people wished to convince him of them.",["OCR \"rncht Bejdall\" read as \"nicht Beyfall\"; \"Avollte\" as \"wollte\"; \"Fernglaß\" as \"Fernglas\". The professor's story is historically garbled in the transcript (Galileo's Jupiter moons are usually meant); I translate what the text says."]],"philippi-s46-007":["Assent cannot be demanded except where there is mathematical or empirical evidence. The chief character of the philosopher is that he knows how to withhold his assent. Whoever is quickly convinced, his assent does not last long, and he often finds cause to sing a palinode. One had better remain somewhat longer undecided than go the wrong way or do labour in vain.",["\"Erfahrungsevidenz\" rendered \"empirical evidence\"; \"Palinodie\" kept in the sense of a recantation."]],"philippi-s47-001":["On Prejudice.",[]],"philippi-s47-002":["Introduction. To reflect [*überlegen*] is entitled to compare something with the laws of the understanding and of reason. If the understanding does not compare its judgments with its laws, it accepts them without reflection.",["\"Ueberlegen\" is rendered \"to reflect\" (also \"deliberate\"); the German is kept in brackets since the next passages turn on it. The literal \"heißt ... vergleichen\" is rendered with \"is entitled\" as the method requires, though the English is awkward."]],"philippi-s47-003":["To investigate [*untersuchen*] is entitled to carry out reflection mediately, or by means of other instruments of truth. Thus not everything that is reflected on is investigated.",["\"Untersuchen\" is rendered \"investigate\" and kept distinct from \"überlegen\"."]],"philippi-s47-004":["Not all modes of knowledge need investigation, but all need reflection. The first principles of reason must not be compared mediately with the laws of the understanding, but may be compared immediately; that is, one may not investigate them, but one must reflect on them.",["\"Erkenntnisse\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"dürfen nicht\" rendered \"must not\" in the sense of \"may not\" (are not permitted/need not)."]],"philippi-s47-005":["Without reflection we speak empty words. Precipitancy in judgment is a lack of investigation. The lack of reflection in judgment is prejudice. Prejudice, however, properly signifies not so much a judgment made without reflection as a rule, a certain canon, for judging without reflection. In prejudice it is not enough that we omit to compare our judgment with the laws of reason; we compare it, besides, with another law which we have adopted, for instance with custom or inclination. Mere precipitancy, in which one does not reflect, does not yet constitute a prejudice. A universal law which is the ground of judgments made without reflection is a prejudice. Principles are opposed to prejudices. As the latter are *principia* for judging without reflection, so the former are *principia* for judging with reflection. It is not single judgments that are opposed to prejudices. If a father does not believe that his son is so godless at school, that is just as little a prejudice as when, on reading the title-page of a book, I form a judgment of the book itself.",["Ueberlegung = 'reflection'; Uebereilung = 'precipitancy'; Vorurtheil = 'prejudice'.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'GeAvohnheit' to Gewohnheit, 'seni' to sein, 'Vorurt heilen' to Vorurtheilen.","In 'Den Vorurtheilen werden die Grundsätze entgegengesetzt' the 'jene'/'diese' pair is resolved by sense: 'jene' = prejudices, 'diese' = principles. I reversed the order in English ('the latter ... the former') to keep the reference clear."]],"philippi-s47-006":["The form after which concepts shape themselves, when they are combined without reflection, [is given by]",["The sentence ends in 'sind' and the list follows in the next passages; 'is given by' is supplied in brackets only to make the English run, with the plural 'sind' left unresolved (the antecedent of the plural may be 'Formen')."]],"philippi-s47-007":["1. Imitation",[]],"philippi-s47-008":["2. Custom",["Gewohnheit rendered 'custom' here and in s47-005, for consistency; 'habit' is also possible."]],"philippi-s47-009":["3. Inclination. These are the three sources of prejudices.",[]],"philippi-s47-010":["We must always compare our judgments with something. If we do not compare them with the understanding, we compare them with the general fashion, or with the *habitus* of judging in a certain way, or with fame. Whenever we judge in this latter fashion, we judge without reflection. A prejudice is always present when we compare our judgment not with the understanding but with other principles which are able to guide the understanding.",["'Habitu' is the dative/ablative form of Latin *habitus*, retained in italics as a technical term."]],"philippi-s47-011":["An analogue (*Analogon*) of prejudice is the provisional judgment. This is a judgment which precedes the investigation, together with the consciousness that an investigation is necessary. Many investigations cannot be undertaken at once; but I must not on that account abstain from all judging. Before the investigation I shall still find some grounds which, if not sufficient for deciding, are at least sufficient for judging. So far as these put anything into my hands, I can always judge. For example, I read the book-fair catalogue; if I am to resolve to read a book, I must judge something about it provisionally. Provisional judgments are very necessary, and he shows a special strength of genius who can infer much from few data. Before anything is taken up as an object of investigation, everyone must judge something about it provisionally. This is also very usual among men. From the scantiest data, for instance from the mere name, they already infer something.",["OCR corrected: 'wild' to wird; 'Schlüssen' (twice) to schließen, the infinitive being required by the sense.","Meßkatalogus = the catalogue of the book-fair; 'Datis' = data.","The sentence 'So viel diese mir an die Hand bieten kann ich immer urtheilen' is smoothed slightly: 'So far as these put anything into my hands'."]],"philippi-s47-012":["One should take pains, in writings and in company, to make the provisional judgment favourable to us. For this provisional judgment is the substratum of everything that the understanding subsequently judges. The understanding always refers back to it, even when, the investigation having been undertaken, it perceives the incorrectness of that judgment. For the most part the provisional judgment serves for deciding. So unwillingly does the understanding alter the [fold (*Falte*)] which it has once assumed! It does not like to have judged in vain and to have to rework everything, especially when custom is added. All inventions in the world have their ground in provisional judgments. The understanding must already have inferred something beforehand, and must have fixed the rule according to which it intends to conduct the investigation, so that it may not make its attempt at random. In regard to many matters we cannot arrive at an investigation; there we can nevertheless prepare ourselves for it by provisional judgments. The provisional judgment is a true judgment; only it is incomplete. As far as the few data extend, so far it is correct. If I see a man going about with splendour and servants, or if in the aspect of another I notice honest features, I judge that the former is rich and the latter good-natured. Suppose the former owes more than he will pay, suppose the latter to be a hypocrite: I have none the less judged correctly from the few data that fell to my share. In the provisional judgment I decide nothing; I say only that from what I know this or that consequence follows, whether I have guessed it or not. It is not false; it is only incomplete, and expresses the relation of judgments to insufficient grounds. But if one holds a provisional judgment to be decisive, then that is prejudice and precipitancy.",["OCR corrected: 'Gesellschat 't t en' to Gesellschaften, 'Fol^e' to Folge, 'Urt heilen', 'Anbbck' to Anblick, 'redHche' to redliche.","'Falte' (fold) is doubtful: it may be an OCR or transcription slip for another word (e.g. Gestalt, Fälle); I translate literally and bracket it.","'ihre Unrichtigkeit' is taken to refer to the provisional judgment, not to the investigation.","'Schlüssen' in 'Der Verstand muß schon vorher was Schlüssen' is read as schließen ('inferred')."]],"philippi-s47-013":["Prejudices are something quite different. Their harmfulness and blameworthiness does not consist in this, that we err (we can often hit upon the truth), but in this, that we adopt a false *modus cognoscendi* and set aside the true one. What matters is not so much what one judges as how one judges. Some believe that there are useful prejudices. But these are the prejudices which impose a blind obedience on others and thereby would abolish all knowledge. What matters is the method according to which one has judged.",["OCR corrected: 'blmden' to blinden. *modus cognoscendi* is kept in Latin (mode of knowing).","Erkenntniß = 'knowledge'."]],"philippi-s47-014":["Judgment is distinguished from prejudice not in matter but only in form. Even truths must be rejected if they proceed from prejudice. For what would become of us if [that] method were dominant and the only one? Logic is a remedy against it. Some laugh at the prejudice of others, though they themselves are sick with another.",["'die Methode' is ambiguous; I take it to mean the method of prejudice, and supply 'that' in brackets.","'dawider ein Mittel': 'a remedy against it', i.e. against this domination of the method of prejudice."]],"philippi-s48-001":["Application.",[]],"philippi-s48-002":["There are prejudices of the understanding.",["The German has no full stop; the sentence is complete as it stands."]],"philippi-s48-003":["There are prejudices of taste.",[]],"philippi-s48-004":["The most powerful prejudice of taste is imitation. It goes so far that the most acute philosopher cannot track down its limit. Often whole peoples praise a usage, or something else, as true beauty, which makes the century ridiculous and fantastic. Then it appears in the end that they have compared their judgment not with taste but with custom.",["'abenteuerlich' rendered 'fantastic' (literally 'adventurous'); 'scharfsinnigste' as 'most acute'."]],"philippi-s48-005":["Prejudices of taste are the grounds for approving something, not from comparison with our own taste but with a foreign one. A prejudice of the understanding is something which the understanding, unmindful of its laws, adopts as its rule.",["OCR: 'Vorgleiehung mit unserni' read as Vergleichung mit unserm; the noun 'Geschmack' is understood after 'unserm'."]],"philippi-s48-006":["We have favourable and unfavourable prejudices. All have one stem. We note",["'ungüstige' read as ungünstige. The passage breaks off at 'Wir merken' and the list follows."]],"philippi-s48-007":["1. Logical egoism. A psychological egoist is one who maintains that he alone exists. In psychology one investigates how this opinion, which is an important problem for philosophers, can have arisen. A moral egoist is one who believes that he exists for himself alone and believes that he can turn everything to his own advantage.",["OCR: 'sejm' read as seyn (sein); the stray '1 .' spacing is silently normalised."]],"philippi-s48-008":["The cause of logical egoism is",[]],"philippi-s48-009":["a. Self-love (*Eigenliebe*), in that I esteem myself alone as possessed of insight and despise others; in that I hold my knowledge to be perfect, from *philautia* (*Philautie*).",["'Philautie' is the Greek-derived term (self-love) as the German text gives it; the Latinised form is added for clarity."]],"philippi-s48-010":["b. Self-conceit (*Eigendünkel*), in that I will not give a hearing to the opinions of others.",[]],"philippi-s48-011":["Logical egoism consists in this, that I accept all judgments only in comparison with my own, so far as they agree with it, and am unconcerned and indifferent about agreement with the understanding of others. If something is incomprehensible to others, he believes that this is because they do not see it. This logical egoism, a source of many important consequences, is at fault in this, that it rejects the proper subjective mark of truth, which consists in the agreement of knowledge with the universal understanding, and holds agreement with a single reason to be a sufficient mark of truth.",["OCR: 'I )er' read as Der; 'Qvelle' = Quelle.","'fehlt darin' rendered 'is at fault in this'. I divided nothing here but kept the long final sentence as in the German."]],"philippi-s48-012":["The egoist transforms all knowledge, even the propositions of reason themselves, into illusion (*Schein*; *apparentia*), by giving them a merely private validity.",["Schein rendered 'illusion', with the Latin *apparentia* retained as Kant's parenthetical equivalent.","Vernunftsätze = 'propositions of reason'."]],"philippi-s48-013":["Sensation and feeling have only private validity; for in them I do not know the things themselves, but only the extent to which my passive state is modified. But knowledge of the understanding [*Verstandeserkenntniß*] is meant to be universally valid, for it knows things as they are. Hence we must compare it not only with my own reason but with reason in general. Man also already feels within himself an inclination to compare his judgments with those of others, and so to verify them. The ground of our understanding's being so communicative is not vanity but an instinct. It is to be seen in common people. For example, when someone sees a northern light in the sky, he calls to his neighbour. A scholar in a lonely desert, or among the savage people of the Negroes, would give up all speculation, because he could not be sure that his judgments were not mere illusion [*Schein*]. But my impulse to communicate my insights to others would be very unreasonable and useless, if I did not also have receptivity for listening to the judgments of others.",["'Instmct' and 'Receptiuitat' in the OCR silently corrected to Instinct and Receptivität.","'mit der allgemeinen' resolved as 'with reason in general' (Vernunft understood).","'Negern' is the period's term, rendered literally as 'Negroes'; the phrase is Philippi's transcript, not modernised.","Sentence 'Weil er nicht sicher wäre...' is joined to the preceding sentence for smoothness."]],"philippi-s48-014":["When one compares one's insights with those of others, and decides the truth from the relation of agreement with the reason of others, this is logical pluralism.",["*Pluralism* kept in Latinate form, as in the German."]],"philippi-s48-015":["We find that even in observations of the senses we consult others, and, if they are many, trust them more than ourselves.",[]],"philippi-s48-016":["When a judgment of another contradicts us, this is already very important.",[]],"philippi-s48-017":["In refutations we have to see to it that we bring the reason of others into agreement with our own, and not that we point out their errors.",["OCR 'Verlas nunft' corrected to 'Vernunft'."]],"philippi-s48-018":["Love of honour is a derived impulse, not a fundamental one.",["*Ehrliebe* rendered 'love of honour'; *Trieb* as 'impulse'."]],"philippi-s48-019":["The authority which a multitude possesses, the degree of approval it always finds, and the embarrassment in which everyone sees himself when he has to appear and speak before a multitude, even though he knows that it lacks the insight which I have, confirm that the participatory inclination of the understanding is an instinct. A multitude inspires respect. To the ignorant it serves as proof of their opinions and as reassurance of their actions. One gladly joins the largest crowd.",["'Theilnehmende Neigung' rendered 'participatory inclination'; sense is the inclination to share in others' judgments."]],"philippi-s48-020":["2. Imitation. This arises",["Breaks off; continued in the following items."]],"philippi-s48-021":["1. from reverence for the multitude.",[]],"philippi-s48-022":["2. from affection for persons.",[]],"philippi-s48-023":["The former is known from experience even in moral matters. We prefer to adopt the opinion which many hold. We always seek, in fortunate or unfortunate events, to see others also involved and not to see ourselves isolated. The multitude of citations from the ancients, as in Morhof, is a result of this prejudice.",["OCR 'sej^' corrected to 'sey'; 'Morhoff' is Morhof (Polyhistor)."]],"philippi-s48-024":["The authority of a person often moves us to accept his propositions as though we had only to learn them. He who cannot raise himself seeks to raise one man above all others; thereby he believes he will bring it about that all others submit to the authority of this extolled sage and so become equal to him. When one can do nothing but copy, one chooses a model which one recommends to all. This often arises not from particular esteem but from envy of others to whom one could otherwise not attain.",["The sense of 'dadurch ... ihm gleich werden' is ambiguous; I take 'ihm' as the one who cannot raise himself (i.e. others become equal to him in submission)."]],"philippi-s48-025":["a. Antiquity. A common principle [*Principium*] lies at the ground here, namely the belief that everything grows old and vanishes through length of time. In this prejudice a true judgment lies at the ground. Individual subjects and pieces of nature grow old through duration; but the species never grows old. In the preservation of the species there is an inexplicable phenomenon: the power of generation does not diminish, nor do any other powers.",["OCR 'Kin.ele' read as 'Einzele' (individual). 'Geschlecht' rendered 'species'; 'Arten' likewise 'species'/kinds."]],"philippi-s48-026":["Under antiquity men represent to themselves honesty and uprightness. Among the ancient Greeks there prevailed freedom and simplicity; [they were] the nurturers of taste.",["OCR 'Frejmeit' read as 'Freiheit'. 'die Ernährer des Geschmaks' is loosely attached; bracketed words supplied."]],"philippi-s48-027":["The children who are born now are endowed not only with the same bodily powers but also with the same faculties of understanding as 3000 years ago.",["'als' in the German is a slip for 'wie'; sense is 'as those of'."]],"philippi-s48-028":["It may further be said that we do not esteem the ancients thus from prejudice, but that they actually deserve to be so esteemed. The cause of this is as follows: time sifts all the products of understanding and taste. Poor products are not valued and so perish; but those which by reason of their special merits were deemed worthy of preservation could remain. In this way the fine selection of the best models has been handed down. One must therefore not attribute such masterpieces to the whole of antiquity. Likewise, if the remotest posterity should read the writings of a Newton and a Hume, and all other poor writings had been lost, it would wrongly conclude that in our time there were nothing but such great scholars.",["OCR 'übergebheben' read as 'überbliebene/übergeblieben'; 'verlornen', 'Schlüssen' read as 'verloren', 'schließen'."]],"philippi-s48-029":["The prejudice in favour of antiquity arises",["Breaks off; the items follow."]],"philippi-s48-030":["1.) from the opinion that all writings were as excellent as those which have come down to our times; whereas these are only the sifted selection of the best, and a swarm of poor ones has been lost.",[]],"philippi-s48-031":["2.) Because the ancients had no predecessors, and the road to learning was therefore very hard for them. For the same reason we rate learning highly in women.",["*Frauenzimmer* rendered 'women'."]],"philippi-s48-032":["3.) From the gratitude which we owe to the ancients as our teachers.",[]],"philippi-s48-033":["4.) Because learning is required in order to be able to read and understand them.",[]],"philippi-s48-034":["5.) From the prejudice that everything grows old and worse.",["OCR 'Veralte' read as 'veralte'; 'Vorurteil' normalised."]],"philippi-s48-035":["We have reason to pass a favourable judgment on the old models of the arts. It is remarkable that we know no poets and no orators who could dispute precedence with Virgil, Homer, Cicero, Demosthenes; sculpture and all other matters of taste are models there.",["OCR 'em' read as 'ein'."]],"philippi-s48-036":["1. One cause may be that among the ancients there was no change of fashions.",[]],"philippi-s48-037":["To all matters of taste nothing is more harmful than fashions. In finding pleasure in aping, we fall at last upon the strangest and most fantastic things, and then hold them to be exceedingly beautiful.",[]],"philippi-s48-038":["The ancients, who had no models before them, also brought no artificiality into their works. Their taste was pure and wholly nature. But we must always form ourselves after models; if we do not, we are censured.",["OCR 'Allem' read as 'Allein'."]],"philippi-s48-039":["2. Among the ancients there was no such great difference between the distinguished and the people. The whole people, which commonly sees with natural eyes, was therefore the object which the ancient artists sought to please. If they wished this, they had to be natural. We now find no taste in it. Men do not gladly remain in one place, even if it were the highest step. Modern times sought variation and altered nature.",["OCR 'Stufte' read as 'Stufe'."]],"philippi-s48-040":["On the other hand we again have an advantage over the ancient times. As with a single man, so the whole human race acquires ever more experience. In mathematics, for example, the ancients did not have algebra, with which we carry out in compendious calculation what they achieved with indescribable labour and prolixity. So too in natural history. Diligence can always add something.",["The sentence 'mit der wir das compendiöse Calculiren ...' is grammatically irregular in the German; smoothed in English."]],"philippi-s48-041":["3. The ancients are masters of taste",["Breaks off; continues in following items."]],"philippi-s48-042":["a. because the books of religion were written in the languages of the ancients.",[]],"philippi-s48-043":["b. Divine services have been celebrated in the Latin language.",[]],"philippi-s48-044":["If the profane writers had not been written in these languages",["OCR 'Prof anscribenten' read as 'Profanscribenten'. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s48-045":["they would long since have perished. Because we must learn from them on account of religion,",[]],"philippi-s48-046":["they are and will be preserved.",[]],"philippi-s48-047":["c. Because a dead language is unchangeable, it happens that the",["OCR 'C.' read as 'c.'; 'geschiehtsdaßder' divided as 'geschieht es, daß der'."]],"philippi-s48-048":["taste with regard to the dead language cannot change.",[]],"philippi-s48-049":["The expressions which were then the best are so still;",[]],"philippi-s48-050":["and if they were not so, they are so now.",[]],"philippi-s48-051":["Taste must have models. Since beauty cannot be learned from concepts but only from intuition, and since the beautiful has no rules for practice and doctrine but only for criticism, models are necessary to us. And these models, if beauty is not to change, must be written in a dead language.",["'Praxi und Doctrin' rendered 'practice and doctrine'; 'Critic' rendered 'criticism' (Kritik, in the sense of judging, not the Critical Philosophy).","Restated the final sentence's word order for English: 'Und zwar müssen diese Muster ... in einer todten Sprache geschrieben seyn.'"]],"philippi-s48-052":["The very circumstance of the dead language contributes much to the ancients' being these models.",["The German is elliptical ('macht viel, daß ...'); I have kept the sense without adding content."]],"philippi-s48-053":["In their case no expression can be censured. Each is the best that the author could have chosen. If a poet has the advantage that his style cannot be censured, nothing remains [to be wanted] but keenness of mind.",["'[to be wanted]' is supplied to complete the sense of 'so bleibt nichts übrig als die Schärfe des Geistes'. The sentence is compressed in the transcript and its sense is somewhat uncertain."]],"philippi-s48-054":["Shall we not also become originals and models for posterity? No, because no living language can be one that is universally learned.",[]],"philippi-s48-055":["Prejudice of novelty. It arises from our judging that the more recent times have before them the instruction of all times. We are for the most part inclined to think highly of a new book; for we believe that the author has made use of everything the ancients wrote, and has added something of his own besides. In the sciences and in the arts of taste, however, this does not hold.",["'Von einem neuen Buch scheint uns mehrentheils immer gewöhnlich viel zu halten' is clumsy (several adverbs piled up); smoothed to 'we are for the most part inclined to think highly of'."]],"philippi-s48-056":["Since imitation is opposed to genius, some, in order to escape the reproach of imitation, fall upon novelty. Many do so because they are true geniuses, many in order to appear as if they had genius.",[]],"philippi-s48-057":["Free republics like old fashions, whereas sovereign states like new fashions, because in the former there is no person of importance who rules, as there is in the latter. Young persons are devoted to novelty, as to variety generally; old persons love old fashions.",["'Heben' in the OCR read as 'lieben' (love). 'dort ... hier' resolved as 'former ... latter' by the sense of the argument."]],"philippi-s48-058":["The inclination toward the new is now stronger than in former times. The cause of this is intercourse with women [*Frauenzimmer*], who now govern everything. Why do we follow the French in matters of taste? Because they give more grace [*Anmuth*] to the thing than do all other peoples. The English resist taste the most.",["'Frauenzimmer' is the period term for women collectively; rendered 'women'."]],"philippi-s48-059":["With regard to the sciences of reason, and especially of experience, we have good ground for preferring the new to the old.",[]],"philippi-s48-060":["Which prejudice should one rather adopt, that of antiquity or that of novelty? In the practical sphere the former is certainly to be adopted; one must often follow custom and old usage, for example, in deciding whether children should be brought up by their parents or by the republic.",["'Republick' rendered 'republic', i.e. the state."]],"philippi-s48-061":["How can one forestall prejudices in children? One should take care that I do not let the child think, nor bring him up, according to the rules of my understanding. Rousseau in his *Émile* demands a negative education: I ought not to talk judgments into the child before his understanding is capable of them; in particular, inclination must be kept separate.",["'Aemil' corrected to *Émile*. The last clause ('besonders muß man Neigung separiren') is terse and its object is unclear; translated literally."]],"philippi-s48-062":["Prejudice of the accepted system of doctrine.",["'Lehrgebäude' rendered 'system of doctrine'."]],"philippi-s48-063":["Our modes of knowledge acquire a certain degree of truth when they agree with one another. But when modes of knowledge agree with one [thing] (*cum uno quodam*), when there is unity in them, then they agree with one another. Now modes of knowledge can, however, be forced together into agreement by leaving out much that is true. Hence it comes about that established systems of doctrine so often introduce false propositions and greatly hinder the truth. To this must be added that authors who have set a system in their heads, even when they see false propositions flowing from it, do not want to have laboured so much in vain.",["OCR 'vno' read as 'uno'; Latin kept in italics. '[thing]' is supplied. 'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge' (plural).","Divided nothing, but 'gleich' in 'wenn sie gleich ... sehen' rendered 'even when'."]],"philippi-s48-064":["On probability and the doubtful",["Heading; continues in the next passage ('der Erkenntniß')."]],"philippi-s48-065":["in knowledge.",["Continues the heading; 'Von der Wahrscheinlichkeit und dem Zweifelhaften der Erkenntniß'."]],"philippi-s48-066":["If, in a mode of knowledge, I have more than half of the number of sufficient grounds of holding-to-be-true (*Vorwahrhalten* [*Fürwahrhalten*]), the knowledge is probable. For example, if the sufficient grounds of holding-to-be-true are twelve and I have eight of them, the knowledge is probable. But if I have only half of the sufficient grounds of holding-to-be-true, the knowledge is doubtful; for example, if I have only six of twelve grounds that are sufficient.",["The text reads 'Vorwahrhalten' throughout, apparently a variant of *Fürwahrhalten*; rendered 'holding-to-be-true' consistently. Italics on the German here only to mark the variant."]],"philippi-s48-067":["The grounds must be compared not with one another but with the sufficient grounds. Accordingly, in disputation it is not enough that grounds and counter-grounds be compared with each other; they must also be compared with the sufficient grounds of holding-to-be-true.",[]],"philippi-s48-068":["Subjective probability is the relation of the grounds of holding-to-be-true which is more certain than that for the contrary. For example, concerning a future life I have more grounds for asserting it than for the contrary. The theory of probability will never reach perfection. Philosophical probability can be felt more than applied.",["'Probabiliität' (OCR) corrected to 'Probabilität'. 'welches gewissser ist als fürs Gegentheil' is elliptical; the sense is the relation that outweighs its opposite, and is left close to the German."]],"philippi-s48-069":["The uncertainty of a mode of knowledge is not yet doubt. A mode of knowledge can be probable and yet uncertain.",[]],"philippi-s48-070":["A doubt is the equilibrium of the grounds of holding-to-be-true. But even if my knowledge is uncertain, it does not leave me in doubt in practice. I can be certain in acting, although I am uncertain in speculation. If a man, for example a general, is not to know whither he should turn, if he is to be *anceps* [wavering], there would have to be doubt not only in his judgment but also in his resolution. Mere uncertainty in speculation is compatible with complete decisiveness in action. Socrates was uncertain whether there was another world; but he acted as if he knew it for certain. Resolutions not only do not require logical certainty, but are also only seldom capable of it.",["OCR 'Avenn' read 'als wenn'; 'kerne' read 'keine'. '[wavering]' is supplied as a gloss on *anceps*, which is kept in Latin.","The sentence on the general is tangled; I restated it but kept the construction ('wenn ... soll ... wenn er anceps seyn soll') in sense."]],"philippi-s48-071":["That is the difference between practical and logical certainty.",[]],"philippi-s48-072":["What is not logically certain can be practically certain. There is a God: very few have a logical certainty of this, but all have a practical certainty.",[]],"philippi-s48-073":["Practical certainty",["Heading continued in the next two passages."]],"philippi-s48-074":["1. according to rules of prudence is the pragmatic [certainty]",["'[certainty]' supplied."]],"philippi-s48-075":["2. according to rules of morality, moral certainty.",[]],"philippi-s48-076":["For practical certainty according to the rules of morality, that is, for moral certainty, still less logical certainty is required than for pragmatic certainty. For example, in the case of an oath it is morally certain that a man does not swear if he has even the slightest suspicion that he is not innocent. So in general, in refraining from evil and in doing good, the slightest suspicions are capable of determining our actions. For example, if I find something, a single suspicion is capable of moving me to return it. I do not do so from fear of a lawsuit or from prudence.",["'determmiren' (OCR) read 'determiniren'; rendered 'determining'. The Latinate *determiniren* is the source of the choice over 'Bestimmung' wording.","Divided the first long sentence from the examples; the German runs on with 'z. E.'"]],"philippi-s48-077":["Many authors mix pragmatic and moral certainty together. Only late have the limits of the latter been found. Moral certainty is something relative (*respective*) to the moral laws.",["OCR 'respectiue' read as *respective*."]],"philippi-s48-078":["Scruples are counter-grounds against holding-to-be-true so far as they are obscure; so far as they are clear they are entitled doubts, and if they are distinct, they are objections. Objections are almost always scruples at the outset.",["'heissen' rendered 'are entitled'. The clear/distinct gradation is Kant's usual logical perfection of concepts; 'Skrupel' kept as 'scruples'."]],"philippi-s48-079":["In many cases we are logically certain, although not practically and morally certain.",[]],"philippi-s48-080":["The hindrance to assent or holding-to-be-true is either subjective or objective, and is called a difficulty.",["OCR 'subiectiv', 'obiectiv' normalised; 'Schwürigkeit' read 'Schwierigkeit'."]],"philippi-s48-081":["Scruples consist in a resistance that our understanding feels. They are grounded on prejudices. They show themselves even in mathematical modes of knowledge. A knowledge of one's own and of other subjects is required, and one must know how men ordinarily think in order to be able to tell others the scruples which they have.",["OCR 'gewöhn! ich' read 'gewöhnlich'. 'Kenntniß' rendered 'knowledge' here, distinct from *Erkenntniß* only by sense."]],"philippi-s48-082":["It is very fine, and serviceable to the advancement of truth, to help a man who cannot unravel his error to resolve it. Then the one will be glad and the other acute. Errors must not merely be answered but also explained. When someone deviates from the laws of the understanding, one must philosophise [to discover] how this comes about.",["'[to discover]' is supplied. 'Indem einer ... abweicht' is loosely connected to the next clause; rendered as 'When ...'."]],"philippi-s48-083":["Scruples always deserve investigation; this one sees if one lets a man talk at length. They are not to be despised, least of all by the man who has them in his own head.",[]],"philippi-s48-084":["Scruples spring not so much from the character of the grounds of holding-to-be-true as from the new fold which the understanding must take on. Hence one must not merely give another person knowledge, but must also accustom him to it. Whenever the understanding is required to give a different form to knowledge, it balks. It is therefore also good to speak at length of a matter that is unfamiliar to the hearer, and to accustom his understanding to the arguments advanced.",["Vorwahrhalten rendered 'holding-to-be-true' (the usual Fürwahrhalten).","'Falte' kept literally as 'fold'; Kant's image is of a habit or set into which the understanding has to be bent."]],"philippi-s48-085":["Doubt.",[]],"philippi-s48-086":["The doubt of postponement [*Aufschubszweifel*], or pure sceptical doubt, is entirely distinct from dogmatic doubt, even in its effects. The one seeks to raise itself above all knowledge; the other seeks to investigate and test the truth. The methods differ. Theoretical doubt, which holds the understanding in suspense (*in suspenso*), is distinct from practical doubt, which holds the will in suspense. I can be in theoretical doubt without any practical doubt. Even if I acknowledge Wolff's proposition that all actions are determined by something, it will not on that account take freedom from my will. Hence the doubt of postponement, in the case of the most important propositions, is better than decided dogmatic judgments, since the will is not determined by speculation.",["OCR 'Auf schubszweifel' corrected to Aufschubszweifel; 'Wolfen' read as Wolff (the genitive).","Sentence 'Der eine … der andere' kept as one, with the colon smoothed to a semicolon."]],"philippi-s48-087":["The arguments of the sceptics were named as follows. 1. The argument of the liar: he who lies and confesses that he lies speaks the truth in lying. 2. The dreamer. 3. Achilles: because he could run very fast he was compared with the tortoise, which was a mile ahead of him and which he outran ten times as fast. 4. The Diallele.",["The Achilles example is garbled in the transcript (the 'mile' and 'ten times' are as given); translated as it stands.","'Diallele' (the circular argument, diallelus) left untranslated."]],"philippi-s48-088":["The groundlessness of a thing is always subjective.",["'Unbegründlichkeit' is unusual; rendered 'groundlessness'. It might also mean 'inability to be grounded'."]],"philippi-s48-089":["Objective difficulty, as soon as one is conscious of it, is entitled doubt.",["OCR 'Obiectivc' corrected to Objective."]],"philippi-s48-090":["Subjective difficulty, of which I am not conscious, is entitled scruple.",[]],"philippi-s48-091":["A faithlessness arises from our being always so much concerned about practice in the case of theoretical propositions. Hence it comes about that the *argumentum ab utili, a tuto* takes the place of reason. One wants to prove the truth of a proposition from the utility that flows from it. But a proposition is not true because it is useful. Practical suspension does not follow from theoretical.",["'Treulosigkeit' is odd in the context (perhaps a mishearing by the transcriber); translated literally as 'faithlessness'.","'um die Praxi' rendered 'about practice'."]],"philippi-s48-092":["We can decide that something is true, and yet say of that very thing that it is not certain.",[]],"philippi-s48-093":["It is evident that the judgment of truth, when it suffices to hold something to be true, yet with the proviso that it is not certain, must stand in a relation to the will. For example, if an accusation is brought against a man of whom we cannot suppose such a deed, my understanding indeed sees from all the circumstances that it is so, but my will resists believing it. Then it is indeed true, but only not certain. Something may be certain to one subject which is not certain to another.",["OCR 'Wülen' corrected to Willen.","'daß es an dem sey' rendered 'that it is so'."]],"philippi-s48-094":["Holding-to-be-true rests not on inclination [*Belieben*] but on grounds of knowledge.",["OCR 'Beheben' read as Belieben (a conjecture); 'Erkennt nißgründen' rejoined as Erkenntnißgründen."]],"philippi-s49-001":["Gradation of holding-to-be-true.",[]],"philippi-s49-002":["1. Assent [*Beyfall*]",[]],"philippi-s49-003":["2. Approval [*Billigung*]",["OCR 'Bilhgung' corrected."]],"philippi-s49-004":["3. When these grounds of holding-to-be-true are insufficient, they yield probability.",["Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Scheinbarkeit (next) = plausibility, a choice, since Kant's own Latin parallel is not given."]],"philippi-s49-005":["4. Plausibility [*Scheinbarkeit*], in so far as the grounds of holding-to-be-true are, as it were, parts of that ground which is good for the subject, or in so far as the grounds, taken together *coordinando*, make up a ground of holding-to-be-true sufficient for the subject; in so far, I say, a mode of knowledge is plausible.",["'der für das Subjekt gut' is probably corrupt or incomplete (perhaps 'zureichend' is meant, as in the alternative clause); translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s49-006":["Objective grounds of holding-to-be-true, since they lie in the object, hold for all. Plausibility holds only for individual subjects. It is always plausible that man will perish after death; the poets, therefore, also represent it so.",["OCR 'smd' read as sind; 'stehen es … vor' read as 'stellen es … vor'."]],"philippi-s49-007":["Understanding belongs to probability; the senses belong to plausibility; and the latter therefore finds place only where judgment is made from appearance [*Schein*]. Only phenomena belong to it, whereas understanding belongs to probability. Objective grounds of holding-to-be-true belong to probability, whereas only subjective grounds belong to plausibility.",["'Phänomena' kept in the Latinate form of the original."]],"philippi-s49-008":["The second degree of certainty is according to rules of identity; for example, a whole is greater than one of its parts. In pure reason there is a pure identity.",["OCR 'Vernunf f t' rejoined."]],"philippi-s49-009":["Moral certainty, in respect of pure morality, comes nearest to the mathematical.",[]],"philippi-s49-010":["Metaphysics is a critique, and it is in metaphysics that certainty is most lacking.",["'Kritic' as in the transcript; the statement is odd, and may be a slip of the transcriber."]],"philippi-s49-011":["Certainty of knowledge is only subjective.",[]],"philippi-s49-012":["If the grounds of holding-to-be-true hold only in relation to the character of the subject, plausibility is present.",[]],"philippi-s49-013":["It was plausible to the ancients, and is so still to all common people, that the sun moves round the earth. To the moderns the opposite is probable.",[]],"philippi-s49-014":["A mode of knowledge that is plausibly certain is entitled persuasion [*Ueberredung*]. The certainty that springs from the understanding, and not from sensible appearance, is conviction [*Ueberzeugung*]. The latter, which springs according to rules of the understanding, is objective certainty; the former is subjective certainty. Intuitive certainty according to rules of the understanding is evidence [*Evidenz*].",["'Diese … jene': 'diese' taken as Überzeugung, 'jene' as Überredung, the order required by the argument.","'anschauende Gewißheit' rendered 'intuitive certainty'."]],"philippi-s49-015":["The weak can hardly be convinced otherwise than by laws of sensibility; so in moral, so also in theoretical propositions. Evidence (self-evidence, *Augenscheinlichkeit*) is distinct from certainty.",[]],"philippi-s49-016":["The sciences which can be submitted to the test of visible appearance can have evidence. Of this kind is mathematics. But philosophical qualities cannot acquire evidence; for example, the proposition that there is a supreme cause can be convincing only to those who are practised in thinking through pure understanding.",["'philosophische Qualitäten' is odd (perhaps 'Quaestionen' or similar was meant); translated literally."]],"philippi-s49-017":["How are we to give such propositions evidence? By showing the truth of such a proposition in objects of the senses. For example, that it is not the utility of virtue and the shadow of vice that fills us with high esteem for the former and with hatred for the latter can be shown in examples. Although men who have deceived foreign enemies, and thereby support others, produce utility; although we profit from those who throw everything away in brainless prodigality; yet the former are abhorrent to us and the latter contemptible.",["The sentence 'daß nicht Nutzen …' is loosely constructed; restated as 'that it is not … that fills us'.","'Obgleich Menschen, die …' is obscure; the sense (those who deceive an enemy for others' benefit are still abhorred) is as given, but the reading is uncertain."]],"philippi-s49-018":["Sensibility can indeed give vividness, but not evidence. Conviction must come about according to laws of the understanding, even when the grounds of holding-to-be-true are borrowed from experience. For prior to all understanding we have indeed sensation, but not experience. Animals have no experience, for only through the understanding can one have it.",["OCR 'Lebhaff tigkeit' rejoined."]],"philippi-s49-019":["Moral modes of knowledge can be very clear, but not evident.",[]],"philippi-s49-020":["Evidence is found only in the combination of parts into a whole, that is, where the understanding coordinates; not, however, in the combination of grounds and consequences, that is, where the understanding subordinates; consequently only in mathematics, not in philosophy.",[]],"philippi-s49-021":["Like certainty, conviction also is either empirical or rational. If the conviction comes about through pure understanding without admixture of experience, and the grounds of the truth can be submitted to sensible visibility, then in that case we have mathematical certainty. This will accordingly consist in evident rational certainty.",["The last sentence ('in der evidenten Vernunfft (rational) Gewißheit') is garbled in the transcript; translated as it stands, with the parenthesis absorbed into 'rational'."]],"philippi-s49-022":["Philosophy is not capable of evidence, and metaphysics least of all.",[]],"philippi-s49-023":["The utility of sceptical doubt is uncommonly great, if it is of the right kind. Since it is permitted to set one proposition against another, and proofs for the opposite are adduced, we are thereby brought to an investigation of our own reason. For since we see, for one proposition, proofs and counter-proofs that establish the opposite, and since a proposition can be only either true or false, the difference must here lie in the subject. And knowledge of the subject is the true object of philosophy. For we have all our modes of knowledge only through knowing ourselves.",["Sentence 'Denn da wir …' divided and smoothed; the sense of 'Unterschied … in dem Subjekt' retained.","'Erkenntnisse' (plural) rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s49-024":["The great dispute arises from our taking subjective grounds to be objective. Because we cannot think a thing otherwise than in a certain way, we believe that it must also be so. A great deal of insight is required before one is convinced that the conditions of the subject are not conditions of the object. Only then does one truly learn that one knows nothing; and with this metaphysics is to occupy itself.",["Semicolon-joined clauses kept close to the German; \"dann lernt man erst recht\" rendered \"only then does one truly learn\"."]],"philippi-s49-025":["Objective knowledge cannot in many cases be attained. But in subjective knowledge one can arrive at certainty.",[]],"philippi-s49-026":["The relation of the grounds to the sufficient ground is considered in connection with probability.",["OCR stray quotation mark before \"Wahrscheinlichkeit\" dropped; \"Verhaltniß\" read as Verhältniß."]],"philippi-s49-027":["Plausibility rests upon the relation of the grounds to probability.",["Scheinbarkeit rendered \"plausibility\"; the choice is mine, the term being unusual."]],"philippi-s49-028":["The rules of prudence prescribe to us how we can become happy, without regard to the worthiness to be so; the rules of morality prescribe only the latter, without looking to the former.",["OCR \"weiden\" read as \"werden\"; \"zu derselben\" taken as referring to happiness."]],"philippi-s49-029":["Opinion is an uncertain knowledge of which we are conscious that it is uncertain. Persuasion is when we hold an uncertain knowledge to be certain. He who lets himself be persuaded has not yet grasped the difference between certainty and probability. One must know what true conviction is if one wants to know where it is lacking; just as slaves must know freedom if they are to complain of their condition.",["Ueberredung = persuasion; Ueberzeugung = conviction."]],"philippi-s49-030":["Even if the utility of mathematics were only that it makes us certain and lets us feel what true certainty is, it would already be inestimable.",[]],"philippi-s49-031":["Hypothesis. The author has not rightly understood it. A philosophical opinion *a priori* is not a hypothesis; the philosophical opinion *a posteriori* is. If I infer from the consequences to the truth of the ground, that is a hypothesis. If I infer from insufficient grounds to consequences, that is opinion, e.g. that there are in the earth bodies heavier than gold. I have grounds for holding this opinion; I infer it from grounds and not from consequences. That is not a hypothesis. The earth has cavities, because every soft body, when it dries, acquires cavities, and the earth in its first state was soft. That is a philosophical opinion, because proofs are present. But if I assume that the earth has cavities for the reason that it can be shown from this how the valleys arose, then I assume the ground because the consequence can be explained from it. That is hypothesis. The more an opinion suffices for the explanation of the consequences, the more probable is the hypothesis. When the opinion becomes sufficient for the explanation of all the consequences, then it has complete certainty. But for that we would have to know all the consequences, and this, it may be, can be done in no case; yet in calculation it is sometimes possible to know all the consequences. If, besides the proofs *a posteriori*, grounds *a priori* are added to a hypothesis, then it has certainty. It is a chief duty in natural science that one also prove *a priori* what one has assumed. E.g. many phenomena can be explained from the ether assumed *per hypothesin*; now I prove that the ether exists. If a hypothesis explains much that is manifold from one ground, it acquires much probability, as Newton's hypothesis of gravity. A *hypothesis subsidiaria* [subsidiary hypothesis] is when, for the explanation of a hypothesis, one assumes several others, as Tycho de Brahe assumed the *epicyclus epicyclorum*.",["OCR \"Mejaiung\", \"rneynen\", \"erldären\" corrected; \"Schlüsse\" read as \"schließe\" (I infer).","The first clause (\"not a hypothesis, but ... a posteriori\") is elliptical; I supply \"is\" without brackets for smoothness.","\"wol in keinem Fall\" rendered \"it may be, in no case\"; the transcript's \"Bis die Meynung\" is rendered \"When\", as the sense requires.","The transcript reads \"epycylos epicyclorum\"; I normalise the Latin to *epicyclus epicyclorum* (doubtful spelling in the source)."]],"philippi-s50-001":["Science [*Wissenschaft*]. §185",[]],"philippi-s50-002":["To know [*wissen*] means to have knowledge with certainty and to hold true. The degrees of holding true are:",["\"Wißen heißt\" rendered \"means\" rather than \"is entitled\", since it is a definition, not a naming."]],"philippi-s50-003":["1. To opine [*meynen*]: to hold something true in the degree in which I do not see how it stands in relation to certainty.",[]],"philippi-s50-004":["2. To believe [*glauben*]: to opine something in such a degree that the opinion has a practical certainty. The opinion of a degree sufficient for action is entitled belief.",[]],"philippi-s50-005":["3. To know [*wissen*]: to recognise the truth sufficiently. Opinion knows something insufficiently; belief knows it sufficiently for practice; science knows it sufficiently according to logical rules. Science in general is nothing other than the knowledge through which we know something. It is contrasted with art, under which one understands everything for which a certain specific perfection of practice is required; science, on the other hand, is where such perfection is required in the theory. Not everything practical is art; the practical skill which is not merely a consequence of theory is entitled an art. If something practical flows from mere knowledge, it is not art. Practical geometry is art. Science must be capable of being taught. Art must be brought about through my own abilities; for this genius is required. Science is further contrasted with critique. All theoretical knowledge is either a doctrine or a critique. Critique is that theoretical knowledge which teaches us to judge a perfection that is already there. Doctrine, however, is a theoretical knowledge which contains the grounds according to which a product can be brought forth. All modes of knowledge that are grounds of practice we shall entitle critiques, e.g. logic, aesthetics.",["OCR \"Erkenn tmß\", \"beurt heilen\" corrected. \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; plural \"Erkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge.","\"erkennen\" rendered \"recognise\"/\"know\" to keep it apart from \"wissen\"; the German of the last sentence is awkward and is kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s51-001":["§ 188",[]],"philippi-s51-002":["Mathematics has evidence in respect of quantity, but not of quality.",[]],"philippi-s51-003":["Mathematical certainty is a rational certainty which is so evident. Evidence is when a truth in our modes of rational knowledge becomes as great as it can be in the case of the senses. Numbers, space and time are regarded as objects of the senses.",["First sentence is garbled in the transcript (\"so evident ist\"); translated as it stands, probably something is missing.","Erkenntnisse = modes of knowledge."]],"philippi-s51-004":["Full [*ausführliche*] certainty arises from the consciousness of the necessity, not, as the author says, of the truth, but of the holding true. We find in ourselves an inner constraint and bond to hold true what we recognise as true.",["\"ausführliche Gewißheit\" is a technical term of Meier's text; \"full\" is a choice, and \"detailed\" is possible."]],"philippi-s51-005":["Conviction is an inner constraint with regard to the truth of the ideas; this is to be met with chiefly in mathematics.",[]],"philippi-s52-001":["§189",[]],"philippi-s52-002":["As regards the two divisions, certainty of the first rank and mathematical certainty of the second rank are distinguished not in form but according to the data. Certainty can be complete either through reason or through experience. When the moon comes between the earth and the sun, an eclipse is mathematically certain through experience. But that in a triangle all three angles make 180° is certain through reason, and this would therefore be a mathematical certainty of the first rank. This division of the author's is, however, not very good. In mathematical certainty one does not look to the object, but only to the form.",["\"Datis\" rendered \"data\". The text is slightly confused about which certainty is of which rank; translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s52-003":["The properties of mathematical knowledge are:",[]],"philippi-s52-004":["1. Complete distinctness.",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness."]],"philippi-s52-005":["2. Complete sufficiency (*completudo*).",[]],"philippi-s52-006":["3. Precision, that what is superfluous be cut off.",[]],"philippi-s52-007":["4. There might also belong to it a certain intuition, to which mathematical modes of knowledge can be subjected.",["OCR \"dem\" read as \"denn\"; the clause \"denn die ... können unterworfen werden\" is obscure, so I translate it loosely and the reading is doubtful. \"Anschauen\" rendered \"intuition\"."]],"philippi-s52-008":["The representations of reason concerning qualities, with which philosophy is occupied, agree with mathematical modes of knowledge in distinctness and in the sufficiency of grounds. Precision and evidence, however, are peculiar to the latter alone.",["\"Vernunff tvorstellungen\" repaired to Vernunftvorstellungen; \"dieser\" taken to refer to mathematical knowledge."]],"philippi-s52-009":["§",["Section number lost in the OCR."]],"philippi-s52-010":["An arbitrary truth (*positio*, not *hypothesis*) is one which expresses what I arbitrarily assume. E.g. the circle is divided arbitrarily into 360°. Objective propositions cannot be assumed arbitrarily, but only subjective ones. That we count up to 10 and regard all other numbers *in ratione decupla* [in tenfold ratio] is arbitrary. Arbitrary propositions relate to the practical, and indicate only what one wills to do, although one could also do otherwise.",[]],"philippi-s52-011":["Every proposition is either demonstrably certain or indemonstrably certain.",["OCR \"er weißlich\", \"uner weißlich\" joined; erweislich = demonstrable."]],"philippi-s52-012":["A mode of knowledge is entitled indemonstrable when it is immediately certain; but when it is certain by means of another mode of knowledge, from which, as from a ground, it can be proved, it is demonstrably certain.",["OCR \"gewTiß\" corrected to gewiß."]],"philippi-s52-013":["A proof is that knowledge by means of which the truth can be known.",[]],"philippi-s52-014":["The matter of a proof is the ground of proof [*Beweißgrund*], or the immediately certain knowledge. The form is the kind of consequence, that is, the way in which the truth of the proposition follows from the ground of proof.",["*Erkenntniß* rendered 'knowledge'.","'Beweißgrund' rendered 'ground of proof'; Kant's spelling kept in the bracket."]],"philippi-s52-015":["If the form of the proof itself cannot be known immediately but only mediately, then this is indeed a proof, but not a demonstration.",["OCR 'kerne' corrected silently to 'keine'."]],"philippi-s52-016":["The fault in a proof may lie either in the matter or in the form.",[]],"philippi-s52-017":["In every proof three elements are assumed.",["'Stücke' rendered 'elements'."]],"philippi-s52-018":["1. The matter of the proof [*Beweißthum*], *seu ratio probandi*.",["Latin kept in italics: *seu ratio probandi* ('or the ground of proving')."]],"philippi-s52-019":["2. The form, or the consequence from it.",[]],"philippi-s52-020":["3. The *conclusio*. For example: Everything that is contingent has a foreign cause; this I prove from contingency. Contingency, then, is the matter. But how it follows from this that everything contingent has a foreign cause, that is the consequence, the form. The matter in any proof can be regarded as the *medius terminus*, and the form as the relation of this to the proposition in hand. All proofs are either empirical or rational. Mathematical proofs, which have evidence [*Evidenz*], precision and [*Anscheinen*], are entitled *demonstrationes*. A rational proof in general is entitled *probatio*. But not all proofs, even when sufficient, are entitled *demonstrationes*; for example, that all temperance stands equally high, of this only an empirical *probatio* can be given. These are nevertheless just as clear as *demonstrationes*. *Probatio* is either *completa* or *incompleta*.",["'Anscheinen' is doubtful (perhaps an OCR or transcription slip, e.g. for 'Anschauung' or 'Einleuchten'); left as in the text.","The example 'daß alle Mäßigkeit gleich hoch stehe' is obscure and possibly corrupt in the transcript; translated literally.","The last sentence of the example ('Die sind aber eben so klar') is restated with 'These' for 'Die'; the antecedent (empirical probations) is a judgment.","Sentence division and 'Verhältniß' = 'relation'."]],"philippi-s52-021":["*Petere principium* [begging the question] means, in logic, to take as a foundation a proposition that has not yet been proved, though it is capable of proof, and to adopt it as the ground of proof of other propositions.",["Bracketed gloss supplied by me for the Latin term."]],"philippi-s52-022":["To prove means nothing other than to make the *identitas implicita* into an *explicita*; and to convince someone of a proposition means to make him aware that the proposition accords with the form of the understanding and its laws. From this it follows that those propositions are unprovable in which the identity is explicit, that is, evident. For example: The whole is equal to its parts taken together. If these propositions are rational and theoretical, they are entitled *axiomata*; if practical, *postulata*.",["'einen bewust machen' rendered 'make him aware'; 'offenbar' as 'evident'.","Latin terms kept in italics: *identitas implicita/explicita*."]],"philippi-s52-023":["Every general experience is provable; every single, particular one is unprovable.",["'erweißlich' rendered 'provable' (capable of proof)."]],"philippi-s52-024":["All provable propositions are either analytic or synthetic. Analytic propositions, or judgments, are those in which, when I analyse the subject, I find the predicate contained in it, and so perceive the identity through analysis. For example, 'Every body is divisible' is an analytic proposition. For it can be expressed thus: what I think under the mark of body, that I also think under that of divisibility; and when I analyse the concept of body, I find divisibility in it. All analytic propositions accordingly stand under the principle of identity and of contradiction, and are proved from it.",["'Satz des Wiederspruchs' = principle of contradiction.","Sentences divided slightly for clarity."]],"philippi-s52-025":["Synthetic propositions, on the other hand, are those in which the predicate does not lie in the notion of the subject but is added as something quite different. For example: Everything that happens has a sufficient ground. If I here analyse the subject, that is, what happens, the occurring [*das Entstehen*], I shall not find in it the sufficient ground that is supposed to belong to it. This is added to occurring, and connected with it, as something quite different.",["'Notion' kept for German 'Notion'.","'das ist' rendered 'that is'; the clause is somewhat irregular and has been smoothed."]],"philippi-s52-026":["Synthetic propositions cannot be known through reason [*Vernunfft*]. For reason cannot coordinate, but only subordinate. Synthetic propositions are either experiences or intuitive propositions of the understanding. The latter are met with in mathematics, the former in physics. Locke calls the first *propositiones coexistentiae*, which was not understood by many. Every empirical proposition is synthetic. For there I connect with the subject marks that are not contained in it.",["'anschauende Sätze' rendered 'intuitive propositions'.","'die erstem' (OCR for 'ersteren') is taken to refer to experiences (the empirical propositions), as the following 'jene in der Physik' suggests; 'Diese ... jene' refers to the intuitive propositions and the experiences respectively."]],"philippi-s52-027":["I note here only that the falsity that concerns the form is much greater than that which concerns the matter. For the matter is accidental, but the form is essential and applicable to many matters. A false form is almost incorrigible, for it is general and has an influence on many propositions. A false proposition can more readily be corrected. The chief falsity of a proposition, which consists more in the form than in the matter, is called the *proton pseudos* [πρῶτον ψεῦδος].",["The OCR 'irpcoTOV ysOSos' is garbled; restored as πρῶτον ψεῦδος (*proton pseudos*), the evident sense.","'accidentell' / 'essentiel' rendered 'accidental' / 'essential'."]],"philippi-s52-028":["Many errors arise from our not knowing sufficiently what is required for complete certainty; Locke therefore urgently recommends the study of mathematics.",[]],"philippi-s53-001":["§196",[]],"philippi-s53-002":["Something can be proved in two ways: directly, from connection with the grounds; and indirectly, where I show from the opposite the truth or falsity of the proposition. The indirect proof shows only that something is true or false, without indicating the sources from which it is so. It is easier to prove negatively than affirmatively.",["Sentence divided."]],"philippi-s53-003":["For if I show of a proposition even a single false consequence, I have proved that the proposition is false. But I should have to know all the consequences if I wished to infer from them to the ground and prove the truth of the proposition. Since, however, I do not know all the consequences, I infer to the truth of the proposition only by conjecture, and it is a hypothesis.",["'Schlüsse' (OCR/orthography for 'schließe') read as the verb 'infer'."]],"philippi-s53-004":["A direct proof, which flows from the connection with the grounds, is genetic and at the same time indicates the sources *a priori*. Indirect proofs are also entitled apagogic.",["'genetisch' kept as 'genetic'."]],"philippi-s53-005":["Strictly speaking, proofs can be conducted in two ways. 1. By inferring from the grounds to the proposition as to the consequences. These are the finest proofs. 2. From the consequences to the proposition as to the ground. In this way one cannot prove an affirmative proposition, because one does not know all the consequences, but one can indeed prove a negative one. For if a consequence is false, the whole proposition is false. This is an indirect or apagogic proof.",["The first member ('aus den Gründen auf den Satz als auf die Folgen') is as in the transcript; it is awkward (the proposition would be the consequence of the grounds), and I have kept it, with the sense that the proposition is inferred as a consequence."]],"philippi-s53-006":["Our representations are either concepts or intuitions.",[]],"philippi-s53-007":["Concept, *perceptio communis*, a representation valid in common.",["'gemeingültig' rendered 'valid in common'."]],"philippi-s53-008":["All perceptions are either *reflectivae* or *intuitivae*.",[]],"philippi-s53-009":["Through logical abstraction [*Absonderung*] we can obtain no concept, but can only give the knowledge a particular form.",["'Absonderung' rendered 'abstraction' as the logical sense suggests."]],"philippi-s53-010":["Every *conceptus communis* can be regarded as a *superior*.",[]],"philippi-s53-011":["§ 197",[]],"philippi-s53-012":["For demonstration, evidence is required, such that it is impossible to withhold [one's] assent. Assent cannot be refused only to that which falls within the senses. There are accordingly few demonstrations. All empirical propositions can have only *probationes*. An aesthetic *probatio* is when something persuades through taste and the passions. These *probationes* are not objective but only subjective. They are persuasions according to rules of appearance [*Erscheinung*]; for example, a man's decorum may serve as proof of his integrity. The spirit of demonstration is cultivated through mathematics.",["OCR 'Geis schmack' corrected to 'Geschmack'; 'umnöglich' to 'unmöglich'.","'one's' supplied in brackets.","'Demonstrirgeist' rendered 'spirit of demonstration'; 'excolirt' as 'cultivated'."]],"philippi-s54-001":["§ 200",[]],"philippi-s54-002":["The blind inclination to demonstrate is entitled the mania for demonstration [*Demonstrirsucht*]. *Sucht* [mania] is entitled an inclination that is not moderated. The mania for demonstration",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s54-003":["1. arises from a supposed facility in demonstrating. To prevent this, one must show the difficulties of a mode of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*].",["'Erkenntniß' in the singular here rendered 'a mode of knowledge' as the sense is of a particular piece of knowledge."]],"philippi-s54-004":["2. from charlatanry.",[]],"philippi-s54-005":["3. from pedantry. This consists in a lack of skill in applying knowledge, when one cannot judge the cases in which a piece of knowledge is suitable. Thus people imagined it necessary either not to judge at all or to demonstrate everything. Wolff did not have the mania for demonstration; his followers, however, did. It is found especially among the ignorant.",["OCR 'sieh', 'bev' corrected to 'sich', 'bey'.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge', with 'a piece of' supplied."]],"philippi-s54-006":["Many sceptics believed that one must accept nothing without demonstration. Some years ago a strong inclination to demonstrate prevailed; now a mania for shallowness seems to be gaining the upper hand. This arises when someone adapts his manner of thinking to the taste of the public. True taste loves thoroughness, as a Pope does.",["'wie ein Pope' is ambiguous: most likely Alexander Pope, the poet, rather than a pontiff; I have kept 'a Pope' but the reference is doubtful."]],"philippi-s54-007":["Which is better, shallowness or the mania for demonstrating? Shallowness; for it is a *malum negativum*, a lack, whereas the other is a *malum privativum*, a *vitium*. In the one case I merely do not use reason; in the other I believe that I am using it, and so deceive it; I use it perversely. The mania for demonstrating is opposed to taste as well as to reason, whereas in shallowness everything is in conformity with taste. Knowledge that is shallow can be gradually perfected; but knowledge that has already been demonstrated as perfectly certain cannot. That is a *vitium* in the form.",["\"Seichtigkeit\" rendered \"shallowness\"; \"Demonstrirsucht\" as \"mania for demonstrating\".","OCR \"em vitium\" corrected to \"ein vitium\"; \"privatiuum\" rendered *privativum*; \"Vernunf ft\" corrected to \"Vernunft\".","The stray \"\\\" after the opening question is treated as an OCR mark and omitted.","The final sentence is obscure: \"jene\" and \"die schon als vollkommen gewiß demonstrirt ist\" are taken to refer to the mania for demonstrating, and \"Das\" to that condition; this is a judgment."]],"philippi-s55-001":["§201",[]],"philippi-s55-002":["Sensation is a representation of the presence of an object, the cause of which lies in my activity.",["The German says the cause lies \"in meiner Thätigkeit\" (my activity), which seems at odds with the passivity asserted of sensation in the next passage; I translate as it stands. The original may be corrupt or Kant may have said otherwise (e.g. *Empfänglichkeit*); doubtful."]],"philippi-s55-003":["One cannot arrive at the *principium materiale* of sensation. A representation of sensation cannot be invented: the matter is given to me through the senses. Experiences and sensations agree in respect of matter. Both have been occasioned by the presence of the object; but in the former the form of reason is to be met with. In experience I am active, in sensation merely passive. A reflected sensation is experience; by this I understand a sensation expressed through a judgment. In sensations there are merely concepts and representations, but in experiences there are judgments. No animal has experience.",["\"Kern Thier\" corrected to \"Kein Thier\" (OCR).","\"jenen\" (the former) is taken to refer to experiences; this is a judgment."]],"philippi-s55-004":["No experience is mediate; for that is self-contradictory; all experience is immediate. What is meant by \"mediate experience\", however, is a judgment of reason in so far as it is inferred through experience.",["\"derVernunfft\" separated as \"der Vernunfft\". Sentence division at the semicolon/\"Es soll aber\" restated for clarity."]],"philippi-s55-005":["The form of all judgments is rational; in respect of matter a judgment may be empirical or rational.",[]],"philippi-s55-006":["All our modes of knowledge arise from three sources:",["\"Erkenntnisse\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\"."]],"philippi-s55-007":["1. from reason *a priori*,",["\"Vernunf f t\" corrected to \"Vernunft\"; original has no comma, supplied for English."]],"philippi-s55-008":["2. from my own experiences.",["The stray \"1\" after \"Erfahrungen.\" is taken as an OCR footnote or page mark and omitted."]],"philippi-s55-009":["3. from the experience of others. *A posteriori*. All proofs are rational. A merely empirical proof is not possible. For without reason no inference can be drawn.",["\"a Posterion-\" read as *a posteriori* with a dash; the placement of this phrase is uncertain and may belong to the preceding or following matter. The OCR \"a\" before \"Posterion\" is kept as it stands.","Running page marker (AA 24:446–447) not translated."]],"philippi-s55-010":["We must not confuse inferences from the experiences with inferences from [i.e. concerning] the experiences. For nothing is more deceptive than when one draws a secret inference of which we are conscious only mediately, and believes that one has derived it immediately from experience. I infer and conclude more than is contained in my senses. If we make the judgment which the appearance gives agree with the judgment about the cause of the appearance, we make out of a *judicium discursivum* an intuitive one [*intuitivum*]. It often happens that one believes one has a *judicium intuitivum primitivum* when one has only a discursive one.",["\"Schlüsse aus den Erfahrungen\" / \"von den Erfahrungen\": distinction kept literally with a bracketed gloss; the sense of \"von\" is doubtful.","\"Ich Schlüsse und folgre\" is garbled; read as \"Ich schließe und folgere\".","OCR \"emem\" corrected to \"einem\"; \"intuitmum\" corrected to *intuitivum* (and *primitivum*); \"Erscheinung\" rendered \"appearance\".","I have put \"intuitive\" in English followed by the Latin in brackets to keep the pairing with *discursivum*."]],"philippi-s55-011":["Experience is at all times a concept (*notio in sensu latiori*). Sense is the foundation of appearance; the understanding that of experience. No animal can have appearance or experience. It acts in accordance with sensations. Experiences are judgments and therefore universally valid, not sensations. A concept of experience can contain something false, if it arises from a false judgment; that is illusory experience [*Scheinerfahrung*]. In experiences errors are easily committed. The greatest evidence is not to be found in the empirical sciences, where errors are always possible, but in mathematics.",["\"Begrif\" read as \"Begriff\"; \"Erfahrungsbegrif\" as \"concept of experience\".","\"Sinn\" rendered \"sense\" (not *Sinnlichkeit*).","\"Wissenschafften\" rendered \"sciences\"; the sentence is reordered slightly for English."]],"philippi-s55-012":["§ 204",[]],"philippi-s55-013":["All proofs are rational in form. For the consequence must be seen through reason. A proof is entitled a proof from reason, however, when its matter is borrowed from reason. Meanwhile the first principles are always empirical. By means of pure reason we can prove something only of objects that are objects of the senses. In mathematics and metaphysics one proves through pure reason. What pure reason has demonstrated needs no proof from any experience, that is, needs no confirmation from it.",["\"heißt\" rendered \"is entitled\".","\"Consequens\" rendered \"consequence\"; it may mean the consequent. \"denObjecten\" and \"Ob jecte\" corrected (OCR).","The sentence \"Vermittelst der reinen Vernunfft ... Objecte der Sinne sind\" is puzzling as it stands, and is rendered literally; doubtful.","\"aus keiner Erfahrung bewiesen zu werden oder bedarf keiner Bestätigung\" smoothed to \"that is\"."]],"philippi-s55-014":["Experiences never prove wholly universal propositions, but only the possibility of propositions. They serve only to illustrate, by concrete cases, the abstract [*Abstractum*] proved through reason. From actuality no property can be borrowed from experience. In 's Gravesande's Logic both kinds of proof are compared.",["\"Logüc\" corrected to \"Logic\"; \"s'Gravesandes\" rendered 's Gravesande's.","\"Die dienen\" refers back to experiences (plural); \"Von der Wirklichkeit ... keine Beschaffenheit\" is obscure and rendered literally."]],"philippi-s55-015":["They serve only to illustrate, by concrete cases, the abstract [*Abstractum*] proved through reason.",["This duplicates a sentence in the preceding passage (here at AA 24:448); translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s56-001":["§205",[]],"philippi-s56-002":["All proofs which relate to actual objects of experience are conducted partly *a priori*, partly *a posteriori*.",[]],"philippi-s56-003":["The union of reason with experience takes place either through coordination or through subordination. In so far as rules of reason are applied to experience, this is a subordination. In so far as reason and experience, each taken separately, are a ground of a particular certainty, this is coordination. In experimental physics one seeks this union. For example, that pendulums, provided they do not exceed 5 degrees, make large and small arcs in equal time I first prove through reason. When reason demonstrates completely, the test from experience can always be borrowed.",["\"Wenn die Vernunfft Regeln auf die Erfahrung appliciret werden\" is ungrammatical; read as \"rules of reason are applied\".","\"wTerden\" corrected to \"werden\". The pendulum sentence is restated in two sentences for clarity; \"Perpendikel\" rendered \"pendulums\"."]],"philippi-s56-004":["Many coordinated proofs that are individually insufficient never give apodictic certainty. When many proofs are already given of a single thing, they are generally of no use.",["\"coordirdrte\" read as \"coordinirte\" (OCR)."]],"philippi-s57-001":["§206",[]],"philippi-s57-002":["To know [*Wissen*] means to believe with certainty, but to know in a degree sufficient for resolution and action. Belief is considered either",["\"Wißen\" rendered \"to know\" (the German is *Wissen*, not *Erkennen*); the phrase \"etwas erkennen\" is rendered \"to know\" in the second clause as the sense requires. The text breaks off with \"entweder\", continued by the next two passages."]],"philippi-s57-003":["1. as a belief in things, or",[]],"philippi-s57-004":["2. as a belief in persons.",[]],"philippi-s57-005":["In the first case I believe something; in the second I believe someone.",[]],"philippi-s57-006":["To hold something immediately true in such a degree as is necessary for action is entitled believing. But to hold something true on the testimony of others is entitled believing someone.",["\"heißt\" rendered \"is entitled\"; \"Aussage\" rendered \"testimony\"."]],"philippi-s57-007":["In many indifferent matters no personal belief is necessary. There need be no *character distinctionis*; every person is an equally great ground of certainty. But there are things of whose certainty we are convinced only through particular persons. They demand such a degree of personal credibility as only a particular character of persons can afford. For example, when we are informed of the divining-rod or of ghosts by persons of credible character, we begin to believe it.",["\"gicbt\" corrected to \"giebt\", \"ehi\" to \"ein\" (OCR). \"Character distinctionis\" kept in Latin; the \"character\" is probably a mark of distinction.","\"Wünschelruthe\" rendered \"divining-rod\"."]],"philippi-s57-008":["A testimony is any holding-true on account of the statement of another.",["\"Vorwahrhalten\" rendered \"holding-true\"."]],"philippi-s57-009":["Belief is either historical or moral. In historical belief something is held true because it is given out as true by another. Moral belief, on the other hand, consists in trust in the uprightness of a man with regard to the future, what is to happen. Historical belief consists in holding true what has happened. For example, when someone tells of his noble descent, we express thereby a historical belief. But when someone promises something, a moral belief. One must be faithful in promising, truthful in narrating.",["\"Rechtschaffenheit\" rendered \"uprightness\"; \"Zutrauen\" rendered \"trust\". The sentence \"Wenn aber jemand was verspricht; dann einen moralischen\" is elliptical; \"belief\" supplied by judgment."]],"philippi-s57-010":["Historical belief also occurs in men who have not the slightest honesty. Moral belief, on the other hand, occurs only in the upright. But if uprightness is added in one who tells something, our historical belief is so much the greater.",["\"um so Adel grösser\" is a garbled reading; read as \"um so viel größer\" (\"so much the greater\"). Doubtful."]],"philippi-s57-011":["Religious belief must be distinguished from theological belief. The former consists in the fidelity that must be kept in civil life; in conscientiousness, for example, in giving credit. Among the Greeks there was no credit. Hence *graeca fide mercari* [to trade with Greek good faith], meaning to buy something for ready money.",["\"Religionsglaube\" rendered \"religious belief\"; \"Jener\" refers to the religious belief. \"Treue\" rendered \"fidelity\".","The Latin phrase is kept in italics with a bracketed gloss added by me; the gloss is a literal rendering, and the idiom itself is Kant's point (the lecture states it ironically)."]],"philippi-s57-012":["Theological belief, like historical belief, has no relation to fidelity; it is logical and concerns the present and the past. It is of such a kind that one does good on faith and hope. Moral belief is the necessary bond of society. There is a moral belief in things, when I believe in a future reward of good actions because it flows from their nature, not because another has said so.",["\"Glaube\" in \"auf Glauben und Hofnung\" rendered \"faith\", since the sense is religious. \"Hofnung\" corrected to \"Hoffnung\". The statement that theological belief is \"logical\" is as in the text; doubtful whether the transcript is accurate."]],"philippi-s57-013":["Natural religious belief is found only in the virtuous.",[]],"philippi-s58-001":["On historical belief.",["Taken as a heading; the passage that follows is not given."]],"philippi-s58-002":["Since it [historical knowledge] is directed to the past, experiences are its objects. Speculative propositions and judgments in general are not objects of it. Whoever believes the judgments of others turns dogmatic knowledge into historical knowledge. Dogmata can admit no other ground of assent than one's own experience and one's own judgment. Of a witness, both logical and moral qualities are required:",["'Er'/'ihm' (\"Da er aufs Vergangene geht\") has no clear antecedent; I supply \"[historical knowledge]\" as the sense requires.","'contemplative Sätze' rendered 'speculative propositions' (Kant's usual contrast with practical); 'Erkenntniß' = knowledge; 'Beyfall' = assent.","'Dogmata' left in Latin form as a technical term.","OCR 'Erkennt niß' silently repaired."]],"philippi-s58-003":["1. He must have sufficient knowledge to make experiments [or observations: *Erfahrungen anzustellen*].",["'Erfahrungen anstellen' is literally 'to institute experiences'; 'observations' or 'experiments' is a choice, and the bracket marks it."]],"philippi-s58-004":["2. He must have retained well what he has experienced.",[]],"philippi-s58-005":["3. He must have the capacity to express himself intelligibly in words. He must",["The passage breaks off at 'Er muß', continued by the next item (4); translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s58-006":["4. have a good will to tell the truth, and no interest in telling falsehood.",["'keinen Nutzen haben die Falschheit zu sagen' is strictly 'have no advantage in telling falsehood'; rendered 'no interest' as the sense of the argument requires."]],"philippi-s58-007":["A statement concerning a dogma is no testimony, but only a statement concerning experience is. A witness is therefore one who declares an experience. The condition of the witness must also be examined: (a) logically, whether he was in circumstances to know the truth; (b) practically, whether he was not determined to his testimony by pressure of necessity.",["'Außage' (OCR; probably 'Aussage') read as 'statement'. 'Bedrängnisse' rendered 'pressure of necessity'; 'bestimmet' = determined."]],"philippi-s58-008":["The greatest part of our historical knowledge springs from belief. It is a great aid to the extension of our knowledge that we can adopt the experience of others. In historical belief one presupposes an inclination to truth which springs from love of honour. Since now learned people and people of rank pay more regard to honour than the common man, who can promise himself little influence of it upon his interest, one presumes greater credibility in the former than in the latter.",["Last sentence is long; kept as one sentence with the relative clause set off by commas. 'Glaubwürdigkeit' = credibility."]],"philippi-s59-001":["§208",[]],"philippi-s59-002":["An eyewitness is one who has his own experience; a hearsay witness is one who has the testimony of another. The latter has only a mediate credibility.",["'Hörenzeuge' rendered 'hearsay witness'; 'mittelbare' = mediate."]],"philippi-s59-003":["In a series of subordinated witnesses historical credibility diminishes; in a series of coordinated witnesses it increases. The series of coordinated testimonies is entitled public rumour; the series of subordinated testimonies, on the other hand, is entitled oral tradition. If in the coordinated testimonies the eyewitness is unknown, it is entitled a common report.",["'heißt' = 'is entitled'. 'Gerüchte' = rumour; 'gemeine Sage' = 'common report' (a choice; 'popular saying' is possible)."]],"philippi-s60-001":["§213",[]],"philippi-s60-002":["Moral unbelief signifies the lack of all confidence in the moral character of another. Credulity consists in an inclination to give assent, on logical grounds, to the credibility of a witness. Inexperience is its source.",["The sentence on credulity is awkward in the German ('aus logischen Gründen' with 'Beyfall zu geben'); I have kept the sense and made the construction plain. The sense may require 'on insufficient logical grounds'; I have not supplied it."]],"philippi-s60-003":["Logical unbelief does not lie in depravity of the heart, as moral unbelief does, but in inexperience in literature.",["'Pravität' = depravity; 'Litteratur' = literature in the old sense of learning."]],"philippi-s61-001":["On practical learned knowledge. §216",[]],"philippi-s61-002":["A mode of knowledge is entitled practical if it has a relation to the movement of our will. All practical knowledge either prescribes ends or teaches only the means to attain any ends whatever. All the doctrines of prudence and skill relate only to means; morality, to ends.",["OCR 'behebigen' read as 'beliebigen' (any ends whatever)."]],"philippi-s61-003":["The author speaks of the relation to the free will. The whole section does not belong to logic; practical perfection belongs to morals.",["OCR 'che' read as 'die'. 'Der Autor' is Meier."]],"philippi-s61-004":["All knowledge is either theoretical or practical. The practical may be either merely a speculation about practice or actual direction. Practical logic is a speculation about practice.",["'Praxi' (Latin dative/ablative form) rendered 'practice'."]],"philippi-s62-001":["On learned concepts.",[]],"philippi-s62-002":["A representation through the senses is entitled sensation; through the understanding, appearance; through reason, (1) a concept. A concept is a universal representation; representations which are not universal are not concepts. Sensations, however universally they may be taken, remain single representations. A single representation is an intuition. (2) For a concept it is required that, in the universal representation, I at the same time see its relation, in the series of subordination, to other representations. Reason subordinates. All appearances are only coordinations.",["The text assigns 'Erscheinung' to the understanding as written; this is Philippi's wording and I have not emended it. 'Erscheinung' = appearance.","OCR 'aUgemeine' and 'einzele' silently corrected ('allgemeine', 'einzelne'); 'Vor-Stellung' hyphenation removed.","The numerals 1. and 2. are kept where they stand; 'Begriff' follows the '1.' in the German."]],"philippi-s62-003":["The matter of all our knowledge lies in the senses. The degrees to which we ascend are",["Breaks off before the list that follows in the next passages."]],"philippi-s62-004":["1. *repraesentatio*, representation in general.",[]],"philippi-s62-005":["2. *conceptus*, universal concepts of the understanding.",[]],"philippi-s62-006":["3. *ideae*, universal concepts of reason. *Repraesentatio eius quod phaenomenis pluribus est commune, est conceptus empiricus.* [The representation of that which is common to several appearances is an empirical concept.]",["OCR 'idcae' read as 'ideae'. The bracketed English gloss of the Latin sentence is mine, supplied as the method allows."]],"philippi-s63-001":["§250",[]],"philippi-s63-002":["Common concepts are better than learned ones. A concept is a result of many representations which I take together and consider as one.",[]],"philippi-s63-003":["An aesthetic concept arises through the multitude of coordinated representations. Its matter is the relation of the marks to feeling and taste; its form is coordination. A concept of reason arises from the subordination of representations. Its matter is the laws of reason itself; its form, subordination. A learned concept is not one that contains much learning, but one to which the learned give great attention.",["'Merkmale' = marks. 'Vernunfft begriff' joined as 'concept of reason'."]],"philippi-s64-001":["§254",[]],"philippi-s64-002":["The author believes that we arrive at concepts through abstraction. But through abstraction we arrive at no knowledge; the knowledge must already be there before the abstraction. By abstraction we change only the form.",["OCR 'Erkennt niß' repaired. 'Abstraktion' spelling normalised to 'abstraction'."]],"philippi-s64-003":["All concepts are for us either",["Breaks off; the alternatives follow in the next items."]],"philippi-s64-004":["1. given, or",[]],"philippi-s64-005":["2. made by the understanding itself through combination. These are fictitious concepts. The given ones are given either through experience, *a posteriori*, or *a priori* through pure reason.",["'erdichtet' rendered 'fictitious'."]],"philippi-s64-006":["Hence all concepts are to be divided into *conceptus*",["Continues in the next items."]],"philippi-s64-007":["1. *abstractos* and *rationales*. A *conceptus abstractus* is, for example, the concept of the genus (*genus*). The matter arises through experience, but the form of universality through abstraction. The concept does not arise through abstraction; it is only made universal by it. Accordingly abstraction is not a second way, different from experience, of arriving at concepts; for it is only the means of making experiences universal.",["'Experiences' for 'Erfahrungen' is used where the plural is needed."]],"philippi-s64-008":["2. We also have pure concepts of reason, which are given *a priori*. Such concepts are the object of pure philosophy, that is, of metaphysics and morals. They are concepts which do not arise through what falls under the senses, which are not fictitious, and which cannot be abstracted from representations through the senses. Whence then do we have them? The understanding acquired them for itself by attending, on occasion of experiences, to its own procedure.",["'Moral' rendered 'morals'. 'Verfahren' = procedure."]],"philippi-s64-009":["It is a *vitium subreptionis* when one passes off as an experience from the senses what is really a concept of reason; as, for example, Hutcheson and Shaftesbury took the concept of virtue to be an empirical concept.",["OCR 'Welten' read as 'hielten' (took/held); the reading is a conjecture from the sense. 'Erfahrung aus den Sinnen' kept as written."]],"philippi-s64-010":["Pure concepts of the understanding therefore express the laws according to which the understanding proceeds. And since every object is nothing other than something which the understanding posits, in the case of a sensation, with certain determinations, it follows that everything which understanding and reason know through concepts is objective, but sensations are something subjective.",["Restated slightly for clarity; 'Be-Stimmungen' = determinations; 'Object' = object. 'Gegenstand' elsewhere is also 'object'."]],"philippi-s64-011":["Whoever has experiences does not on that account yet have empirical concepts. To acquire them one must attend to many objects and to their agreement, and distinguish the *notas primarias*, that is, the essential, from the *notis secundariis* [OCR: *secundasiis*], or the accidental. A philosophical mind is required for this.",["OCR 'secundasiis' emended to 'secundariis' (doubtful, and Latin case as printed is 'notis'). 'Geist' = mind."]],"philippi-s65-001":["§259",[]],"philippi-s65-002":["In logical abstraction we compare many concepts with one another and see what they have in common. If many concepts have the same mark, I make the mark into a concept in which all these concepts are then contained.",["'Absonderung' rendered 'abstraction' since the context (§259) concerns *Abstraktion*; the German word is 'separation/isolation'."]],"philippi-s65-003":["Abstraction is an act of subordination. I subordinate concepts under one.",[]],"philippi-s65-004":["Abstraction can take place in the case of pure concepts of reason. But because these are taken not from objects but from the activity of pure reason, they are not called abstract, abstracted concepts. Professor Kant, in his Disputation, calls them abstracting [*absondernde*, *abstrahirende*] concepts.",["'Disputation' refers to Kant's Latin inaugural dissertation (1770); the identification is mine."]],"philippi-s65-005":["In experience abstraction takes place most. Hence the universal empirical concepts are properly entitled abstracted. All abstract concepts are universal, but not all universal concepts are abstract.",[]],"philippi-s66-001":["§260",[]],"philippi-s66-002":["All fictions arise either through arbitrary combination of that which is separate in experience, or through arbitrary separation of that which is combined in experience.",["'Erdichtungen' = fictions."]],"philippi-s66-003":["A concept is entitled a mark [*Merkmal*] when it belongs, as a part, to the whole concept. But the mark is also, in its turn, a ground of knowledge [*Erkenntnißgrund*], that is, a universal concept under which are contained all the concepts in which it is contained as a part.",["Original has \"Erkennt -nißgrund\" (line-break); silently joined. \"Erkenntnißgrund\" rendered \"ground of knowledge\".","Second sentence: \"allgemeiner Begriff\" rendered \"universal concept\"; I added \"that is\" to bring out the apposition after \"oder\". The pronoun \"es\" is resolved as the mark."]],"philippi-s66-004":["A mark can therefore be considered in a twofold respect [*respectu*] ... It is",["The passage breaks off; the ellipsis is in the source and is kept. The continuation is in the next passage."]],"philippi-s66-005":["1. a universal concept under which belong all the concepts of which it is the mark. This is in respect of subordination [*respectu subordinationis*].",[]],"philippi-s66-006":["2. It is a part of each of these concepts, in respect of coordination [*respectu coordinationis*].",[]],"philippi-s66-007":["The multitude of things that are contained under a concept, as a common mark [*nota communis*], constitutes the sphere [*Sphaera*] of the common concept [*Conceptus communis*]. The more things, therefore, a concept [*conceptus*] has in common, the greater is its sphere.",["\"Je mehrern Dingen ... gemein ist\" is rendered \"the more things ... has in common\"; strictly, the concept is common to more things. \"Sphäre\" is rendered \"sphere\" (the logical extension)."]],"philippi-s66-008":["The more a concept contains under it, the more things it is common to, the less it contains in it; that is, the smaller a part it is of each concept which it comprehends under itself.",["\"Begrif\" is the spelling in the source; \"fassen\" is rendered \"comprehends\"."]],"philippi-s66-009":["Just as the marks in coordination agree in constituting the universal concept, so they also stand to one another in subordination. For a concept has marks; these in turn have marks of their own, and so on in a series of subordination [*in serie subordinationis*]. The more sensible [*sinnlich*] the concepts are, the greater is the coordination of the marks; the more they contain in them, but the less under them. The more the concepts are derived from reason [*Vernunft*], the more they contain under them, but the less in them.",["OCR corrections: \"wemger\" read \"weniger\"; \"Vernunf f t\" read \"Vernunft\".","\"sinnlicher\" is rendered \"more sensible\" (pertaining to sense), not \"more sensuous\". \"Je mehr die Begriffe aus der Vernunft sind\" is rendered \"derived from reason\"."]],"philippi-s66-010":["Euler has tried to make this sensible by means of figures. But the difficulty is that only the coordination, and not the subordination, can be shown in figures. For the senses, or rather the understanding, can only coordinate; reason subordinates.",["\"Es ist aber schlimmer daß\" is smoothed to \"the difficulty is that\". \"Vernunfft\" is read as \"Vernunft\"; I render it \"reason\".","\"sinnlich zu machen\" is rendered \"make sensible\" (render perceptible to the senses)."]],"philippi-s66-011":["A concept [*Conceptus*] is entitled superior [*superior*] in so far as it contains another concept under it, and inferior [*inferior*] in so far as it is contained under one. What in one relation is a superior concept can in another become an inferior concept, and conversely.",[]],"philippi-s66-012":["The higher a concept is, that is, the more remote in the series of subordinates, the greater its sphere, until the absolutely superior concept, that is, the highest, is of the greatest sphere and contains all objects under it.",["Latin rendered in English because it is not a technical term here but running Latin text; the technical words *conceptus*, *sphaera* are given in the neighbouring passages."]],"philippi-s66-013":["The lower a concept is, the smaller its sphere, until the lowest concept is of no sphere, that is, is singular [*singularis*].",["\"nullius sphaerae\" is rendered \"of no sphere\"."]],"philippi-s66-014":["The concept [*Conceptus*] whose sphere is only a part of another sphere is narrower [*angustior*].",[]],"philippi-s66-015":["Thus the highest concept, that is, that of an object in general, and the lowest, that is, the singular, are the limits of subordination.",[]],"philippi-s66-016":["Every common concept [*conceptum communem*], when it is considered as contained in a lower mark [*nota inferiori*], I consider *in concreto*. For example, when I speak of education in general and then pass to the education of princes, I consider the concept of education *in concreto*.",["OCR: \"coneeptum\" read \"conceptum\"; \"Z. E.\" expanded as \"For example\"."]],"philippi-s66-017":["Every common concept [*Conceptus communis*] is entitled a species [*species*] with regard to its superior, and a genus [*genus*] with regard to its inferior. There is therefore a highest genus [*genus summum*] and a lowest species [*species infima*] as the limits of subordination.",["OCR: \"suinmum\" read \"summum\"."]],"philippi-s66-018":["This is logical subordination, in which I represent to myself a concept as contained under a universal one. Real subordination, however, is the subordination of the consequence to the ground, when by the ground everything that is contained in the consequence is posited. Early in youth we already subordinate concepts logically; but the faculty of subordinating really we attain only gradually.",["\"Folge\" rendered \"consequence\" and \"Grund\" \"ground\"; \"real\" kept as \"real\". \"dazu erlangen wir nur nach und nach das Vermögen\" is smoothed."]],"philippi-s66-019":["Every common concept [*conceptum communem*], when it is considered as contained under the superior mark [*nota superiori*], I consider *in abstracto*. What is abstract I judge much of few things; what is concrete, little of many things...",["The source reads: \"Das abstracte urtheile ich von viel Dingen wenig in concreto von wenig Dingen viel.\" i.e. in the abstract I judge little of many things, in the concrete much of few things; my first draft above is therefore wrong and is replaced by the following rendering.","Corrected rendering: \"I judge the abstract, of many things, little; *in concreto*, of few things, much.\""]],"philippi-s66-020":["Through the understanding I know little in things, but distinctly; through the senses, much, but confusedly.",["\"deutlich\" rendered \"distinctly\", \"verworren\" \"confusedly\"."]],"philippi-s66-021":["A distinct knowledge [*deutliche Erkenntniß*] through insufficient marks is entitled a description [*Beschreibung*]. It contains such marks as suffice to distinguish the thing from many others; the definition [*Definition*], however, distinguishes it from all things.",["\"Die enthält\" is resolved as referring to the description."]],"philippi-s67-001":["On Explanations [*Erklärungen*].",["\"Erklärung\" is rendered \"explanation\" here, but in the following passages it also covers \"definition\" in the wider sense; I keep the same word."]],"philippi-s67-002":["To give the explanation of explanation is not so difficult as it seems.",[]],"philippi-s67-003":["The logical perfections ascend thus:",[]],"philippi-s67-004":["1. Clearness, when I am conscious of a representation.",[]],"philippi-s67-005":["2. Distinctness, when I am conscious of the marks of the representation.",["Sentence is continued across passages 005–006 (\"bewust / bin\"); rendered here and in 006 as it stands."]],"philippi-s67-006":["[am conscious.]",["This is only the broken-off verb \"bin\" of the preceding sentence; it is put in square brackets to show it is a supplied fragment, not a separate sentence."]],"philippi-s67-007":["3. When I represent to myself clearly all the marks that belong to it.",["The following passage names this third perfection \"Completudo\"; the heading seems to have been displaced in the transcript."]],"philippi-s67-008":["Completeness [*Completudo*]. 4. Precision [*Praecisio*]. When I reduce the marks to the smaller limits [*ad minores terminos*].",["OCR: \"terniinos\" read \"terminos\"; \"reducire\" read \"reducere\"/\"reduce\". The position of \"Completudo\" before \"4.\" is as in the source."]],"philippi-s67-009":["A distinct concept is entitled complete when the marks taken together are equal to the whole concept. I can know marks of the marks, and indeed *in indefinitum*, continuing always. Thus the intensity of distinctness has many degrees.",["\"intensiuitaet\" read \"intensity\"; \"zwar\" rendered \"indeed\"."]],"philippi-s67-010":["An adequate concept [*conceptus adaequatus*] is a definition.",[]],"philippi-s67-011":["The matter of definition consists of elementary propositions [*Elementarsätze*], that is, first marks and representations, which I bring out through judgments. The form is the completeness of the marks and their precision, so that the concept is adequate.",[]],"philippi-s67-012":["There is a difference between making a concept distinct and making a distinct concept.",[]],"philippi-s67-013":["If a concept is given to me as to its matter, or its marks, and I seek the marks clear by analysis [*per analysin*], I make a given concept distinct. But if I bring forth a concept together with its distinctness, I make for myself a distinct concept.",["\"suche ... die Merkmale klar\" is translated literally; the sense is \"seek to make the marks clear\"."]],"philippi-s67-014":["Definitions are either nominal, when the concept expresses sufficiently all that I know of the thing; or real, when the concept suffices for deriving from it everything that belongs to the thing itself (the object).",["\"zureicht\" read as \"suffices\"; \"Object\" is rendered \"object\"."]],"philippi-s67-015":["The former are easier; for there I can soon find all the marks which, taken together, exhaust my whole concept. But it is difficult to specify the marks from which all the determinations that belong to the thing itself are to be derived. The nominal definition is also entitled the logical essence, that is, the first fundamental concept of all that I know in the thing. The real definition, however, must contain everything that is possible in the thing itself. I am to specify not only all the marks that make up my concept, but all the marks that will ever be obtained of it. I am to define the thing, not the name.",["OCR: \"defmiren\" read \"definiren\". \"Die erstern\" is read for \"Die erstem\". \"Wesen\" rendered \"essence\"."]],"philippi-s67-016":["Many definitions can be used as true propositions, although not as definitions.",[]],"philippi-s67-017":["If a proposition is therefore false even as a definition, it may yet be true as a judgment. The definition gives a sufficiency for every purpose.",["\"Sufficienz\" rendered \"sufficiency\"."]],"philippi-s67-018":["If the mark is not a ground of the distinctness of the subject, the concept which I thereby obtain is indistinct.",["\"undeutlich\" rendered \"indistinct\"."]],"philippi-s67-019":["The marks in a definition must not be subordinated but coordinated. Otherwise it is entitled an indeterminate, vague concept [*Conceptus indeterminatus, vagus*].",[]],"philippi-s67-020":["The concept is adequate to the thing [*Conceptus rem adaequat*]; that is, the definition has in it neither more nor fewer marks than belong to the concept.",["\"adaequat\" is taken as a verb form (\"is adequate to\"); the Latin phrase is partly garbled in the source and the reading is doubtful."]],"philippi-s67-021":["1.) All definitions of empirical concepts are nominal. Real ones are not possible. For these are supposed to specify all the determinations and marks; but of the things of nature I know only very few of them.",[]],"philippi-s67-022":["The more concrete an empirical concept [*Erfahrungsbegriff*] is, the less can it be defined. If it is quite abstract, for example *body*, the thing can be done.",["\"so gehts an\" is rendered \"the thing can be done\"."]],"philippi-s67-023":["I lack the completeness of the marks in the case of objects of the senses. More and more marks are discovered every day. Hence it is useless in physics to look for definitions.",["\"Physiken\" rendered \"physics\"."]],"philippi-s67-024":["Empirical definitions serve only to fix the meaning of a word; they are grammatical, not logical.",[]],"philippi-s67-025":["II. In the case of intellectual concepts real definitions are possible, because my concept contains everything that the thing is. My concept constitutes the thing.",[]],"philippi-s67-026":["It is also necessary to define intellectual concepts, because through my concept the thing is given.",[]],"philippi-s67-027":["How, then, do I make definitions of intellectual concepts? I make them *per analysin*, namely by unfolding and developing that out of whose composition the understanding has formed the concept for itself, that is, the elementary propositions. For at the basis of every intellectual concept there lie elementary propositions, out of which the understanding has made it for itself. For example, body is a concept of reason; it is what is common to all sensations. If I analyse it further, I find that the concept of body is composed of these elementary propositions:",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Hegen' → 'liegen', 'Eiementarsätze' → 'Elementarsätze', 'Vernunff tbegrif' → 'Vernunftbegriff'.","'Vernunftbegrif' is rendered 'concept of reason', though the sentence began with 'intellectual concepts' (intellectuale Begriffe); the sense of 'it is what is common to all sensations' is as given in the transcript and is perhaps garbled, but is translated as it stands.","*per analysin*: Latin kept, in italics."]],"philippi-s67-028":["1. of extension",[]],"philippi-s67-029":["2. of impenetrability.",[]],"philippi-s67-030":["3. of inertia.",["'Trägheit' is rendered 'inertia'."]],"philippi-s67-031":["4. of shape. So I obtain the definition of body. In mathematics all things are likewise given through concepts, so that there too, and indeed there most easily, real definitions can be given. [The mathematician] says: let us think a figure that has 6 equal sides and call it a hexagon; very well, then it is a hexagon.",["'Er sagt' has no antecedent in the passage; I supply '[The mathematician]' in brackets on the strength of the context (mathematics).","'Sechseck' is rendered 'hexagon'."]],"philippi-s67-032":["Here I make distinct concepts arbitrarily by means of definitions; but in philosophy I make given concepts distinct by means of definitions.",["'willkürlich' rendered 'arbitrarily'; 'deutlich' as 'distinct'."]],"philippi-s67-033":["If, then, I want to make definitions, I must",["The sentence is continued in the next two items."]],"philippi-s67-034":["1. seek true propositions",[]],"philippi-s67-035":["2. pick out all the marks that lie in the concept and make them clear.",["'Merkmale' rendered 'marks'."]],"philippi-s67-036":["In the case of [arbitrary] definitions, as in pure reason and mathematics, the confused concept comes first.",["The OCR reads 'anämischen'; this is plainly corrupt. I conjecture 'willkürlichen' (arbitrary) from the context, or possibly 'synthetischen', and have bracketed it. The reading is doubtful.","The statement that the confused concept comes first in mathematics is translated as it stands; the transcript may be defective here."]],"philippi-s67-037":["Completeness (*completudo*) and precision (*praecisio*) therefore constitute an adequate concept, and not, as with Wolff, merely that the marks of a concept be again distinct.",["'Praecision' is rendered 'precision', with the Latin supplied in brackets by analogy with 'Completudo'. 'Wolff' is the philosopher Christian Wolff."]],"philippi-s67-038":["For a definition it is required that all the marks be stated. But if I have only some of the marks of a concept, can I not already infer from them everything that follows from it, without having all the other marks together? For example, I know of body only that it is divisible. Can I not infer with certainty from this that it is composite, although I do not know impenetrability, extension, etc., etc., which are also marks of body?",["'Schlüssen' (infinitive 'schließen') rendered 'infer'; OCR capitalisation retained is ignored."]],"philippi-s67-039":["Four acts are necessary for the examination and construction of a definition.",[]],"philippi-s67-040":["1. whether the definition, as a proposition, is true",["OCR 'l.' read as '1.'."]],"philippi-s67-041":["2. whether, as a concept, it is distinct",[]],"philippi-s67-042":["3. whether, as a distinct concept, it is full (*ausführlich*), complete, and whether",["'ausführlich' is rendered 'full', with the German given, since the sense depends on it (cf. 'ausführlicher Begrif' in no. 44)."]],"philippi-s67-043":["the marks are true?",["Continues the sentence from the preceding item."]],"philippi-s67-044":["4. whether, as a full concept, it is precise; whether some",[]],"philippi-s67-045":["marks are not one and the same.",["Continues the preceding item."]],"philippi-s67-046":["All arbitrary concepts must first be defined. Unless definitions go before, I can say nothing. But with given concepts definitions are often not even necessary, still less need they come first.",[]],"philippi-s67-047":["The origin of arbitrary [concepts] is synthesis. From analysis no concept can arise.",["The text reads 'willkürlicher Sätze' (arbitrary propositions), though 'Begriffe' is clearly intended, as the next sentence speaks of 'Begriff'; I translate with the bracketed correction, the reading being doubtful.","OCR 'x4us' is read as 'Aus'."]],"philippi-s67-048":["All definitions are meant to serve, through the analysis of them, to bring us to know the object better.",["'derselben' is ambiguous (the definitions or the concepts); I take it of the definitions."]],"philippi-s67-049":["From this one sees that propositions can already be used before the definition, and that definitions are not always necessary. They are necessary only when I need all the marks.",["OCR 'smd' read as 'sind'."]],"philippi-s67-050":["I can use the elementary propositions before all definitions; and they must also go before.",[]],"philippi-s67-051":["For in philosophy, where concepts are defined *per analysin*, definitions are not the first but the last.",[]],"philippi-s67-052":["In mathematics, where the explanation creates the object, they must go first.",["'Erklärung' rendered 'explanation'; 'sie' refers to definitions."]],"philippi-s67-053":["Definitions are to be a means to distinctness.",[]],"philippi-s67-054":["Distinctness is obtained only through judgments.",[]],"philippi-s67-055":["Every definition is to be regarded as a judgment, in which the *definitum* constitutes the subject and the *definitio* the predicate. If the predicate is the same as the subject, explicitly or implicitly, the definition is tautological and no means to distinctness.",[]],"philippi-s67-056":["An explanation is *latior suo definito* [wider than what it defines] if it does not suffice for knowledge of the difference; *angustior suo definito* [narrower than what it defines] if it does not suffice for knowledge of the sameness. In the former case it has too many marks, in the latter too few. Witlings make definitions of the former kind, subtle quibblers (*Grübler*) those of the second kind.",["The English glosses of the Latin are mine. The wording of the explanation follows the German, which is itself somewhat odd (the 'too many/too few marks' gloss seems at odds with 'wider/narrower'); I have not altered it.","OCR 'Defimtionen' read as 'Definitionen'."]],"philippi-s67-057":["If definitions are true merely as propositions, even if they do not have all the marks, they can often still have as many as suffice to distinguish the thing from all those I know, or merely to give a fair degree of distinctness; and then they are already very serviceable, and one must not reject them on account of the defect of completeness (*completudo*). Most of Wolff's definitions are of this sort.",[]],"philippi-s67-058":["Definitions (*definitiones*) of arbitrary concepts, as in mathematics, can never err; for the *definitum* there is nothing other than what I have fixed through the definition. But the philosopher's are [subject to error] constantly. If he can infer from the preliminary or elementary propositions as much as he needs, he must rather leave defining alone and use it only in case of need.",["'aber des Philosophen seine sind beständig' is doubtful as printed (perhaps 'beständig irrig' or similar has dropped out, or the OCR is faulty). I supply '[subject to error]' in brackets as the sense the contrast requires; the reading is uncertain.","'Schlüssen' read as 'schließen' (infer)."]],"philippi-s67-059":["No object of experience can be defined.",["'erkläret werden' rendered 'defined' (explained), in accordance with the context of definition."]],"philippi-s67-060":["With all arbitrary concepts the definition must make the beginning.",[]],"philippi-s67-061":["§ 280",["Section number of Meier's text."]],"philippi-s67-062":["A verbal explanation (*Worterklärung*) is the distinct representation of the meaning of the word. Considered at bottom, they are not means for obtaining a distinct concept: they give me only better-known synonymous words.",["OCR 'Worterklarung' read as 'Worterklärung'."]],"philippi-s67-063":["Real explanations are said to have a twofold use,",["Continued in the next item."]],"philippi-s67-064":["1. an outer, to distinguish the thing from all others, and 2. an inner, to derive all the determinations of the thing.",["'Bestimmungen' rendered 'determinations'."]],"philippi-s67-065":["All definitions are real, for nominal definitions are properly not definitions (*definitiones*). Yet they are of uncommon use in rational modes of knowledge, in order that I may make clear to myself the sense of a concept of reason. But these concepts also admit of real explanations, which contain everything that belongs constantly to the thing, as in morals and metaphysics. In short, intellectual concepts can be explained both nominally and really; concepts of experience nominally only.",["'Erkenntnissen' rendered 'modes of knowledge', in accordance with the method.","OCR 'nominalitcr' read as 'nominaliter'; 'Vernunff tbegrifs' as 'Vernunftbegriffs'."]],"philippi-s67-066":["A mathematician who defines a given concept goes badly wrong, for he is then philosophising. But the mathematician must not explain through analysis, nor the philosopher through synthesis. Through the definition, in mathematics, the concept is first given to me.",["OCR 'Matheniatikus' read as 'Mathematikus'; 'Mathematic' left as written."]],"philippi-s67-067":["If I judge anything about an arbitrary concept, I must first fix it. If they are modes of knowledge of the understanding, analysis must explain it.",["'Verstandeserkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge of the understanding'. The 'it' of 'ihn' in the second sentence is taken to be the concept."]],"philippi-s67-068":["Analytic definitions never make the beginning. Synthetic ones must always make the beginning.",[]],"philippi-s67-069":["Description is a comparative definition. All objects of experience allow no definition, but only description. If the definition is objectively complete, it is a real definition; if subjectively complete, a nominal definition.",[]],"philippi-s67-070":["§ 284",["Section number of Meier's text."]],"philippi-s67-071":["Definitions must not be tautological in form, although they may be identical in matter.",["OCR 'avtologisch' read as 'tautologisch'; 'nacht' as 'nach'."]],"philippi-s67-072":["The mania for defining springs from the inclination to have everything complete, and from the mania for demonstrating, which finds its best progress thereby, and from imitation of mathematics.",["'Definirsucht' rendered 'mania for defining'; 'Demonstrirsucht' 'mania for demonstrating'. 'alle' ('everything') is as in the text; the object is elliptical."]],"philippi-s67-073":["Through the mania for defining which Wolff introduced, men withdrew from the analysis of concepts and unlearned true philosophy.",["OCR 'Defhürsucht' read as 'Definirsucht'; 'Avahre' as 'wahre'."]],"philippi-s68-001":["Division § 285",["'Eintheilung' rendered 'division'; § number of Meier's text."]],"philippi-s68-002":["Every concept of the understanding is universal. Every universal concept has many [things] under it, and this constitutes its sphere.",["'Sphäre' rendered 'sphere' (*sphaera*)."]],"philippi-s68-003":["Logical division is not the division of the concept but the division of the sphere of the concept. The universal concept, which has much under it, has less in it than each of the things which it has under it.",[]],"philippi-s68-004":["For logical division there is required",["Continued in the following items."]],"philippi-s68-005":["1. disjunction: the opposition between the members; I must be able to say either—or.",[]],"philippi-s68-006":["2. All the members of the division, taken together disjunctively, must be reciprocal with the concept (*conceptui reciproque*) and make up an equal sphere with it. One must guard here against joining members of a subdivision to the members of the division.",["*conceptui reciproque*: Latin kept; 'Sphaera' rendered 'sphere'.","'disiunctiue' read as *disjunctive*, rendered 'disjunctively'."]],"philippi-s68-007":["Divisions serve in the sciences for retaining [what has been learned].",["'zum Behalten' is elliptical; I supply a bracketed object in the sense of memory."]],"philippi-s69-001":["On Learned Judgments.",["Heading. 'gelehrten Urtheilen' rendered literally as 'learned judgments'."]],"philippi-s69-002":["When I judge, I compare a concept with others and investigate whether they belong to it or are opposed to it, that is, whether or not they are marks [*Merkmale*] of it. Only through this act, through judging, do concepts become distinct. A distinct concept is nothing other than a judgment.",["*Merkmal* rendered \"mark\", the usual English term for a partial concept constituting a concept; *deutlich* = \"distinct\".","*Begrif* (sic) silently normalised to \"concept\"."]],"philippi-s69-003":["If the mark is connected with the concept, this is a judgment of connection; if it is opposed to it, it is a judgment of conflict.",["*Wiederstreit* rendered \"conflict\"; *Verknüpfung* \"connection\"."]],"philippi-s69-004":["In all judgments matter and form are to be distinguished. The matter is the concept of the thing, and then the concept which I connect with it as a mark, or separate from it. The concept of the thing which I wish to make distinct through judgments is entitled the subject; the concept which I regard as either belonging to the former as a mark or as opposed to it is entitled the predicate.",["The first sentence of the second statement is loosely constructed in the German; I have kept its sense without adding to it.","\"is entitled\" for *heißt*."]],"philippi-s69-005":["The form is the relation of the subject to the predicate, and is expressed by the copula.",[]],"philippi-s69-006":["In this relation I see either the connection between predicate and subject or the conflict. In the first case the judgment is affirmative; in the second, negative, where the *non* attaches to the copula. This negation, however, is to be understood only of the form, not of the matter, which may be as it will.",["\"which may be as it will\": *die mag seyn wie sie wolle*; the antecedent is the matter."]],"philippi-s69-007":["If the *non* is attached not to the copula but to the matter, the judgment is affirmative, as *anima est non mortalis*. Such judgments, in which the copula is not negative, are entitled infinite propositions.",["*unendliche Säzze* = \"infinite propositions\" (*judicia infinita*)."]],"philippi-s69-008":["The subject is entitled *terminus minor* because it is contained under the predicate; the predicate, *terminus maior*, because it contains the concept of the subject and, besides this, other concepts as well. It is a *nota communis*.",["OCR \"terrninus\" corrected to *terminus*.","\"It\" in the last sentence is presumably the predicate."]],"philippi-s69-009":["No definition can be given through non-essential marks.",["*außerwesentliche Merkmahle* rendered \"non-essential marks\"."]],"philippi-s69-010":["The division [*Theilung*] of a concept consists in this, that I see what is contained in the concept. *Divisio realis*.",["*Theilung* (analysis into parts) is distinguished from *Eintheilung* (classification) in the next passage; both would be \"division\" in English, so the German is given."]],"philippi-s69-011":["The classification [*Eintheilung*] of a concept consists in this, that I see how much is contained under it.",["OCR \"Einth eilung\" corrected. \"Classification\" chosen to mark the contrast with *Theilung*; \"how much\" (*wie viel*) refers to the extension."]],"philippi-s69-012":["The less a concept can be divided [*getheilt*], the more it can be classified [*eingetheilt*].",[]],"philippi-s69-013":["Concepts which are contained under another are entitled *membra dividentia*.",[]],"philippi-s69-014":["To take disjunctive concepts together is to represent their opposition.",["Obscure; translated literally."]],"philippi-s69-015":["The representation of things through a concept is a judgment.",[]],"philippi-s69-016":["In respect of quantity all judgments are either universal or particular; singular judgments belong under the universal. In respect of quality, they consist in affirmation and negation.",["The second sentence is elliptical; \"they consist\" is a smoothing supply."]],"philippi-s69-017":["Judgment rests on the relation in thinking between the predicate and the subject, not on the thinking of the concept.",[]],"philippi-s69-018":["§ 297",[]],"philippi-s69-019":["A judgment which is immediately certain has no condition. Judgments which are known *per medium terminum* are conditioned. If a condition which was not contained in the concept of the subject is added to it accidentally, the judgment is entitled a determinate [*bestimmtes*] judgment; for example, all men must (naturally) die. But if the predicate is compared with the subject by means of a condition which lies in the subject, this must be developed *per analysin*; for example, whatever exists exists somewhere and at some time. Here it must be developed how the concept of existence stands under the concept of place as a condition, although it is not possible [?].",["The last clause, *ob es gleich nicht möglich*, is broken off or defective; translated as it stands, and the bracketed query is mine.","OCR \"hegt\" read as *liegt*, \"Conditio n\" as *Condition*; \"Subjekt\" etc. normalised.","*bestimmtes Urtheil* rendered \"determinate judgment\" (cf. *Bestimmung*)."]],"philippi-s69-020":["All empirical propositions without exception are synthetic. All synthetic propositions of reason are never objective but subjective; for example, everything that happens has a sufficient ground.",["*Vernunfftsätze* rendered \"propositions of reason\"."]],"philippi-s69-021":["This is not an analytic but a synthetic proposition, and therefore subjective; it expresses that I can represent nothing to myself through reason except by means of a ground.",[]],"philippi-s70-001":["§301",[]],"philippi-s70-002":["A judgment is either",["Breaks off; continued in the following passages."]],"philippi-s70-003":["a. universal, where the predicate is compared with all the concepts which belong to the sphere",["Continues in the next passage. *Sphaera* = \"sphere\"."]],"philippi-s70-004":["of the subject.",[]],"philippi-s70-005":["(β. where it is compared with only some, that is a particular",["The OCR \"(3.\" is read as the Greek β, matching α and γ."]],"philippi-s70-006":["judgment.",[]],"philippi-s70-007":["γ. a singular judgment, where it is compared with only a single concept.",["OCR \"y.\" read as γ; \"Avird\" as *wird*."]],"philippi-s70-008":["If the sphere of the subject is regarded as wholly contained in the notion of the predicate, or as wholly outside it, the judgment is in the first case a universal affirmative, and in the latter case a universal negative judgment.",[]],"philippi-s70-009":["A singular concept has no sphere; it is a point, and must therefore likewise fall either wholly outside the sphere of the predicate or wholly within it. Consequently a singular judgment is equivalent to universal judgments.",["*den allgemeinen Urtheilen ein einzelnes gleich*: \"equivalent to universal judgments\" is a smoothing of the dative plural."]],"philippi-s70-010":["If the sphere of the subject falls partly within that of the predicate and partly not, this yields the particular judgment.",["*particulaire*, left as \"particular\"."]],"philippi-s70-011":["A *judicium modale* is one in which the predicate is ascribed to the subject under a special determination [*Bestimmung*]; for example, all men are naturally mortal. In contrast to it is the judgment which has no such determination.",[]],"philippi-s70-012":["In all judgments I compare nothing other than the spheres of concepts, and see whether one is contained in the other or not.",[]],"philippi-s70-013":["The universality of affirmative propositions rests partly on this, that the condition of truth already lies in the notion of the subject, partly on this, that if it does not lie in it, it is added; for example, all men are changeable. The condition of truth lies in the concept of man.",["OCR \"hegt\" read as *liegt*."]],"philippi-s70-014":["Judgments are either simple or composite. A simple judgment is one in which I compare only one subject with one predicate; a composite judgment, on the other hand, one in which I compare two predicates with one subject, or two subjects with one predicate. The latter are entitled *judicia copulativa*.",["\"The latter\" refers to composite judgments; *copulatiua* normalised."]],"philippi-s70-015":["Judgments are further divided into",["Continues in the next passages."]],"philippi-s70-016":["1. *ordinaria*, in which one concept is compared with another.",[]],"philippi-s70-017":["2. *extraordinaria*, where one judgment is compared with another.",[]],"philippi-s70-018":["These are of two kinds: a. *judicia hypothetica seu conditionalia*; b. *disiunctiva*.",["\"These\" = the *extraordinaria*."]],"philippi-s70-019":["Every relation is either a relation of connection or of conflict. If one judgment stands in the relation of conflict with another, this is a disjunctive judgment; for example, the soul is either simple or composite. Here there is a conflict between the judgments: the soul is simple, and the soul is composite. If I assume the one, the other drops out.",["OCR \"Verhält niß\" and \"Widerstreit\" normalised."]],"philippi-s70-020":["If one judgment stands with another in the relation of connection, so that if I posit the one the other must also be posited, this is a hypothetical judgment. The former is the condition of the other. If . . . then . . .",[]],"philippi-s70-021":["Here we always attend only to the form, to the relation, whether the matter of the judgment be true or not. One looks only to the consequence. The condition is entitled the *antecedens*, the proposition following from it the *consequens*. The relation of both is entitled *consequentia*.",["The sentence \"Man sieht nur auf die Consequens\" seems to be truncated or garbled in OCR (perhaps *Consequenz*); translated as \"consequence\" tentatively."]],"philippi-s70-022":["Of disjunctive judgments it is to be noted that only two judgments ever come into opposition, and that if there are more, they must be reduced to two, to two pure oppositions.",["OCR \"iudicus\" read as *iudiciis*."]],"philippi-s70-023":["For only two propositions can be purely opposed; the others are sub-oppositions [*Unteroppositiones*]. This holds provided the opposition is to be capable of being understood through reason. Where experience is added, there can be more.",["OCR \"sej'\" read as *seyn*; \"Dafern ... soll\" smoothed into a separate sentence."]],"philippi-s70-024":["Disjunctions in logic serve to make complete logical divisions.",[]],"philippi-s70-025":["For a logical division it is required that all the *membra dividentia* together make up the whole *conceptus divisus*, and that they stand in *oppositio*. If a member is lacking, the logical classification and the disjunctive judgment are false.",["\"disiunetive\" normalised."]],"philippi-s70-026":["All judgments are either",["Continues in the following passages."]],"philippi-s70-027":["1. *Judicia problematica*, in which I merely consider the relation of the predicate",[]],"philippi-s70-028":["to the subject.",[]],"philippi-s70-029":["2. *Judicia assertoria*, in which I posit it. To prevent errors I must judge problematically as much as possible.",[]],"philippi-s71-001":["§310",[]],"philippi-s71-002":["*Exponiren* means to set apart [*aus einander sezzen*]. There must therefore be something mixed together. If two judgments lie hidden in one, they must be developed. That is an exponible judgment.",["OCR \"TJrtheil\" read as *Urtheil*; *exponibles* left as \"exponible\"."]],"philippi-s71-003":["The exponible judgment, when it is unfolded, contains two judgments, of which one affirms and the other denies. For example, 'Few men are pious' means the same as: 'Some men are pious and others are not pious.'",["*exponible* (German *exponibel*) kept as Kant's technical term for a judgment requiring exposition.","Restated the colon-and-dash construction as two sentences; 'ausgewickelt' rendered 'unfolded'."]],"philippi-s71-004":["An exponible judgment therefore contains (1) one subject and (2) one predicate, but two kinds of quality and quantity.",["'zweierley Qualität und Quantität' is compressed; rendered literally as 'two kinds of quality and quantity'."]],"philippi-s72-001":["§311",[]],"philippi-s72-002":["Judgments which are not merely logical but also seek to move our mind, such as exclamations, must never appear in a mode of knowledge in which logical perfection is the chief aim. It is absurd to intermingle sensations and appearances in philosophy. Meier and Feder are guilty of this fault. When an author says, 'Such judgments are very practical,' he ought to say, 'They are very moving and charming.' Many authors have lost the value of their philosophy by allowing themselves to be tempted to mix feeling and taste into it. Rousseau is one of the greatest geniuses. But he mixes into his writings something of the romantic, and in consequence his acute mind is not rightly understood by everyone, and the strength of his arguments remains unrecognised by a part of his readers. Hume, because he mixes much that is new into his writings, has been regarded by many rather as an eloquent man than as the acute philosopher that he really is.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'rubren' (rühren), 'Hauptabsieht', 'Meyer' (Meier).","'Art der Erkenntniß' rendered 'mode of knowledge'; 'Empfindungen und Erscheinungen' kept literally, though 'Erscheinungen' here is odd and may be a transcriber's slip.","'etwas romanhafftes' rendered 'something of the romantic' (literally, novel-like)."]],"philippi-s72-003":["One must not write against taste; in morals and philosophy one must be negative with regard to taste.",["'negativ seyn' kept literally: neutral or abstaining as regards taste."]],"philippi-s72-004":["When taste and reason are mixed, it is also misleading for the hearer; he is then not sure whether it is the strength of the rational grounds, or the taste and agreeableness of the style, that has won his assent. Voltaire has the fault that, by particular turns of phrase deliberately introduced and by something droll, he knows how to bring the reader over to his side. For where I find pleasure, to that party I attach myself.",["'verführerisch' rendered 'misleading' (literally, seductive).","Reading 'poßierliches' = possierliches ('droll')."]],"philippi-s73-001":["§312",[]],"philippi-s73-002":["Learned judgments are either *theoretica* or *practica*. The former are either *obiectiua*, which are correct and good in and for themselves, or *subiectiua*, which indicate only a relation to my free will.",["'Die erstem' is taken to mean the theoretical judgments, though the logic of the division (objective/subjective) fits the practical judgments more naturally; the transcript may be confused.","'gelehrte Urtheile' rendered 'learned judgments'."]],"philippi-s73-003":["§",["Section number apparently lost in the OCR."]],"philippi-s73-004":["Learned judgments are either demonstrable or indemonstrable. The latter are immediately certain; the former only by means of certain intermediate judgments. It is of great importance to distinguish demonstrable judgments from indemonstrable ones. Only metaphysics can teach us this, since it shows us the laws and limits of our understanding.",["OCR 'Zwischennrtheile' corrected to Zwischenurtheile; 'erweislich/unerweislich' rendered 'demonstrable/indemonstrable'.","'Es ist viel daran gelegen' rendered 'It is of great importance'."]],"philippi-s73-005":["All too often we hold judgments to be immediately certain which are so only by means of certain intermediate judgments that we pass over.",[]],"philippi-s74-001":["§314",[]],"philippi-s74-002":["In every proof we have to consider the matter and the form. The former is the mark which I compare first with the subject and then with the predicate; the latter is the manner of the comparison. For example: 'Every body is divisible, for it is composite.' I compare body with the composite, and then with the divisible, and say: every body is composite; everything composite is divisible; consequently every body is divisible. We see here how proofs can be brought into syllogisms. In general, the *principia materialia* of proofs are the marks already known to us as certain in regard to the thing, the *medii termini*; the *principia formalia* are the laws in accordance with which the understanding perceives the agreement of the *medius terminus* (1) with the subject, (2) with the predicate that is to be proved. They contain the rules which determine in what manner something follows, as a conclusion, from certain grounds. We have only two of these, the principle of identity and that of contradiction. *Principia materialia* we have in countless number. These *principia materialia* are in the last resort grounded upon elementary propositions. An element is a part of a whole which is itself no part of anything else. Elementary propositions are those that contain the *notas constitutiuas primitiuas alicuius entis*. A proposition is demonstrable only by means of a *nota media*.",["OCR 'Bejr' (Bei), 'formaha' (formalia), 'Theü' (Theil) silently corrected.","'wie aus gewissen Gründen etwas zu Schlüssen sey' is obscure; rendered by sense, 'in what manner something follows, as a conclusion, from certain grounds'.","Split the long opening comparison and the final sentences; 'Satz der Identität und des Wiederspruchs' rendered 'principle of identity and that of contradiction'.","'Sie' ('They') taken to refer to the *principia formalia*."]],"philippi-s74-003":["Judgments, therefore, in which I compare a *nota immediata* with the subject cannot be proved. They are the elementary propositions.",[]],"philippi-s75-001":["§ 315",[]],"philippi-s75-002":["The purpose of judgments is distinctness. Distinctness consists in my making hidden, obscure propositions manifest; this is done through judging. Thereby I turn a latent identity (*identitatem latentem*) into a patent one (*patentem*). If judgments have explicit identity (*identitatem explicitam*), they are tautological; but if they have only implicit identity (*implicitam*), they can be a means to distinctness and consequently are not at once empty.",["OCR 'patenti' read as *patentem* (accusative, parallel to *latentem*); 'tavtologisch' = tautologisch; 'Deutlichkeit' rendered 'distinctness'.","'Die Deutlichkeit besteht darum, daß' rendered 'consists in'."]],"philippi-s75-003":["*Postulata* are indemonstrable practical judgments. If one postulates what can still be proved, this is entitled begging (*petere principium*).",["'erbetteln' rendered 'begging', with Kant's Latin *petere principium* retained."]],"philippi-s76-001":["§ 319",[]],"philippi-s76-002":["Locke divides judgments into",["Passage breaks off; the divisions follow in the next passages."]],"philippi-s76-003":["1. *Judicia intuitiua empirica*, that is, singular judgments in so far as they",["Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s76-004":["are immediately known.",[]],"philippi-s76-005":["2. *Judicia reflecta seu discursiua*, which are drawn from others by an inference",["Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s76-006":["[drawn].",["The German is only the participle 'gezogen werden' ('are drawn'), completing the preceding line; bracketed word supplied only to avoid repetition, and it could equally be omitted."]],"philippi-s76-007":["All universal judgments are *reflexa*. Strictly, I do not experience the universal but the singular.",["*reflexa* retained; the preceding text has *reflecta*."]],"philippi-s76-008":["As regards the *judicia intuitiua*, the *intuitus* is either (1) *sensitiuus*, that is, the feeling occasioned by a single object of the senses. Thus all immediate representations of experience are *intuitus*. Through imagination I can also represent an object to myself *per intuitum sensitiuum*. Not all immediate judgments are intuitive; only those are so whose form is sensitive. For example: 'The sun is round.'",["OCR 'sensit! uus' read as *sensitiuus*; *intuitus* left in Latin, since it is not clear whether it is to be 'intuition' here.","'Gefühl' rendered 'feeling'; 'Erfahrungsvorstellungen' rendered 'representations of experience'."]],"philippi-s76-009":["(2) *Intuitus purus*, the representation in accordance with rules of reason, for example the representation of space and time, of mathematical figures, extension, etc. It is the form of sensible sensation.",["OCR 'Vernunfft' kept as 'reason'; 'Empfindung' rendered 'sensation', as the method requires, though 'sinnlicher Empfindung' sits oddly with 'form'."]],"philippi-s76-010":["The axioms of geometry are *iudicia intuitiua*, but of reason, not of experience. So too the universal representation of proofs is an *intuitus purus*.",[]],"philippi-s76-011":["*Axiomata philosophica carent intuitu: sunt discursiua*; for the philosopher derives everything from universal propositions. *Iudicia intuitiua* are therefore either judgments of empirical or of pure *intuitus*.",["'Urtheile intuitu empirici oder puri' is as in the text; the Latin forms are grammatically irregular and kept as they stand."]],"philippi-s76-012":["I can form no intuitive concept of numbers except by counting, and consequently through time.",[]],"philippi-s76-013":["Universal empirical judgments also always admit of a proof.",[]],"philippi-s76-014":["Empirical judgments are never negative. That the moon has no light of its own is not an immediate proposition of experience. Accordingly all negative judgments of experience are mediate judgments and have arisen from reflection.",[]],"philippi-s76-015":["§",["Section number apparently lost in the OCR."]],"philippi-s76-016":["All propositions are",["Sentence continues in the following passages."]],"philippi-s76-017":["1. theoretical, which express the relation of one concept to another;",[]],"philippi-s76-018":["2. practical, which express the relation of actions to concepts.",[]],"philippi-s76-019":["Both are",[]],"philippi-s76-020":["1. either indemonstrable, and then the former are entitled *axiomata*, the latter *postulata*; or",["'denn' here is 'dann' (then); 'die erstem/die andern' taken as theoretical/practical propositions."]],"philippi-s76-021":["2. demonstrable through *ratiocinia polysyllogistica*, and then the theoretical propositions are entitled *theoremata*, the practical *problemata*.",[]],"philippi-s76-022":["A proposition which is drawn from another by an inference is entitled a *corollarium*.",["'Corollarium' kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s76-023":["If I adopt a proposition without proof which is, however, proved in another science, it is entitled a *lemma*. For example, that there is a God is a *lemma* in morals; it is proved in *theologia naturalis*.",[]],"philippi-s76-024":["A proposition which serves not for proof but only for elucidation is entitled a *scholion*.",[]],"philippi-s77-001":["§338",[]],"philippi-s77-002":["Generally one may say that every *ratio tautologica* is a *qualitas occulta*. For example, if one names antipathy as the cause why certain things do not agree with one another, the word means nothing other than impatience [?].",["The OCR 'Ungeduldsanikeit' is garbled; read tentatively as Ungeduldsamkeit ('impatience'), but the original word may be different, perhaps one expressing mutual intolerance. Reading doubtful.","OCR 'AVort' corrected to 'Wort'."]],"philippi-s77-003":["The *qualitas occulta* is very much concealed in philosophy. The first principle of Crusius, where he places the criterion of truth in the nature of human reason, is tautological and a *qualitas occulta*; for this consists in nothing else than that the ground stands to the consequence in *identitas implicita*.",["'in identitate implicita' rendered as implicit identity; 'Crusius' as in the text."]],"philippi-s77-004":["Explanations in a circle come very close to the *qualitates occultae*. One still finds *qualitates occultae* frequently in philosophy, most of all in logic and morals. There one gives causes which say no more than the effect.",[]],"philippi-s78-001":["§341",[]],"philippi-s78-002":["An immediate inference is one drawn from a single judgment *sine nota intermedia* [without an intermediate mark].",["The Latin gloss in brackets is supplied by me."]],"philippi-s78-003":["According to the division of the logicians, an immediate inference proceeds",["The sentence is completed by the list that follows in the next passages."]],"philippi-s78-004":["1. *per iudicia aequipollentia* [through equipollent judgments].",[]],"philippi-s78-005":["2. *per iudicia subalternata* [through subalternate judgments].",[]],"philippi-s78-006":["3. *per iudicia opposita* [through opposed judgments].",[]],"philippi-s78-007":["4. *per conversionem logicam* [through logical conversion].",["The source reads 'conuersionem'; the u/v spelling is normalised."]],"philippi-s78-008":["5. *per contrapositionem* [through contraposition].",[]],"philippi-s78-009":["I. The immediate inference *per iudicia aequipollentia* is no true conclusion; for here I merely put another, more suitable word in place of one. For example: Not all, or some, men are virtuous. This substitution is no conclusion.",["The example, as transcribed, is probably corrupt or abbreviated; I translate it as it stands.","'Schicklicher' rendered 'more suitable'."]],"philippi-s78-010":["II. Through *iudicia subalternata* an immediate inference arises; I subsume a particular judgment under a universal one. For example: all men are mortal, therefore kings also [are]. The universal judgment is entitled *iudicium subalternans*; the particular, however, *iudicium subalternatum*.",["'[are]' supplied to complete the elliptical conclusion."]],"philippi-s78-011":["III. *Per iudicia contradictorie opposita*, that is, purely opposed. When two judgments are contradictorily opposed, then if the one is true the other is false, and if the one is false the other is true.",["'i. e. pure opposita' is rendered as 'that is, purely opposed'."]],"philippi-s78-012":["IV. The immediate inference *per conversionem* takes place when the predicate is changed into the subject. For example: All bodies are divisible. Some divisible [things] are bodies.",["'[things]' supplied for the substantive 'Theilbare'."]],"philippi-s78-013":["The judgment which is to be converted is entitled *convertens*; that which arises from the conversion, *conversum*. *Conversio simplex* is when the quantity is not altered; *conversio per accidens*, when it is altered.",[]],"philippi-s78-014":["The quality always remains the same.",[]],"philippi-s78-015":["V. In the immediate inference *per contrapositionem* the quality also is altered. For example: All men can err. What cannot err is no man. The rule is: *remoto praedicato tollitur subiectum* [if the predicate is removed, the subject is removed].",["Bracketed Latin gloss is mine."]],"philippi-s78-016":["The *iudicia subcontraria*, because they merely set one particular concept against another and have no universality, do not make a contradiction.",["'Begrif' is read as 'Begriff' (concept); possibly Kant or the transcriber means 'judgment', but I follow the text."]],"philippi-s78-017":["In the opposition of two universal judgments there is again too much for contradiction; the particular is lacking.",["'Wieder' rendered 'again'; the sense is that the universals overshoot contradiction."]],"philippi-s78-018":["In the case of *iudicia contradictorie opposita* one can infer from the truth of the one to the falsity of the other, and from the falsity of the one to the truth of the other. These therefore belong to immediate inference.",["OCR 'Bej*' silently corrected to 'Bey'."]],"philippi-s78-019":["But in the case of *iudicia contrarie opposita* one cannot infer from the falsity of the one to the truth of the other; and in the case of *iudicia subcontrarie opposita*, on the other hand, one cannot infer from the truth of the one to the falsity of the other; hence these do not belong to immediate inference.",["Sentence is long; kept as one sentence with a semicolon."]],"philippi-s78-020":["The *conclusiones mediatae* may be entitled judgments of reason [*Vernunfturtheile*]; the *conclusiones immediatae*, however, judgments of the understanding [*Verstandesurtheile*]. Judgments are opposed to one another",["The passage breaks off at 'opposed to one another'; the continuation is in the next passage."]],"philippi-s78-021":["1. contradictorily, when a particular negative judgment is set against a universal affirmative, and a particular affirmative against a universal negative. 2. Subcontrarily, when of two particular judgments having the same subject and predicate, the one affirms and the other denies.",["'Contradictorie' and 'subcontrarie' are rendered adverbially, parallel to the Latin forms in the preceding passage."]],"philippi-s78-022":["3. Contrarily, when a universal negative proposition is set against a universal affirmative, and vice versa.",["*vice versa* left in Latin."]],"philippi-s78-023":["It is strange that the logicians have called contrariety a contradiction. In a *iudicium subcontrarium* both can be true, but not both false.",["The statement as transcribed is correct for subcontraries (both may be true, not both false)."]],"philippi-s78-024":["The Tenth Section.",[]],"philippi-s79-001":["On the Learned Inferences of Reason. §353",["'Gelehrten Vernunftschlüssen' rendered 'learned inferences of reason'."]],"philippi-s79-002":["The understanding judges; reason infers [*schließt*], that is, it judges mediately. The understanding knows immediately that a predicate belongs to a certain subject; reason, however, knows it only by means of a certain mark (*nota intermedia*) which it",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passages.","'Erkennt' rendered 'knows'."]],"philippi-s79-003":["1. [compares] with the predicate",["'[compares]' supplied from the verb 'zusammenhält' in the next fragment."]],"philippi-s79-004":["2. holds together with the subject, and then draws the consequence. For example: that a body is composite, this I know immediately. But that it is alterable I know only by means of a *nota intermedia*, by means of the mark of divisibility. I say",["OCR 'erkenneich' silently corrected to 'erkenne ich'; 'notae intermediae' is a Latin genitive after 'vermittelst'."]],"philippi-s79-005":["Every body is divisible.",[]],"philippi-s79-006":["Everything divisible is alterable.",[]],"philippi-s79-007":["Therefore every body also is alterable.",[]],"philippi-s79-008":["The foundation and basic formula of all inferences of reason is this: *Nota notae est nota rei ipsius* [a mark of a mark is a mark of the thing itself]. For example: alterability is a mark of divisibility, as a *nota* of body; consequently it is also the mark of body itself.",["Bracketed Latin gloss is mine."]],"philippi-s79-009":["Three judgments therefore occur in an inference of reason. In the first I compare the predicate, or the *nota remota*, which I cannot know immediately as belonging to the subject, with the *nota intermedia*. Since the predicate always has a greater sphere than the subject, it is entitled the *terminus maior*, and the whole first judgment the *propositio maior*.",["'Sphäre' rendered 'sphere'."]],"philippi-s79-010":["In the second judgment I compare the subject with the *nota intermedia*. Since the subject is contained in the sphere of the predicate, it is entitled the *terminus minor*, and the whole judgment the *propositio minor*. The middle concept is entitled the *medius terminus*. The judgment which I finally bring out is entitled the *conclusio*. In it I then see, through the comparison that has been made, the relation of the *nota remota* to the subject. I must proceed *per synthesin* from the *nota remota* to the *nota propior*, and from this to the subject. The two upper judgments are also entitled premisses.",["'nota propiore' in the source is an ablative-looking form; read as *nota propior* [nearer mark]; the Latin is irregular."]],"philippi-s79-011":["First judgment:",[]],"philippi-s79-012":["Everything divisible is alterable.",[]],"philippi-s79-013":["Second judgment:",[]],"philippi-s79-014":["Every body is divisible.",[]],"philippi-s79-015":["Third judgment:",[]],"philippi-s79-016":["Every body is alterable.",[]],"philippi-s79-017":["C-B",["Schematic letters, translated as they stand."]],"philippi-s79-018":["A-C",["Schematic letters, as they stand."]],"philippi-s79-019":["A. B.",["Schematic letters, as they stand."]],"philippi-s79-020":["The synthetic method, which requires that one begin from the grounds, also demands that in the first judgment one compare the middle concept with the predicate.",["OCR 'eisten' corrected to 'ersten'; 'Mittel -begrif' to 'Mittelbegriff'."]],"philippi-s79-021":["In inferences of reason we must distinguish the matter, that is, the premisses, or the three concepts (subject, predicate and middle concept), and the form, which consists in the manner in which the conclusion is inferred from the premisses.",["OCR 'lind' corrected to 'und', 'Praeinissen' to 'Praemissen'."]],"philippi-s79-022":["The truth or falsity of an inference of reason in respect of matter concerns the truth or falsity of the premisses. For example: All matter is eternal; the world consists of matter; therefore it is eternal.",[]],"philippi-s79-023":["But even if the premisses are true, the conclusion can nevertheless be false. For then the fault lies in the form. The form is the essential, and the faults in it are the most important; faults in the matter are accidental. For the form is the universal rule; the matter is only a concrete case to which the form is given, or which is subsumed under the universal rule. The falsity in the form of inferences is the cause of the falsity of the matter.",["The last sentence is obscure in the transcript (perhaps 'Falschheit der Materie' should be read otherwise); I translate it as it stands.","'Denn' at the start of the third sentence is rendered 'For then', i.e. 'in that case'."]],"philippi-s79-024":["We have accordingly a twofold correctness: one of the matter and one of the form. The rules of the matter of inferences of reason are properly the *principia iudiciorum* [principles of judgments], that is, the principle of identity and of contradiction: the former for all affirmative, the latter for negative propositions.",["'Jener ... dieser' rendered 'the former ... the latter'."]],"philippi-s79-025":["All inferences of reason are either regular, where there are three *termini*: subject, predicate and the middle concept; or irregular [*ausserordentliche*], where two or more judgments are compared with one another. All conditional and disjunctive judgments give *ratiocinia extraordinaria*. A conditional inference of reason is one in which the *propositio maior* is a conditional, a disjunctive one where it is a disjunctive judgment.",["'Ordentliche/ausserordentliche' rendered 'regular/irregular', echoing *ratiocinia extraordinaria*."]],"philippi-s80-001":["§364",[]],"philippi-s80-002":["The proposition by virtue of which one can infer from the ground to the consequences is the ground and the *principium* of all *ratiocinia hypothetica* [hypothetical inferences].",["The source has 'hypotheticorum' (genitive plural), kept in sense as 'of all hypothetical inferences'."]],"philippi-s81-001":["§365",[]],"philippi-s81-002":["The *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria* [principle of the excluded middle between two contradictories] is the *principium ratiociniorum disiunctivorum* [principle of disjunctive inferences].",["'disiunctiuorum' normalised."]],"philippi-s82-001":["§366",[]],"philippi-s82-002":["The *conclusiones immediatae* are no *ratiocinia*. For example: All men are mortal, consequently some are mortal. Here the *medius terminus* is lacking; that is why they are no *ratiocinia*.",[]],"philippi-s82-003":["The author's judgment that judgments are true if they can be accepted without detriment to truth is entirely tautological.",["'Tavtologisch' rendered 'tautological'. 'The author' is Meier."]],"philippi-s83-001":["§363",["The section number is as printed; the order (§363 after §366) is the source's."]],"philippi-s83-002":["As regards the *principia* of the form of inferences of reason, the Scholastics had the *dictum de omni et de nullo*. This means as much as:",["Breaks off; the explanation follows in the next passage."]],"philippi-s83-003":["What is affirmed of a universal concept is also affirmed of the particular; and what is denied of a universal concept is also denied of everything particular that is contained under it. Now every inference of reason is nothing other than the subsumption of a particular judgment under a universal. We say that the formal principle of affirmative judgments is: *nota notae est nota rei ipsius*, and *repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi*. The former is the *dictum de omni*, the latter the *dictum de nullo*. What belongs to the universal, or conflicts with it, also belongs to the particular contained under it, or conflicts with it. The relation is twofold:",["Restated the semicolon sequence as separate sentences only lightly; the Latin formulae are left in Latin, as the instructions require.","The text calls the formal principle that of 'affirmative judgments' yet gives both the affirmative and the negative formula; I have kept this as it stands."]],"philippi-s83-004":["α. logical, where I consider the concepts according to the relation of their spheres,",["OCR 'a.' read as α, matching the following 'ß' (β)."]],"philippi-s83-005":["[consider] them,",["The OCR leaves only 'betrachte,' on its own line; it is the verb ending the previous clause (passage s83-004), so the clause is not repeated here. The bracketed word is supplied only to make the fragment readable."]],"philippi-s83-006":["β. metaphysical, when the *notiones* are represented as they",["OCR 'ß' read as β; 'Notioncs' corrected to *Notiones*. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s83-007":["are contained in one another.",[]],"philippi-s83-008":["The subject is contained under the predicate, that is, under its sphere; but the predicate is contained in the subject, that is, as a constituent (*constitutivum*) of the concept.",["'constitutiuum' corrected to *constitutivum*."]],"philippi-s84-001":["§369",[]],"philippi-s84-002":["The doctrine of the syllogistic figures, of the rules of each figure, of the moods according to quality and quantity, and of the reduction of the other figures to the first, aims at showing how inferences of reason can be formed without understanding, by mechanical artifices. We know from what has gone before that in every inference of reason the middle concept is compared with the subject and with the *nota remota* (the predicate). Since its position can be altered in four ways, the four figures have arisen from this.",["'modis qualitate, quantitate' rendered as 'moods according to quality and quantity'; the Latin is left to the sense. 'Vernunftschluss' is rendered 'inference of reason' throughout."]],"philippi-s84-003":["In the first figure the *medius terminus* stands in the *maior propositio* in the place of the subject (*loco subiecti*), and in the minor in the place of the predicate (*loco praedicati*).",[]],"philippi-s84-004":["In the second figure it stands in both premisses in the place of the predicate (*loco praedicati*); in the third figure it stands in both premisses in the place of the subject (*loco subiecti*); and in the fourth it stands in the major in the place of the predicate, in the minor in the place of the subject.",[]],"philippi-s84-005":["Everything, then, depends on the positions of the middle term (*medius terminus*).",[]],"philippi-s84-006":["Human reason always infers in the first figure and cannot do otherwise. In all the other figures it infers only by first reducing them to the first figure. This it does by thinking, in addition to the two propositions, an immediate inference. Accordingly all the *ratiocinia* of the second, third and fourth figures are *ratiocinia hybrida*, and only those of the first figure are *pura*.",["'immediate Conclusion' rendered 'immediate inference' (*conclusio immediata*)."]],"philippi-s84-007":["In the second figure a *conversio logica* always takes place in thought in the major proposition. In the third there is a *contrapositio*. For example: All the learned have an improved understanding; some of the learned are vicious. Here I convert: some vicious persons are learned; therefore some vicious persons have an improved understanding. In the fourth figure both alterations, *conversio* and *contrapositio*, must be introduced.",["The example is a third-figure syllogism, and the conversion in it is of the minor, which is not quite what the preceding sentence says; I have translated the transcript as it stands.","'conuersio' corrected to *conversio*."]],"philippi-s84-008":["If the middle term is not regarded as a *nota* of the subject, reason does not infer.",["'sie' taken to refer to reason (Vernunft), as in the preceding paragraphs."]],"philippi-s84-009":["Hence in the third figure, where this happens, no inference of reason is possible.",[]],"philippi-s84-010":["The rule of the second figure is therefore: *maior sit vniuersalis, conclusio negatiua*.",["The Latin is kept as printed ('vniuersalis', 'negatiua' for universalis, negativa)."]],"philippi-s84-011":["The rule of the third figure is: *minor sit affirm[ans], conclusio particularis*. For affirmative propositions can be converted only in such a way that a particular proposition results.",["OCR 'affirmus' emended to *affirmans*, with the supplied letters bracketed."]],"philippi-s84-012":["In the first figure the conclusions are affirmative; in the second, negative; in the third, particular, affirmative or negative; in the fourth they are fixed under certain conditions, and are either universal negative or particular. The reason why universal affirmative conclusions are to be met with in the first figure alone is that in the other three a conversion must always take place.",["The first sentence is loosely put in the transcript (the first figure also yields negative conclusions); I have not corrected it."]],"philippi-s84-013":["The mood (*modus*) in syllogistic signifies the quality and quantity of the three propositions. Quality and quantity are expressed by the vowels a, e, i, o, to which, for the sake of euphony, consonants are joined; from this the words *Barbara* etc. arose. *Asserit a, negat e sed vniuersaliter ambo; asserit i negat o sed particulariter ambo.* Four magnitudes can be permuted 64 times if only three are always taken together; thus 64 moods are possible. All the general rules of inferences of reason: *Conclusio sequi debet partem debiliorem*, *ex puris negatiuis et puris particularibus nihil sequitur*, and the special rules of each figure, eliminate so many of the 64 moods that only 10 useful moods (*modi vtiles*) remain.",["OCR 'ml sequitur' emended to *nihil sequitur* (cf. the rule at s85-011).","The arithmetic ('4 Größen 64 mal' taken three at a time, i.e. 4³) is as the transcript has it.","The mnemonic verse is left in Latin; 'vtiles' = *utiles*."]],"philippi-s84-014":["Since reason infers only in the first figure, one must know how to reduce all inferences of reason of the other figures to the first. For those that do not set the concepts apart from one another only entangle them the more. This artifice of finding out the rules is entitled *reductio*.",["The second sentence is obscure in the transcript; I give the plainest sense of 'die die Begriffe nicht aus einander sezzen' and make no emendation.","'die Regeln aufzufinden' is rendered literally; the text may be corrupt or elliptical."]],"philippi-s84-015":["The consonants which are added to the moods have a significance in this connection, and teach us mechanically to reduce the other figures to the first.",["'in der Absicht' rendered 'in this connection'."]],"philippi-s85-001":["*S. vult simpliciter verti, P. vero per accidens* [S. requires simple conversion, P. conversion *per accidens*]",["The bracketed English is supplied as a gloss on the mnemonic."]],"philippi-s85-002":["*M. vult transponi, C. per impossibile duci.* [M. requires transposition, C. a proof *per impossibile*.]",["Bracketed gloss supplied; the transcript explains these in the lines that follow."]],"philippi-s85-003":["*P. vero per accidens* means this much: from a universal affirmative or negative proposition a particular affirmative or negative proposition must be made.",[]],"philippi-s85-004":["*M. vult transponi* means this much: the major is made the minor.",[]],"philippi-s85-005":["*C. per impossibile duci* means: the major must be contraposed or converted simply.",["As the transcript gives it; the standard doctrine differs, but nothing is emended."]],"philippi-s85-006":["By this artifice of syllogistic, I mean the moods, one is not taught to infer more correctly. The inference becomes almost more entangled. One makes use of them only to play with the middle term (*medius terminus*).",[]],"philippi-s85-007":["The general rules are:",[]],"philippi-s85-008":["1. The syllogism must contain no more than three *termini*. Here care must be taken that a word is not taken in a twofold meaning. For example: What you have not lost, you still have; you have not lost horns: therefore you have some.",["OCR 'Avelche' corrected to 'welche'."]],"philippi-s85-009":["2. *Medius terminus ne ingrediatur conclusionem*; for it belongs to the premisses.",[]],"philippi-s85-010":["3. *Ex puris negatiuis*. In every inference of reason the *medius terminus* is a *nota* of the subject, and the predicate is again a *nota* of it, or *nota notae*. The proposition, therefore, in which the middle term is regarded as a *nota* of the subject must necessarily be affirmative.",["The rule is left elliptical, as in the transcript; *nihil sequitur* is understood from rule 4 but not supplied in the text."]],"philippi-s85-011":["4. *Ex puris particularibus nihil sequitur*; one of the premisses must be universal; if it is to be a pure inference of reason, it must be the *maior*, where the predicate is compared with the middle term. This already follows from the *dictum de omni et nullo*.",["OCR 'vniuersel' read as 'universal'; 'inaior' corrected to *maior*. The sentence is somewhat loose in the German; I have kept its sequence."]],"philippi-s85-012":["5. *Conclusio sequi debet partem debiliorem*. The *pars debilior* is the negative and the particular proposition. If one of the premisses is negative, the conclusion must also be negative; if one is particular, the conclusion must also be particular.",["OCR 'parteni debüiorem' corrected to *partem debiliorem*."]],"philippi-s85-013":["6. The conclusion must contain neither more nor less than is contained in the antecedent judgments.",["'Vorderurtheile' rendered 'antecedent judgments' (premisses)."]],"philippi-s85-014":["The special rule of the first figure is: *maior sit vniuersalis, minor affirmans*. This is the rule of all inferences of reason.",[]],"philippi-s85-015":["The *ratiocinia* are either",[]],"philippi-s85-016":["1. *ordinaria*, where there are three *termini*, where I compare subject and predicate with the middle concept.",["OCR 'terinini' corrected to *termini*."]],"philippi-s85-017":["2. *extraordinaria*, where in the propositions not two concepts but two judgments are compared. For example: If the world is necessary, all substances are uncreated. In a *ratiocinium extraordinarium*, therefore, the *maior propositio* is a *iudicium extraordinarium*. Hence there are two kinds of *ratiocinia extraordinaria*:",["OCR 'Praepositionibus' read as *propositionibus*; 'ratiocmio' corrected to *ratiocinio*."]],"philippi-s85-018":["1. hypothetical, where the major is a hypothetical judgment, that is, one in which two judgments are considered in the relation of connection.",[]],"philippi-s85-019":["The relation is here considered problematically, not dogmatically.",[]],"philippi-s85-020":["Hypothetical *ratiocinia* have no *medius terminus*. The minor occurs in both premisses.",["OCR 'kernen' read as 'keinen'. 'Der minor kommt in beyden Prämissen vor' is translated as it stands; the sense is doubtful, since it is not the minor premiss that occurs in both."]],"philippi-s85-021":["The hypothetical inferences of reason are",[]],"philippi-s85-022":["1. affirmative, and then the inference must be made *a positione antecedentis* (as the ground) *ad positionem consequentis* (as the consequence).",[]],"philippi-s85-023":["2. negative; and then the inference must be made *a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis*.",[]],"philippi-s85-024":["We cannot infer from the positing of the consequence to the positing of the ground; for the consequence may also have followed from another ground, and not from the first alone.",["\"Sezzung\" (modern Setzung) rendered \"positing\"; \"Folge\" = consequence, \"Grund\" = ground."]],"philippi-s85-025":["But from the absence of the consequence I can infer the absence of the ground; I cannot, however, infer the absence of the consequence from the absence of a certain ground, for the very same consequence may be true from another ground.",["\"Schlüsse/Schlüssen\" read as the infinitive \"schließen\" (\"infer\")."]],"philippi-s86-001":["§395",[]],"philippi-s86-002":["A disjunctive inference of reason [*Vernunftschluß*] is one in which the *maior propositio* is a disjunctive judgment.",[]],"philippi-s86-003":["Such a *ratiocinium* always enunciates a logical division, and many judgments are considered in the relation of opposition [*Widerstreit*]. Moreover, it is not one member of the *divisio* that is contradictorily opposed to another, but each to all the others.",["Second sentence divided in two for clarity. \"Widerstreit\" = opposition (conflict)."]],"philippi-s86-004":["The *conceptus divisus* is the subject; all the *membra dividentia* together make up the predicate.",[]],"philippi-s87-001":["§ 396",[]],"philippi-s87-002":["The modes of disjunctive inferences, like those of conditional inferences, are twofold. (1) *Modus ponens*, of which the rule is this: if all the members of the division are false except one, then that one is true.",["Split into two sentences; the numeral \"1.\" retained."]],"philippi-s87-003":["(2) *Modus tollens*: if the one member is true, then all the rest are false.",[]],"philippi-s87-004":["In disjunctive inferences, if one wishes to test their correctness, one has to look to the following:",[]],"philippi-s87-005":["1. The truth of the *minor propositio*.",["\"minoris propositionis\" is genitive after \"auf die Wahrheit\"."]],"philippi-s87-006":["2. The correct logical division: whether some members are not missing, and whether they are all contradictorily opposed to one another.",[]],"philippi-s87-007":["In hypothetical inferences one has to look to",[]],"philippi-s87-008":["1. The truth of the antecedent or of the consequent.",[]],"philippi-s87-009":["2. The correct inference [*Folgerung*].",[]],"philippi-s87-010":["If the *minor propositio* is a hypothetical proposition whose consequent is a disjunctive judgment, such a judgment is entitled a dilemma (*syllogismus cornutus*), because it drives one hard into a corner. The dilemma is proved by the rules of the *syllogismus hypotheticus*; ordinarily the *modus tollens* is always what holds, since I deny through all the *membra*.",["\"Saz\" = proposition. \"in die Enge treibt\" = literally drives into a narrow place.","\"negire\" read as \"negiere\"."]],"philippi-s88-001":["§399",[]],"philippi-s88-002":["The *ratiocinium crypticum* and the formal [*ratiocinium*] must be distinguished from one another. Both are *ratiocinia ordinaria*. *Ratiocinia cryptica* are genuine, regular inferences of reason in which, for the sake of brevity, one omits what is known. To these belong:",["\"formale\" supplied with *ratiocinium* in brackets."]],"philippi-s88-003":["1. The *enthymema*, in which I leave out one of the premisses. All men can err, therefore the Pope also.",["\"Mensehen\" corrected to \"Menschen\" (OCR)."]],"philippi-s88-004":["2. *Ratiocinia contracta*, in which I infer *per medios terminos*. All bodies are changeable because they are composed.",["OCR \"«eil\" corrected to \"weil\"."]],"philippi-s88-005":["Although all inferences of reason consist in inferring from the universal to the particular, yet in modes of knowledge from experience one also infers (though admittedly not with full strictness) from the particular to the universal. This kind of inference is entitled *inductio*.",["\"Erfahrungserkenntnissen\" = modes of knowledge from experience."]],"philippi-s88-006":["Analogy is the ground here. This consists in a philosophical relation. If I have an effect and know its cause, and then see a similar effect elsewhere, I infer that there too a similar cause exists. So too I infer from similar causes to similar effects. Further, if I see that two things agree in most respects, I judge that they agree entirely. Such inferences from the particular to the universal, however, very often fail, because one does not know all the individual particulars; only reason can infer universally with correctness. For example, if I wished to infer that all electricity is lost in water, because every body I know and have tested loses it, that would be false, for the electric eel retains its electricity in water.",["\"Diese besteht im philosophischen Verhältniß\" = a qualitative relation, in contrast with the mathematical (see s88-009).","\"Zitteral\" is taken as the electric eel (Zitteraal); \"fehlen\" read as \"fail\", \"Schlüsse\" as \"infer\"."]],"philippi-s88-007":["Travellers often make such incorrect inductions. If, for example, they have been badly received in two or three inns, they believe all inns are like that; indeed they believe that the character of the whole nation is not of the best.",[]],"philippi-s88-008":["One infers from the particular to the universal only with presumption.",["\"Vermuthung\" = presumption, conjecture."]],"philippi-s88-009":["Analogy, which consists in the equality of two philosophical relations, is very much in conformity with mathematical relations. In physics and in natural theology, in respect of the attributes of the Deity, most of our inferences are made through analogy.",[]],"philippi-s88-010":["Theoretical logic indicates the conditions of the perfection of knowledge.",["OCR \"Erkennt niß\" rejoined."]],"philippi-s88-011":["If an argument contains too little in itself, it nevertheless proves a part of the matter, and lacks only the *complementum* needed for sufficiency [*Sufficienz*].",[]],"philippi-s88-012":["What is more than true is false. If a ground of proof proves more than is true, it is false; for example, we have no right to take the life of a being, since we did not give it.",["The example is as in the text; its point is not explained in the passage."]],"philippi-s89-001":["§402",[]],"philippi-s89-002":["An inference of reason that is false in form is entitled a paralogism. If the fault is concealed, it is entitled a sophism. The sophisms of the ancient dialecticians are veritable monuments of ancient folly.",[]],"philippi-s90-001":["§ 404",[]],"philippi-s90-002":["The *fallacia accidentis* is a true conclusion *a dicto secundum quid ad dictum secundum quid*, and not, as the author says, *ad dictum simpliciter*; e.g. whoever says that the respondent is a man speaks the truth; but whoever says that the respondent is a Moor says that he is a man; therefore [...].",["\"Autor\" is presumably Meier, the textbook author; the conclusion is left as in the text (\"ergo\"). \"Mohr\" = Moor."]],"philippi-s90-003":["The *fallacia medii* is when the *terminus medius* is taken now without, now with a qualification [*Bestimmung*].",[]],"philippi-s91-001":["§406",[]],"philippi-s91-002":["*Ratiocinia composita* consist in this, that several inferences of reason are not coordinated but subordinated.",["OCR \"subordhiirt\" read as \"subordinirt\"."]],"philippi-s91-003":["*Ratiocinatio polysyllogistica* consists in this, that the conclusion of one syllogism becomes a premiss of another. The syllogism that is subordinated is entitled *episyllogismus*; that to which one is subordinated is entitled *prosyllogismus*. In disputation the inference is made first episyllogistically and then prosyllogistically.",["\"der dem einer untergeordnet wird\" is garbled in the German; I render it by the sense required. The usage of epi- and prosyllogism looks inverted relative to the usual one, but I follow the text."]],"philippi-s92-001":["§408",[]],"philippi-s92-002":["A sorites is a heaped-up inference of reason (*ratio polysyllogistica ratiociniorum contractorum*).",[]],"philippi-s92-003":["§ 409",[]],"philippi-s92-004":["If the syllogisms linked together are hypothetical, the sorites will be a *sorites hypotheticus*.",[]],"philippi-s93-001":["§411",[]],"philippi-s93-002":["*Petere principium* means to lay down as indemonstrable, as a ground, something that nevertheless has still first to be proved. Thus the Crusian proposition, that everything is somewhere and sometime, is a *petitio principii*.",["\"Crusianische\" = of Crusius."]],"philippi-s93-003":["*Circulus in probando* is when one adduces the consequence as the ground. Such circles are frequent in extensive systems when one constructs lemmata.",["\"Diese\" is plural, apparently referring to such circles; I made it explicit."]],"philippi-s93-004":["A hypothesis. *Postulatio principii* is the proposition: The whole is greater than a part of it.",["The opening \"Eine Hypothese.\" is fragmentary and its connection with what follows is unclear; translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s94-001":["The second main part. On the method of teaching [*Lehrart*] of learned knowledge.",[]],"philippi-s94-002":["Method is the manner in which many thoughts and modes of knowledge are to be connected. It signifies the form of a logically perfect whole. This logical perfection is twofold:",[]],"philippi-s94-003":["α. according to rules of sound understanding, and",["OCR \"oc.\" read as α."]],"philippi-s94-004":["β. according to rules of science.",["OCR \"ß\" read as β."]],"philippi-s94-005":["All method has regard [*respicirt*] to",[]],"philippi-s94-006":["1. the object that is to be known;",[]],"philippi-s94-007":["2. the nature and the faculty of knowledge through which the object is to be known;",["\"Erkenn tnißfähigkeit\" rejoined."]],"philippi-s94-008":["3. the particular constitution of the subject that is to apprehend, grasp and understand the object.",["\"einsehen, faßen und verstehen\" rendered as three verbs."]],"philippi-s95-001":["I.",[]],"philippi-s95-002":["The object of philosophy is the quality [*Beschaffenheit*] of things; that of mathematics, however, is quantity. This makes the method very different. If the object is an object of rational knowledge, the method becomes rational; if it is an object of sensible knowledge, it is to be treated historically.",["\"Beschaffenheit\" = quality, in contrast with quantity. \"er\" in the last clause refers to the object (Gegenstand)."]],"philippi-s96-001":["II.",[]],"philippi-s96-002":["The method must differ according to the circumstances of the subjects. For children the manner of teaching is different from that for adults, for women different from that for men; so too with differing abilities and inclinations. The method must also suit the person who is lecturing, and it must also accommodate itself to an individual subject. In this respect it is hard in a lecture course to arrange one's method according to everyone's wish. One is flighty and wants always to be encouraged; another is profound and wants always to be instructed.",["\"Frauenzimmer\" = women. \"auch die verschiedene Fähigkeiten und Neigungen\" is elliptical; I supplied \"so too with\". \"instruirtso\" corrected to \"instruirt so\"; \"Collegio\" = lecture course."]],"philippi-s97-001":["§421",[]],"philippi-s97-002":["Anything, so far as it is demonstrated, is entitled a science [*Scientz*]. All sciences are either rational or historical. The rational sciences are either sciences whose form alone is rational but whose matter is empirical, e.g. theoretical physics; or sciences of the pure understanding, whose matter and form have both sprung from the pure understanding; or, to speak strictly, sciences whose object is the form or procedure of the understanding, and which properly have no matter at all.",["'so fern' rendered 'so far as' (= *sofern*). 'Scientz' is the transcriber's spelling of *Scientia*; kept in brackets.","The second 'oder' clause is ambiguous in the German: the 'or, to speak strictly' clause is read as a correction of the second kind (pure understanding), not as a third kind; I have kept the three-fold surface form but the sense may be two kinds only.","'Verstand' = understanding; 'Materie'/'Form' rendered 'matter'/'form'."]],"philippi-s97-003":["The modes of knowledge of pure reason are",["Fragment: breaks off; the list continues in the next passages. 'Erkenntnisse' = 'modes of knowledge'."]],"philippi-s97-004":["1. dogmatic in morals",[]],"philippi-s97-005":["2. critical in metaphysics.",["'critisch' rendered 'critical'."]],"philippi-s97-006":["Both sciences are related.",["OCR 'Wissen schafften' read as *Wissenschafften*."]],"philippi-s97-007":["The science of the quantity of objects, that is, mathematics, demonstrates everything with evidence [*euident*], because it is grounded entirely upon space and time, which are sensible intuitions [*sinnlicher Intuitus*]. Mathematics can be evident because it is occupied only with magnitudes, that is, with how often something is posited, whatever it may be. Philosophy cannot; for it endeavours to develop what it is that is posited.",["OCR 'che' read as *sie*; 'Intuitus' kept in Latin form in brackets (here equivalent to Anschauung, 'intuition').","'gesezt' rendered 'posited'."]],"philippi-s98-001":["§422",[]],"philippi-s98-002":["Analysis and synthesis can be considered in coordination and in subordination. Analysis of coordination is where I proceed from the whole to the parts.",[]],"philippi-s98-003":["Synthesis of coordination is where I proceed from the parts to the whole.",[]],"philippi-s98-004":["Analysis of subordination is the derivation of the consequences from their grounds.",["'Gründe'/'Folgen' = 'grounds'/'consequences'."]],"philippi-s98-005":["Synthesis of subordination is when I proceed from the consequences to the grounds.",["OCR 'S3'nthesis' read as *Synthesis*."]],"philippi-s98-006":["Meanwhile there is a great difference between the relation of grounds and consequences and that of the parts to the whole, and a quite special procedure of the understanding in each.",["OCR 'Theüe' read as *Theile*. 'in dem ... und in dem' compressed into 'that of'."]],"philippi-s98-007":["Mathematics and all empirical sciences have the synthetic method; for all of these put their science together little by little, in that they continually discover more.",["OCR 'sezzen' = *setzen*."]],"philippi-s98-008":["The method of philosophy, however, especially of pure philosophy, is analytic. The whole of knowledge is there; all the concepts are given. One has only to make them distinct [*deutlich*] by skilful analysis. Then I proceed from the universal to the particular.",["'deutlich' rendered 'distinct'. 'Zerlegung' rendered 'analysis'."]],"philippi-s98-009":["That pure philosophy has made no progress for centuries, while mathematics has almost reached the highest stage, comes from this: that its method has been misunderstood and that men have wished to philosophize synthetically.",["OCR 'Stuf fe' = *Stufe*; 'philosopliiren' = *philosophiren*."]],"philippi-s98-010":["§",["Section number lost in the OCR."]],"philippi-s98-011":["The author here defines art. But not everything need be defined. The artificial method of teaching is contrasted with the natural. Just as a distinction is to be made between a knowledge that is rational [*vernünfftig*], that is, one that does not contradict reason, and a knowledge that is rational [*vernünftig*] in the sense of arising from reason, so too a knowledge is entitled natural in a twofold sense: first, when it agrees with the nature of the object and subject; and second, when it arises from nature.",["The transcript has 'vernünfftigen ... vernünftigen' for the two senses (presumably *vernunftmäßig* / *aus Vernunft*); both rendered 'rational' with the German in brackets, the distinction being the point of the sentence. Possible slip in the transcript.","OCR 'defdnirt' = *definirt*; 'Vernunff t' = *Vernunft*; 'in zwiefachem Verstände' rendered 'in a twofold sense'."]],"philippi-s98-012":["The method of teaching must be natural not so much subjectively as objectively, that is, adapted to the object. A natural method in general is not so easy a matter.",[]],"philippi-s98-013":["The artificial method of teaching is the method of the schools.",[]],"philippi-s99-001":["§428",[]],"philippi-s99-002":["The *methodus tabellaris*, the sketching of a whole science in tables, is very fine; but insight is required not to make too many divisions, nor useless ones. Daries has a special talent for this method, which is excellent not so much for exposition as for repetition. The method of teaching that consists in the division of a whole body of doctrine is no method of teaching. The manner in which the parts are put together constitutes the body of doctrine [*Lehrgebäude*].",["'Daries' (Joachim Georg Darjes) as in the transcript. 'Theüe' = *Theile*. 'Zertheilung' rendered 'division'."]],"philippi-s100-001":["§429",[]],"philippi-s100-002":["*Affectatio* is entitled an unnatural art. Everything is entitled affected by which one wishes to appear artificial. Thus it is affected when one shows not one's art but traces of an artificial instruction. The affected is commonly bound up with pedantry, when one applies a single method to everything, whether it suits or not.",["OCR 'af feetüte' read as *affectirte*; 'applitirt' as *applicirt*. The last sentence is a fragment in the German ('Wenn man ...'); I have joined it to the preceding by 'when', which is a smoothing."]],"philippi-s101-001":["§430",[]],"philippi-s101-002":["The Socratic method of teaching is teaching by conversation, in which, by well-arranged questions, one draws everything out of the pupil and leads him to answers that are quite new to him. It has the greatest utility. We see distinctly how knowledge arises, and we are put in a position to answer for ourselves all questions put to us. It is a species of the catechetical method; but it is very wearisome, and tedious for experienced readers. Moses Mendelssohn has imitated it best. The Platonic method of teaching consisted in free discourse, in which one becomes aware of a system.",["OCR 'ehie' = *eine*. 'Quaestionen' rendered 'questions'."]],"philippi-s102-001":["§432",[]],"philippi-s102-002":["In the historical sciences there are two methods, the chronological and the geographical. Both can be united. The latter seems to me preferable to the former, although it is not made use of.",["'die leztere' = geographical, 'die erste' = chronological, by the order given."]],"philippi-s102-003":["In all connected modes of knowledge one must consider the whole before its parts, and, among the parts, the large before the small, and the superior before the subordinate divisions.",["'Ober- als Untereintheilungen' rendered 'superior ... subordinate divisions'."]],"philippi-s103-001":["§434",[]],"philippi-s103-002":["Of history the author says that it belongs to no doctrine; but it is just as much a doctrine as are dogmatic truths. A distinction must be made:",["OCR 'kemer' = *keiner*. 'Doctrin' kept as 'doctrine'."]],"philippi-s103-003":["a. Doctrine [*Doctrin*]: a connection of various modes of knowledge and teachings.",[]],"philippi-s103-004":["b. Discipline [*Disciplin*]: when this connection is brought into a method.",[]],"philippi-s103-005":["c. Science [*Wissenschafft*]: when knowledge has been completely worked out according to such a method.",["OCR 'e.' read as 'c.'."]],"philippi-s103-006":["Science is a complete discipline.",[]],"philippi-s103-007":["In the case of doctrine I direct my attention only to what is taught; in the case of discipline I look to the method.",[]],"philippi-s103-008":["Doctrine and discipline therefore have a place in historical just as much as in dogmatic modes of knowledge. In sciences there must always be doctrine.",[]],"philippi-s103-009":["Criticism [*Critic*] is contrasted with doctrine. The teaching by which I give no addition to a piece of knowledge, but only judge it, is entitled *Dokimastik*.",["'Dokimastik' (art of testing) kept as in the transcript."]],"philippi-s104-001":["§ 435",[]],"philippi-s104-002":["One thinks tumultuously [*tumultuarisch*] when one thinks without method.",["OCR 'Tumult uarisch' read as *tumultuarisch*."]],"philippi-s104-003":["§ 436",[]],"philippi-s104-004":["To meditate [*meditiren*] does not mean to recall modes of knowledge one has had, but to produce new ones one has not yet had. The author believes that one must meditate methodically; but methodical meditation hinders one from procuring sufficient materials for meditation. At the beginning one meditates tumultuously. One must write down whatever occurs to one, when thoughts sometimes come into one's mind that one has never had in one's life. First write down all thoughts as one has them, without order. Afterwards begin to coordinate, and then to subordinate. If one wishes to produce something, one must certainly first complete the skeleton of the system in general, and then divide it into chapters. Hence three labours must precede every piece of composition:",["OCR 'nickt' = *nicht*; 'Außarbeitung' = *Ausarbeitung*. 'man hat' in 'die man in seinem Leben nicht hat' read as 'has had' (sense required). The passage breaks off at a colon; the list follows in the next passages."]],"philippi-s104-005":["1. One writes down all thoughts without order.",[]],"philippi-s104-006":["2. One makes a general plan.",["OCR 'emen' = *einen*."]],"philippi-s104-007":["3. One works out all the parts.",[]],"philippi-s104-008":["As for meditation itself, one must doubtless be naturally inclined to it; yet a beginning is made if one practises, among words, representing things as present in the imagination; if one becomes inwardly aware, as it were, of what the words signify; and if one attends to what goes on within us. Doubting exercises one in reflection, but so does the endeavour to understand rightly everyone's opinion and to be able to put oneself in its position.",["'unter den Worten' is obscure; rendered 'among words' (perhaps 'with words'). 'Einbildung' rendered 'imagination'. 'Vorstellen' = 'representing'."]],"philippi-s104-009":["It is good to alternate meditation with other sciences that are recreative.",[]],"philippi-s105-001":["The Third Main Part. On Learned Exposition. The First Section. On the Use of Words.",[]],"philippi-s105-002":["The nature of words is a worthy object of the philosopher's consideration.",["The German 'Es ist die Beschaffenheit ...' has an irregular word order; smoothed."]],"philippi-s105-003":["The right use of words is that by which they designate a thought exactly.",[]],"philippi-s105-004":["There are no synonyms that are in fact identical. They are all, though often very finely, distinguished; for example, pride [*Stolz*] and haughtiness [*Hochmuth*]. Of a man we may say that he is proud without censuring him; but we always censure him if we say that he is haughty. The proud man is one who will not let himself be despised; the haughty man is one who despises others. Self-love [*Eigenliebe*] and self-conceit [*Selbstliebe*] are also different. Self-love is when one wishes oneself well; self-conceit, however, is when one loves oneself more than others.",["The OCR 'Selbsthebe' is silently corrected to 'Selbstliebe'; the text gives 'Selbsthebe' twice, and the correction follows the parallel with 'Eigenliebe'. 'Self-conceit' is an interpretative rendering, chosen to mark the contrast the passage draws ('loves himself more than others').","'Sej'' corrected to 'sey'. 'Hochmuth/hochmüthig' rendered 'haughtiness/haughty'.","The first sentence is restated slightly: 'öffters aber sehr fein unterschieden' is smoothed into 'though often very finely'."]],"philippi-s105-005":["Whoever, therefore, wishes to express himself synonymically can never express himself with logical perfection; for one word always signifies something other than another word.",["'synonymisch' kept close to the German; the sense is 'by using synonyms indifferently'."]],"philippi-s105-006":["An expression receives different names. If it is known to all, it is entitled *vulgaris*. If it is known only among the learned, it is entitled *receptus*, that is, [received] *ab eruditis*. If an expression is in use only among persons of a certain art, it is entitled *terminus technicus*. A *terminus technicus* is only a makeshift where the *usus communis* does not suffice. Such terms are often used for show [*Ostentation*]. They should, however, be used only within the art, and not at all outside it.",["OCR 'reeeptus' corrected to 'receptus'; 'Wemi' to 'Wenn'; 'vsus' to 'usus'. 'sc.' (scilicet) rendered 'that is' and the bracketed 'received' is supplied by me.","'Kunst' rendered 'art' (craft or discipline) rather than 'science'."]],"philippi-s105-007":["It is a great aid to our understanding [*Verstand*] that we have words even for the commonest concepts [*Begriffe*]; and here reason [*Vernunft*] ought to take pains to seek out the proper meaning that is bound up with the word. All moral expressions are of this kind. If we consider the word 'openheartedness' [*Offenherzigkeit*], we see that it is the result of the sum of many other expressions. The word comprehends in itself that a man conceals nothing; and he conceals nothing because he is conscious of nothing evil in himself. This must flow from goodness [*Bonitaet*].",["OCR slips silently corrected ('soU' to 'soll', 'morahsche' to 'moralische').","'Dieses' is taken to refer to the whole disposition, i.e. the absence of concealment, which 'must flow from' goodness; the antecedent is not wholly certain."]],"philippi-s105-008":["Here we see that the mind [*Gemüth*] must have made many reflections before it was able to find a suitable word for all ideas. So it is in many other cases. The German language is admirable in that it often includes in a single word so many excellent ideas. One also understands the words, and the ideas lying in them, very easily.",["OCR 'liier' corrected to 'hier', 'hegende' to 'liegende'."]],"philippi-s105-009":["It is uncommonly important that one find the right word where one uses it. In moral matters, if one hits upon the right word, one can often make one's thoughts more intelligible than the best definitions and paraphrases would do.",[]],"philippi-s106-001":["§ 449",[]],"philippi-s106-002":["A *terminus inanis* is one whose meaning we believe we understand, but in fact do not understand. For example, the *entelechia* of the ancients and the quintessence of Theophrastus Paracelsus.",["'Theophrasti Paracelsi' is the Latin genitive; rendered as the name in English."]],"philippi-s106-003":["An expression is deceptive (*deceptrix*) where we believe that something is possible and it is nevertheless impossible. For example, the greatest number, the very swiftest motion.",["OCR 'aliergeschwindeste' corrected to 'allergeschwindeste'."]],"philippi-s107-001":["§ 450",[]],"philippi-s107-002":["A *terminus familiaris* is an expression whose sense has never been investigated, because it has never been noticed. Every language has such terms. I say, for example, 'süchtig' [sickly, addicted]. What the word signifies no one can say; yet everyone understands it. Such *termini*, on closer investigation, are also often deceptive expressions.",["OCR 'famüiaris' corrected to 'familiaris'. The gloss on 'süchtig' is mine, in square brackets, since the word is left in German as Kant's example."]],"philippi-s107-003":["§ 452",[]],"philippi-s107-004":["The word-mongering [*Wortkrämerey*] of the learned is a fraudulent trade, in that words are passed off for things. What is more common than that?",[]],"philippi-s108-001":["§ 454",[]],"philippi-s108-002":["An expression is entitled intelligible [*verständlich*] if it can be understood. Something can be intelligible without being distinct [*deutlich*]. An expression becomes intelligible simply by my putting a less confused one in place of a confused one, or a more usual word in place of an unusual one.",["The second sentence of the German is restated slightly for smoothness. 'sezze' read as 'setze'."]],"philippi-s109-001":["§ 457",[]],"philippi-s109-002":["If two expressions have one meaning, or at least if their meanings are not very different, they are entitled synonymous expressions. If an expression has more than one meaning, it is entitled *terminus vagus*, *ambiguus*, *anceps*. The French language has many of these. Ambiguities furnish a play of wit.",["'Wizzes' read as 'Witzes'."]],"philippi-s110-001":["The second section. On the learned manner of writing.",[]],"philippi-s110-002":["*Style* properly means the instrument of a sculptor. Here style means the character of expressing one's thoughts. Everyone has his own style, just as everyone writes his own particular hand. Style is to be considered",["'Bildhauer' rendered 'sculptor'; the Latin *stilus* (writing instrument) is presumably what is meant, but the text speaks of a sculptor. The passage breaks off before the list that follows."]],"philippi-s110-003":["1. in so far as it is suited to the subject-matter, the object.",[]],"philippi-s110-004":["2. to the person (subject) and his station.",[]],"philippi-s110-005":["3. to those for whom one writes.",[]],"philippi-s110-006":["A particular style suits each person, and no one can bring his thoughts into the form of another.",[]],"philippi-s110-007":["Question. How is one to find which style is suited to each?",["'Qu.' expanded as 'Question'."]],"philippi-s110-008":["One must read many books; the book whose manner of writing stands in the greatest sympathy with our thoughts, its style I must make my own.",["OCR '( redanken' read as 'Gedanken'."]],"philippi-s110-009":["Schoolmasters greatly corrupt style, in that they want to teach fine writing, which is nevertheless a matter of genius. For mere imitation, however, if that is what they demand, no genius is needed. This does such great harm that, on leaving school, one needs a long time to forget the school style; and only when the mind has come into its natural form, and one has forgotten it, can one begin to practise oneself in writing.",["The German is irregular here ('und wenn das Gemüth ... und wenn man ihn vergißt, denn kann man erst anfangen'); I have smoothed the repeated 'wenn' clauses into one, losing nothing of substance. 'ihn' is taken to refer to the school style."]],"philippi-s110-010":["Style must be suited to the subject-matter. If this is lofty, the style must also consist of lofty expressions; if it is lowly, the style must indeed not be impure, but yet popular. The order in the presentation of our thoughts, and the arrangement of the concepts, has great influence on liveliness. The affected style is the worst to guard against, and is more harmful than simplicity. The latter merely fails to please; the former disgusts.",["'Einfalt' rendered 'simplicity'; 'populair' kept as 'popular'."]],"philippi-s110-011":["In style there must be no anxiousness shining through in the mere form of expression. Opposed to style is impurity of language, when words are not in current use [*usuell*], or when their combinations run contrary to grammar. Style can be pure without being affected. It is not impermissible to depart from the rules of language when the rules of reason demand it.",["OCR 'St}d' corrected to 'Styl'. 'Dem Styl ist entgegengesetzt' is taken literally, though 'good style' may be meant. 'Vernunfftregeln' rendered 'rules of reason'."]],"philippi-s111-001":["§ 473",[]],"philippi-s111-002":["The suitability [*Füglichkeit*] of the manner of writing consists in its being so constituted that it is not too grand but fitting for the subject. The euphony [*Wollaut*] of the manner of writing is something that belongs more among chimeras than among what is true. The English say that Virgil's poems sound well; but if we heard an Englishman recite the verses, it would be intolerable to our ears. The *sonorum* consists in the measure of the syllables.",["'Sonorum' kept in Latin and italicised as a technical term."]],"philippi-s112-001":["§ 477",[]],"philippi-s112-002":["The congruence [*Congruentz*] of the manner of writing consists in the style's being suited to the subject-matter and especially to the purpose. This can be learned only through experience. Segner has written on arithmetic and geometry, but instead of making it distinct [*deutlich*] he becomes indistinct.",["'vornemhch' read as 'vornehmlich'. 'Segner' is presumably Johann Andreas Segner (a mathematician); 'es' in 'es deutlich zu machen' is taken to refer to the subject-matter."]],"philippi-s113-001":["The third section.",[]],"philippi-s113-002":["On a learned discourse.",[]],"philippi-s114-001":["§ 490",[]],"philippi-s114-002":["On the *donum didacticum* and the requisites of teachers. Two things in general must be found in a teacher. He must possess",["'dono didactico' is the ablative as it stands in the text, 'Vom dono didactico'; I give the nominative in the translation. The numeral '1' before 'sich überhaupt 2 Stücke' looks like an OCR stray and is left out."]],"philippi-s114-003":["a. a condescension [*Condescendenz*] to the weaker, and",[]],"philippi-s114-004":["b. a 'coadscendence' [*Coadscendenz*] to the higher capacities.",["'Coadscendenz' is an odd form (perhaps for *ascendenz*, a rising to); I keep it in quotation marks rather than guess. 'Fähigkeiten' rendered 'capacities'."]],"philippi-s114-005":["I. Every teacher must know how to put himself in the place of his hearers. I can instruct another only through his own understanding; hence the material I have to work upon is one that already lies in the other's head. This is called *condescendence* (*Condescendenz*), and it presupposes that one knows how to put oneself in the place of those whom one teaches. Very few teachers of religion, or other teachers, are able to do so. Furthermore, a teacher must see to what the child shows most genius. It is hard to discover to what it has most inclination and aptitude for comprehension. Many authors have written manuals on the guidance of youth. Basedow is at work on an elementary book on the education of youth. This is a great undertaking. It requires more art to impart first principles to a person than to cultivate further (*excoliren*) the abilities of one who has already learned something.",["\"Fälligkeiten\" is read as OCR for \"Fähigkeiten\" (abilities).","\"Leiter-Weisung\" is rendered \"guidance\"; the compound is odd and the reading is uncertain.","\"Faßlichkeit\" is rendered \"aptitude for comprehension\".","\"Genie\" is rendered \"genius\", in the older sense of natural talent."]],"philippi-s114-006":["II. The second requisite (*Requisitum*) of a teacher is *Coadscendenz*: that one ascend to talents which are raised above the middle rank. He who knows little cannot do this. The best course is to choose a middle degree.",["\"Requisituni\" is read as \"Requisitum\" (OCR slip).","\"Coadscendenz\" is left as printed, since the form is unusual and may be the transcriber's.","\"Es ist der beste Fall\" is rendered freely as \"the best course\"."]],"philippi-s114-007":["But how is one to conduct oneself in public lectures, where one does not know the capacity of the auditors? One can do nothing better than provide at once for able and for poor minds, so that both find something for themselves; the rest they may leave aside. For recreation there is a lecture of amusement. If anything is to please the mind, it must be woven into the subject itself. This is done through good examples. Learning must be the source of the jest. Illustrations and similes must be becoming to a learned man.",["\"öff entheben\" is read as \"öffentlichen\", and \"se}m\" as \"seyn\" (OCR slips).","\"Auditorum\" is rendered \"auditors\"; the genitive plural is as in the source."]],"philippi-s115-001":["§ 493",[]],"philippi-s115-002":["The question here is what method one must employ in order to refute someone. To impart an opinion to a person is different from refuting a person's opinion. In the first case the subject is empty; in the second I must first empty it. Before any instruction I must first have the data showing wherein the other errs. Every error is to be regarded as a phenomenon which deserves an explanation. I must first find out how he came to fall into the error. Then I must also track down the part of truth that is contained in his error. For no one will tolerate being told that he has erred wholly. Nor is that ever the case; and if I deny him all sound reason, it is absurd to want to instruct or refute him at all. To reply to something (*auf etwas antworten*) and to answer something (*etwas beantworten*) are different. A reply can be made to everything, but that everything has been answered is seldom accomplished in disputations. To answer is to bring to conviction. In all learned controversies the aim should be to bring out the truth. It is absurd that truth should be brought out by strife; it is brought out only through the agreement which is perhaps mediated by the strife. *Modus litem finiendi est amicabilis compositio* [the way of ending a dispute is amicable settlement]. I must show what is true in the other's judgment and what is false in my own. Then let us see whether we understand each other well, and thereupon it will appear whether the one proposition is not true in part.",["\"dannists\" is read as \"dann ists\"; \"Averden\" as \"werden\"; \"an dem\" in \"niemals an dem\" is taken as \"the case\" (idiom).","The English in square brackets after the Latin maxim is supplied.","The last sentence is a little loose in the German; \"partial\" is taken adverbially as \"in part\"."]],"philippi-s116-001":["§ 501",[]],"philippi-s116-002":["A dispute about words (*Wortstreit*) occurs when persons believe themselves to be contesting different opinions and yet are at variance only in words, while in the thing itself they are at one. Of predestination some have believed that it was more a dispute about words than about the thing.",[]],"philippi-s117-001":["§ 504",[]],"philippi-s117-002":["An *argumentum ad hominem* is an argument which is indeed not true for everyone, but nevertheless serves to reduce someone to silence. This is so when, for example, I derive something *ex concessis* [from what has been conceded] and from the particular propositions which another holds. These are good means of getting rid of an opponent and ending the dispute, but not of finding the truth.",["The gloss in square brackets after *ex concessis* is supplied."]],"philippi-s118-001":["§ 509",[]],"philippi-s118-002":["*Retorsiones* and *reprehensiones* occur in personal disputes. Distinctions serve well to extricate oneself from a difficulty. An *instantia* [instance, counter-example] arises when something is used as general and I show that it is not general.",["\"sieh\" is read as \"sich\" (OCR slip).","The gloss on *instantia* is supplied."]],"philippi-s119-001":["§ 512",[]],"philippi-s119-002":["A defence which is directed to the person is entitled an *apologia*; if it is directed to the judgments of others, it is entitled a *refutatio*.",["\"Urt heile\" is read as \"Urtheile\"; Kant's Latin forms are given in italics, with *Apologie* rendered by the Latin."]],"philippi-s120-001":["§ 514",[]],"philippi-s120-002":["A formal disputation is the procedure in which, in the presence of learned persons, one offers certain propositions to be defended against all objections. Disputation might yield great benefit if it were not a *certamen personarum* [contest of persons], and if one sought the truth not through strife but through agreement. In disputing, one must not let the opponent slip away from the *status controversiae* [the point at issue]; otherwise one falls into side-issues. One can soon confuse the opponent by retorsion, by argumentation, by questioning him so that he must explain himself, and by distinctions. The opponent can be prepared for the possible questions and answers. In all disputations language is the most essential thing; it seems to be a *exercitium scholasticum*. The chief purpose is merely to keep a dead language still alive. The ancients called the art of disputation *dialectica eristica*, combative dialectic.",["\"Gegen wart\" is read as \"Gegenwart\", and \"gerät h man\" as \"gerät man\" (OCR slips).","The English glosses in square brackets after the Latin terms are supplied.","\"Preiß giebt\" is rendered \"offers\" in the sense of exposing propositions to attack."]],"philippi-s120-003":["The Fourth Section.",[]],"philippi-s121-001":["On Learned Writings. § 518",[]],"philippi-s121-002":["All learned writings are either historical or dogmatic. A writing may be historical in respect of its matter, when it deals with something that has actually happened, or is a cognition of the individual; and it may also be so in respect of its form. A form is entitled historical when it is not rational, that is, when I do not derive something from general knowledge of grounds. The instruction of children in religion, for example, is historical in form; it is subjectively historical, although objectively dogmatic.",["\"Indiuiduo\" is read as \"Individuo\"; \"subiectiv\" and \"obiectiv\" are modernised in the English.","\"Kenntniß\" is rendered \"cognition\" to keep it distinct from \"Erkenntniß\", rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"philippi-s121-003":["It is wholly perverse to present *dogmata* historically; for they can spring only from understanding and reason, and hence must be grasped by the understanding and known rationally.",["\"undVernunfft\" is read as \"und Vernunfft\"."]],"philippi-s121-004":["Historical modes of knowledge are to be grafted upon the sensible faculty of knowledge, upon imagination and memory. A knowledge is entitled historical-dogmatic in respect of form when the *data* are *dogmata* but are presented historically; in respect of matter, when some of it is historical and some of it *dogmata*.",["\"Erkenntnißkrafft\" is rendered \"faculty of knowledge\".","\"Historisch dogmatisch\" is hyphenated in the English for clarity."]],"philippi-s121-005":["Everything that happens is considered in connection with space and time. When what happens is considered in so far as it occurs at different times, this is entitled history; in so far as it occurs at the same time, it belongs to the field of geography. There are different kinds of geography as well as of history. One may enumerate the things of the world in so far as they occur at the same time; this then belongs to natural or physical geography. One may relate what has occurred in nature at different times; this is then entitled natural history. A political geography would be the science in which the constitution of the state, as it is at one time, is considered.",["\"i>t\" is read as \"ist\" (OCR slip)."]],"philippi-s121-006":["A theological geography would be one in which the religions of different peoples living at one time were considered. Alexander Ross has written such a geography.",[]],"philippi-s121-007":["A moral geography would be one which weighs the customs of peoples, as they are now or as they were in ancient times.",["The German has no main verb of being; \"would be\" is carried over from the preceding sentence."]],"philippi-s121-008":["[Hi]story, that is, the consideration of events that have happened in succession, cannot be separated from geography, any more than geography can be separated from it. For every event is connected with circumstances of place and with other things; and I cannot properly know the present state if I do not know the changes that preceded it.",["The opening is garbled in the OCR (\"( '<< schichte\"); it is read as \"Geschichte\", and the missing letters are marked by square brackets in the English.","\"Geschichte\" and \"Historie\" are not distinguished here; the sense is that of history."]],"philippi-s121-009":["There is also a geography of learning, when I show at what stage the sciences now stand. For geography comprehends everything that is in the world at the same time.",[]],"philippi-s121-010":["But if I show what fortunes (*fata*) learning has had, that belongs to history.",["\"fata\" is kept in Latin, with the English beside it."]],"philippi-s122-001":["§ 519",[]],"philippi-s122-002":["The distinction the author makes in dividing history into political, ecclesiastical, learned and private history is determined by the different objects of historical knowledge. There might also be a history of morals, which would contain the manner of common life and also immoral matters, for example, how people ate; this would certainly be very fine. The learned Hume found that among the Romans the rate of interest was already 6 per cent, and in Poland as much as 10 per cent. Hamberger has written a history of the sciences, and Winckelmann a history of sculpture and the plastic arts. Biography is the life of a single subject. All history, of whatever kind it may be, ought to have the aim of extending reason. It ought to furnish the material for the use of reason.",["OCR corrections: 'Karchen-' read as 'Kirchen-' (ecclesiastical); 'Winkelmami' read as Winckelmann; 'gegeßen' read as 'gegessen'.","The OCR text 'z. E. \"wie sie gegeßen enthielte' is garbled (a stray quotation mark). I have restructured it as 'for example, how people ate'; the reading is doubtful.","'Hamberger' is retained as printed (presumably Georg Christoph Hamberger, historian of learning); I have not altered the name.","Restated 'welche … enthielte' as a main clause for smoothness."]],"philippi-s122-003":["§ 520",[]],"philippi-s122-004":["History is entitled pragmatic when it is useful through general rules. This usefulness may stand in relation either to speculation or to practical reason. If one merely learns the names of rulers and knows their reigns, this furnishes little material for the use of reason, that is, for speculation or for practical rules. Yet in the beginning one must abstract from the use of reason and, in good hope, provide oneself with much historical knowledge. Newton, at any rate, was able to draw a conclusion from this alone, having found that each prince on the average reigns twenty years.",["'Vernunfft gebrauch d. i.' read as 'Vernunftgebrauch, d. i.' (OCR word break).","'Newton' is as in the transcript (the anecdote is commonly attributed to him)."]],"philippi-s122-005":["Learned history becomes pragmatic when one considers learning merely in its relation to human reason, and looks to its growth or to the causes by which it has been held back. It has been observed that great models set the sciences back for a time, for everything then merely imitates the model and no one strives to become original. So it has happened with Aristotle, Leibniz, Descartes and Newton.",["'Cartes' rendered as Descartes."]],"philippi-s122-006":["There are two methods of composing dogmatic writings, the tumultuary [*tumultuarisch*] or the methodical; the latter method produces a system. A system is any whole. Systematic writings are distinguished from tumultuary writings in that the former constitute a coherent whole. Extracts and epitomes are actual systems. In dogmatic writings the *principia* of the *dogmata* must be set forth, then weighed *in concreto*, and thereupon something historical must be interspersed in order to give evidence [*Evidenz*].",["'prineipia' corrected to *principia*.","*Evidenz* is rendered 'evidence' in the sense of clarity or certainty; the sense is somewhat uncertain.","Latin terms kept in italics."]],"philippi-s123-001":["§ 525",[]],"philippi-s123-002":["The method by which one does not draw a piece of knowledge from its own sources, but takes it from those who have drawn from the source, is entitled compiling. History is compiled if I have written it together not from the first sources but from later ones. One can also compile philosophical propositions, by reading together what others have thought through reason. Yet one shows skill when one gathers together with prudent choice the most important matters from authors who possess great acuteness, from the most thorough histories. The plagiarist [*Plagiarius*] is to be distinguished from the compiler: he pretends to have drawn from the first learned sources themselves, although he merely presents the product of another. Among the learned this is what gross theft is among citizens.",["'spätem' read as 'späteren' (later sources); 'Qvellen' normalised.","'Bürgerlichen' rendered 'citizens' (ordinary civil life); 'Dies … Diebstal' rendered as an analogy."]],"philippi-s124-001":["§ 526",[]],"philippi-s124-002":["The French are especially of the character that among them the sciences grow more *extensive* than *intensive*. Much understanding is required for epitomes, in order to be able to choose what is most important and most easily grasped.",["The Latin *extensive*/*intensive* are kept in italics, but the transcript's emphasis is not recoverable, so italic here marks the Latin terms only.","'epitomis' rendered 'epitomes'."]],"philippi-s125-001":["Fourth Main Part. On the Character of a Scholar.",[]],"philippi-s125-002":["The character of a scholar is twofold: the character of the head and the character of the heart. The former concerns knowledge, the latter inclination. The character of the heart has a great connection with the character of the mind [*Geist*], if learning is to be promoted. The constitution of the heart has a great influence on the direction of the understanding, and it is therefore not a matter of indifference even in merely speculative sciences.",["'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; 'Geist' kept in brackets since the shift from 'Kopf' to 'Geist' is the transcriber's.","'dieselbe' resolved as referring to the constitution of the heart."]],"philippi-s126-001":["§ 530",[]],"philippi-s126-002":["The character of the head concerns understanding, reason and taste. The faculty of imitation is precisely opposed to the faculty of genius. Those who possess the capacities of genius at times have no spirit of imitation at all, whereas this is often present in a high degree in those who show no genius. The Russians are said to have the fault that genius is lacking in them, and that for this very reason they cannot teach others; hence science always dies out among them. Genius is required for philosophy, and likewise for the fine arts, but for useful sciences the spirit of imitation. Mathematics needs only imitation. Learning and good taste are distinct from one another.",["OCR 'Wissenschaf f timmer' read as 'Wissenschaft immer'; the reading is fairly certain.","'Vermögen' rendered 'faculty'; 'Fähigkeiten' rendered 'capacities'."]],"philippi-s127-001":["§ 533",[]],"philippi-s127-002":["Mother wit [*Mutterwitz*] is the natural capacity of sound understanding and sound reason. This cannot be learned; what is afterwards learned is entitled scholastic wit [*Schulwitz*].",["'Mutterwiz' and 'Schulwiz' kept in German in brackets because no English term is exact."]],"philippi-s127-003":["All instruction already presupposes a certain knowledge, or at least the capacity for it. Thus scholastic wit supposes mother wit. It is more tolerable to lack scholastic wit than to have it without mother wit; for then what has been learned is fine in the man, but he himself is absurd in its application. Hence comes pedantry, and through scholastic wit one becomes even more absurd than otherwise, because one has more materials. Scholastic wit is a perfecting of mother wit.",["OCR 'daim' read as 'dann'; 'Materiahen' read as 'Materialien'.","'Pedanterie' rendered 'pedantry'."]],"philippi-s127-004":["§ 540",[]],"philippi-s127-005":["Among the particular learned exercises belongs",["The passage breaks off as a lead-in; the enumeration follows in the next passage."]],"philippi-s127-006":["I. Learning. This takes place through oral instruction or through the reading of books. One learns by either producing new modes of knowledge or reproducing modes of knowledge one has had. To teach is properly to let modes of knowledge be produced through others. The learner is passive in this respect, but he must also be active; he must have receptivity [*Receptivität*], that is, capacity of understanding, and must also apply it.",["'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge' per the method.","'Receptiuitaet' corrected to *Receptivität*; 'Verstandesfähigkeit' rendered 'capacity of understanding'."]],"philippi-s127-007":["Oral instruction has a great advantage over the written, for the oral is not so artificial as the written. It is also more easily grasped, since there is one act of the mind fewer than in reading. In reading I must also hear in thought.",["'Handlung des Gemüths' rendered 'act of the mind'."]],"philippi-s128-001":["§ 541",[]],"philippi-s128-002":["II. The reading of learned writings.",["OCR 'IL' read as 'II.'"]],"philippi-s128-003":["Very much depends first upon the order in which one reads and upon the choice of writings with which one begins. The question is: should compendia or large works be read first? One should not begin with compendia that contain many thoughts in little space, but with those that contain few thoughts in a wide space. In history and mathematics one must first make oneself acquainted with the general. One must first proceed synthetically; then one is afterwards in a position to proceed analytically as well. Historical books chosen for reading must at first concern not so much the exact events as the general ones.",["'die genaue Begebenheiten … die allgemeinen' left elliptical: 'the general ones' supplies 'events' by judgment; the German is slightly irregular."]],"philippi-s128-004":["One must follow a certain order, and read books of speculation in the morning and evening, historical books at midday. One must read much and, so to speak, digest what one has read, and the field must not be changed very often. Every book must be read at least twice, once quickly, marking for oneself the passages which need elucidation. Some books are of great importance and demand much reflection; these one must read often, for example Hume, Rousseau, Locke, who can be regarded as a grammar for the understanding, and Montesquieu on the spirit of the laws.",["'Montesquieu von dem Geist der Gesezze' rendered as the title *De l'esprit des lois* in sense."]],"philippi-s129-001":["§ 544",[]],"philippi-s129-002":["Too frequent repetition is just as harmful as too long an interruption.",["'Aussezzung' rendered 'interruption' (suspension of study); the sense is a judgment."]],"philippi-s130-001":["§ 546",[]],"philippi-s130-002":["Imitation in matters of style is not advisable.",[]],"philippi-s131-001":["§ 548",[]],"philippi-s131-002":["No rule can be given for the discovery [*Erfindung*] of new truths.",[]],"philippi-s132-001":["§ 549",[]],"philippi-s132-002":["An autodidact [*Autodidaktus*] is one who makes himself learned through meditation and reading. This is always a great genius. Oral instruction is nevertheless always to be preferred. One can make more reflections.",["'Avtodidaktus' corrected to *Autodidaktus*."]],"philippi-s133-001":["§ 553",[]],"philippi-s133-002":["Scholarly diligence signifies the strength of the exertion which one applies to learning. Man is by nature indolent, and idleness is itself the final end of his diligence. The prospect of future repose is the spur that sets men in activity. Yet the greatness and worthiness of the objects can greatly excite diligence. Scholarly diligence, when it continues, is entitled steady diligence. Different from this is vehement diligence, which consists in an excessively strong exertion that lasts only a short time. The mind suffers under vehement diligence; the representations soon die away, for the mind will not be coerced. Variation of objects greatly disturbs diligence. If one has to be enticed to something merely by novelty, that is an indication of a lukewarm and slackened industry.",["'Mas zu lernen' read as 'was zu lernen' (OCR slip); translated 'to learning', which is the sense the clause requires; the reading is not certain.","'Gemüth' rendered 'mind'; 'Vorstellungen' 'representations'; 'heißt' 'is entitled'.","'Objekte' (Kant's Latinate form here) rendered 'objects'; the uncorrected OCR 'dauret' is the transcript's own spelling for 'dauert'.","I divided nothing here; the original sentences are short and were kept as they stand."]],"philippi-s134-001":["§554",[]],"philippi-s134-002":["A student must first weigh well the end and the extent of his powers. It is a pity that others prescribe the end for us, and that one does not yet know these [powers] oneself when one begins to study. One must never study with vehement diligence, but one must never cease to learn. One first knows how to judge what one has to learn when one is near the end. Hence that philosopher complained that one learns to live only when one must leave this world. It also belongs to the character of the scholar that he be a person without title, station or civil rank; above all, that he be a man of all estates, that his knowledge be of universal use, and that it not be suited merely to the form of the schools. To this end he must associate with men. It is only to be regretted that ordinary conversations have nothing but shallow matters as their aim, and that in our parts it is almost pedantic to let science [Wissenschafft] enter into a conversation and to make its extension the aim. In this respect the French and the English have a great advantage over the Germans, in that they seek their dignity in learning [Gelehrsamkeit]. The ultimate end of learning should be to give the human race its true form, to free it from prejudices, to refine morals, and to elevate the powers of the soul. Then it is a benefaction to the human race; but if it is used merely for operation [Operation], it is to be regarded only as a kind of luxury.",["Silently corrected OCR slips: 'wemi' to 'wenn', 'istt' to 'ist', 'sejm' to 'sein'.","'Kräffte' rendered 'powers' (Vermögen/Kräfte); 'Seelenkräffte' 'powers of the soul'. 'Erkenntniß' 'knowledge'.","'daß man sie selbst noch nicht kennt': 'sie' is ambiguous (the ends or the powers); I took it as the powers [Kräffte], by judgment, and supplied the bracket.","'hauptsächlich daß er ein Mann von allen Ständen sey' is joined to the preceding clause by a semicolon and 'that' repeated, to smooth the German's loose construction.","'Operation' is kept in the original with the German in brackets; it is probably meant as mere practice or routine in the sense of a craft, but the sense is not certain.","'Wissenschafft' is rendered 'science' in the broad sense of systematic learning."]],"philippi-s135-001":["End of the Logic.",[]]};
